          "text": "Let me understand the situation. The user is Irina, a student in a preparation program for an internship at Inobitec. She's starting stage 16 — CMake (meta-build system). She's given me her context prompt.\n\nI'm in plan mode — read-only. I need to research the current state and build a plan for moving from Makefile to CMake.\n\nLet me first note the critical issue: the files in the repo are in CP1251 encoding (the Makefile and source files show garbled characters in my reads, because the tool reads as UTF-8 presumably). This is a known aspect of the project — sources are in CP1251 per § layer 4. I should be careful about encoding when I eventually write files.\n\nWait, actually from the journal: \"перевела конспекты на UTF-8\" — the notes were converted to UTF-8, but the code files (Makefile, main.cpp, etc.) — let me check. The Makefile shows garbled cyrillic. The main.cpp shows garbled. So those files are still CP1251. The AGENTS.md and README.md render fine (UTF-8). Actually README.md shows fine Russian. Hmm, but README might be UTF-8. Let me check encodings.\n\nActually, let me note: `.editorconfig` has charset setting. Let me read .editorconfig and .clang-format.\n\nKey facts gathered so far:\n\n**Structure:**\n- Tasks t1-t4, each in `_c` and `_cpp` directories with `main.c` / `main.cpp`\n- `common/` has geometry and string_utils in both C and C++ versions, with COMMON_API export macros (COMMON_STATIC / COMMON_EXPORTS / default dllimport)\n- `tests/` has run_case.cpp and check.cpp tools\n- `build/` has CONFIG subdirs (debug/release)\n- Makefile is the current build system (stage 15)\n\n**Makefile architecture:**\n- Windows (MSVC cl) and Linux (gcc/g++) blocks via ifeq\n- Debug/release via CONFIG variable\n- Static lib for t1-t2 (COMMON_STATIC), dynamic dll for t3-t4\n- On Windows: dynamic = dll + import lib; grouped targets `&:`\n- Tools run_case/check built with filter-out of /Zi /fsanitize\n- format and format-check phony targets with clang-format\n- test target: builds .ok files via RUN_CASE (exit code checks) and CHECK (--equal / --contains)\n- SET_DLL_PATH sets PATH/LD_LIBRARY_PATH\n\n**Stage 16 goal (CMake):**\nFrom AGENTS.md §5 layer 16: Мотивация после переносимого Makefile — не «переносимость» (она уже есть), а: автоматические header-зависимости (то, что на слое 15 делалось руками), абстракция toolchain-флагов (ifeq-блоки исчезают), `find_package` + vcpkg для зависимостей, `target_precompile_headers`, генерация под разные сборщики и IDE.\n\nAlso from §4.9: одна точка входа — с появлением make — цели make; now with CMake, `cmake --build build`. §4.1: одна система сборки — переходный сценарий удаляется когда появляется сборщик. So the Makefile should be deleted when CMake fully replaces it.\n\nFrom journal 2026-08-31: \"из gitignore убрала ссылки на ref-ы\" — done.\n\nCurrent date in env: Mon Aug 31 2026. Journal last entry 2026-08-31. So we're at the point right after. Wait, but there are more journal entries... The journal says entries through 2026-08-31. Today is 2026-08-31 per env. So we're continuing on this day.\n\n**Important considerations for the plan:**\n\n1. **CMake availability**: cmake 3.30.5-msvc23 is installed (bundled with VS 2022 at D:\\VisualStudio\\Community\\Common7\\IDE\\CommonExtensions\\Microsoft\\CMake\\CMake\\bin\\cmake.exe). Good. Need to make sure it's on PATH in the Native Tools prompt... Actually the VS-bundled CMake is available. We can use it.\n\n2. **Project structure for CMake**: We have a project with 8 executables (4 tasks × C/C++) that share common code, plus test tools (run_case, check). \n\nArchitecture options:\n- Single top-level CMakeLists.txt with everything\n- Subdirectory CMakeLists per task — probably overkill; tasks are single-file mains.\n\nThe core question is how to model `common` as a library. Currently:\n- t1-t2 link statically (COMMON_STATIC)\n- t3-t4 link dynamically (dll/import lib on Windows, .so on Linux)\n\nIn CMake, the idiomatic approach:\n- `add_library(common_c STATIC ...)` and `add_library(common_cpp STATIC ...)`\n- `add_library(common_c_shared SHARED ...)` etc.\n\nBut wait — there's a subtlety. The current Makefile builds the SAME common sources into both a static lib (for t1-t2) and a shared lib (for t3-t4). The difference is only the COMMON_STATIC vs COMMON_EXPORTS define. CMake can handle this: two targets from the same sources, different defines.\n\nActually, is the whole static-vs-shared split still needed at CMake stage? This is a teaching decision. In CMake, one typically has ONE version of the library. The dual build (static + shared) was a teaching exercise at layer 9 (separate stages, static lib, dynamic lib). At stage 16 with CMake, the question is whether to keep the dual build or simplify.\n\nThis is worth asking the user / raising as a design decision. Actually the plan says layer 16 motivation includes \"генерация под разные сборщики и IDE\" — not specifically about static/shared. But keeping both static and shared in CMake is straightforward.\n\nHmm, but there's a deeper question. Let me think about what's the cleanest CMake design that preserves all the learned behavior:\n\nOption A: One `add_library(common_cpp SHARED)` + one `add_library(common_c STATIC)` etc. But then t1-t2 that currently use static would link the shared one. Changing behavior.\n\nOption B: Keep both variants:\n- `common_cpp_static`, `common_cpp_shared`, `common_c_static`, `common_c_shared`\n- t1-t2 link static variants; t3-t4 link shared variants.\n- On Windows shared → DLL + import lib; CMake handles the .lib import library automatically.\n- The COMMON_API macro: for static target define COMMON_STATIC; for shared target define COMMON_EXPORTS when building it, and nothing (→ dllimport) when consuming. Actually with CMake, `target_compile_definitions(common_cpp_shared PRIVATE COMMON_EXPORTS)` and consumers just `target_link_libraries(... common_cpp_shared)` — CMake automatically adds the import lib to the link line on Windows. The consumer's compile needs COMMON_API to be `dllimport`. Since the consumer does NOT define COMMON_STATIC and does NOT define COMMON_EXPORTS, the default branch → dllimport. \n\nFor the static consumer: define COMMON_STATIC via `target_compile_definitions(common_cpp_static PUBLIC COMMON_STATIC)`? Wait — but the consumers are separate targets (main exes). If the static lib target has `PUBLIC COMMON_STATIC`, then when main links it, the define propagates to main's compile. That's exactly the modern CMake way! \n\nActually careful: For static lib, the define needed at consumer compile time is COMMON_STATIC (so that COMMON_API → empty). If we set `target_compile_definitions(common_cpp_static PUBLIC COMMON_STATIC)`, then:\n- building the static lib: COMMON_STATIC defined → COMMON_API empty ✓\n- consuming: PUBLIC propagates → COMMON_STATIC defined → dllimport avoided, empty macro ✓\n\nFor shared lib:\n- `target_compile_definitions(common_cpp_shared PRIVATE COMMON_EXPORTS)` → building the lib: dllexport ✓\n- consuming: no definitions propagate → default → dllimport ✓ on Windows. On Linux COMMON_API is empty anyway. ✓\n\nThat's clean and idiomatic CMake. The ifeq blocks and COMMON_FLAG target-specific variables disappear.\n\n3. **Debug/Release**: CMake has CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE (single-config generators like NMake Makefiles, Makefiles, MinGW) vs multi-config generators (Visual Studio). For console workflow with cl, we'd use a single-config generator — the classic \"NMake Makefiles\" generator, or better \"Ninja\" (is Ninja available? cmake -G Ninja requires ninja.exe). Let me check. Actually with MSVC, we might want `cmake -G \"NMake Makefiles\"` or `cmake -G Ninja`. But the stage says console-based, no IDE. The user's workflow: vcvars64 then cl. For CMake single-config: `cmake -B build/debug -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug -G Ninja` then `cmake --build build/debug`.\n\nWait — but §4.7 says режим (Debug/Release/Coverage) — параметр сборки с раздельными каталогами артефактов. With single-config generators, build type is baked at configure time via CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE, and separate build dirs for Debug/Release. That matches \"раздельные каталоги артефактов\" (build/debug and build/release, as currently in Makefile).\n\nActually the current Makefile uses `build/$(CONFIG)` with CONFIG=debug/release. So CMake equivalent: `cmake -B build/debug -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug`, `cmake -B build/release -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release`.\n\nBut CMake has its own preset system: CMakePresets.json. Given layer 16 mentions \"генерация под разные сборщики и IDE\" and the learning goals, presets might be nice but might be a later addition. Let me think about what's pedagogically right.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the generator choice. Options on Windows with MSVC:\n- `-G \"NMake Makefiles\"` — uses nmake as the underlying build tool. Works in vcvars environment.\n- `-G Ninja` — needs ninja; VS ships ninja.exe? Actually VS 2022 ships ninja at `...\\Common7\\IDE\\CommonExtensions\\Microsoft\\CMake\\Ninja\\ninja.exe` I believe. CMake's VS-bundled version... Actually the bundled CMake directory usually has Ninja too. Let me check.\n- `-G \"Visual Studio 17 2022\"` — multi-config, but that's IDE territory; the course says no IDE on this stage. Also it generates .sln/.vcxproj — \"кнопка Build\" territory. Not appropriate.\n\nNinja is the modern standard and is faster; but it's an extra tool to install. NMake Makefiles uses nmake which ships with VS. Hmm, nmake syntax was explicitly excluded in the course (\"Родной для MSVC nmake в курсе не используем\"). But that was about writing Makefiles by hand, not about CMake generating nmake files. Still, pedagogically, Ninja is the industry standard companion to CMake. \n\nActually, wait — is CMake's default generator on Windows for `cl`? If you run `cmake -B build` with MSVC cl on PATH, CMake may pick the Visual Studio generator if VS is installed (default). We should explicitly choose. Let me check what generators are available. But I'm in plan mode — I can run read-only commands like `cmake --help`. Let me check `cmake --help` for available generators and check for ninja.\n\n4. **Tests**: Currently tests are orchestrated in the Makefile via run_case/check tools, producing .ok files. In CMake, the idiomatic approach is `add_test()` + `ctest`. The test targets (run_case, check) become part of the build. Then define add_test for each case: exit-code tests via run_case, and file-compare tests via check.\n\nBut there's a design question: ctest runs each test as a separate command. For exit-code tests, run_case wraps the actual program and checks exit code. In ctest we can use `set_tests_properties(... PASS_REGULAR_EXPRESSION)` or just run run_case and rely on its exit code. Actually simplest: each ctest invokes `run_case \"<cmd>\" <expected>`; run_case returns 0/1; ctest treats nonzero as failure. That works and preserves the two-channel verification (exit code + stderr message via --contains check).\n\nBut the current Makefile also writes .ok files and runs programs with input/output redirection inside run_case command string. In ctest, the working directory / command line is per-test. We need input files (tests/input_data) and expect files (tests/expect). These are data files; ctest can reference them by path.\n\nHowever — there's a subtlety about running t3/t4 which need the DLL path (SET_DLL_PATH). In ctest, we can set `ENVIRONMENT` property per test (e.g., PATH). Or better: use CMake's `$<TARGET_FILE_DIR>` generator expressions to compute the path to the dll directory.\n\nActually, cleaner: instead of run_case wrapping a full shell command with redirections, we could refactor the tests to be more CMake-idiomatic:\n- exit-code test: `add_test(NAME t1_cpp_low COMMAND run_case ...)` — run_case takes a command string... but the redirection `< file > nul 2> nul` inside run_case's std::system is Windows shell-specific and already has Linux handling via `$@`. Hmm.\n\nLet me think about how to best preserve behavior. The run_case tool's command argument includes redirections like `< tests/input_data/t1_abc > nul 2> nul`. On Windows this relies on cmd. On Linux the Makefile passes `< ... > /dev/null`. This is baked into the Makefile recipes, not into run_case. So when moving to CMake, the redirections need to be expressed somewhere.\n\nOption 1 (minimal change): Keep run_case taking a full shell command. In CMake tests, `add_test(NAME x COMMAND run_case \"prog < input > out 2> err\" 65)`. The command string must be shell-syntax; on Windows ctest uses cmd? No — ctest runs the executable directly via CreateProcess, not through a shell. So `< file` redirection in the argument string would NOT work — the string is passed as an argument to run_case, and run_case does std::system(argv[1]) which spawns cmd. So actually the redirection happens inside run_case's std::system call. That works cross-platform already (std::system uses the platform shell). \n\nSo Option 1: pass the whole command as one argument (need to be careful with quoting — the current Makefile passes the entire `$(call WCMD,...)` as a single quoted arg to run_case). In ctest, `add_test(NAME t1_cpp_low COMMAND ${RUN_CASE} \"${RUN_CASE_ARGS}\" ...)` — hmm, but the command string contains the path to the exe. We can use generator expressions like `$<TARGET_FILE:...>`. But mixing generator expressions inside a shell command string is doable: `\"$<TARGET_FILE:t1_dist_matrix_cpp> < ...\"`.\n\nHmm wait, there's an issue: on Windows, generator expression `$<TARGET_FILE:...>` gives path with forward slashes or backslashes? It gives the actual path which CMake uses. In cmd, forward slashes usually work for command invocations. Actually in the current Makefile they convert to backslashes (WCMD). In ctest, the command is run by run_case via cmd's std::system. cmd generally handles forward-slash paths for running exes fine (e.g., `build/debug/t1_dist_matrix_cpp/main.exe` works in cmd? Actually cmd is