run program, capture stderr to file, then check contains\" pattern: \n```\nadd_test(NAME t3_cpp_empty_case\n  COMMAND run_case \"prog < input > nul 2> outfile\" 66\n  COMMAND check --contains outfile expected)\n```\nIf run_case fails, ctest fails before running check. \n\nBut wait — there's a subtlety: the order of stderr/stdout redirection in the run_case command. Fine.\n\nActually, a cleaner CMake approach: instead of redirection inside the command string, use ctest's per-test output capture? ctest can capture output but doesn't redirect to a named file easily. The redirection-in-command approach via run_case is simplest.\n\nAnother subtlety: On Windows, `2> file` inside std::system cmd works. On Linux, `2> file` works in /bin/sh. The command string differs (nul vs /dev/null, backslashes). We already have the WCMD subst in Makefile. In CMake we can do platform checks with if(WIN32). The test commands become CMake code with if(WIN32) choosing nul vs /dev/null and backslash handling.\n\nHmm, this is getting complex. This is the reality of preserving the current test matrix (about 60 test cases) in CMake. Let me count: t1: 6+6, t2: 8+8, t3: 14+14, t4: 20+20 = ~96 .ok tests. That's a lot of add_test calls. We need a way to write them without tons of duplication — a CMake function! `function(add_case name exe input expect exitcode mode)` that generates the add_test. CMake functions can encapsulate the pattern.\n\nThis is actually the pedagogical payoff: CMake functions (like make's `define`/`call`, but structured). And it's a good lesson: don't hand-write 96 add_test calls — write a function once.\n\n5. **format / format-check**: In CMake, we can add custom targets. There's no standard built-in format target. Options:\n- `add_custom_target(format COMMAND clang-format -i ...)`\n- `add_custom_target(format-check COMMAND ...)`\n\nWe can use CMake's file(GLOB ...) for the source list, but §4.1 policy... Actually the course policy: format target in the build system. With CMake we can use `file(GLOB_RECURSE)` or `target_sources`. But GLOB is discouraged for sources (CMake won't track new files without reconfigure). For format-check purposes, globbing is fine (just formatting all sources). For the actual build sources, we should list them explicitly (or use `CONFIGURE_DEPENDS` on GLOB). Best practice for a learning stage: list sources explicitly per target. That also teaches that GLOB hides changes (a real CMake gotcha worth learning — new file added, GLOB doesn't re-run, build silent-succeeds without the file).\n\nActually there's a pedagogical decision here about whether to teach GLOB + CONFIGURE_DEPENDS or explicit lists. The course values \"не магией\". I'd lean explicit source lists for targets, GLOB for the format-check helper (since it's a check, not a build dependency). Or even explicit lists everywhere for consistency. Let me think... format-check needs to know all .c/.cpp/.h/.hpp. GLOB_RECURSE for that is natural and harmless.\n\n6. **Versioning / other**: Layer 12 (kVersion + --version) — the tasks don't have --version yet; that's stage-2 material per §6.1 задел. Not needed now.\n\n7. **CMakeLists structure decision**: \n- Single top-level CMakeLists.txt vs subdirectory per component.\n- Given the project is small (8 exes + common libs + 2 tools), a single CMakeLists.txt at root is simplest and most readable. But we could also do `add_subdirectory(common)`, `add_subdirectory(tests)`. Hmm. The course's next stage (17: E2E with generator, 18: vcpkg find_package, 19: GoogleTest) will benefit from subdirectory structure. But premature. \n\nActually, let me reconsider. Layer 16 motivation explicitly: \"абстракция toolchain-флагов (ifeq-блоки исчезают)\". The ifeq blocks in Makefile are per-platform flags. In CMake these are handled by the generator + built-in variables (CMAKE_C_FLAGS_DEBUG, etc.) and by setting them per-target. We should map:\n- MSVC: `/W4 /permissive- /EHsc /std:c++latest` → target_compile_options with MSVC guard: `target_compile_options(... PRIVATE /W4 /permissive- /EHsc)` and `set_target_properties CXX_STANDARD 23, CXX_STANDARD_REQUIRED ON, CXX_EXTENSIONS OFF`.\n- GCC/Clang: `-Wall -Wextra`, CXX_STANDARD 23.\n- Debug flags: MSVC `/Od /Zi /MDd /fsanitize=address`; GCC `-O0 -g -fsanitize=address`.\n- Release: MSVC `/O2 /Zi /DNDEBUG /MD`; GCC `-O2 -g -DNDEBUG`.\n\nCMake has per-config flags built in: CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_DEBUG etc. We can set:\n```\nset(CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_DEBUG \"/Od /Zi /MDd /fsanitize=address\")  # MSVC\n```\nBut /MDd — the CRT selection. CMake sets /MDd by default for Debug on MSVC (CMAKE_MSVC_RUNTIME_LIBRARY). Actually by default MSVC targets use /MD for release and /MDd for debug unless CMAKE_MSVC_RUNTIME_LIBRARY is set. So /MDd comes for free in Debug. And ASan needs to be added. `/fsanitize=address` — MSVC supports it. It needs `/Zi` (comes with Debug default? MSVC Debug default includes /Zi? Actually CMake adds /Zi for Debug by default I believe — CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_DEBUG includes /Zi). Hmm, need to verify. The important thing: run_case/check tools should NOT have ASan (per journal 08-28 decision: test programs built without Zi and asan). In CMake we can just not add ASan to those targets. Good — simpler than make's filter-out hack.\n\nWait, actually there's a subtlety: the journal 08-28 decision was made because run_case/check don't need ASan. In CMake, since tools are separate targets, we simply don't add the sanitize flag to them. This is cleaner. But should we still give them /Zi? Journal says removed /Zi from tools too. So tools get only base flags + warnings. Fine.\n\nASan on MSVC: `/fsanitize=address`. CMake has first-class support: `add_compile_options($<$<CONFIG:Debug>:/fsanitize=address>)` or via CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_DEBUG. Let me think — since we set it per config, we can append to CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_DEBUG. But that applies to all targets including tools. To exclude tools, better to apply sanitizer per-target: `target_compile_options(common_cpp PRIVATE $<$<CONFIG:Debug>:/fsanitize=address>)` and `target_link_options(common_cpp PRIVATE $<$<CONFIG:Debug>:/fsanitize=address>)`. Per-target generator expressions — that's the modern idiomatic way and precisely solves the \"tools don't get ASan\" problem that was a hack in Makefile. \n\nHmm, but that means adding the sanitize option to ~10 targets. We can create an interface library or a function. E.g., a function `make_asan(target)` or an INTERFACE library `asan_flags` that targets link. Actually cleanest: define a helper function `irina_configure_target(target)` that applies common warnings, standard, and ASan-in-Debug, then call it for each target. That's the \"DRY in CMake\" lesson. Nice.\n\nWait, but should warnings /W4 apply to tools too? In Makefile, tools used $(filter-out /Zi /fsanitize=address,$(CXXFLAGS)) — so they got /W4 /permissive- /std etc., just no /Zi and no ASan. So tools DO get warnings. Good — the helper function should apply warnings to all, but sanitizer only to the real programs. Hmm, but actually should tools get sanitizer? Journal decision: no. So function with a flag, or separate: base warnings for all + asan only for main programs.\n\nActually, hold on. Let me reconsider whether to keep this complexity. The 8 main programs get ASan in Debug; the 2 tools don't. A single function `apply_flags(target ASAN)` or just accept a small duplication. In CMake the natural grouping:\n- Library targets: common_c, common_cpp (static), common_c_shared, common_cpp_shared — get ASan.\n- Main exe targets: t1_c, t1_cpp ... t4_c, t4_cpp — get ASan.\n- Tools: run_case, check — no ASan.\n\nWe can set directory-level: `add_compile_options()` won't discriminate. Per-target is right. I'll propose a function.\n\n8. **Generator choice — need to verify what's available.** Let me check `cmake --help` and ninja availability. In plan mode, running `cmake --help` is read-only, fine. But actually I should be careful: running cmake commands that write files is forbidden. `cmake --help` is fine. `where ninja` fine.\n\nActually, an important consideration: The default CMake generator on Windows with VS installed is \"Visual Studio 17 2022\" (multi-config). For our console workflow we want a single-config generator so that CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE works and artifacts go to build/debug and build/release mirroring the Makefile structure. Options:\n- \"NMake Makefiles\" — ships with VS, no extra install.\n- \"Ninja\" — need ninja.exe; VS 2022 ships ninja (in the CMake bundle dir: D:\\VisualStudio\\...\\CMake\\Ninja\\ninja.exe). Since CMake is the VS-bundled one, ninja likely present next to it. Let me verify.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: with VS 2022's bundled CMake, the default generator might be Ninja? No, the default is still VS if found. But CMake can be told via `-G`. \n\nThe course already uses GNU make; nmake is rejected for hand-written Makefiles. For CMake backend, Ninja is the modern default and recommended. But is Ninja \"another tool\"? It ships with VS 2022 (and with the CMake bundle). Let me verify it exists. If not, NMake Makefiles is a zero-extra-install option.\n\nHmm, but there's a subtlety with single-config generators and ASan + build type. With NMake Makefiles, configure with -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug produces build/debug dir artifacts. Good.\n\nAlso, the user's README says \"Запуск - из корня проекта\" because of DLL path. With CMake, we can set the runtime path properly so this constraint disappears — that's actually a nice improvement to highlight! With CMake on Windows, `target_link_libraries(t3_cpp PRIVATE common_cpp_shared)` → CMake automatically ensures the DLL is found at runtime? Actually on Windows, for a shared lib target, CMake copies the DLL next to the exe or adds the dir to PATH? It doesn't automatically. But CMake does handle the build-tree runtime path: if both exe and dll are built in the same build tree, CMake adds the DLL directory to the PATH when running tests via ctest? Hmm. Actually there's `$<TARGET_RUNTIME_DLLS>` and the VS generator handles it. For single-config generators, the DLL is in build/debug/common/... and exe in build/debug/t3_bbox_cpp/... — the exe won't find the DLL unless PATH or copy.\n\nOptions:\n- Put DLL and exe in same output dir (set LIBRARY_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY to bin).\n- Add the DLL dir to PATH via ctest ENVIRONMENT property for t3/t4 tests, and document running the exe with the dll dir on PATH (as now).\n- Use `$<TARGET_FILE_DIR:...>` in ENVIRONMENT.\n\nThe current Makefile uses SET_DLL_PATH. In CMake, the cleanest for tests: `set_tests_properties(... PROPERTIES ENVIRONMENT \"PATH=$<TARGET_FILE_DIR:common_cpp_shared>;$ENV{PATH}\")`. Hmm, generator expressions in ENVIRONMENT? ENVIRONMENT property does support generator expressions? I think `ENVIRONMENT` property does support generator expressions since CMake 3.20? Not fully sure. Alternative: set `CMAKE_RUNTIME_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY` per config so all exes and dlls land in the same dir (e.g., bin/debug or build/debug). Actually that might be the simplest: put ALL runtime artifacts (exes + dlls) into one bin dir. Then the exe finds the DLL next to it on Windows (Windows searches the exe's own dir first for DLLs!). Yes — Windows DLL search order includes the application directory. So if common_cpp.dll sits next to t3_bbox_cpp.exe, no PATH needed at all. That eliminates the \"run from root\" constraint — a real UX win and a good CMake lesson (output directory structure).\n\nBut wait — journal 07-28: deepseek advised against putting all artifacts in one build dir (\"будет конфликт с make\"). That was about the make era. With CMake, output dirs are handled by CMake and targets keep distinct artifact names. Collapsing runtime outputs to one bin dir is a standard CMake pattern. However, the .obj files still go to per-target subdirs (CMake handles that). So the collision concern (same .obj names from different tasks) doesn't apply — obj files live in per-target build subdirs. Good.\n\nBut hold on — this changes the