r or use CMAKE_CURRENT_BINARY_DIR. The check --contains needs the captured stderr file. We'll capture to a test output dir. In CMake, we can use `${CMAKE_CURRENT_BINARY_DIR}/tests`. Fine.\n\nAlso gen_cloud — that's referenced in §6.1 but was it ever built? The journal says gen_cloud made for t3 (2026-07-01) but in Makefile I don't see gen_cloud targets. Let me check gen_cloud dir. Actually §4.9 п.3 says эталоны gen on the fly; Makefile doesn't have gen_cloud. The README doesn't mention it. Let me check the gen_cloud folder and whether it's referenced anywhere. Also hello/ — standalone, probably not part of make. Let me check both.\n\nLet me also double check: are there .bat files still around? README mentions them but journal says deleted t1-t3 bats (08-17). Let me verify no .bat files remain. And check .clang-format/.editorconfig contents, and whether t2/t4 c/cpp main files compile with the flags.\n\nAlso need to check: does the project currently build green with make test? I can run `make test` — that's read-only-ish? It writes build artifacts and runs tests. In plan mode, \"commands may ONLY read/inspect\" — running make test writes files (build dir, .ok). That's a modification to the filesystem. Strictly forbidden. I'll avoid running builds. But I could run `make -n test` (dry run, no execution) to inspect? `make -n` doesn't write files — it just prints commands. That's read-only in effect. It might be useful to confirm nothing is broken, but I already have the full Makefile. I'll skip.\n\nLet me verify tool availability: ninja presence, and what generators cmake supports. Also check whether VS-bundled cmake works from a plain PowerShell (needs cl on PATH). The user runs from \"x64 Native Tools Command Prompt\" (vcvars64). CMake needs cl, link in PATH at configure time. That's the same environment as make. Fine.\n\nLet me run a few read-only commands to confirm: cmake --help (generators), where ninja, where clang-format, check gen_cloud/hello contents, check for