c\n3. Contains (stderr to file, check contains): `run_case \"<prog> < input > nul 2> errfile\" CODE` then `check --contains errfile expect`\n   - t3 error cases (empty_case(66), not_digit(65), too_few(65), too_much(65), extra_nan(65), x_y_z_4k(65), x_y_z_k4(65), inf_coord(65), x_ky_z(65), x_yk_z(65))\n   - t4 coords error cases (symbol_coords, too_few, empty_coords(66), too_much, extra_nan, x_y_z_4k, x_y_z_k4, inf_coord, x_ky_z, x_yk_z)\n\nNote: for t3/t4 the program needs the DLL path → ctest ENVIRONMENT PATH.\n\nFor t4, the program takes radius as argv[1]. run_case command string includes the arg: `\"prog 8 < input > out 2> nul\"`.\n\nSo function `add_case(TARGET_NAME ...)`? Better a function with named args:\n\n```\nfunction(add_case name prog input expect code mode)\n  # mode: EXIT_ONLY / EQUAL / CONTAINS\n  set(redir_in \"< ${input}\")\n  set(null_out \"> ${NULLDEV}\")\n  ...\n  if(mode STREQUAL \"EXIT_ONLY\")\n    add_test(NAME ${name}\n      COMMAND ${RUN_CASE} \"${prog} ${redir_in} ${null_out} 2> ${null_out}\" ${code})\n  elseif(mode STREQUAL \"EQUAL\")\n    add_test(NAME ${name}\n      COMMAND ${RUN_CASE} \"${prog} ${redir_in} > ${out} 2> ${null_out}\" 0\n      COMMAND ${CHECK} --equal ${out} ${expect})\n  ...\nendfunction()\n```\n\nWhere prog = `$<TARGET_FILE:t1_cpp>` generator expression. On Windows, $<TARGET_FILE> yields forward-slash path? It yields the path with the platform separator... Actually $<TARGET_FILE:target> returns the full path using forward slashes? I recall it returns the path CMake-style, and it works in cmd invocation generally. But run_case passes it to cmd via std::system. cmd handles `build/debug/t1_dist_matrix_cpp/main.exe < input` fine (forward slashes ok for the exe path; and redirections `< input` fine). I believe it works. But we should TEST it. If cmd chokes on forward slashes for the exe (it usually doesn't — `build\\...\\main.exe` vs `build/.../main.exe` both work in cmd), fallback: use `cmake -E` or `file(TO_NATIVE_PATH)`.\n\nHmm wait — actually there might be an issue: the path with forward slashes in cmd — `C:/Users/.../build/debug/.../main.exe` works in cmd (cmd accepts forward slashes for paths in most cases, though some built-ins like `dir` don't). For executing an exe it works. OK.\n\nTo be safe and correct, we can compute native paths via CMake variables with `file(TO_NATIVE_PATH)`, or just pass `$<TARGET_FILE>` and rely on forward slashes. I'll note in plan that we verify cmd behavior; if problems, use TO_NATIVE_PATH.\n\nAlternatively — a cleaner ctest approach avoiding shell entirely: use `$<TARGET_FILE>` directly as COMMAND and use ctest's `COMMAND` chaining with `cmake -E echo` for file creation? No — the redirect of stdin is the crux. ctest cannot redirect stdin natively. So run_case's std::system wrapper remains necessary. OK.\n\nNow, ENVIRONMENT for PATH on Windows for t3/t4:\n```\nset_tests_properties(t3_cpp_* PROPERTIES ENVIRONMENT \"PATH=${DLL_DIR};$ENV{PATH}\")\n```\nwhere DLL_DIR = $<TARGET_FILE_DIR:common_cpp_shared>. Generator expression in ENVIRONMENT property: The ENVIRONMENT property does support generator expressions? Let me recall... CMake docs: \"test property ENVIRONMENT ... This property is initialized from the variable CMAKE_... \" Hmm. Generator expressions ARE supported in ENVIRONMENT since CMake 3.22? I'm not 100% sure. To avoid relying on uncertain API (course: don't invent APIs), the safe approach: compute the dll directory at configure time since we know the build dir layout: `set(COMMON_CPP_DIR ${CMAKE_CURRENT_BINARY_DIR}/common)`. Since with single-config generators, the dll dir is deterministic: ${CMAKE_CURRENT_BINARY_DIR}/common. Actually with per-target default output dirs, common_cpp.dll goes to ${CMAKE_CURRENT_BINARY_DIR}/common/ (mirroring source layout common/). And main.exe to ${CMAKE_CURRENT_BINARY_DIR}/t3_bbox_cpp/. So the PATH is `.../common`. Deterministic at configure time. So no generator expression needed. \n\nWait — will CMake mirror the source dir structure in build dir? By default, for single-config generators, object files go into per-target subdirs based on target name under CMAKE_CURRENT_BINARY_DIR, and the exe also goes to the target's output dir which defaults to the target's... Actually the default: executables go to `${CMAKE_CURRENT_BINARY_DIR}` directly? No. For a target defined in a CMakeLists, the default output dir for single-config is the \"binary dir of the directory where target defined\" — i.e., the exe lands at build/debug/t1_dist_matrix_cpp/main.exe only if each task is a separate subdirectory. If ALL targets are in ONE top-level CMakeLists, then exes land in build/debug/main.exe — COLLISION! All 8 tasks have main.cpp/main.c named \"main\"! 8 targets all producing \"main.exe\"? No — target names differ (t1_cpp, t1_c, ...) but OUTPUT name defaults to target name. So output would be t1_cpp.exe etc., not main.exe. Unless we set OUTPUT_NAME main. Collision then.\n\nSo with a single top-level CMakeLists: exe names = target names: t1_dist_matrix_cpp.exe, etc. That CHANGES the exe names from main.exe to target-name-based. Is that a problem? The tests reference the exe via $<TARGET_FILE> so no. But README and manual runs reference main.exe. And the user's habit. Hmm.\n\nAlternative: use subdirectories per task so the layout mirrors source and exes stay as main.exe in build/debug/t1_dist_matrix_cpp/main.exe. With add_subdirectory(t1_dist_matrix_cpp), each subdir's binary dir is build/debug/t1_dist_matrix_cpp, exe = main.exe there. This preserves EXACT current layout including names! And it teaches the subdirectory pattern (which we'll need later for GoogleTest, gen_cloud, vcpkg anyway). \n\nBut 8 subdirs each with a CMakeLists? That's a lot of small files. Alternatively keep single top-level CMakeLists but set `RUNTIME_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY` per target to `${CMAKE_CURRENT_BINARY_DIR}/<task>` so exe names stay main.exe? We can set:\n```\nset_target_properties(t1_cpp PROPERTIES RUNTIME_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY ${CMAKE_CURRENT_BINARY_DIR}/t1_dist_matrix_cpp OUTPUT_NAME main)\n```\nHmm, OUTPUT_NAME main for each — but each target is in its own dir, so no collision. This keeps single-file CMakeLists but manual layout management. Ugly.\n\nBetter: use add_subdirectory per task dir. Clean, idiomatic, each dir's CMakeLists is tiny (4 lines), layout matches make exactly, and it sets up the structure for later layers (tests subdir, gen_cloud subdir). I'll recommend subdirectories.\n\nActually wait — but do we want CMakeLists.txt files inside each task dir? The course is about learning. add_subdirectory is a fundamental CMake concept (like include/define in make). Yes, teaching it now is right.\n\nHmm, but there's another consideration. With subdirectories, the common library is defined where? In common/ subdir (add_subdirectory(common)) producing targets common_c, common_cpp, common_c_shared, common_cpp_shared. And a tests/ subdir building run_case and check and adding ctest tests. The top-level CMakeLists orchestrates.\n\nBut t3/t4 need the dll at runtime. With subdirs, dll lands in build/debug/common/. exe in build/debug/t3_bbox_cpp/. Runtime PATH needed — same as now. OK.\n\nAlternatively use `add_library(common_cpp SHARED ...)` and set LIBRARY/RUNTIME_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY to a bin dir; and also RUNTIME_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY for exes to same bin dir → dll next to exe → no PATH. But that breaks the mirror layout. Trade-off. I'll present as the DLL-discovery decision.\n\nActually, you know what — there's a cleaner standard: put DLL and EXEs in the same directory is the common Windows pattern (like bin/). This kills the \"run from root\" constraint entirely and is very satisfying. But the user has been building the \"separate dirs\" mental model. Let me ask via the question tool, or present both in plan with a recommendation.\n\nLet me now also reconsider: should I ask questions now, or present a plan and ask? The plan-mode guidance says ask clarifying questions when weighing tradeoffs. There are a few genuine decision points:\n1. Generator choice (Ninja vs NMake vs VS).\n2. Keep dual static/shared libs or simplify to one shared? \n3. Artifact layout / DLL path handling.\n4. Makefile deletion timing.\n5. Whether to add CMakePresets.\n\nI think I should do more research first (check ninja, cmake generators), then ask a focused set of questions, then produce the plan.\n\nLet me run the read-only checks now.\n\nActually, let me reconsider #2 (static/shared). The whole static+shared duality was a layer-9 teaching exercise. In CMake, maintaining both is doable but adds complexity (4 library targets, export macros, etc.). The course material for layer 16 doesn't explicitly say to preserve it. But the tasks t1-t2 statically link and t3-t4 dynamically — this is current behavior. Preserving behavior is the safe default for a migration (the test suite is the same). I'll recommend preserving both, because the \"static vs dynamic\" axis is itself a curriculum theme (linker, import libs) that CMake should continue to exercise. And CMake makes it much easier (no grouped targets hack). I'll present as recommended-preserve.\n\nLet me check the environment facts now.\n\nLet me run parallel read-only commands:\n- cmake --help (list generators)\n- where ninja, ninja --version\n- check gen_cloud/, hello/ contents\n- .clang-format, .editorconfig\n- check for .bat files\n- check t2, t4 main files briefly (to confirm includes) — actually not necessary.\n\nLet me do these.",
