CMake doesn't have loops as nicely — but we can use a function to add each task.\n\n   Hmm, wait. The current Makefile has a lot of duplication for t1-t4 (each has c and cpp, tests, etc.). CMake can handle this with a function. This is actually a nice learning point: function() in CMake to define a task target + tests.\n\n   Let me think about the cleanest structure:\n   - Root CMakeLists.txt: cmake_minimum_required, project(), C/C++ standard settings, common flags.\n   - common/CMakeLists.txt: geometry_cpp, geometry_c, string_utils_cpp, string_utils_c as libraries (static + shared variants?).\n   - t1_dist_matrix_cpp/CMakeLists.txt ... etc.? 8 more files. That's a lot.\n   \n   Alternative: one CMakeLists.txt at root. But that gets long (~300+ lines). \n\n   Actually, I think a reasonable middle ground:\n   - Root CMakeLists.txt\n   - common/CMakeLists.txt (4 libraries)\n   - Each task dir has its own CMakeLists.txt (8 files, each small).\n   - tests/CMakeLists.txt (run_case, check tools + add_test registration).\n\n   This teaches add_subdirectory which is fundamental. And the task CMakeLists are small.\n\n   But hold on — the static/shared split for common: t1-t2 link static, t3-t4 link shared (dll/so). This is a distinctive feature of this project. In CMake, we'd have:\n   - common_c_static, common_cpp_static (STATIC)\n   - common_c_shared, common_cpp_shared (SHARED)\n   \n   With the COMMON_STATIC/COMMON_EXPORTS define handling via target_compile_definitions. The headers use #ifdef COMMON_STATIC / COMMON_EXPORTS to decide dllexport/dllimport/empty. In CMake:\n   - For static libs: target_compile_definitions(common_cpp_static PUBLIC COMMON_STATIC) — actually PRIVATE or PUBLIC? The define needs to affect how the consumer compiles too (main.cpp includes geometry.hpp and must see COMMON_STATIC → empty COMMON_API). So PUBLIC COMMON_STATIC on the static library target — when main links common_cpp_static, it inherits COMMON_STATIC. \n   - For shared libs: the library itself compiles with COMMON_EXPORTS (dllexport), consumers compile with default (dllimport on Windows). So target_compile_definitions(common_cpp_shared PRIVATE COMMON_EXPORTS).\n   \n   This is exactly the kind of toolchain abstraction layer 16 talks about. \n\n3. **Header dependencies**: CMake gives this automatically via target_sources / add_library with the source files — the compiler emits dependency info and ninja/CMake tracks it. This removes the manual CPP_OBJ_HDR dep lists from the Makefile. \n\n4. **Tests**: Currently tests are Makefile targets producing .ok files via run_case and check. In CMake, the idiomatic way is add_test() + ctest. This is a significant architectural shift. \n   - run_case and check remain as executables (tools).\n   - add_test(NAME t1_cpp_abc COMMAND ... ) with the run_case invocation.\n   - ctest runs them; set_tests_properties for pass/fail.\n   \n   This maps the Makefile test targets to ctest. The .ok file mechanism disappears — ctest itself tracks pass/fail. This is a real learning gain: ctest is the native test runner.\n\n   But there are ~96 tests (t1: 6×2, t2: 8×2, t3: 14×2, t4: 20×2 = 48+... let me count: T1_CPP 6 + T1_C 6 = 12; T2 8+8=16; T3 14+14=28; T4 20+20=40. Total 96 tests). Registering 96 add_test calls — we can do it in a loop or function. In CMake, add_test is not a function that can be called in foreach easily... actually it can: foreach over a list, add_test with computed name. But the test definitions differ (some need input, some expect, different exit codes, --equal vs --contains, some need DLL path env, some need radius args for t4). We can group them into categories with a function that takes parameters.\n\n   This is substantial. Let me think about how to structure. A function `add_case(name exe input expect exitcode checkmode)` that adds the test. Then call it ~96 times. That's a lot of calls but each is one line.\n\n   Actually, since this is a learning exercise, maybe I should propose structuring tests with add_test + ctest, using a CMake function to reduce duplication. The ENVIRONMENT property can set PATH for the DLL (Windows) or LD_LIBRARY_PATH (Linux) for t3-t4 tests.\n\n   Wait, actually, there's a subtlety: for the DLL path. Currently the Makefile sets PATH=.../common;%PATH% before running t3-t4 exes. In ctest, we can set ENVIRONMENT property per test: `set_tests_properties(... PROPERTIES ENVIRONMENT \"PATH=...\")`. The dll dir is ${CMAKE_CURRENT_BINARY_DIR}/common (or wherever the shared lib lands). We can use generator expressions: ENVIRONMENT \"PATH=$<TARGET_FILE_DIR:common_cpp_shared>;$ENV{PATH}\". Actually generator expressions in ENVIRONMENT property work since CMake 3.22 for `$<TARGET_FILE_DIR>`? Hmm, ENVIRONMENT property supports generator expressions? Let me recall... Actually yes, the ENVIRONMENT test property supports generator expressions (that's documented). But I should verify — the AGENTS.md rule says \"проверь по документации\" for API. I'll mark this as needing verification.\n\n   Alternatively, we can just compute the dll directory path at configure time (it's deterministic: ${CMAKE_CURRENT_BINARY_DIR}/common/). Since with Ninja single-config, the shared lib output dir is where we set it. Actually we can control output dirs. We can set CMAKE_RUNTIME_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY and CMAKE_LIBRARY_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY. Hmm.\n\n   Actually there's a bigger question: should t3-t4 still dynamically link, and t1-t2 statically? This dual-build is a layer-9 lesson (static lib, dll+import lib). Moving to CMake, we could keep the same architecture (two common lib variants) OR simplify. The course said layers build on existing; task setup §6.1 says \"задел на этапы: общая геометрическая логика ... кандидат на общий модуль\". The dynamic linking was taught at layer 9. CMake makes it easy to have both STATIC and SHARED. Keeping both preserves the behavioral contract (t3-t4 produce dll, tests pass). I'd lean to keep both, because the goal of layer 16 is to replace the build system, not change program architecture. But I should ask the user — this is a genuine design decision. Actually, let me consider: the simplest CMake that reproduces current behavior = keep static for t1-t2, shared for t3-t4. This also exercises CMake's handling of SHARED libraries on both platforms (import lib on Windows, .so on Linux) — which is a valuable lesson. I'll propose keeping it, and note the alternative.\n\n5. **Encoding**: CMakeLists.txt with Russian comments — the repo sources are CP1251. CMake reads files as UTF-8 by default; CP1251 comments with Cyrillic could garble in some tools but CMake generally passes comments through. Actually the risk: if CMakeLists.txt is saved in CP1251 but CMake expects UTF-8, non-ASCII in strings (not comments) could break. We can avoid non-ASCII in CMake code strings, keep comments ASCII or Russian in CP1251 (comments are fine). Hmm, but if the file is CP1251 and contains Cyrillic comments, tools like VS Code would garble. Simplest: write CMakeLists.txt comments in Russian but... the tool (opencode/me) writes UTF-8. The Makefile is CP1251. \n\n   This is a real decision: what encoding for CMakeLists.txt? Options: (a) UTF-8, consistent with AGENTS.md/README (both UTF-8), breaking from Makefile's CP1251; (b) CP1251 to match Makefile. Since AGENTS.md says sources (C/C++) are CP1251 because of Russian string literals in compiled code — CMakeLists.txt has no compiled Russian literals, so UTF-8 is fine and actually better (clang-format doesn't touch it, editors handle UTF-8 well). I'll recommend UTF-8 for CMakeLists.txt and note it.\n\n6. **format / format-check**: Makefile has format and format-check targets. In CMake we can add custom targets: add_custom_target(format COMMAND clang-format -i ...) and add_custom_target(format-check ...). Also the \"test\" prerequisite on format-check. In ctest we could add a format-check test. Hmm. Currently `make test` depends on format-check. In CMake/ctest, we can add a custom target that depends on the format check, or add a test that runs clang-format --dry-run. Let me propose: a custom target `format-check` and make `test` a custom target that runs format-check then ctest. Or simply register format-check as a ctest case too. I'll present this in plan.\n\n7. **What happens to the Makefile?** Per §4.1 p.6: \"Вторая параллельная система сборки не заводится — она неизбежно расходится с первой\". So when CMake fully replaces make, the Makefile gets deleted. This is a big step — the Makefile is the culmination of layer 15. The course rule is explicit: no two parallel build systems. So once CMake is green (build + tests pass on both platforms), delete Makefile and update README. I should confirm with user the trigger for deletion.\n\n8. **gen_cloud**: Not in Makefile currently. §6.1 says mass input generator for tasks 3-4 (sizes 10/100/10^6). The Makefile doesn't build it. Hmm, actually the journal says gen_cloud was made for t3 (2026-07-01). But the Makefile doesn't reference it. So gen_cloud is standalone. For CMake layer, should we include it? The layer 17 is \"E2E с генератором\". So gen_cloud belongs to layer 17, not 16. I'll note it stays out of scope (or optionally add it since it exists). I'll leave it out to match layer scoping, but mention it.\n\n   Wait, actually — the current test suite for t3-t4 uses small input files in tests/input_data, not the generator. So gen_cloud is genuinely not wired in. Layer 17 will handle it. Keep out of scope for layer 16, but could be a follow-up.\n\n9. **hello/**: standalone, not in build system. Keep as is. Not in scope.\n\n10. **Cross-platform check**: The Makefile supports Windows MSVC + Linux GCC. CMake should too — same CMakeLists works for both; only compiler flags differ (handled via generator expressions / if(MSVC) / if(UNIX)). The course requires cross-platform Makefile at layer 15; CMake layer 16 continues this. The user has an Ubuntu VM. So verification on Linux is part of the acceptance. I should include a Linux verification step.\n\n11. **cmake_minimum_required / project version**: cmake 3.30 available. Use something conservative like 3.20+ for the features we need. Actually generator expressions in ENVIRONMENT need checking. Let me not overcommit; use 3.22 to be safe? Or just min 3.16 with warning. Hmm. Since vcpkg is coming (layer 18), CMake 3.19+ is typical for vcpkg toolchain. I'll suggest 3.22 (safe, recent enough, available 3.30).\n\n12. **Standard settings**: \n    - C17: set(CMAKE_C_STANDARD 17), C_EXTENSIONS OFF.\n    - C++23: set(CMAKE_CXX_STANDARD 23), CXX_EXTENSIONS OFF. Note: MSVC doesn't have full C++23; /std:c++latest is what Makefile uses. CMake's CXX_STANDARD 23 maps to /std:c++latest for MSVC? Actually CMake maps CXX_STANDARD 23 → /std:c++latest on MSVC? Let me recall: CMake's default for MSVC: for C++23 it uses /std:c++20 if 23 not fully... Actually MSVC supports /std:c++23 (since VS 2022 17.9+). Hmm. CMake with CXX_STANDARD 23 sets /std:c++23 if available. The Makefile uses /std:c++latest. Slight difference. We can set CXX_STANDARD 23 and if needed CXX_STANDARD_REQUIRED ON. This is a subtle point worth flagging — need to verify what CMake emits and that std::print works (requires /std:c++latest or /std:c++23 with the right MSVC). Actually std::print needs <print> header which is available in VS 2022 with /std:c++latest or c++23. I'll note verification.\n\n    Also warnings: /W4 /permissive- /EHsc on MSVC; -Wall -Wextra on GCC. Set via add_compile_options or per-target. And the debug/release flags: /Od /Zi /MDd /fsanitize=address vs /O2 /Zi /DNDEBUG /MD. In CMake, per-config flags can go in CMAKE_C_FLAGS_DEBUG etc. And ASan: /fsanitize=address on MSVC works with Debug. On GCC: -fsanitize=address. For tools (run_case, check) the Makefile filters out /Zi and /fsanitize=address. In CMake we can give the tools different flags (no ASan).\n\n    This is where the \"абстракция toolchain-флагов\" comes in: instead of ifeq blocks, CMake uses if(MSVC)/else() in the CMakeLists plus generator expressions.\n\n13. **Directory layout of build artifacts**: Currently build/debug/t1_dist_matrix_cpp/main.exe etc., with common dll in build/debug/common. In CMake with add_subdirectory, binary dirs mirror source dirs: build/debug/t1_dist_matrix_cpp/main.exe, build/debug/common/common_cpp.dll — this matches nicely! But the tools: build/debug/tests/run_case.exe. And test output files (.ok / result files) currently go into build/.../t1_... . In ctest, the working directory is set per test (WORKING_DIRECTORY property). We can use ${CMAKE_CURRENT_BINARY_DIR}/test or similar.\n\n    Actually the Makefile writes result files to $(BINDIR)/t1_dist_matrix_cpp/t1_test1 etc. In ctest we can use WORKING_DIRECTORY per test so relative output paths land in a predictable place. Since we use file paths like tests/expect/..., we might need absolute paths in tests. Hmm, ctest tests run with WORKING_DIRECTORY property. If we set it to ${CMAKE_CURRENT_BINARY_DIR}, then relative paths to tests/input_data (source dir) won't resolve. We should use absolute paths (${CMAKE_CURRENT_SOURCE_DIR}/tests/input_data/t1_abc) in the test commands. That's cleaner anyway.\n\n    Actually wait — run_case takes the whole command string including redirects: `\"$(call WCMD,$(T1_CPP_EXE) < tests/input_data/t1_abc > $(NULLDEV) 2> $(NULLDEV))\"`. In CMake add_test, the COMMAND is a list of arguments, not a shell string. So we need to restructure: add_test(NAME t1_cpp_abc COMMAND ${RUN_CASE} \"${CMD}\" 65). The CMD would be built with generator expressions and platform-conditional null device. E.g., on Windows: \"$<TARGET_FILE:t1_cpp> < ${SRC}/tests/input_data/t1_abc > nul 2> nul\". But $<TARGET_FILE> inside a shell command string... the run_case uses std::system(argv[1]) which spawns cmd — so the command is passed to the shell. Generator expressions would be evaluated by ctest at test time? No — generator expressions in add_test COMMAND are evaluated at generate time (CMake configure/generate). $<TARGET_FILE:...> is supported in add_test COMMAND. And when the test runs, the resolved path is passed as argv[1] to run_case, which passes to std::system → cmd. The path would have forward slashes? $<TARGET_FILE> gives the full path with... on Windows it may use backslashes or forward slashes. cmd can handle both mostly, but redirects and quotes... This is getting complex. This is a real design consideration.\n\n    Alternative cleaner approach: don't use shell redirects inside run_case; instead restructure tests. But that changes run_case's contract. Hmm.\n\n    Actually, maybe simpler: keep run_case taking a full command string, and construct that string in CMake with explicit paths. On Windows the redirect syntax `> nul 2> nul` differs from Linux `> /dev/null 2> /dev/null`. We can use if(WIN32) to build the null device. The command string can be assembled as a CMake variable at configure time and passed to add_test.\n\n    But there's a wrinkle: the exe path with spaces? Repo path: C:\\Users\\User\\Desktop\\inobitec_stud — no spaces. But CMake build dir with spaces could break the shell string. We can quote paths. run_case's std::system(command) — the command is a single string; cmd parsing applies. Quoting paths inside should work: `\"${exe}\" < \"input\" > nul`. Hmm, cmd quoting is finicky. This is exactly the kind of thing the Makefile already battles (WCMD with forward/backslash). \n\n    Let me think about whether there's a cleaner ctest-native way. ctest add_test runs the executable directly (no shell) with argv. So for exit-code tests we could run the exe directly and use WILL_FAIL? No — WILL_FAIL just inverts (non-zero → pass). We need SPECIFIC exit codes (64/65/66/74). ctest has PASS_REGULAR_EXPRESSION / FAIL_REGULAR_EXPRESSION on output, but not on exit code directly. So run_case exists precisely to assert a specific exit code. Keep run_case. Good.\n\n    For the command string, we can build it carefully. Actually — an even cleaner idea: since ctest passes argv directly (not via shell) to run_case, we can restructure run_case to accept the command parts as separate args? No — the redirects `< file` and `> out` are shell features; run_case needs to feed them to the shell. std::system is the vehicle. So the whole command must be one shell string. OK.\n\n    So the plan for tests: build the command string at configure time in CMake using target file locations and platform-conditional redirect syntax, then add_test with run_case + command + expected code, and check --equal/--contains for content tests. And a helper function `add_case()` to avoid 96 lines of near-duplicate add_test.\n\n    Hmm wait, but there's an even simpler consideration: the Makefile's redirect for content tests writes result to a file then compares via check. In ctest we can do the same: run_case runs \"exe < in > out 2> err\" and returns 0; then a second test or same test with check --equal. add_test supports multiple COMMAND entries that run in sequence! add_test(NAME x COMMAND run_case \"...\" 0 COMMAND check --equal out expect). Yes, ctest runs multiple commands and each must succeed. That maps directly to the Makefile `&&` chains. \n\n    For the DLL path (t3-t4), set_tests_properties ENVIRONMENT. On Windows PATH, on Linux LD_LIBRARY_PATH. We can compute the shared lib dir and add to ENVIRONMENT. Verify generator expression support in ENVIRONMENT property (I believe supported since 3.22; else compute path at configure time). Let me flag it for verification.\n\n14. **What to do with .ok files and the \"result\" files**: ctest replaces .ok. The result/err files written by tests into build — fine, they live in build/ which is gitignored.\n\n15. **Version control / commits**: This is a big change. Should be done in stages with regular commits (§4.1). Probably: (a) CMake skeleton + build t1-t2 static working; (b) add common shared + t3-t4; (c) tools + ctest; (d) format targets; (e) Linux verification; (f) delete Makefile, update README. Each commit format-clean, W4-clean.\n\n16. **README update**: Layer 2 says README must stay current. When CMake replaces make, README's \"Как собрать\" changes to cmake commands. Also the layer-16 note in AGENTS.md journal needs updating (but that's the user's job; I can note it).\n\nNow, the AGENTS.md §5 layer 16 text: \"CMakeLists.txt → cmake -B build → cmake --build build. Мотивация после переносимого Makefile — не «переносимость» (она уже есть), а: автоматические header-зависимости (то, что на слое 15 делалось руками), абстракция toolchain-флагов (ifeq-блоки исчезают), find_package + vcpkg для зависимостей, target_precompile_headers, генерация под разные сборщики и IDE.\"\n\nSo the layer 16 acceptance is about these specific things. Good — my plan should hit each:\n- automatic header deps ✓ (CMake/ninja does it)\n- ifeq blocks disappear ✓ (if(MSVC) etc.)\n- find_package + vcpkg — this is layer 18 really; layer 16 mentions it as motivation. I'll note it as future.\n- target_precompile_headers — layer 14 was PCH; CMake has target_precompile_headers. Should we set up PCH in CMake? Layer 14 said \"Польза измеряется секундомером\". CMake's PCH via target_precompile_headers is the modern way. This could be part of layer 16 or deferred. I'll ask/mark optional.\n- генерация под разные сборщики и IDE — the point of CMake. We pick Ninja now; the same CMakeLists can generate VS/NMake. This is itself the lesson.\n\nNow — important check: does the user actually have vcvars set up and how do they invoke cmake? The cmake found is VS-bundled. In the Native Tools prompt, is cmake on PATH? The VS-bundled cmake at D:\\VisualStudio\\Community\\Common7\\IDE\\CommonExtensions\\Microsoft\\CMake\\CMake\\bin — this is on PATH in the \"Developer Command Prompt\" but maybe not in plain cmd. Actually VS 2022's `cmake` might be available. Also winget install cmake is an option. Since ninja is VS-bundled too, and cl comes from vcvars — using VS-bundled cmake+ninja from the Native Tools prompt is the most consistent. We should verify cmake and ninja are reachable in the vcvars environment. I can't run vcvars from here (it's interactive-ish but actually vcvars64.bat works in cmd). Hmm, I'm in PowerShell. Let me not run heavy verification; I'll note it as a first step in the plan: confirm tooling reachable in vcvars console.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the generator choice more carefully because it affects everything. \n\nOptions:\n- **Ninja**: needs ninja.exe on PATH (or CMAKE_MAKE_PROGRAM). Single-config. Fast. Cross-platform (Linux too). Best default. Build type via CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug/Release → separate build/debug, build/release dirs.\n- **NMake Makefiles**: uses nmake from VS. Single-config. Windows-only. The course avoided hand-written nmake but this is CMake-generated. Slower, more legacy.\n- **Visual Studio generator**: multi-config, produces .sln. Against the \"console-only\" ethos but the layer 16 mentions \"генерация под IDE\" as a property. Using it as a demo of cross-generator capability could be a nice lesson but as the primary path it conflicts with \"no IDE\".\n\nRecommend Ninja. Also note: on Linux the user will use Ninja or Makefiles generator (GCC). Ninja works on Linux too if installed; else `Unix Makefiles`. CMakeLists must not depend on generator — that's the point.\n\nOne more: CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE and the flags. Makefile debug flags include /MDd (debug CRT) and /fsanitize=address; release /MD. CMake by default on MSVC uses /MDd for Debug and /MD for Release already (CMAKE_MSVC_RUNTIME_LIBRARY default = MultiThreadedDLL). So that's automatic. ASan must be added to Debug flags explicitly. And /Zi for Debug (CMake adds /Zi for Debug by default? Actually CMAKE_C_FLAGS_DEBUG includes /Zi for MSVC by default. Yes, Debug default includes /Zi). Release default includes /O2 /Zi? Release default is /O2 /DNDEBUG, /Zi added by default too (CMAKE_C_FLAGS_RELEASE = /O2 /DNDEBUG and MSVC adds /Zi via... hmm, actually /Zi is in CMAKE_C_FLAGS_RELWITHDEBINFO not RELEASE). The Makefile wants /Zi in Release too (pdb always). So we may need to add /Zi to Release flags explicitly. And linker /DEBUG /OPT:REF /OPT:ICF. We can set these via target or global link options.\n\nOK. I have enough understanding. Let me also quickly check: does the current `make test` even pass right now? I can't run it (plan mode read-only, and running make test would write build artifacts — that's a system change, forbidden in plan mode). So I won't run it. But I could note that step 0 of implementation is verifying make test is green (baseline) before starting CMake work.\n\nLet me also consider the tools' flags: run_case and check are built without /Zi and ASan (per journal 08-28). In CMake: they're separate targets; we simply don't add ASan to them. But they still need /W4 /permissive- /std. Fine.\n\nNow about scope of the plan and what to ask the user. Genuine decision points:\n1. **Structure**: single root CMakeLists vs subdirectories (common/, each task/, tests/). This affects file count. I lean subdirectories (teaches add_subdirectory, scales to vcpkg/GoogleTest later). But single-file is simpler for a 96-test project... Actually tests can be in a tests/CMakeLists.txt with add_subdirectory(tests) and add_test there. Hmm. Let me present the choice.\n\n   Actually, thinking about the learning path: layer 18 vcpkg and 19 GoogleTest will be added. A subdirectory-based structure prepares for that. But too many tiny files also have cost. Middle ground: root CMakeLists + common/CMakeLists.txt + tests/CMakeLists.txt, and the 8 task targets defined in root (they're 8 near-identical targets, a function in root is fine). OR put each task in its own dir. \n\n   Let me ask the user, with a recommendation. Actually — the course says don't over-ask. But structure is a genuine architectural axis (module structure). §4.2 anti-sycophancy: name architectural axes before fixing. Structure of modules is one. I'll ask.\n\n