          "text": "Let me formulate the plan. I've gathered a lot. Let me think carefully about the CMake plan.\n\nKey facts:\n- CMake 3.30.5 available (VS-bundled), Ninja available too.\n- Sources: C (C17) and C++ (C++23) with MSVC on Windows, GCC on Linux.\n- Common library: geometry + string_utils in C and C++ variants, with COMMON_API macros (COMMON_STATIC / COMMON_EXPORTS / dllimport).\n- t1-t2 link static (COMMON_STATIC define), t3-t4 link dynamic (dll/so).\n- Tests: run_case (exit-code check) and check (--equal/--contains). 94 test cases via .ok files.\n- Format targets: format, format-check with clang-format, integrated into make test.\n- Build dirs: build/debug, build/release. CONFIG variable.\n- Working directory matters for t3-t4 (dll path via PATH/LD_LIBRARY_PATH).\n- The .bat files were removed already (journal 08-17 deleted t1-t3 bats). Let me check if any bat remains. Actually from the journal \"удалила .bat к t1-t3\" and earlier t4 bat existed... need to check. Actually README still references .bat files. Let me check.\n\nLet me check for any remaining .bat files and the gen_cloud/hello. gen_cloud/main.cpp exists. It's a generator for tasks 3-4. Not part of Makefile currently. Hmm, interesting - gen_cloud not in Makefile. Let me verify. Actually §6.1 requires gen_cloud for tasks 3-4 mass input. But the Makefile doesn't reference it. Let me check the gen_cloud file quickly and whether bat files remain.\n\nActually the important thing for planning: the plan should be a comprehensive plan for layer 16. Let me think about the structure of CMake migration.\n\nCMake plan considerations:\n\n1. **Generator choice**: Ninja (available, modern, fast, cross-platform) vs NMake Makefiles. The course says GNU make syntax is industrial standard; for CMake, Ninja is the industrial standard companion. NMake Makefiles uses nmake which the course avoided for hand-written makefiles but here it's CMake-generated. Actually the layer 16 description says: `cmake -B build` → `cmake --build build`. It doesn't specify generator. Ninja is the natural choice on Windows MSVC, and works on Linux too (need ninja on Linux). On Linux, could use Unix Makefiles or Ninja. For consistency, Ninja both places (if available). But careful: single-config generators (Ninja, Makefiles) bake CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE at configure time; multi-config (Visual Studio) supports --config. For build/debug vs build/release separation, with Ninja we configure separate build dirs with -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE. This matches current build/debug and build/release.\n\nActually the Makefile uses CONFIG=debug|release → build/$(CONFIG). With CMake: `cmake -B build/debug -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug -G Ninja`. That preserves the directory structure. Good.\n\n2. **Flags mapping**. The Makefile maps debug/release flags for MSVC and GCC. In CMake we can use:\n   - CMAKE_C_STANDARD 17, CMAKE_CXX_STANDARD 23, C_STANDARD_REQUIRED, CXX_STANDARD_REQUIRED, C_EXTENSIONS OFF / CXX_EXTENSIONS OFF.\n   - Warnings: add_compile_options(/W4 /permissive-) for MSVC, -Wall -Wextra for GCC.\n   - Debug: /Od /Zi /MDd /fsanitize=address; Release: /O2 /Zi /DNDEBUG /MD.\n   - CMake default already sets /MDd for debug and /MD for release on MSVC (CMAKE_MSVC_RUNTIME_LIBRARY default). So we don't need to set CRT explicitly — CMake handles it. Actually default for MSVC is MultiThreadedDLL for all configs. Yes.\n   - /EHsc: add for C++ MSVC.\n   - /fsanitize=address: add to both compile and link for Debug, MSVC and GCC. We can use generator expressions: target_compile_options with $<$<CONFIG:Debug>:...> or set CMAKE_C_FLAGS_DEBUG etc.\n   - The tools run_case and check are built without /Zi and /fsanitize=address — separate targets with their own flags. In CMake, we just don't add asan to those targets.\n\n3. **Library model**: keep static (t1-t2) and shared (t3-t4).\n   - For shared libraries: add_library(common_cpp SHARED). On Windows produces common_cpp.dll + import lib common_cpp.lib. The COMMON_EXPORTS define needs to be set when compiling the library sources: target_compile_definitions(common_cpp PRIVATE COMMON_EXPORTS). And consumers (t3-t4) need to NOT define COMMON_STATIC, so they get dllimport via the default branch. \n   - For static: add_library(common_cpp STATIC), target_compile_definitions(... PUBLIC COMMON_STATIC) so that consumers compiling main.cpp also see COMMON_STATIC (so COMMON_API is empty). Since t1-t2 main.cpp includes geometry.hpp, they need COMMON_STATIC defined during their compilation. PUBLIC propagates. Good — this is the CMake-idiomatic way and replaces the target-specific COMMON_FLAG hack.\n   - For shared consumers t3-t4: no define, so they get dllimport. Good. But the library itself must be compiled with COMMON_EXPORTS PRIVATE.\n   \n   On Linux: SHARED produces libcommon_cpp.so, COMMON_API is empty anyway. Fine.\n\n4. **Tests**: Move from .ok-file pattern to ctest. run_case and check remain as executables (tools). add_test for each case. But there are 94 test cases. Writing add_test for each is verbose but that's the CMake way — or we can use a function/macro to add cases compactly. Actually the test definitions have patterns:\n   - Exit-code only tests: run_case \"<exe> < input > nul 2> nul\" CODE\n   - Equal tests: run_case \"...\" 0 && check --equal result expected\n   - Contains tests: run_case \"...\" CODE (stderr to file) && check --contains errfile expected\n   - t4 tests need args (radius) and no input for radius cases.\n   \n   We can write a CMake function `add_case(name exe input_file args expected_exit_mode expected_code check_mode check_file)` etc. Hmm, that's complex. Let me think about how to keep it manageable and teach CMake functions.\n\n   Actually, let me reconsider. ctest add_test: each test is a command invocation. For exit-code tests, we run run_case which returns 0 if exit code matched. For equal/contains, we need two steps: run the program capturing output, then run check. ctest supports multiple COMMAND invocations in one add_test (they run sequentially and each must succeed). So:\n   ```\n   add_test(NAME t1_cpp_norm COMMAND run_case \"... < input > out 2> nul\" 0 COMMAND check --equal out expected)\n   ```\n   ctest runs both commands; passes if all succeed. Good.\n\n   Working directory: tests that read input files — need absolute or correct relative paths. run_case executes via std::system which uses the shell, and the working directory is whatever ctest sets (default is the build dir of the test target... actually ctest runs tests from the build directory by default). Paths to tests/input_data are relative to the source dir. So we should use absolute paths in the commands, or set WORKING_DIRECTORY. Better: use ${CMAKE_CURRENT_SOURCE_DIR}/tests/input_data/t1_abc absolute paths. And output files go into ${CMAKE_CURRENT_BINARY_DIR}/... (build dir), which is under build/.gitignore ignores build/. Good.\n\n   The Makefile currently writes outputs into $(BINDIR)/t1_dist_matrix_cpp/t1_test1 etc. In CMake we can write to ${CMAKE_CURRENT_BINARY_DIR}/test/... or just use a dedicated output dir.\n\n   DLL path for t3-t4: ctest ENVIRONMENT property. We can set ENVIRONMENT \"PATH=...;...\" for t3/t4 tests on Windows, and LD_LIBRARY_PATH on Linux. Or better: use $<TARGET_FILE_DIR:common_cpp_shared> in a generator expression? ENVIRONMENT property supports generator expressions? Not directly for ENVIRONMENT... Actually set_tests_properties ENVIRONMENT does support $<TARGET_FILE_DIR> since CMake 3.22? Let me recall — ENVIRONMENT property supports generator expressions in newer CMake (3.22+). Hmm. Actually the docs say ENVIRONMENT property: \"the value of this property can contain generator expressions\"? I'm not 100% sure. The safest path: compute the dll directory at configure time. Since build dir layout is known: build/debug/common/ (for shared libs). Or set LIBRARY_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY. Actually simplest robust approach: for t3/t4 tests, prepend the directory of the shared library to PATH. We can compute it as ${CMAKE_CURRENT_BINARY_DIR} ... hmm, but binary dir of common is build/debug/common. If we add_subdirectory(common), CMAKE_CURRENT_BINARY_DIR there is build/debug/common. We can set a variable from there.\n\n   Alternative simpler approach: build shared libs and executables into the same output directory (RUNTIME_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY). But the course already decided layout mirrors source. Let me keep it simple: set ENVIRONMENT per test using a computed path variable. I'll verify during implementation. This is a detail to flag.\n\n   Actually there's a cleaner CMake idiom: since t3-t4 link the shared lib, on Linux CMake automatically sets rpath to find the .so in the build tree (by default CMAKE_BUILD_RPATH / INSTALL_RPATH handling). On Windows, CMake does NOT copy the dll next to the exe by default. But ctest CAN copy... no. The common approach: use the $<TARGET_FILE_DIR:...> genex in ENVIRONMENT, or add a post-build copy. For learning purposes, keeping the PATH/LD_LIBRARY_PATH approach (as the Makefile does) is actually more transparent and matches what they did. Let me keep ENVIRONMENT approach but verify genex support. If not supported, compute path at configure time.\n\n   Actually, on Linux, CMake will handle rpath automatically so LD_LIBRARY_PATH may not even be needed for in-tree tests. On Windows we need PATH. This asymmetry matches the Makefile's SET_DLL_PATH. Fine.\n\n5. **format / format-check**: \n   - `format`: custom target running clang-format -i on sources. In CMake we can use add_custom_target(format COMMAND clang-format -i <list>) with a list of source files. Need to collect all .c/.cpp/.h/.hpp. We can use file(GLOB_RECURSE) or explicit list. Course prefers explicit? For format target, globbing is fine and simpler (it's a helper, not a dependency). But globbing can hide new files. Hmm. The Makefile used $(wildcard **/*.cpp **/*.c ...). We can mirror with file(GLOB_RECURSE FORMAT_SOURCES CONFIGURE_DEPENDS ...) or just GLOB. \n   - `format-check`: if clang-format found → run --dry-run -Werror; else warn. In CMake: find_program(CLANG_FORMAT clang-format); add_custom_target(format-check). If not found, we can make format-check print a message. But custom target with empty command... We can add a CMake script? Or use add_custom_target with a conditional command. Hmm. Actually we could add format-check as a ctest test as well (the Makefile runs it as prereq of test). \n\n   The Makefile runs format-check as prerequisite of `test`. In CMake, we could add a test `format-check` registered via add_test that runs clang-format --dry-run if present, else passes with warning. But the behavior \"tests run even if clang-format missing\" needs care.\n\n   Alternative: add_custom_target(format-check) + add_dependencies(test ...)? Actually `make test` maps to ctest. The current Makefile's `test` runs format-check then all tests. In CMake world, ctest runs registered tests. We could register a format-check test as the first one. That keeps \"format-check is part of test\". Let me think about what's cleaner for the course: The course says (layer 15) test not green without format-cleanliness. In CMake/ctest we can add a \"format-check\" test via add_test with a CMake script (-P) that checks clang-format availability and runs dry-run. That's elegant: `add_test(NAME format-check COMMAND ${CMAKE_COMMAND} -P ${CMAKE_CURRENT_SOURCE_DIR}/cmake/format_check.cmake)`. The script handles missing clang-format by exiting 0 with a warning, or running --dry-run -Werror. This keeps the same semantics and integrates with ctest. But it introduces a .cmake script file. Is that acceptable? It's a reasonable \"helper\". Alternatively inline with add_custom_command... Let me keep it as a small cmake script or inline. I'll propose a cmake/ directory with a format_check.cmake script. Actually could do it inline in CMakeLists with if(CLANG_FORMAT) add_test(...) else() add_test that just echoes. Simpler: two cases:\n   ```\n   find_program(CLANG_FORMAT clang-format)\n   if(CLANG_FORMAT)\n     add_test(NAME format-check COMMAND clang-format --dry-run -Werror ${FORMAT_SOURCES})\n   else()\n     add_test(NAME format-check COMMAND ${CMAKE_COMMAND} -E echo \"clang-format not found, skip format check\")\n   endif()\n   ```\n   That's clean and stays in CMakeLists. But passing 90+ files as one command line might exceed limits? On Windows command line limit ~32k chars; source list is small (~30 files). Fine.\n\n   Hmm wait — ctest exit code: add_test passes if command returns 0. `-E echo` returns 0. Good. clang-format --dry-run -Werror returns non-zero on unformatted → test fails. Good.\n\n6. **Working dir / paths**: Tests read files relative to source tree. We'll use absolute paths: ${CMAKE_CURRENT_SOURCE_DIR}/tests/input_data/... and output to ${CMAKE_CURRENT_BINARY_DIR}/...\n\n7. **Where do .ok files go?** They disappear; ctest tracks pass/fail internally. build/ output files (result/err) can go to build/... under gitignore. Good — actually cleaner.\n\n8. **gen_cloud and hello**: Not part of Makefile. gen_cloud/main.cpp exists. Should we add it? The layer 17 is \"E2E с генератором\". gen_cloud belongs to layer 17. So for layer 16 we can leave it out, or optionally include. The Makefile doesn't build it. So keep out of scope, note it. hello/ also not built by make; keep as is (it's a manual hello world). Actually could add hello as a CMake target too for completeness but it's trivial and not in the build graph. Keep out of scope; mention.\n\n9. **Makefile removal**: Per §4.1/§4.9 p.1: \"Вторая параллельная система сборки не заводится\". So when CMake is working fully (build + test + format green), the Makefile is deleted. This is a big decision point — the plan must include Makefile deletion and README update. Also .bat files — let me check if any remain. README references them but journal says they were deleted. Let me verify in the repo.\n\n10. **CMake version / minimum**: cmake_minimum_required(VERSION 3.22) or similar. project() with C and CXX.\n\n11. **Encoding**: CMakeLists in UTF-8 per decision. Fine — comments in Russian will be UTF-8. clang-format doesn't touch .txt/.cmake/CMakeLists (only c/cpp/h/hpp). format-check globs only c/cpp/h/hpp so CMakeLists won't be formatted — good, it's not C.\n\n12. **Exit codes** in tests: run_case expects specific codes 64/65/66/74. These are literals in test invocations. In Makefile they were variables USAGE/DATA/etc. In CMake we could define variables and use them in add_test... add_test COMMAND arguments can use ${} substitution at configure time. So set(USAGE 64) etc., then add_test(NAME ... COMMAND run_case \"... \" ${USAGE}). Good.\n\n13. **Tool flags**: run_case and check built without /Zi and /fsanitize=address. In CMake, these are separate targets; we simply don't add asan to them. But we still want /W4 /permissive- /EHsc for them (Makefile filtered out only /Zi and asan). So apply warning flags to all targets, asan only to libs and mains. \n\n   How to apply common flags in CMake cleanly? Use add_compile_options at top level for warnings and standard (apply to all targets including tools), then asan via target_compile_options on specific targets with genex $<$<CONFIG:Debug>:/fsanitize=address> and target_link_options similarly. That's the idiomatic way. Actually MSVC asan: /fsanitize=address needs both compile and link. GCC too. Use generator expressions for config-specific. \n\n   Alternatively use CMAKE_C_FLAGS_DEBUG += ... but that applies to all debug targets including tools. The Makefile deliberately excluded tools from asan. To preserve that, use per-target asan.\n\n   Let me define:\n   - Top-level: warnings (/W4 /permissive- for MSVC; -Wall -Wextra for GCC), standards.\n   - A helper function `add_asan(target)` or just repeat target_compile_options for each lib and main.\n   \n   Hmm, there might be a subtlety: /fsanitize=address on MSVC with /MDd — fine, matches Makefile.\n\n14. **CMAKE_MSVC_RUNTIME_LIBRARY**: default is \"MultiThreadedDLL\" for all configs? Actually default for MSVC generator is /MD for release, /MDd for debug. The policy CMP0091. With CMAKE_MSVC_RUNTIME_LIBRARY unset, CMake defaults to MultiThreaded$<$<CONFIG:Debug>:Debug>DLL which gives /MDd debug, /MD release. That matches the Makefile (debug /MDd, release /MD). Good, nothing to do.\n\n15. **/Zi in debug and release**: Makefile adds /Zi in both debug and release (with pdb). CMake default Debug adds /Zi already (CMAKE_C_FLAGS_DEBUG includes /Zi). Release doesn't by default. The course policy: pdb always. We should add /Zi to Release too. And linker /DEBUG in release. CMake: set CMAKE_C_FLAGS_RELEASE to include /Zi? Or per target. Hmm. Actually we can add /Zi to Release flags globally: add_compile_options($<$<CONFIG:Release>:/Zi>) on MSVC, and link options $<$<CONFIG:Release>:/DEBUG:/OPT:REF:/OPT:ICF>. On GCC, -g in both debug and release (Makefile does -O2 -g for release). So add -g always.\n\n   This is getting detailed — good, it's the \"абстракция toolchain-флагов\" part. But I don't need to fully solve in plan; I need to lay out the mapping and flag the details.\n\n16. **PCH**: layer 16 mentions target_precompile_headers as motivation. But the tasks are small; PCH benefit measured by stopwatch (layer 14). For CMake stage, we could add target_precompile_headers for common headers (e.g., the STL headers each main uses). But the course says PCH only for stable heavy headers and benefit measured. The mains use <print>, <vector>, <string>, <ranges> etc. Could set up PCH per executable. But maybe defer — layer 16 says \"target_precompile_headers\" is one motivation, but not necessarily must-implement now. Given PCH complexity and that it's a learning goal, I could make it an optional stretch. Actually the layer description lists it as a capability. Hmm. Let me include it as an optional final step (measure with Measure-Command), not core. Or skip and note. I'll propose: defer PCH to when there's a real need (or do it as a stretch exercise at the end, measured). Keep the plan focused.\n\n17. **Header dependencies**: CMake auto-tracks via deps (Ninja/compiler). This replaces the manual CPP_OBJ_HDR lists. Good — highlight this as the payoff of the layer.\n\n18. **What about the tools' compile: Makefile used $(filter-out /Zi /fsanitize=address,$(CXXFLAGS))**. In CMake we define tool targets with only warnings + std, no asan, no /Zi. Cleaner.\n\n19. **Order of work / commits**: \n   - Phase 0: verify current make test passes (baseline).\n   - Phase 1: minimal CMake skeleton: cmake_minimum_required, project, standards, warnings; build hello or t1 as proof. Actually start with t1 (both languages) + static common lib. Commit.\n   - Phase 2: t2 static; t3-t4 shared; commit.\n   - Phase 3: tools run_case/check as CMake targets; commit.\n   - Phase 4: ctest registration for all 94 cases (function to reduce repetition); commit.\n   - Phase 5: format/format-check as custom targets + ctest integration; commit.\n   - Phase 6: remove Makefile, update README, update .gitignore if needed. Commit.\n   - Phase 7: verify on Linux VM (GCC, Unix Makefiles or Ninja), verify clean clone one-command. \n   \n   Actually layer says \"вторая параллельная система сборки не заводится\". So while transitioning, should the Makefile stay until CMake fully green? The rule: \"Проект собирается с нуля одной командой\" and \"Вторая параллельная система сборки не заводится — она неизбежно расходится с первой; переходный сценарий удаляется, когда появляется сборщик.\" So Makefile must be removed once CMake works. During development, we keep Makefile until CMake is green, then remove. That's acceptable (it's a migration, not two permanent systems).\n\n   Hmm, but the .bat files: they were already deleted per journal. Let me verify no .bat remains. Let me check quickly.\n\n20. **Ctest integration with CTest**: add_enable_testing() or include(CTest). enable_testing() in root. Or top-level `enable_testing()`.\n\n21. **Build commands for the course**:\n   - `cmake -B build/debug -G Ninja -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug`\n   - `cmake --build build/debug`\n   - `ctest --test-dir build/debug` or `cmake --build build/debug --target test` (test is built-in if enable_testing).\n   - Actually `cmake --build build/debug -t test` runs ctest. Good.\n\n   README must be updated to document this. And the \"запуск из корня\" constraint: with CMake, ctest ENVIRONMENT handles PATH so tests run from anywhere; manual run of t3-t4 exe still needs PATH set. Could we set BUILD_RPATH so even manual runs work? On Windows rpath doesn't exist. CMake can copy the dll next to exe via post-build. But course didn't do that. Keep manual run requiring PATH (documented). Actually, to make manual runs from anywhere, we could set RUNTIME_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY for both exes and dlls to a common bin dir. But that changes layout. Keep as-is, matching Makefile behavior.\n\nLet me verify remaining files: any .bat files, check gen_cloud content, check t2/t4 mains, check string_utils.c/h. Let me also check tests/input_data and expect naming to design the ctest function. Let me look at a couple test files and the expect/input_data listing to design add_case.\n\nLet me also verify the current git status (clean?) to know baseline.\n\nLet me run a few read-only inspections.",
