          "text": "Let me understand the gen_cloud and hello are standalone not part of Makefile. gen_cloud is layer 17 material (E2E with generator). So layer 16 CMake — do we include gen_cloud? The layer 16 is about CMake meta-build. gen_cloud belongs to layer 17 \"E2E с генератором\". So probably keep gen_cloud out of scope for now (or include it as a target since it exists). Actually it's an existing file. But the Makefile doesn't build it. We shouldn't expand scope. But maybe mention it as optional. Actually, layer 16 is CMake — the Makefile's scope includes t1-t4, tools run_case/check, format. gen_cloud and hello are not built by Makefile. So CMake should replicate exactly what Makefile builds. Keep gen_cloud/hello out. Note in plan.\n\nLet me check the tests/input_data files for encoding (they're LF now, expected). And check t4 tests need args (radius) — tests like t4_radius_empty run without input, just `t4_filter_cpp > null 2> err`. And t4 test1-3 run with radius argument like `t4_filter_cpp 8 < input > out 2> null`.\n\nNow let me think about the CMake design in detail.\n\n## CMake design\n\n### Structure chosen\n- Root `CMakeLists.txt` — project config, standards, flags, add_subdirectory(common), add_subdirectory(tests), task executables via function.\n- `common/CMakeLists.txt` — static + shared libs for C and C++.\n- `tests/CMakeLists.txt` — run_case, check tools + add_test registration.\n\nWait, but the tasks themselves — where do their executables live? The user chose \"Корень + common/ + tests/\". So tasks t1-t4 are defined in root CMakeLists via a function `add_task()`. That's fine.\n\nLet me think about the design of libraries.\n\n### Libraries (common)\n- C++ static: `common_cpp_static` from geometry.cpp, string_utils.cpp. Defines COMMON_STATIC (needs to be PUBLIC so consumers see it too — actually the consumers also compile main.cpp which includes geometry.hpp and needs COMMON_API empty. If we define COMMON_STATIC PUBLIC on the library target, it propagates to linking executables. That's the modern way: `target_compile_definitions(common_cpp_static PUBLIC COMMON_STATIC)`.\n- C++ shared: `common_cpp_shared` from same sources. Defines COMMON_EXPORTS PRIVATE when compiling the library (so dllexport), consumers get default dllimport. `target_compile_definitions(common_cpp_shared PRIVATE COMMON_EXPORTS)`.\n- Same for C: `common_c_static`, `common_c_shared`.\n\nOn Linux, SHARED → .so, and exports are default; the macros are empty. That's fine.\n\nWait — there's a subtlety. The C++ shared library on Windows: linking an exe to `common_cpp_shared` automatically links the import lib `common_cpp.lib`. CMake handles that: when you `target_link_libraries(t3_cpp PRIVATE common_cpp_shared)`, on Windows CMake links against the import library and the DLL must be found at runtime. ctest needs PATH set for tests, or CMake can set build-tree rpath on Windows? No — Windows doesn't have rpath. Need PATH env for tests. CMake has `$<TARGET_FILE_DIR:...>` generator expressions and `set_tests_properties(... ENVIRONMENT ...)`. The ENVIRONMENT property supports generator expressions since CMake 3.22? Actually I'm not sure. Let me think. \n\nFor ctest, the common approach: `set_tests_properties(... PROPERTIES ENVIRONMENT \"PATH=...\")`. Generator expressions in ENVIRONMENT property — I believe ENVIRONMENT supports generator expressions in newer CMake. Actually, I recall that `ENVIRONMENT` test property supports generator expressions since 3.22. Let me flag this as \"проверь по документации\" — we can compute the DLL directory at configure time since we know build dir layout. Actually simpler: since binary dir for common is `${CMAKE_CURRENT_BINARY_DIR}` in common/ subdir, and with Ninja single-config, DLL goes to `build/debug/common/common_cpp.dll`. But exe is in `build/debug/t3_bbox_cpp/`. So for t3-t4 tests we need PATH to include `build/debug/common`. \n\nBetter approach: set `CMAKE_RUNTIME_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY` so all exes and dlls go to one place? No — that would mix everything. Actually a common pattern is to put DLLs next to executables, or set output directories. But the course wants to preserve build/debug/t1_dist_matrix_cpp/... layout. Let me keep it simple: keep default per-target output dirs (build/debug/<target>/), and for t3-t4 tests set ENVIRONMENT PATH to include the shared lib dir. Use generator expression if supported, else compute path.\n\nActually there's a cleaner way: `$<TARGET_RUNTIME_DLLS:t3_cpp>` or `$<TARGET_FILE_DIR:common_cpp_shared>`. Let me flag ENVIRONMENT genex support as \"проверь\" — confidence medium. Alternative that definitely works: `add_test(...)` then `set_tests_properties(NAME PROPERTIES ENVIRONMENT \"PATH=${CMAKE_CURRENT_BINARY_DIR}/../common;$ENV{PATH}\")`. But hardcoding path is fragile. Hmm, on Windows with Ninja single-config, we can compute: the shared lib output dir is `${CMAKE_BINARY_DIR}/common`? Actually the default for a target defined in common/CMakeLists.txt: runtime output dir defaults to `${CMAKE_CURRENT_BINARY_DIR}` = `${CMAKE_BINARY_DIR}/common`. With single-config Ninja, there's no per-config subdir. So DLL = `build/debug/common/common_cpp.dll`. So PATH needs `build/debug/common`. We can get that via `$<TARGET_FILE_DIR:common_cpp_shared>` in a generator expression within ENVIRONMENT — I'm fairly confident CMake supports genex in test ENVIRONMENT property since 3.22. Let me note it as verify.\n\nAlternatively — simpler and robust: instead of PATH juggling, configure `add_test` for t3/t4 with a wrapper that sets PATH. Actually run_case runs the exe via std::system which inherits the parent environment. So the PATH must be set on the ctest process. So ENVIRONMENT property is the way.\n\nActually, there's an even cleaner CMake idiom: set `CMAKE_RUNTIME_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY` per config to a common `bin/` and `CMAKE_LIBRARY_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY`. But that changes layout from Makefile. Let me keep per-target defaults and handle PATH.\n\nHmm, wait. Actually there's another consideration. On Linux, the Makefile used LD_LIBRARY_PATH because .so had no rpath. In CMake, for build-tree, CMake automatically sets build rpath (`CMAKE_BUILD_RPATH`) so executables find the .so in the build tree without LD_LIBRARY_PATH! That's the \"известное ограничение слоя 15 уходит автоматически\" I noted. On Windows, no rpath concept — DLL search includes the exe's directory, system dirs, and PATH. So Windows t3/t4 still need PATH for tests. OK.\n\n### Flags mapping\nMakefile CXXFLAGS/CFLAGS per config. In CMake:\n- Set standards: `set(CMAKE_C_STANDARD 17)`, `set(CMAKE_CXX_STANDARD 23)`, `CMAKE_C_STANDARD_REQUIRED ON`, `CMAKE_CXX_STANDARD_REQUIRED ON`, `CMAKE_C_EXTENSIONS OFF`, `CMAKE_CXX_EXTENSIONS OFF`.\n  - Note: MSVC `CXX_STANDARD 23` → `/std:c++23`? Actually CMake maps C++23 to `/std:c++23` if supported, else `/std:c++latest`. CMake 3.30 with MSVC: for standard 23, CMake uses `/std:c++23`? Hmm, MSVC supports `/std:c++23` since VS 2022 17.8. The user has VS 2022 (D:\\VisualStudio\\Community). Let me check — I can't easily check MSVC version. But CMake handles mapping; `/std:c++23` vs `/std:c++latest` — both give `<print>`. Should be fine. Actually CMake 3.30 sets `/std:c++23` for CXX_STANDARD 23 on MSVC. Since the Makefile used `/std:c++latest`, there might be a subtle difference, but `std::print`, `std::expected`, ranges all work in `/std:c++23`. Fine. Note as verify.\n- Warnings: `add_compile_options(...)` conditioned on MSVC vs GCC:\n  - MSVC: `/W4 /permissive-`, C++ needs `/EHsc`. `/permissive-` for C too (Makefile used it for C as well). `/EHsc` only for C++.\n  - GCC: `-Wall -Wextra`.\n- Per-config flags. Instead of CMAKE_*_FLAGS_DEBUG etc., cleaner to use target_compile_options with generator expressions `$<$<CONFIG:Debug>:...>`. But warnings + standards apply to all targets. Tools run_case/check should NOT get ASan and /Zi. So per-target control is cleaner. \n\nLet me design a helper function:\n```\nfunction(configure_target tgt)\n  target_compile_options(${tgt} PRIVATE ${warnings})\n  if(MSVC)\n    target_compile_options(${tgt} PRIVATE $<$<CONFIG:Debug>:/Od /Zi /fsanitize=address> $<$<CONFIG:Release>:/O2 /Zi /DNDEBUG>)\n  ...\n```\nHmm, but /MDd vs /MD — CMake default for MSVC: `CMAKE_MSVC_RUNTIME_LIBRARY` default is MultiThreadedDLL which is `/MD`, but for Debug it's `/MDd`? Actually the default CMAKE_MSVC_RUNTIME_LIBRARY is `MultiThreaded$<$<CONFIG:Debug>:Debug>DLL` → `/MD` release, `/MDd` debug. Yes, CMake handles CRT automatically. Good — matches Makefile `/MDd` debug, `/MD` release.\n\nASan: on MSVC `/fsanitize=address`. On GCC `-fsanitize=address` both compile and link. And /Zi for pdb — CMake Debug already adds /Zi? Actually CMake default `CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_DEBUG` includes `/Zi` and `/Od`. Hmm — CMake's default debug flags for MSVC: `/D_DEBUG /MDd /Ob0 /Od /RTC1`? Let me recall. CMake sets: Debug: `/Ob0 /Od /RTC1` plus `/MDd`. Wait it's not /Zi by default? Actually I think CMake does NOT add /Zi by default for MSVC Debug; `/RTC1` is in the default. Hmm, this matters. Let me think about what's the cleanest: We could override `CMAKE_C_FLAGS_DEBUG`, `CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_DEBUG`, `CMAKE_C_FLAGS_RELEASE`, `CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_RELEASE` to match the Makefile exactly. That's the \"абстракция toolchain-флагов\" — in one place. That might be the cleanest for this project: set the four config flag variables at the top per compiler, then everything inherits. But tools run_case/check need to NOT get ASan/Zi. If we set CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_DEBUG globally, tools get them too.\n\nOptions:\n1. Per-target function with genex — most control, matches \"tools без ASan\".\n2. Global flags + `target_compile_options(tools ... )` to remove? Can't remove.\n\nSo per-target approach is better to preserve the \"tools built without /Zi and asan\" property from Makefile. Let me design:\n\n```\n# root CMakeLists.txt\nif(MSVC)\n  set(COMMON_WARNINGS /W4 /permissive-)\n  set(COMMON_DEBUG_COMPILE /Od /Zi /fsanitize=address)\n  set(COMMON_RELEASE_COMPILE /O2 /Zi /DNDEBUG)\n  ...\nelse()\n  set(COMMON_WARNINGS -Wall -Wextra)\n  set(COMMON_DEBUG_COMPILE -O0 -g -fsanitize=address)\n  set(COMMON_RELEASE_COMPILE -O2 -g -DNDEBUG)\nendif()\n```\n\nWait, MSVC Release also has link flags `/DEBUG /OPT:REF /OPT:ICF`. CMake: `set(CMAKE_EXE_LINKER_FLAGS_RELEASE \"/DEBUG /OPT:REF /OPT:ICF\")` or per-target target_link_options. For MSVC, `/DEBUG` implies `/OPT:REF` and `/OPT:ICF`? Actually /DEBUG defaults to /OPT:REF and /OPT:ICF? Hmm. The Makefile explicitly added them. We can do per-target link options with genex too.\n\nHmm, but there's a subtlety: MSVC ASan requires the link too: `/fsanitize=address` must be passed at link (CMake handles? For MSVC, /fsanitize=address at compile implies linker flag? Actually you must also pass at link stage on MSVC? I believe clang-cl and MSVC need /fsanitize=address at link too. CMake's `target_link_options` needed. In the Makefile, the LINK_FLAGS for debug was just `/DEBUG` — no /fsanitize on link! Interesting. On MSVC, `/fsanitize=address` at compile time causes the linker to pick up the ASan runtime via /DEFAULTLIB? Let me recall — MSVC ASan: you compile with /fsanitize=address and link with the same; actually the compiler driver cl handles both. When you compile an .obj with /fsanitize=address, cl adds `/include:__asan_init` and references to ASan runtime, and linking requires the ASan lib. With `link` separately, you might need to pass /fsanitize=address too, which link accepts and adds asan runtime lib. Hmm, but the Makefile links with plain `link /DEBUG` and it worked. So MSVC's /fsanitize=address at compile bakes in everything? Not sure. Actually the obj references `__asan_init` and MSVC's linker auto-resolves from default libs? I recall MSVC requires `/fsanitize=address` at link for the runtime. But the Makefile worked... because `cl` isn't just compiler - `/fsanitize=address` when passed to cl in compile-only mode embeds `/DEFAULTLIB:clang_rt.asan_dynamic-x86_64`? Hmm, honestly not sure. It worked in practice, so something resolved it. \n\nFor CMake, the safe approach: pass `/fsanitize=address` to both compile and link for debug on MSVC. CMake may even warn. Let me flag this as \"проверь по документации / на практике\". The plan shouldn't over-specify; implementation will verify.\n\nActually for tools: Makefile used `filter-out /Zi /fsanitize=address,$(CXXFLAGS)` for run_case/check. In CMake, tools are separate targets; we just don't add ASan/Zi to them. But we still want /W4 /permissive- /EHsc /MDd (debug CRT). So a helper function that applies: warnings + std + CRT (CMake default) always, and config-specific opt/asan/pdb only for \"product\" targets.\n\nLet me define:\n```\nfunction(apply_product_flags tgt)\n  # warnings always\n  target_compile_options(${tgt} PRIVATE ${PROJECT_WARNINGS})\n  if(MSVC)\n    target_compile_options(${tgt} PRIVATE $<$<CONFIG:Debug>:/Od /Zi /fsanitize=address> $<$<CONFIG:Release>:/O2 /Zi /DNDEBUG> /EHsc)\n    target_link_options(${tgt} PRIVATE $<$<CONFIG:Debug>:/DEBUG /fsanitize=address> $<$<CONFIG:Release>:/DEBUG /OPT:REF /OPT:ICF>)\n  else()\n    target_compile_options(${tgt} PRIVATE $<$<CONFIG:Debug>:-O0 -g -fsanitize=address> $<$<CONFIG:Release>:-O2 -g -DNDEBUG>)\n    target_link_options(${tgt} PRIVATE $<$<CONFIG:Debug>:-fsanitize=address>)\n  endif()\nendfunction()\n```\n\nHmm wait, on Linux debug, Makefile used `-fsanitize=address` and link `-fsanitize=address`. OK.\n\nAnd a `apply_tool_flags` for run_case/check: warnings + /EHsc + std, no asan/zi.\n\nActually, simpler: one function with a flag whether to add ASan. Or two functions. Let me keep two: `configure_product_target` and `configure_tool_target`.\n\nHmm, but this is getting into implementation detail. The plan should outline the approach and flag the uncertain bits. Let me keep it at the level of \"перечисляем флаги в одном месте через if(MSVC)/else() и genex-выражения; ASan+Zi только для продуктов, для инструментов — нет\" and mark MSVC link-asan as verify.\n\n### Standards\nMSVC C standard: `/std:c17`. CMake `CMAKE_C_STANDARD 17` → maps to `/std:c17` on MSVC 19.28+. C++: `CMAKE_CXX_STANDARD 23` → `/std:c++23` (supported in VS 2022 17.8+). Fine. `C_EXTENSIONS OFF`, `CXX_EXTENSIONS OFF`.\n\nNote: The `permissive-` in MSVC — add as warning/compile option.\n\n### Tools: run_case, check\nBuilt in tests/CMakeLists.txt. They're C++. No ASan, no /Zi. Output name: run_case.exe, check.exe. Put them where? Makefile puts them in `build/debug/tools/`. In CMake, if defined in tests/CMakeLists.txt, default output is `build/debug/tests/run_case.exe` (since binary dir = build/debug/tests). That changes layout from `build/debug/tools/`. Do we care? The layout is internal; README documents it. We could set `RUNTIME_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY` to a common location. Hmm. Minor. Let me not fuss; I'll note that tools land in `build/debug/tests/`. Or set `set_target_properties(run_case PROPERTIES RUNTIME_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY ${CMAKE_BINARY_DIR}/tools)` to preserve layout. Optional. I'll mention.\n\nActually wait — this matters for tests: ctest invokes run_case/check. If we use generator expressions `$<TARGET_FILE:run_case>` in add_test COMMAND, paths are resolved automatically regardless of where they land. So layout is cosmetic. Good — use genex in add_test.\n\n### add_test design\nThe Makefile's 94 tests can be generated in CMake with loops/functions. Design:\n\nFor each task, tests come in three flavors:\n1. exit-code only: `run_case \"exe < input > nul 2> nul\" CODE` (t1 abc/float/low/high/nul, t2 eof/empty/negative/fractional/nan, t4 radius cases)\n2. equal: `run_case \"exe < input > out 2> nul\" 0 && check --equal out expect`\n3. contains: `run_case \"exe < input > nul 2> err\" CODE && check --contains err expect` (t3 error cases, t4 coords error cases)\n4. t4 has args: `t4_filter_cpp 8 < input > out 2> nul` — radius is argv[1], and some tests (radius_*) pass only args, no input file.\n\nAlso t1 norm: no args, `t1_test1` input → equal check.\nt2 norm: t2_input → t2_norm equal.\n\nSo a CMake function `add_task_case(name exe args input exitcode mode checkfile)`. Something like:\n\n```\nfunction(add_case target_name exe args input expect exitcode mode)\n  ...\nendfunction()\n```\n\nHmm, the tricky part: on Windows the exe invocation uses `exe args < input > out 2> nul`; on Linux `exe args < input > out 2> /dev/null`. And nul vs /dev/null. Also path separators: on Windows, cmd.exe needs backslashes for the exe path (though cmd usually handles forward slashes too — but earlier journal said forward slashes cause issues in some contexts; the Makefile converts via WCMD). run_case runs `std::system(argv[1])` which on Windows goes through cmd.exe. Paths with forward slashes generally work in cmd for executables, but the input redirection `< path/with/forward/slashes` — cmd handles forward slashes in redirection? Usually yes. But to be safe, we can convert to native separators.\n\nCMake approach: build the command string at configure time:\n```\nset(NULL_DEV \"nul\")  # or /dev/null\nif(WIN32) ...\n```\nAnd exe path via `$<TARGET_FILE:${exe}>` genex — but genex inside a string passed as run_case arg... The command string contains redirects, so run_case needs the whole shell command. We can construct: `\"$<TARGET_FILE:task_cpp> < ${CMAKE_CURRENT_SOURCE_DIR}/tests/input_data/...\"` — but genex `$<TARGET_FILE:...>` inside an add_test COMMAND argument gets evaluated. So add_test(NAME t1_cpp_abc COMMAND $<TARGET_FILE:run_case> \"$<TARGET_FILE:t1_c> < ... > ${NULL} 2> ${NULL}\" 65). The genex inside the quoted string will be expanded by CMake. Yes — generator expressions are expanded in add_test COMMAND arguments.\n\nWait, but the input path has forward slashes; cmd redirection with forward slashes... The exe path from $<TARGET_FILE> on Windows uses forward or back slashes? CMake returns native path (backslashes) I think. Actually $<TARGET_FILE> returns the full path with forward slashes on all platforms? I believe CMake uses forward slashes in genex expansion historically, then for native tools it converts. Hmm. This is exactly the WCMD dance from Makefile. To avoid cmd quirks, we could avoid shell entirely by restructuring run_case to not need redirects... but that changes the tool. \n\nAlternative: keep run_case exactly as-is and build the command string carefully. On Windows use `file(TO_NATIVE_PATH)`? But genex expansion happens after configure; can't apply TO_NATIVE_PATH to genex easily. \n\nHmm, there's a cleaner CMake idiom: don't pass redirection through a shell at all. But run_case's contract is `run_case \"command\" code` where command includes redirects. We must respect it (it's existing tested tool).\n\nSimplest robust approach: define per-platform variables at configure time:\n```\nif(WIN32)\n  set(NULL_DEV \"nul\")\n  set(EXE_PREFIX \"$<TARGET_FILE:\")  # can't do this with genex names\n```\nHmm genex complicates.\n\nAlternative: pass the exe path as a separate CMake variable known at configure time? But target output paths for single-config Ninja are deterministic: `build/debug/t1_dist_matrix_cpp/main.exe`. We know binary dir layout: `${CMAKE_BINARY_DIR}/${target_binary_dir}`. Actually for an executable target `t1_c` defined in root, output = `${CMAKE_BINARY_DIR}/t1_c` unless OUTPUT_NAME set. Makefile produced `main.exe` in `build/debug/t1_dist_matrix_cpp/`. In CMake, if we keep target in root CMakeLists, exe lands in `${CMAKE_BINARY_DIR}/t1_dist_matrix_cpp_c.exe`? No — target name t1_c → output `t1_c.exe` in `${CMAKE_BINARY_DIR}`. That changes artifact names (main.exe → t1_c.exe). Do tests care? Tests use genex $<TARGET_FILE> so no. README documents main.exe though. \n\nHmm — but for teaching \"одна точка входа\" and keeping continuity, maybe set OUTPUT_NAME main and RUNTIME_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY per task to `build/debug/t1_dist_matrix_cpp/`. That reproduces the exact layout. But that's a lot of fiddly properties. Alternatively accept new naming. This is a decision point for the user maybe. But it's minor; I'll propose reproducing layout via per-target OUTPUT_NAME/RUNTIME_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY OR simpler: let CMake use its natural layout and update README. Actually the course cares about consistency of build layout for the README. Let me propose: keep the per-task subdirectory layout (build/debug/t1_dist_matrix_cpp/main.exe) via `RUNTIME_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY` property set in the add_task function, OUTPUT_NAME main. That keeps continuity and README mostly intact. It's a small function detail.\n\nBut then genex $<TARGET_FILE:t1_c> still gives correct path. Good.\n\nNow for the shell command strings with redirects: I think the cleanest is to construct them with configure-time variables for paths where possible, but exe paths need genex (they depend on target). Actually with single-config Ninja and deterministic output, the exe path is `${CMAKE_BINARY_DIR}/t1_dist_matrix_cpp/main.exe` — known at configure time! Because we set RUNTIME_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY explicitly. So no genex needed at all; we can build full strings with file(TO_NATIVE_PATH) and pass as plain variables. That's robust and simple.\n\nWait, but `CMAKE_BINARY_DIR` for build/debug = `.../build/debug`. And RUNTIME_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY `main` = `${CMAKE_BINARY_DIR}/t1_dist_matrix_cpp`. So exe = `${CMAKE_BINARY_DIR}/t1_dist_matrix_cpp/main.exe` (with .exe appended on Windows). We can compute `set(EXE_EXT $<$<PLATFORM_ID:Windows>:.exe>)` — no, simpler: the actual file has extension; we can append \".exe\" under WIN32.\n\nHmm, this is getting deep. Let me step back. The plan should present the approach at the right granularity and flag the fiddly bits. I'll describe:\n\n- add_test registration in tests/CMakeLists.txt via a `add_case()` function.\n- Command strings built at configure time using known output paths + `file(TO_NATIVE_PATH)` + platform null device (nul vs /dev/null).\n- exit-code assertion via run_case, content via check --equal/--contains, chained in one add_test (multiple COMMAND).\n- For t3/t4 tests set ENVIRONMENT PATH (Windows) / LD_LIBRARY_PATH (Linux) to the shared lib dir — via `$<TARGET_FILE_DIR:...>` if supported in ENVIRONMENT (проверь) else computed path.\n- The `.ok` files disappear — ctest tracks pass/fail itself. This is actually a simplification gain. But §4.9 rule about tests checking all three channels stays: run_case checks exit code; check verifies stdout/stderr content. Good.\n\nAlso important: ctest runs tests in parallel by default (`ctest -j`)? No, ctest default is sequential. `ctest --test-dir build/debug`. Actually `cmake --build build/debug --target test` invokes ctest. Good.\n\nFormat-check integration: The Makefile made `test` depend on format-check. In CMake, we could add format-check as a custom target and add a dependency so `ctest` runs it? ctest is separate from build targets. Options: register a format-check test in ctest (add_test with clang-format --dry-run -Werror) — that preserves \"test не зелёный без формат-чистоты\". The Makefile's format-check had \"warn if clang-format missing, don't fail\". In CMake: `find_program(CLANG_FORMAT clang-format)`, if found add_test(format-check ...), else add_test that echoes warning (passes). That's elegant and keeps the property \"tests run even without clang-format, with warning\".\n\nAlso `format` custom target: `add_custom_target(format COMMAND clang-format -i <sources>)`. Need source list. The Makefile used `$(wildcard **/*.cpp **/*.c **/*.h **/*.hpp)`. In CMake we could `file(GLOB_RECURSE ...)`. But course says avoid glob for build sources; for format target it's a helper, glob is acceptable but can be flaky. Better: build the list from the explicit sources we already know (each target's sources + headers). We can collect sources in variables as we declare targets. E.g., define `PROJECT_SOURCES` list explicitly. Since we know all files, explicit is better (matches \"неважного кода в репо нет\", explicit deps). I'll propose an explicit list assembled from the target source lists plus headers. Actually simpler: create a variable listing all .c/.cpp/.h/.hpp at top and reference it both for format and format-check. Explicit list in root CMakeLists.\n\n### Headers / dependencies\nCMake auto-tracks header deps via compiler (Ninja scans deps; MSVC /showIncludes handled by CMake's dependency scanner). This replaces the manual `CPP_OBJ_HDR` lists — that's a stated payoff of layer 16. Good, call it out.\n\n### Target include dirs\nCurrently sources include `\"../common/geometry.hpp\"` — relative include from source file location, works because main.cpp is in t1_dist_matrix_cpp/ and ../common is relative to the including file. That works regardless of build system since it's relative to source file. So we don't need include dirs. Good — but the \"proper\" CMake way is `target_include_directories(common PRIVATE)` and consumers `target_link_libraries` — linking brings include dirs via PUBLIC. Since relative includes already work, minimal change. But for cleanliness we could add `target_include_directories(common_cpp_shared PUBLIC ${CMAKE_CURRENT_SOURCE_DIR})` and change mains to `#include \"common/geometry.hpp\"`. That's a source change though. Keep sources as-is (relative includes) to minimize diff; note that include-dirs-via-target is the more robust pattern but not required now. Actually wait — layer says don't change program architecture; minimal diff. Keep relative includes. Fine.\n\n### CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE and build dirs\nUser workflow: `cmake -B build/debug -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug -G Ninja` then `cmake --build build/debug`. For release: `build/release` + `-DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release`. That matches Makefile's CONFIG. Could also add CMakePresets.json for convenience (`cmake --preset debug`) — but presets might be a later layer / extra. I'll offer as optional. Actually layer 16 mentions \"генерация под разные сборщики и IDE\" — that's about generators. Ninja chosen. Could mention that the same CMakeLists works with `-G \"Visual Studio 17 2022\"` for IDE, but that contradicts \"без IDE\". Keep Ninja.\n\nWait — one important check: does the VS-bundled CMake work from the \"x64 Native Tools Command Prompt\"? Yes, cl needs to be on PATH via vcvars64, and cmake/ninja need to be found. CMake is at D:\\VisualStudio\\Community\\Common7\\IDE\\CommonExtensions\\Microsoft\\CMake\\CMake\\bin\\cmake.exe — that path is on PATH in the Developer prompt? Actually the developer prompt adds it. And ninja at ...\\CMake\\Ninja\\ninja.exe — also in dev prompt PATH. When running cmake, if Ninja not on PATH, specify `-DCMAKE_MAKE_PROGRAM=<path>`. I'll note: verify cmake/ninja reachable in the vcvars environment; if ninja not on PATH, pass CMAKE_MAKE_PROGRAM.\n\nAlso on Linux: same CMakeLists with gcc/g++, generator default Unix Makefiles or Ninja. Course wants cross-platform; the CMakeLists with if(MSVC) else() handles it. Verify on Ubuntu VM.\n\n### What about `.bat`? None remain — good (they were removed at layer 15). git status clean.\n\n### Makefile deletion\nPer §4.9.1: \"Переходный сценарий удаляется, когда появляется сборщик\" — but here we're REPLACING make with cmake. When CMake is green, delete Makefile. Update README. This is part of the plan. Note §4.9.1 says \"Одна точка входа\": with CMake it's `cmake -B build/debug && cmake --build build/debug && ctest --test-dir build/debug`. Or a presets file. README must document. Also \"make test\" → \"ctest\".\n\nActually wait, careful: the AGENTS.md says layer 16 is CMake and the Makefile should be removed when CMake is fully functional (green tests both platforms). Also update README \"Как собрать\". Also update .gitignore? build/ already ignored. Makefile not ignored (it's tracked). Deleting Makefile = git rm.\n\n### Commits/order of work (teaching plan)\nPhased approach with checkpoints and commits:\n1. Phase A: CMake skeleton in a NEW directory, do NOT delete Makefile yet (two systems temporarily, violating §4.9.1 strictly, but it's a transition). Hmm — the course says \"Вторая параллельная система сборки не заводится\". But you can't atomically replace. The pragmatic approach: build CMake files while Makefile still exists, verify parity (both build + both test green), then delete Makefile in the same logical step/commit sequence. I'll frame it as: transition period where CMake is being verified is acceptable and short; the Makefile is deleted the moment parity is proven. Actually the rule's intent: don't maintain two permanent systems that drift. A bounded transition where both exist and are kept in sync by tests is exactly the migration; once CMake green, delete Makefile. I'll note this explicitly.\n\nBetter ordering to minimize drift:\n- Commit 1: root CMakeLists + common/CMakeLists + t1 (c/cpp) static linking builds. Verify parity of t1 exe behavior.\n- Commit 2: t2 static + t3/t4 shared (both languages), DLL/so handling. Verify runs with PATH.\n- Commit 3: tools run_case/check as CMake targets + tests/CMakeLists with add_case function registering all ~94 ctest cases. ctest green on Windows.\n- Commit 4: format/format-check targets + clang-format; `format`/`format-check` custom targets, format-check wired into ctest.\n- Commit 5: delete Makefile, update README, `.gitignore` check, AGENTS.md journal entry.\n- Commit 6: verify clean clone one-command + Linux VM verification (build + ctest), fix any GCC/if(MSVC) leaks.\n- Final: baseline make test parity double-check at each step, format-clean commits.\n\nActually a cleaner parity check: after Phase 3, run `make test` and `ctest` — both green. Good.\n\n### ASan / permissive / /analyze\nCourse: /W4 /permissive- always; /analyze — each message discussed. Makefile didn't include /analyze in the flags (only /W4 /permissive-). Wait, Makefile WARN = /W4 /permissive-. /analyze was mentioned in §4.1.4 but not in Makefile. So CMake should match Makefile (W4 permissive-), not add /analyze. Good — don't add features, replicate.\n\n### Exit codes in tests\nrun_case handles. No change.\n\n### One more: `CONFIG` vs `CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE`\nMakefile used `CONFIG ?= debug`. CMake uses CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE. On single-config generators you must pass -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE. If not passed, CMake leaves it empty → no config flags (no /Od, no ASan). That's a footgun: default should be Debug. Options: in root CMakeLists add:\n```\nif(NOT CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE)\n  set(CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE Debug CACHE STRING ...)\nendif()\n```\nThis makes `cmake -B build/debug` default to Debug like Makefile. Good, note it. Also presets could encode debug/release. I'll include the default-Debug-if-unset snippet.\n\n### .clang-format and CMakeLists\n.clang-format only affects c/cpp/h/hpp, not CMakeLists.txt. Fine. CMakeLists in UTF-8 per decision. Note: mixed encoding in repo — README/AGENTS UTF-8, sources CP1251, CMakeLists UTF-8. The .editorconfig has charset utf-8 only for .md. Should we add charset for CMakeLists? .editorconfig charset supports utf-8; CMakeLists.txt could get `[CMakeLists.txt] charset = utf-8` in .editorconfig. Nice touch. Also format-check doesn't cover CMakeLists. Fine.\n\nHmm, actually one concern: clang-format with `SortIncludes` and `IncludeBlocks: Regroup` — not relevant.\n\nAlso: CMake comments in Russian UTF-8 — fine.\n\nLet me also double check: the tests reference files by name like `t3_cpp_x_ky_z.ok` etc. In ctest, test names don't need .ok. Name them e.g. `t3_cpp_x_ky_z`. ctest shows PASS/FAIL. Good.\n\nLet me also verify how run_case command string is constructed in Makefile for the `--equal` case:\n```\n$(RUN_CASE_CMD) \"$(call WCMD,$(T3_CPP_EXE) < tests/input_data/t3_test1 > $(BINDIR)/t3_bbox_cpp/t3_test1 2> $(NULLDEV))\" 0 && $(CHECK_CMD) $(EQ) $(call WCMD,$(BINDIR)/t3_bbox_cpp/t3_test1 tests/expect/t3_test1) && $(TOUCH) $@\n```\nSo output file written to build dir, then check --equal. In ctest: single add_test with two COMMAND entries:\n```\nadd_test(NAME t3_cpp_test1\n  COMMAND $<TARGET_FILE:run_case> \"<exe> < input > out 2> nul\" 0\n  COMMAND $<TARGET_FILE:check> --equal <out> <expect>)\n```\nOutput file path must be unique per test to avoid parallel collisions — but ctest runs sequential by default. Still, name outputs in a build/test-results subdir per test. Fine.\n\nWait — add_test multiple COMMAND: \"When more than one command is specified, they are executed in order and all must pass.\" Yes. Good.\n\nNow, the tricky part: building the shell command string cross-platform. Let me define in tests/CMakeLists.txt:\n\n```\nif(WIN32)\n  set(NULL_DEV \"nul\")\nelse()\n  set(NULL_DEV \"/dev/null\")\nendif()\n```\nAnd exe paths: compute from known output dirs. But maybe cleaner: pass exe as `$<TARGET_FILE:...>` genex inside the string; CMake expands it. On Windows $<TARGET_FILE> yields `C:/.../main.exe` (forward slashes) I believe. Does cmd.exe handle forward slashes for the program path in `std::system(\"...\")`? cmd runs the command; forward slashes usually work for invoking exe (e.g., `C:/foo/main.exe` works in cmd). Redirection `2> C:/path` works too. Actually cmd redirection with forward slashes works. The Makefile's WCMD conversion was needed partly because of how make passed things and findstr etc. I think forward slashes generally OK in cmd for these invocations. But to be safe, we can construct paths at configure time with `file(TO_NATIVE_PATH ...)` since we know output locations (RUNTIME_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY is set explicitly). That yields backslashes on Windows. So no genex needed; use variables.\n\nBut input/expect files: `${CMAKE_CURRENT_SOURCE_DIR}/../tests/input_data/...` — use TO_NATIVE_PATH too.\n\nOutput/result files: write into `${CMAKE_CURRENT_BINARY_DIR}/test_out/<name>` — under build/, gitignored. Actually `CMAKE_CURRENT_BINARY_DIR` in tests/ = build/debug/tests. So outputs land in build/debug/tests/... Good (gitignored via build/).\n\nSo the add_case function computes:\n- exe native path (passed in)\n- input native path or empty\n- output native path (auto from test name)\n- null dev\n- builds command string accordingly.\n\nI think that's implementable. I'll describe it in the plan at this level, not write the code.\n\n### Linux verify\nThe user has Ubuntu VM. Plan includes running there: install cmake? VM may not have cmake. `sudo apt install cmake` + ninja-build or use Unix Makefiles. gcc version must be 14+ for std::print (C++23). Journal noted gcc needs 14+ for std::print and expected GCC 12+. The Makefile already targets GCC with -std=c++23, so the VM presumably has a recent-enough GCC (since make test passes on Linux per journal 08-30). Good. Note: same CMakeLists works; verify.\n\n### Table of flags mapping (MSVC/GCC) for plan\nPresent mapping Makefile → CMake.\n\nNow let me also confirm: does run_case take command as argv[1] as a single quoted string — yes. CMake passes each COMMAND arg as separate argv. So `\"$<TARGET_FILE:...> < ... > ...\"` must be ONE argument (quoted in CMake). In add_test(COMMAND run_case \"string\" 65) the string is one arg. Good. But note genex inside string: CMake does expand genex inside a quoted argument that contains genex. Actually generator expressions must be a full argument or appear within? CMake docs: genex can appear in any argument, expansion happens. Yes.\n\n### T4 radius tests\n`t4_cpp_radius_empty.ok`: `t4_filter_cpp > nul 2> err` (no args) exit 64, check contains. So radius tests: no input file, just optional args. e.g. `t4_filter_cpp 2 3 > nul 2> err` for too_much. And coords tests: `t4_filter_cpp 1 < input > nul 2> err` exit 65/66 contains. And normals: `t4_filter_cpp 8 < input > out 2> nul` exit 0 equal. So add_case needs an `args` parameter. Fine.\n\n### Let me also make sure about \"test\" target name\nCMake auto-creates a `test` target when enable_testing() is called (add_subdirectory tests or top-level enable_testing()). `cmake --build build/debug --target test` → runs ctest. Good — \"одна команда\" preserved.\n\nAlso `enable_testing()` must be in top-level CMakeLists (it sets up test for whole project). `include(CTest)` adds BUILD_TESTING option. Simpler: enable_testing() in root.\n\n### Presets — optional\nI'll mention CMakePresets.json as optional polish (configure debug/release with one command). Keep out of core scope; can add at end if desired. Actually since the course says CMake step involves presets possibly — no, layer 16 doesn't mention presets. Skip / optional.\n\n### gitignore\nNothing new needed: build/ covered. But CMake generates `CMakeCache.txt`, `CMakeFiles/` inside build/ — covered by build/. If user configures in-tree accidentally — warn to never do that (always -B). Fine.\n\n### Encoding note for CMakeLists + .editorconfig\nAdd `[CMakeLists.txt]` charset = utf-8 to .editorconfig. Also common/*.txt? Only CMakeLists.txt. Good.\n\n### Risk/uncertainty items to flag (anti-sycophancy):\n- [ПРЕДПОЛОЖЕНИЕ, СРЕДНИЙ] MSVC ASan: whether `/fsanitize=address` needs to be passed at link too (Makefile didn't; CMake might warn). Verify on first build.\n- [ПРЕДПОЛОЖЕНИЕ, СРЕДНИЙ] `/std:c++23` vs `/std:c++latest`: CMake maps CXX_STANDARD 23 to `/std:c++23`; if MSVC version is older, might fall back — verify `<print>` compiles.\n- [ПРЕДПОЛОЖЕНИЕ, НИЗКИЙ] genex in test ENVIRONMENT property — verify on docs; fallback to configure-time computed path.\n- cmd and forward slashes: mitigate by TO_NATIVE_PATH.\n- CMake finds ninja: may need CMAKE_MAKE_PROGRAM.\n\nLet me also verify MSVC version available. Can't easily; I'll note as verification step. Actually I could check via `where cl` / version but cl needs vcvars env which isn't loaded in this shell. Skip.\n\nNow, the plan should be comprehensive but concise. Let me structure it:\n\n1. Цель и рамки этапа 16\n2. Решения (зафиксированные)\n3. Архитектура CMake (файлы, таргеты)\n4. Маппинг флагов Makefile → CMake (таблица)\n5. Перенос тестов в ctest (add_case функция, 94 кейса, каналы проверки, DLL path)\n6. format/format-check\n7. Порядок работ с коммитами (фазы)\n8. Проверка кроссплатформенности + чистый клон\n9. Что удаляем/обновляем (Makefile, README, .editorconfig, AGENTS журнал)\n10. Неопределённости, требующие проверки\n11. Что НЕ входит в этап (gen_cloud, hello, PCH? layer mentions target_precompile_headers as motivation but not required; I'd note PCH optional/skip)\n\nWait — layer 16 text: \"target_precompile_headers\". The motivation list includes it. Should we do PCH now? Layer 14 already covered PCH (make-mechanics). In CMake, target_precompile_headers is easy. But benefit measured by stopwatch; tasks are small. I'll note it as optional stretch, not core. Keep focus.\n\nAlso gen_cloud and hello are not in Makefile, so not in CMake scope. Note that gen_cloud belongs to layer 17 (E2E generator). hello is standalone.\n\nLet me draft the plan. Keep it reasonably concise but complete. Since this is a plan for a big milestone, some length is warranted, but I'll keep tight.\n\nI should present it in Russian (per AGENTS §0: язык — русский).\n\nLet me write the plan now. I won't use todos (plan mode, read-only). Present plan and ask for confirmation / feedback on open points.\n\nLet me double-check a couple technical claims before writing, to be accurate:\n- CMAKE_CXX_STANDARD 23 mapping to /std:c++23 on MSVC: CMake 3.20+ supports C++23; for MSVC it uses /std:c++latest for versions before /std:c++23 existed, and /std:c++23 when available. MSVC 17.8+ supports /std:c++23. Reasonable.\n- CMAKE_MSVC_RUNTIME_LIBRARY default: MultiThreadedDLL ( /MD, /MDd in Debug) — yes, default is per-config dynamic CRT. Matches Makefile.\n- CMake Debug default flags include /Zi? Not necessarily. Actually CMake's default CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_DEBUG for MSVC includes /Zi and /Od. Let me recall: `CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_DEBUG_INIT = /D_DEBUG /MDd /Ob0 /Od /RTC1`. Hmm it does NOT include /Zi by default? Actually I think newer CMake does not add /Zi. Wait — but we're overriding via genex anyway per-target. If we set genex $<$<CONFIG:Debug>:/Od /Zi /fsanitize=address>, we add explicitly. There's also /RTC1 default in Debug which conflicts with /O2? /RTC1 is default Debug init; with ASan it may conflict (RTC1 + ASan incompatible!). MSVC: /RTC1 conflicts with /fsanitize=address? I believe ASan requires /RTC1 removed (they're incompatible). So we should remove /RTC1 from debug flags. Setting CMAKE_MSVC_RUNTIME_LIBRARY doesn't remove RTC. We can override CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_DEBUG. Better approach for this project: explicitly set CMAKE_C_FLAGS_DEBUG / CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_DEBUG and _RELEASE to match Makefile exactly, replacing CMake defaults. That's cleaner and matches course's \"флаги в одном месте\". So:\n\n```\nif(MSVC)\n  set(CMAKE_C_STANDARD 17) ...\n  set(CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_DEBUG \"/MDd /Od /Zi /fsanitize=address\")\n  set(CMAKE_C_FLAGS_DEBUG \"/MDd /Od /Zi /fsanitize=address\")\n  set(CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_RELEASE \"/MD /O2 /Zi /DNDEBUG\")\n  set(CMAKE_C_FLAGS_RELEASE \"/MD /O2 /Zi /DNDEBUG\")\n  set(CMAKE_EXE_LINKER_FLAGS_DEBUG \"/DEBUG /fsanitize=address\")\n  set(CMAKE_EXE_LINKER_FLAGS_RELEASE \"/DEBUG /OPT:REF /OPT:ICF\")\n  set(CMAKE_SHARED_LINKER_FLAGS_DEBUG \"/DEBUG /fsanitize=address\")\n  set(CMAKE_SHARED_LINKER_FLAGS_RELEASE \"/DEBUG /OPT:REF /OPT:ICF\")\nelse()\n  ...\nendif()\n```\nThis removes /RTC1 (good, ASan compatibility) and matches Makefile. Warnings (/W4 /permissive- /EHsc) via add_compile_options or target. /EHsc only for C++ — add via `add_compile_options($<$<COMPILE_LANGUAGE:CXX>:/EHsc>)` or target_compile_options on C++ targets. Hmm, /EHsc on C sources would error? Actually cl accepts /EHsc for C? It's C++ exception handling; for C files it's ignored/no error? Makefile passed /EHsc only in CXXFLAGS, not CFLAGS. So gate by language.\n\nBut wait — tools (run_case/check) are built in tests/CMakeLists; if we set config flags globally in root before add_subdirectory, tools inherit /MDd /Zi /fsanitize in debug — but Makefile excluded /Zi and asan from tools! So global flags would apply to tools too. To exclude, we can in tests/CMakeLists override per-target: `set_target_properties(run_case PROPERTIES COMPILE_OPTIONS ...)`. Hmm, you can't remove a global flag per-target easily. Alternative: don't put /Zi/asan in global config flags; instead apply them via a helper function to product targets only. That's the per-target approach. \n\nSo: global/standard flags: warnings, /EHsc (for C++), standards, /MD CRT (via CMAKE_MSVC_RUNTIME_LIBRARY or leave default). Config-specific /Zi/asan/O2: via helper `configure_product_target()` applied only to t1-t4 mains and common libs. Tools get only base flags.\n\nBut linker flags /DEBUG /OPT: also config-specific; tools need /DEBUG? Tools are not debugged (Makefile excluded /Zi but still linked with... Makefile linked tools with LINK_FLAGS which included /DEBUG in debug). Actually Makefile tools rule: `$(CXX) $(OBJ_OUT)... $(EXE_OUT)$@ $(filter-out /Zi /fsanitize=address,$(CXXFLAGS)) $<` — this is compile+link in one cl invocation, CXXFLAGS (no /Zi/asan), and LINK_FLAGS not used! cl link embeds /DEBUG? CXXFLAGS debug = /Od /MDd ... without /DEBUG. So tools get no /DEBUG in Makefile. OK so tools: no Zi, no asan, no /DEBUG. Matches \"тестовые программы не нуждаются в проверке\".\n\nSo per-target helper it is. Tools built with just warnings + /MDd + /Od? They don't need /Od either — Makefile tools had /Od from CXXFLAGS (debug) since filter-out only removed /Zi/asan. Eh, /Od harmless. Keep simple: tools get warnings + std + CRT. Fine.\n\nActually to minimize divergence I'll say: helper `setup_target(<tgt> [SANITIZE])` applies warnings+EHsc+std; if SANITIZE, adds config compile/link flags. This is the \"абстракция toolchain-флагов\" — one function, no ifeq blocks.\n\nOK. Also for C standard: CMAKE_C_STANDARD 17 sets /std:c17 on MSVC. C extensions off. Good.\n\nNow /fsanitize=address at link for MSVC — Makefile didn't pass it at link. If we add it and it's accepted, fine; if not, drop. Flag as verify. Actually MSVC link.exe accepts /fsanitize=address? I'm not sure link.exe has that option. cl passes it through. Hmm. When linking via cl (as Makefile tools do with single cl invocation), cl handles it. When using separate link.exe... The Makefile products use `link /DEBUG` only (no asan on link) and it worked with ASan — meaning MSVC bakes asan runtime refs into obj and default libs resolve it. Actually MSVC's /fsanitize=address: the compiler adds `/DEFAULTLIB` and references; linking with link.exe works if the asan runtime lib is found via default search. I think link.exe doesn't need the flag. So we should NOT add /fsanitize=address to MSVC link flags — match Makefile. For GCC, -fsanitize=address IS needed at link (driver g++ handles). So: MSVC link flags: only /DEBUG etc; GCC link: add -fsanitize=address in debug. \n\nI'll put this nuance in the plan (table) since it's a real difference the user should learn.\n\nAlso, ctest on Windows PATH for t3/t4: the dll dir. Compute: common shared target binary dir = `${CMAKE_BINARY_DIR}/common` (single-config). So PATH env = `\"${CMAKE_BINARY_DIR}/common\"`. Set via set_tests_properties(NAME PROPERTIES ENVIRONMENT \"PATH=${CMAKE_BINARY_DIR}/common;$ENV{PATH}\") — but $ENV{PATH} at configure time is the configure-time PATH (vcvars env), which is fine to inherit as baseline; ctest merges? ENVIRONMENT replaces entire env? No — it sets/overrides listed vars, others inherited from ctest's env. So `ENVIRONMENT \"PATH=...;${saved_path}\"`? If we set ENVIRONMENT \"PATH=build/debug/common\" alone, ctest runs with PATH = just that? Documentation: \"the environment variable is set to the given value\" for each `NAME=value` — if we specify PATH=... only, other vars (SystemRoot etc.) inherited from ctest process. But PATH would be replaced, losing System32 — bad. So prepend current PATH. We can capture `set(_cur_path \"$ENV{PATH}\")` at configure and build full value. That works. Or use genex `$<TARGET_FILE_DIR:common_cpp_shared>` — verify if ENVIRONMENT supports genex (I believe it does NOT reliably; docs say ENVIRONMENT property does not support generator expressions? Actually CMake docs: test property ENVIRONMENT \"supports generator expressions\"? I'm not sure. I'll present both, recommend configure-time path, mark genex as verify).\n\nHmm, actually I recall ENVIRONMENT property DOES support generator expressions since CMake 3.22. But not fully certain. Recommend computed path (robust), mention genex as alternative to verify.\n\nOn Linux, LD_LIBRARY_PATH not needed because CMake build rpath handles .so. So ENVIRONMENT only on Windows. Or set unconditionally? On Linux, PATH env would be harmless. Use if(WIN32).\n\nAlright. Also need to make sure tests' working directory: run_case command strings use absolute native paths, so cwd doesn't matter. Good.\n\nNow the add_case function signature — let me finalize a clean design to describe:\n\n```\nfunction(add_case task_name lang label mode exit_code input expect)\n  # target exe: t1_cpp etc.\n  # mode: exit / equal / contains\n  set(exe \"$<TARGET_FILE:${task_name}_${lang}>\")  -- or native path computed\n  ...\nendfunction()\n```\n\nGiven the complexity, maybe simpler: since we know output paths deterministically, compute exe native path from RUNTIME_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY + OUTPUT_NAME + EXE_EXT. But TARGET_FILE genex is more robust. The command string passed to run_case is a shell string; we need it as a single CMake argument containing the exe path. If we use genex, the whole string must contain genex expansions — CMake expands them. That's fine: `set(cmd \"$<TARGET_FILE:t1_cpp> < ...\")`. Wait but then file redirection paths: also could be genex-free computed variables. Mixing genex and plain vars in one string is fine; CMake expands both (genex at generate, vars at configure). But TO_NATIVE_PATH can't be applied to genex. On Windows, $<TARGET_FILE> yields forward slashes? Docs: $<TARGET_FILE> returns full path \"using / as directory separator\"? Hmm, I think CMake genex path outputs use forward slashes. cmd generally accepts forward slashes 