or for Makefiles generator CMake adds -MMD -MP itself, or uses compiler dependency scanning). So it becomes implicit.\n- `-fPIC` is in PIC_FLAGS, used for the static-compiled-into-shared objs. In CMake, SHARED library targets get POSITION_INDEPENDENT_CODE automatically. So also becomes implicit.\n\nSo the answer: yes, in the Makefile they're Linux-only (Windows side uses /showIncludes-analog via /Fi? no, Windows side has manual header dep lists — the CPP_OBJ_HDR stuff; and PIC_FLAGS is empty on Windows). And the point is that in CMake both become automatic — which is precisely the \"автоматические header-зависимости\" motivation.\n\nNow the bigger teaching question: what is CMake fundamentally, and why do it if make works?\n\nLet me think about how to explain this well for Irina, who has just spent a huge amount of effort on layer 15 (the cross-platform Makefile). She's invested. Her pushback is legitimate: the Makefile IS cross-platform and works. The course materials say layer 16 motivation is \"not portability (already there), but: automatic header deps, toolchain flag abstraction, find_package+vcpkg, target_precompile_headers, generation for different build systems and IDEs.\"\n\nI should be honest: the \"you need CMake because it's the industry standard\" argument is real but weak on its own. The real arguments for her specifically:\n\n1. **The header-dependency maintenance problem is exactly the right scale now.** She has 8 tasks, ~10 headers, and already hand-writes dep lists. The journal (2026-08-25) shows she ALREADY hit the stale-dependency problem: adding `double pad` to Point required rebuilding things, and she noted make doesn't track headers on MSVC. The bug where main.exe used a stale .hpp and ASan caught a struct size mismatch — that's a real cost of manual header deps. So \"не так трудно помнить про них\" — but she already forgot once and it caused a real bug. That's the honest, concrete argument: it's not about difficulty, it's about the class of errors that manual dep lists create.\n\n2. **CMake is a build-system generator, not a build system.** This is the fundamental concept. make builds directly. CMake *describes* the project once and *generates* build files for many build tools (Ninja, Makefiles, NMake, Visual Studio, Xcode, etc.). One description → many backends. This is the \"абстракция\" — the Makefile is one artifact; CMake is a meta-level.\n\n3. **The declarative model is different from make's imperative model.** Make = graph of file targets + recipes (procedural, \"how to make this file from these files\"). CMake = declared targets with properties (what sources, what flags, what links, what to link to), and CMake figures out the \"how\". Key contrast: in make she writes *recipes* (cl /c ... link ...); in CMake she declares *targets* and *dependencies between targets* (add_executable, target_link_libraries). This is the fundamental conceptual shift.\n\n4. **The \"why\" honest list for her specific situation:**\n   - Automatic header deps on both platforms (she already has the bug history).\n   - Toolchain abstraction: the Makefile has TWO copies of every flag (ifeq blocks, ~150 lines of platform branching). CMake: one place, conditional blocks shrink to if(MSVC)/else() for the few real differences. The flags themselves (warnings, std) are largely the same expressed as target properties.\n   - Real dependency management with external libraries: vcpkg integration, find_package. Her next stages (layer 18) need this. When a real project has 20 dependencies, the Makefile approach of hand-writing paths collapses. This is the \"this is where the industry is\" point — but I should frame it as: the course will require it (vcpkg/GoogleTest), not abstractly.\n   - Multi-config/build-type handling: the axis of Debug/Release becomes a first-class concept (CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE / configs), already anticipated in §4.7.\n   - IDE generation — for her, the VS \"Build button\" era. But careful: the course says console-first. The IDE generation is a property, not the point. Actually, for Inobitec stack, plugins/IDE — she will likely end up in VS or another IDE; CMake is how industrial C++ projects are consumed.\n\n5. **A fair counterpoint (anti-sycophancy):** Is CMake necessary right now? Honestly: no, the Makefile works. The course builds layers deliberately. But the course's rationale (02-praktika) is that the layers are pedagogical steps to production competency, and the industry reality is that make remains used but CMake dominates for C++ projects, and her stage-2 requirements (vcpkg, GoogleTest, Release, optimization ladder) land much more naturally on CMake. Also — one Makefile with hand-maintained deps works at 8 tasks; it becomes the thing that breaks at 20 modules with external libs. The skill being trained is \"understand the build system\" — make gave the foundation (she now understands what cl/link/obj/lib/dll are and what a build graph is). CMake layer teaches the industry layer on top of that foundation.\n\nLet me also address the specific phrasing \"зачем мне это вообще надо если сейчас makefile кросплатформенный и отлично работает\". I should validate the pushback — it's actually correct thinking! The course itself says the motivation is NOT portability. Good engineering instinct. But then explain what the real, concrete pain points are that CMake removes, tied to things that already happened in her journal:\n- 2026-08-25: the `double pad` / stale .hpp / ASan struct mismatch — manual dep list forgot → real bug.\n- The journal 2026-08-12: the run_case.exe 0-byte file mystery, \"make doesn't know which commands depend on order\".\n- The WCMD/backslash/slash pain, the `%-pattern` escaping pain (2026-08-12: `$(BINDIR)\\%.obj` escaping), grouped-targets hack for dll+lib (2026-08-17).\n- The vc140.pdb parallel-write issue (2026-08-26) — make parallelizing writes to one PDB.\n\nThese are all real things she already fought that CMake removes. That's the honest, concrete answer: \"ты уже платила эти налоги — вот список твоих же записей в журнале\".\n\nI want to explain CMake conceptually with a good analogy. She likes analogies. Let me craft:\n\n**Analogy: рецепт vs заказ в ресторане / спецификация чертежа.** \n\nmake — это конвейер/сборочный цех с инструкциями для каждой детали: \"вот из этих исходников собери так\". Ты описываешь КАК (шаги) и ЗАВИСИМОСТИ (что от чего).\n\nCMake — это конструкторское бюро: ты описываешь ЧТО есть (проект = набор таргетов, у каждого — исходники, флаги, что с чем линкуется, какая это библиотека), а CMake уже САМ решает КАК это собрать под конкретную систему. Он генерирует файлы для разных сборщиков (make, ninja, nmake, VS).\n\nAnother good analogy: компилятор vs архитектор. Or: make — язык ассемблера сборки, CMake — C++ сборки. Hmm, that's too meta. Let me think.\n\nActually a good one: \n- Makefile = \"как приготовить\": пошаговая инструкция для конкретной кухни (печь духовка, плита газовая).\n- CMake = \"рецепт в абстрактном виде\": ингредиенты и свойства, не привязанные к кухне. Из одного рецепта повар (генератор) делает инструкцию под конкретную кухню.\n\nOr engineering drawing analogy — design intent vs fabrication instructions. CAD! She does CAD (T-FLEX). That's a GREAT analogy for her background:\n- CMake = CAD-модель: ты задаёшь параметры детали (материалы, размеры, сопряжения) — модель параметрическая.\n- Makefile = техпроцесс (CAM): конкретные операции на конкретном станке с конкретными скоростями.\n- Генератор CMake = постпроцессор: из одной модели делает техпроцесс под конкретный станок.\nIf you change the material, CAD updates the model; you re-postprocess for each machine. In make, you edit each machine's program by hand.\n\nThat's very fitting given her T-FLEX background. Let me use that.\n\nAlso the \"why CMake over make\" — the decisive argument that isn't \"industry standard\": **the build description becomes data, not code.** In her Makefile, build logic (recipes) is code that runs; in CMake, it's a model that tools can query (IDE integration, IDE shows targets, ctest knows tests, cpack knows packaging, vcpkg knows dependencies). And dependencies between targets are explicit typed relationships (link vs compile vs test), so tools can reason about them.\n\nAlso: **the \"сколько команд на поддержку\" question.** Her Makefile is 1048 lines for 8 tiny programs + ~94 tests. That's a LOT of build description per unit of functionality. CMake will be ~10x less. That's not because make is bad — it's because the Makefile encodes by hand what CMake generates. The maintenance cost per added feature (e.g., \"add a 5th task\") in make: new ~20 lines of targets + ~15 test lines each; in CMake: one add_task() call + a few add_case() calls.\n\nLet me also be honest about what's NOT gained: CMake does not make builds faster, doesn't fix her code, won't remove the need to understand linking. And the course is right that she should have done make first — make taught the mental model (build graph, targets, recipes, deps). CMake assumes you understand that. So the Makefile was NOT wasted — it's the foundation that makes CMake comprehensible. This addresses potential \"I wasted all that time\" feeling.\n\nNow, question 1 specifics. Let me re-verify the Makefile details to answer precisely.\n\nFrom Makefile Linux section:\n```\nCFLAGS := $(CSTD) $(WARN) -MMD -MP $(CONFIG_FLAGS)\nCXXFLAGS := $(CXXSTD) $(WARN) -MMD -MP $(CONFIG_FLAGS)\n```\nSo yes, -MMD -MP in Linux C/CXXFLAGS (auto deps). \n\nPIC_FLAGS:\n```\nPIC_FLAGS := -fPIC   (Linux)\nPIC_FLAGS :=          (Windows)\n```\nUsed in the static lib rules:\n```\n$(BINDIR)/common/geometry_cpp$(OBJ_EXT): common/geometry.cpp ... $(PIC_FLAGS) ...\n```\nWait — but the shared lib on Linux uses `$(CPP_OBJ)` = the same \"static\" objs. And the main exes compiled via pattern rule don't get -fPIC (correct, exes don't need it). So -fPIC is applied to the common module objs on Linux because they get linked into a .so. Correct.\n\nOn Windows: PIC_FLAGS empty — MSVC doesn't need PIC; DLLs on Windows are position-independent by default (different mechanism, import/export tables). Correct.\n\nSo answer: Да. -MMD -MP — в Linux-части (Windows полагается на ручные деп-листы CPP_OBJ_HDR, что и есть предмет боли). -fPIC — в Linux-части, только для объектников common, которые идут в .so (exe его не требует; под Windows он не нужен в принципе — там другой механизм, import/export). В CMake оба станут неявными: депы — автоматом, -fPIC — CMake ставит POSITION_INDEPENDENT_CODE для SHARED таргетов сам.\n\nLet me be careful about -MMD -MP and CMake: For Ninja generator with GCC, CMake uses `-MD -MT ...` or depfile. Actually CMake generates deps automatically through the compiler (it knows the flags). For the Makefiles generator, CMake also handles deps itself (uses -MD/-MMD under the hood or cmake's dependency scanning). Either way, the user doesn't write -MMD. So I confirm: in CMake these flags become the generator's job.\n\nNow — how detailed should I be? The user asked for an explanation of what CMake is fundamentally and why it's needed. This deserves a substantive teaching answer. She's in a learning program; the mentor reviews; depth is good here. But I should keep it structured and not bloated. Given the tone guidance \"concise\", but this is an explicit request for explanation (\"расскажи мне о том, что такое CMake принципиально\"), so detail is warranted. I'll write a solid explanation but tight.\n\nI should also mark categories [ФАКТ/МНЕНИЕ/ПРЕДПОЛОЖЕНИЕ] and confidence per the anti-sycophancy protocol, since she's now running that protocol in session. Actually, that protocol was set up in the second message. I should apply it. Let me include category/confidence tags where relevant, at least on claims that matter.\n\nAlso I must stay in plan mode: no edits. Just answer + maybe refine the plan with the teaching clarifications. I can also add to the plan if there are adjustments. The user hasn't approved