 same: understanding of cl/link/lib/obj/dll, linking, flags — all still needed; CMake sits on top.\n3. Why, concretely, given her working Makefile.\n   - Honest framing: the Makefile is not wasted; it was the foundation. Industry-layer reasoning.\n   - The real pains she already paid (with journal citations) that CMake removes.\n   - The scalability/typed-dependency/one-model-many-backends argument.\n   - Forward-looking: vcpkg/GoogleTest/Release/optimization ladder (stage 2) need it; the course's layer list is deliberate.\n   - Counterpoint: is it necessary NOW? Not strictly. But the course deliberately sequences it; and the \"remembering headers\" argument is already disproven by her own history.\n4. Maybe a small note on what changes in the plan (nothing structural, maybe add a plan note that the Windows hand-dep lists disappear).\n\nI should keep it readable. Use headers/bullets. Russian. Not too long but substantial — this is a teaching conversation.\n\nLet me verify the \"double pad\" incident from the journal to cite accurately. Journal 2026-08-25:\n\"добавила double pad перед x в common/geometry.hpp. make test -> 1)компиляция geometry_cpp.obj и link t1_..cpp/main.exe ... 4)линк /common_cpp.dll. потому что только у t1_...cpp/main.exe прямая зависимость от geometry_cpp.obj, а у него - от geometry.hpp. и внутри main работа только с .x .y Point-a - при добавлении pad они просто сдвигаются в памяти... но хотелось бы ещё и пересборку для main t3-t4, т.к. там тоже есть include geometry.hpp... при запуске отдельно main.exe к t3 cpp ... всё упало и asan вывел сообщение - что Point max_border = 24 байта ... а я пытаюсь обратиться к .z который теперь уже за границами 24 байтов. main.exe не обновляется и \"внутри\" остаётся старый .hpp...\"\nYes — exactly the stale-header bug. And she then fixed by making obj depend on all headers of its side, over-approximation, \"лишние заголовочники\". Perfect citation.\n\nAlso 2026-08-17: \"нашёл достаточно много неточностей - забыла h/hpp в зависимостях, мёртвый код, забыла изменить пререкв.\" — yes.\n\nAlso 2026-08-12: the 0-byte run_case.exe incident and \"make не гарантирует порядка выполнения команд\" / \"$(BINDIR)\\%.obj\" escaping. Also WCMD/backslash. And grouped-targets for dll+lib 2026-08-17. And vc140.pdb parallel conflict 2026-08-26. These are all \"make taxes\". Good citations but I shouldn't overload — pick the strongest 2-3.\n\nNow write the answer. Keep tone teacher-like, honest, not preachy. Include category/confidence tags on key claims.\n\nI'll also incorporate into the plan? The plan stands; maybe I add a note that the header-dep hand-lists disappear (already in the table) and add the FAQ content as plan motivation. I think I can just mention at the end: \"план не меняется; если хочешь — в начало плана добавлю блок 'мотивация' с этим объяснением.\" Actually I could propose adding a \"мотивация\" section to the plan doc... but the plan is in chat, not a file. Fine.\n\nLet me draft.\n\nFormat: use headers, bullets, a table for the flags. Keep it tight but complete. This is a case where more than 4 lines is clearly warranted (explicit conceptual request).\n\nI need to be careful about tone instructions: \"concise, direct\". But the user explicitly asked for a conceptual explanation. I'll be substantial but disciplined — no fluff.\n\nAlso: the user asked Q1 \"верно?\" — I must confirm precisely. Let me restate the exact lines:\n- Linux CFLAGS/CXXFLAGS: `-MMD -MP` (lines 110-111)\n- Linux `PIC_FLAGS := -fPIC` (line 87), applied only in the 4 common-module object rules (lines 328-337), because on Linux the .so is linked from those objs.\n- Windows: PIC_FLAGS empty; no -fPIC needed (PE/import-export mechanism); deps manual via CPP_OBJ_HDR (lines 302-303).\n\nIn CMake:\n- deps: automatic via the generator/compiler depfiles on both platforms (no -MMD, no hand lists). [ФАКТ, ВЫСОКИЙ]\n- -fPIC: for SHARED targets CMake sets position-independent code by default (POSITION_INDEPENDENT_CODE ON); for STATIC it's OFF by default — and since on Linux the static objs feed the .so, in CMake we won't hand-add -fPIC; either the shared lib compiles its own sources (cleanest) or we set PIC explicitly. Wait — in my plan, the static lib (t1-t2) and shared lib (t3-t4) are separate targets each compiling their own objs. So on Linux, common_cpp_shared compiles its sources itself → CMake adds -fPIC automatically for SHARED. The static lib objs don't need PIC. So the Makefile's \"compile once with -fPIC and reuse for both static and shared\" trick disappears — CMake compiles the module twice (once for static, once for shared) which is actually cleaner and matches the Makefile's Windows approach (which also compiled _dll objs separately). Good point to mention: on Windows make already compiles common twice (normal + _dll), on Linux once (reused with -fPIC); CMake will compile twice on both — this is normal and correct, cost is negligible.\n\nGood. Let me write.\n\nActually, let me double-check the Makefile: does the Linux shared lib reuse the same objs? Lines 382-388: `CPP_OBJ := $(BINDIR)/common/geometry_cpp$(OBJ_EXT) ...` and lines 411-415 link `$(CPP_SHARED): $(CPP_OBJ)`. Yes. And those objs were built with -fPIC (line 328). And COMMON_STATIC define (they're the \"static\" variant objs). On Linux COMMON_API is empty anyway, so the macro doesn't matter. So yes, one set of objs with -fPIC, used for both the static lib (t1-t2 link directly to objs) and the .so (t3-t4). Confirmed.\n\nIn CMake, the static and shared are separate targets → sources compiled twice on both platforms. Minor, fine.\n\nNow write the final answer.",
