          "type": "reasoning",
          "text": "The user is testing her understanding. She has two claims:\n\n1. With CMake, she could say: \"test cases will be named like X, input/expected data names match by %, exit codes should be such-and-such\" and CMake would write all those tests for her in the Makefile.\n\n2. She understood that CMake adds .d files itself and -fPIC automatically, so CMake has a \"representation\" of the project — it can add flags automatically when it \"sees\" the need.\n\nI need to verify her understanding — the anti-sycophancy protocol: be critical, don't just agree. Let me analyze both claims carefully.\n\n**Claim 1: CMake writes tests \"for you\" declaratively**\n\nThis is mostly right but needs nuance. CMake doesn't \"write tests in the Makefile\" per se. What happens:\n\n- You write `add_test(NAME t3_cpp_test1 COMMAND run_case \"...\" 0 COMMAND check --equal ...)` — you register tests declaratively.\n- In a Makefile-based generator, CMake generates a `CTestTestfile.cmake` and a `test` target that calls `ctest`. The `.ok` file mechanism disappears entirely — ctest tracks pass/fail itself.\n\nKey correction: CMake does NOT infer test names/inputs/expected files from filename patterns (the `%` matching she describes). That's not a thing. CMake is not that \"smart\" — it won't scan `tests/expect/` and auto-discover that `t3_cpp_test1.ok` corresponds to input `t3_test1` and expected `t3_test1`. There's no pattern-based test auto-generation built-in. You still describe each test explicitly (name, command, expected exit code). \n\nBUT — and this is important — you can write a CMake function (or loop) that encodes the \"pattern\" once, and then each test becomes a short call. In my plan, the `add_case()` function does exactly this: it encodes the pattern \"test named X, runs exe with these args, exit code E, compares with expect file\" once, and the 94 cases become function calls. So the spirit of her idea is right — the pattern is declared once, not repeated — but it's not CMake doing magic pattern-matching on filenames. It's her writing a function that captures the pattern.\n\nAlso: CMake doesn't write \"in the Makefile\" — it writes a separate test description (CTestTestfile.cmake), and ctest runs tests independently of make. The `.ok` files — she shouldn't keep them; ctest replaces them.\n\nAlso one more correction: exit codes — she says \"выходные коды должны быть такие-то\". Right: you pass the expected exit code to run_case (which checks it). CMake itself doesn't have a native \"expect this exact exit code\" feature (there's WILL_FAIL but that's just inversion). So her existing run_case tool remains the mechanism. ctest just runs run_case and checks its exit status.\n\nSo claim 1: correct in spirit (declare once, pattern captured), wrong in detail (no `%` filename matching; tests not written \"into the Makefile\"; .ok files disappear; explicit function needed).\n\n**Claim 2: CMake has a \"representation\" of the project, adds flags automatically**\n\nThis is a subtle one. She's half right. Let me be careful:\n\n- Does CMake add `.d` files itself? On the Linux/GCC side, yes — for a Makefile generator, CMake handles dependency scanning itself (it generates `.d` handling or uses compiler dependency scanning). For Ninja it uses the compiler's depfile. So yes, header deps are automatic — this is TRUE. But nuance: it's not that CMake \"sees the need\" — it's that the generator knows the compiler emits dependency info and wires it into the build graph. It's a built-in feature of the build tool (Ninja/CMake Makefiles), keyed to the compiler, not a \"consciousness.\"\n\n- Does it add `-fPIC` automatically? Yes — for SHARED library targets, CMake sets `POSITION_INDEPENDENT_CODE` by default (actually for SHARED and MODULE it's ON by default). So a SHARED target compiled on Linux gets -fPIC. TRUE.\n\n- The general claim \"he has a representation of the project, so he can add flags automatically when he sees the need\" — this is a dangerous overgeneralization. CMake is NOT a smart system that understands \"your code uses std::print so I add -std=c++latest\". It does NOT do semantic analysis. What it does:\n  - It has a *declarative model* of targets (sources, include dirs, link libs, library type, standard).\n  - From that model it derives *concrete* build rules, including things that follow *mechanically* from target properties: e.g., SHARED → -fPIC; linking a target with another → adds its include dirs and import lib; standard set on the project → adds the std flag.\n  - But it never \"infers\" something you didn't declare. If you don't tell it the sources, it won't find them. If you don't say `target_link_libraries(t3 PRIVATE common_cpp_shared)`, it won't link — and worse, won't add the dllimport define for the consumer (that's YOUR macro wiring via COMMON_EXPORTS/COMMON_STATIC, which CMake doesn't know about — it's your `target_compile_definitions`).\n\nSo the right mental model: CMake's \"intelligence\" is *mechanical derivation from declared properties*, not *observation/inference*. It's like a form that expands based on what you filled in. The flag-following-from-property works because CMake KNOWS the toolchain's flag conventions (it has per-compiler knowledge: \"for SHARED on GCC, you need -fPIC\"; \"for Debug on MSVC, /MDd\"). That's real and valuable — but bounded. It does not know what your code does.\n\nI should confirm her good parts and correct the overgeneralization. This is exactly the anti-sycophancy moment: she's reaching for a \"magical\" understanding and I should sharpen it.\n\nLet me also tie to her .d observation: actually the nuance — CMake doesn't add \".d files\" as a flag. It wires dependency tracking into the build tool. On the Makefile generator with GCC, CMake uses `-MD`/`-MMD` style or compiler dependency scanning (actually modern CMake uses CMAKE_DEPENDS... the generated makefiles have a \"depend\" step and per-source dep files). The *observable* effect is the same as