          "text": "Let me parse what the user is asking. She's answering my comprehension-check question and raising new points.\n\nHer answer (point 0): She's responsible for declaring all necessary files in the \"model\", CMake helps \"finish\" based on what she built - same flags. But if she forgets that for common_...dll she needs geometry_...obj, CMake won't guess it's needed there. And she thinks not all flags will be set by CMake - like /EHsc, /W4, std... she still has to add them herself.\n\nPoint 1: \"то есть я в CMakeLists пишу функции, в которых есть протопипы, и потом вызываю их с разными параметрами. ну и переменные всякие могу объявить\" - she's asking to confirm her understanding that CMakeLists has functions with \"prototypes\" she calls with different parameters, and can declare variables.\n\nPoint 2: \"то есть 'тестирование' через makefile уходит, и отдаётся ctest? кратко расскажи что он там внутри делает для тестирования\" - she asks to confirm that testing moves from makefile to ctest, and wants a brief explanation of what ctest does internally for testing.\n\nI'm in plan mode - read only. I need to answer her questions, correct/clarify misconceptions, and confirm understanding. I should be critical (anti-sycophancy protocol from AGENTS.md §4.2).\n\nLet me address each:\n\n**Point 0 - her understanding of who's responsible for what:**\nShe's mostly right. Her claim: she declares files, CMake \"finishes\" flags based on what she built. If she forgets the obj/dependency, CMake won't guess. And she thinks not all flags are auto-set (EHsc, W4, std... she adds them herself).\n\nThis is correct. CMake does NOT set /W4 /permissive- /EHsc automatically. Those are project policy - CMake has defaults that are minimal (for MSVC, default is no /W4). std: CMake sets the standard via CMAKE_CXX_STANDARD / set_target_properties - you declare it. /EHsc - actually CMake sets exception handling flags by default on MSVC (/EHsc is default in newer CMake for C++). Let me be careful. Actually CMake by default on MSVC compiles C++ with /EHsc since... hmm. Actually the default CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS for MSVC includes /EHsc. Let me think. MSVC default CMake flags: CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS default includes /EHsc (it's added by CMake as part of its default MSVC flags). Yes - CMake's default for MSVC C++ compilation includes /EHsc. But /W4, /permissive- are NOT default - those are policy additions you make. So her statement \"EHsc, W4, std... всё же я сама добавлять должна\" - partly right, partly not. /EHsc comes by default from CMake for MSVC (it's in CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS). /W4 - no, you add. /permissive- - no, you add. std - you declare via CXX_STANDARD property (which then emits /std:c++20 or similar).\n\nSo I should correct this nuance: /EHsc is actually on by default for MSVC in CMake (in CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS). Let me verify... In CMake, for MSVC, the default CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS is \"/DWIN32 ... /EHsc\" - yes, /EHsc is part of CMake's default flags for MSVC C++ compiler. Actually I recall CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS for MSVC includes /EHsc by default. Yes.\n\nBut the deeper point: the distinction between \"build policy flags\" (/W4, /permissive-, warnings) which are project conventions and CMake doesn't guess, vs \"mechanical derivations\" (PIC for shared, runtime lib by config, /EHsc default, std from declared property, /Od//O2 by build type). She's right that /W4 and /permissive- she must add. Good correction to refine.\n\nAlso her example: \"если я забуду, что для common_...dll мне нужен geometry_...obj, То он не догадается\" - correct! Sources must be listed. CMake won't find sources by magic. Right.\n\n**Point 1 - \"в CMakeLists пишу функции, в которых есть протопипы\" - functions with prototypes?**\n\nThis needs a correction. CMake functions are NOT like C/C++ functions with prototypes. A CMake function() is a macro-like command that you define with function(name args...) ... endfunction(), and call with name(arg1 arg2). There are no prototypes/headers. The function body is in the same file (or included). It's more like a template/command definition. The parameters are named and referenced as ${arg}. It's not \"прототипы\" in the C sense. Let me correct this carefully - she might be conflating CMake functions with C prototypes. Also important: unlike C, a CMake function must be DEFINED before it's called (the call executes the body). No forward declarations.\n\nActually - important correction: CMake function is closer to a macro in shell/C preprocessor sense. It doesn't \"declare\" then \"define\" elsewhere. It's a command that you define inline (function(...) ... endfunction()) and it must appear textually before use.\n\nVariables: yes, she can declare variables with set(). Correct.\n\n**Point 2 - ctest internal mechanism:**\n\nShe asks briefly what ctest does internally. Let me explain:\n- When you call enable_testing() or include(CTest), CMake writes a file called CTestTestfile.cmake (in build dir) containing add_test(...) entries you declared.\n- ctest reads these, runs each test as a subprocess (executes the COMMAND), captures output, and compares against properties like PASS_REGULAR_EXPRESSION, or just checks exit code (0 = pass).\n- It reports PASS/FAIL, aggregates into a summary.\n- ctest test runs with working directory = build dir by default, or WORKING_DIRECTORY property.\n- ENVIRONMENT property sets env vars for the test process.\n- It can run in parallel (-j), run specific tests by regex (-R), etc.\n- The \"make test\" target just invokes ctest.\n\nSo the .ok-file mechanism disappears; ctest IS the harness. run_case and check still exist as executables that ctest launches.\n\nLet me also tie to her project: for the ~94 cases, each becomes an add_test. ctest runs run_case (which checks exit code) as a test command; the test passes if run_case returns 0.\n\nI should also address her implicit question of the workflow: `cmake --build build` then `ctest` (or `make test` equivalent). And that in plan, tests are in tests/CMakeLists.txt.\n\nNow let me be concise but thorough. She's asking several things. Format: numbered responses matching her points. Keep language Russian. Be critical where needed (anti-sycophancy): correct the \"прототипы\" misconception, correct the /EHsc default nuance, confirm the ctest understanding but add the internal mechanism.\n\nAlso I should keep plan mode - no edits. This is a comprehension/teaching conversation. At the end, perhaps confirm the plan is still valid and ask if we proceed. Actually the user is in the middle of comprehension check - I should answer and maybe ask if she's ready for commit 1.\n\nLet me structure:\n\n0. Your understanding of responsibilities - correct, with two refinements:\n   - Correct: you declare sources, CMake won't guess missing obj/dependency.\n   - Correct: /W4 /permissive- you must add - CMake has no idea about your warning policy.\n   - Refinement: not ALL flags - /EHsc on MSVC is actually default in CMake; /Od//O2 by build type, /MDd//MD by config, PIC for shared, std from declared property - these CMake derives. So the border: \"policy flags\" (warnings, permissive, sanitizers) = yours; \"mechanical flags\" (runtime lib by config, PIC by lib type, opt level by build type, std from property) = CMake's.\n   - Her example with geometry_obj is exactly right.\n\n1. Functions - correct the \"прототипы\" wording:\n   - function()/endfunction() defined inline, no prototypes/headers.\n   - Must be defined before call (unlike C forward declaration).\n   - Called by name with args; args bound to named params.\n   - Variables: set() - yes.\n   - It's like a command you invent; CMake also has built-in commands.\n   - Clarify analogy: not like C functions with prototype; more like a macro/recipe you invoke.\n\n2. ctest - confirm + internal mechanism:\n   - enable_testing() → CMake writes CTestTestfile.cmake.\n   - ctest reads it, for each add_test launches the COMMAND as separate subprocess in the test working dir.\n   - Pass =