          "type": "reasoning",
          "text": "The user asks: what is genex (generator expressions)? She understands CMake isn't simple, that under the hood it generates makefiles. She wants to understand what generator expressions are.\n\nI'm in plan mode, read-only. I should just answer the question. Let me explain genex clearly, in Russian, with her project examples.\n\nGenerator expressions are CMake expressions that are evaluated at *generate* time (when CMake writes the build files), not at configure time. They let you write flags/values that depend on the configuration (Debug vs Release), platform, target properties, etc.\n\nSyntax: `$<...>`\n\nKey point: normal variables `${VAR}` are expanded when CMakeLists.txt is *processed* (configure time). But at that moment, some things aren't known yet — e.g., which configuration is being built (that's known only when the build tool runs). genex are resolved later, when CMake generates the actual build files.\n\nLet me give concrete examples from her project:\n- `$<$<CONFIG:Debug>:/fsanitize=address>` — \"if Debug config, use this flag\". \n- `$<TARGET_FILE:run_case>` — \"the full path to the run_case target's executable\".\n- `$<TARGET_FILE_DIR:common_cpp_shared>` — directory of the shared lib.\n\nSo there's a time dimension. Let me explain the three times:\n1. Configure time — CMake reads CMakeLists.txt, variables `${VAR}` expand.\n2. Generate time — CMake writes build.ninja/Makefile. genex `$<...>` expand here.\n3. Build time — ninja/make runs cl/link.\n\nAlso worth mentioning: nested expressions like `$<$<CONFIG:Debug>:...>` — the inner one is a condition, outer is a ternary-like.\n\nLet me keep it concise but complete, with an analogy maybe. She likes everyday analogies. The idea of \"value known only when you actually build\" — like a recipe where you don't know the oven temperature until you know which kitchen.\n\nActually a good analogy: variables are like \"write in advance\" — you fill them when writing the recipe. genex are like instructions \"take the temperature that will be at the moment of cooking\" — evaluated later, when the food is actually being made, because the value depends on which kitchen/oven is used at that moment.\n\nLet me structure:\n\n1. Short answer: genex = expressions evaluated at generate time, not configure time.\n2. The three phases timeline (configure → generate → build).\n3. Why normal variables aren't enough — example with CONFIG.\n4. Concrete examples from her project.\n5. Note: don't need to fully master now; we'll meet them in the plan (flags like ASan per-config, paths in add_test).\n\nKeep it focused. She asked a specific definitional question. Don't overlong. But be complete.\n\nI should stay in plan mode — just answer, no edits. End by asking if she's ready to confirm plan / start.",
