          "type": "reasoning",
          "text": "The user is asking two questions while studying the CMake skeleton I gave:\n\n1. If I don't set cmake_minimum_required, what are the consequences? Like, will I later use something that's only available after a certain version?\n\n2. CMAKE_C_STANDARD is the C language version, what are the other two params with CMAKE_C_? And why is C++ 23 and not latest?\n\nLet me answer these carefully and correctly.\n\n**Question 1: cmake_minimum_required**\n\nConsequences of not setting it:\n- CMake policy behavior: each CMake version changes behavior (policies, CMPxxxx). When you set `cmake_minimum_required(VERSION 3.22)`, CMake sets all policies up to that version to NEW behavior. Without it, CMake emits a warning (since 3.12: \"Compatibility with CMake < 3.x will be removed\"), and policies default to OLD behavior, which can cause subtle bugs.\n- Actually the behavior without cmake_minimum_required: CMake uses the current running version's behavior but policies remain at OLD defaults for compatibility? Let me think. Actually, when there's no cmake_minimum_required, CMake treats the project as if it were created by an ancient version — it sets policies to OLD behavior to maintain backwards compatibility. And it warns. Modern CMake requires the first line (well, before project()) ideally. Since CMake 3.12+, `cmake_minimum_required` warning: \"Compatibility with CMake < 3.5 will be removed from a future version\". Without any cmake_minimum_required, CMake assumes an old version (like 2.8) and enables compatibility modes.\n- Consequences concretely: warnings, policies behave in old way. For example CMP0091 (MSVC runtime library selection), CMP0128, CMP0135, etc. With our build we rely on MSVC runtime library selection default (/MDd /MD) which comes via CMAKE_MSVC_RUNTIME_LIBRARY and CMP0091. If policy not set to NEW, the behavior may differ.\n- Also, does it block using newer features? No — if your CMake is 3.30, it will still work with newer commands. But the behavior of policies is governed by minimum required version. So setting minimum required tells CMake \"you can use behaviors from up to this version\". If you use a feature that requires a specific policy/version without declaring it, you can get the OLD (buggy/compat) behavior.\n- So the answer: consequences are not \"it won't work\", but: (a) warning at configure, (b) policies stay in OLD/compat mode → subtle behavioral differences, e.g. CMP0091 for MSVC runtime lib, CMP0048 project version, etc. (c) it's a contract about which behaviors you rely on.\n- Also important: cmake_minimum_required should come before project(). And CMake 4.0 requires minimum 3.5+. Also, as a student in course, declaring min version is good practice.\n\nAlso relevant: I should verify claims. The MSVC runtime library default: When policy CMP0091 is NEW (default for 3.15+), CMAKE_MSVC_RUNTIME_LIBRARY is respected. Its default value is \"MultiThreaded$<$<CONFIG:Debug>:Debug>DLL\" which is /MD for release, /MDd for debug. When CMP0091 is OLD, CMAKE_MSVC_RUNTIME_LIBRARY is ignored and old flag insertion happens. This matters for our build since we want /MDd debug /MD release. Let me mark this as [ФАКТ, СРЕДНИЙ] — I'm fairly confident about CMP0091 but the exact default details I should flag as \"проверь\". Actually let me be careful — I'm quite confident CMP0091 controls this. Good.\n\n**Question 2: CMAKE_C_STANDARD and the other two**\n\nThe three variables:\n- CMAKE_C_STANDARD — C language standard (e.g., 17)\n- CMAKE_C_STANDARD_REQUIRED — if ON, the compiler must support that standard; if it doesn't, configuration fails with error. If OFF, CMake uses the highest standard the compiler supports (and falls back with warning if not supported).\n- CMAKE_C_EXTENSIONS — whether to enable compiler-specific extensions (e.g., GNU extensions for GCC like -std=gnu17 vs -std=c17; MSVC doesn't really have a distinction). OFF means strict standard, uses -std=c17 on GCC, /std:c17 on MSVC. ON means gnu extensions -std=gnu17.\n\nSo the other two are REQUIRED and EXTENSIONS. And same for CXX: CMAKE_CXX_STANDARD, CMAKE_CXX_STANDARD_REQUIRED, CMAKE_CXX_EXTENSIONS.\n\nWhy C++23 not latest: Because CMake's CMAKE_CXX_STANDARD is a declared target: you say \"I target C++23\". CMake picks the appropriate flag. \"latest\" is not a standard. And there's CMAKE_CXX_STANDARD with value like 23. There's also `target_compile_features` / cxx_std_23 meta-feature. But \"latest\" — MSVC /std:c++latest — is a rolling target, not a standard. The course uses /std:c++latest in Makefile because MSVC's C++23 wasn't fully... actually MSVC has /std:c++23 now (VS 2022 17.8+). But the original choice of /std:c++latest was to get std::print etc.\n\nThe question: why 23 and not latest. Answer: CMake's model is standard-based, not \"latest\". You declare the standard you code against. With CMAKE_CXX_STANDARD 23, CMake maps to /std:c++23 on MSVC (or /std:c++latest if 23 unsupported? Actually MSVC support: for C++20 it maps to /std:c++20, for 23 to /std:c++latest or /std:c++23 depending on version). Hmm — important nuance: CMake maps C++23 for MSVC: I believe older CMake mapped cxx_std_23 to /std:c++latest when /std:c++23 not yet available; with /std:c++23 flag available in VS 2022 17.8, newer CMake maps to /std:c++23. We're on CMake 3.30.5, MSVC presumably 17.x. So it will map to /std:c++23 if the compiler supports it. If the compiler doesn't support /std:c++23, but CXX_STANDARD_REQUIRED ON, configure fails. If REQUIRED OFF, falls back to /std:c++20 or such, and std::print would break!\n\nThis is an important point to flag: the \"why not latest\" has a practical consequence for our project: std::print needs a flag that enables it. On MSVC, std::print is available with /std:c++latest or /std:c++23 (VS 17.8+). If CMake maps 23→/std:c++23 and the compiler supports it, fine. This is exactly one of the uncertainties I flagged earlier. So I should keep the verification step: check which flag CMake actually emits.\n\nAlso I should mention: C++23 in CMake lists: values like 98, 11, 14, 17, 20, 23, 26. CMake 3.30 supports 23 and 26.\n\nLet me also address: \"if not setting cmake_minimum_required, will I later use something available only after this version?\" — clarify that it's not about the installed CMake version; the installed 3.30 can use everything. The minimum_required is about declaring which behavior/policies you rely on. If you use commands that only exist in newer versions without declaring, CMake still runs them (it's the running version that matters), but the project becomes non-portable and policies may be OLD.\n\nActually let me be precise about the warning. Without cmake_minimum_required at all: CMake treats the project as minimum version very old (like 2.6), enabling compatibility. There's a warning. With cmake_minimum_required(VERSION 3.22), policies ≤3.22 set to NEW.\n\nAlso, does cmake_minimum_required need to be first line? Best practice is before project(), but it can be after; policies are applied when it's processed, and project() may trigger some policy behavior, so recommend it before project(). Actually there's subtlety: cmake_minimum_required() should be called before project(). If it's after, some policy settings won't apply to the project() call's behavior. The course/typical: first line.\n\nLet me also note a real consequence for our project: CMAKE_MSVC_RUNTIME_LIBRARY (CMP0091) — if minimum required is < 3.15, CMP0091 is OLD → CMAKE_MSVC_RUNTIME_LIBRARY ignored → different CRT flags. Since our Makefile relies on /MDd debug and /MD release, and my skeleton relies on CMake default /MDd//MD, keeping min 3.15+ (we use 3.22) preserves NEW policy → correct behavior.\n\nAlso CMP0128? Not relevant. CMP0135? File download... not relevant.\n\nAlso I should mention the project() version: With minimum 3.12+, if project() has VERSION, CMP0048 becomes NEW and version becomes first project arg. Not relevant now.\n\nLet me also answer \"and why C++ 23 not latest\" more thoroughly, connecting to what /std:c++latest means: it's MSVC-specific rolling flag. \"latest\" means whatever MSVC has implemented most recently, which can change with updates → not reproducible/portable. Declaring a concrete standard (23) is portable across compilers (GCC -std=c++23, MSVC /std:c++23). Also the course states C++23 target in §3. So 23 is the target; latest would be both non-portable and a moving target. However, honest caveat: if the MSVC installed doesn't support full C++23, std::print etc. need latest. Actually MSVC now supports /std:c++23. And GCC 14 supports -std=c++23. There's also the nuance that some C++23 features are not in /std:c++23 on MSVC but in latest. std::print IS available in /std:c++23 since VS 17.10 (I think). Let me flag uncertainty.\n\nActually, there's an important subtlety I recall: MSVC /std:c++23 — std::print is available. But some things like std::expected were in latest first, and under c++23 after. geometry.cpp uses std::expected — available in /std:c++23 in recent MSVC. So it should be fine, but flag: verify the actual flag emitted and that it compiles.\n\nLet me also mention CMAKE_CXX_EXTENSIONS: for MSVC it doesn't matter much (no GNU extensions), for GCC it's -std=c++23 vs -std=gnu++23. We set OFF for portability/strictness.\n\nNow, how to present: The user asked 2 questions. I should answer clearly, in Russian, with [ФАКТ/МНЕНИЕ] tags where appropriate per the anti-sycophancy block, and honestly flag the things I'm less sure about (CMake policy specifics, MSVC flag mapping). Since plan mode, I just answer and note this stays in the plan (no edits).\n\nLet me keep it structured and not too long. Two sections.\n\nAlso, I should verify my claim about what happens without cmake_minimum_required with a quick read? I can't install/run cmake configure (that would write files - forbidden in plan mode). But I could read cmake --help or documentation? I could use webfetch to check CMake docs for cmake_minimum_required behavior. That would be good to be accurate. Let me consider. The instruction: verify by docs rather than guessing. I have decent knowledge but let me be careful. I could fetch the CMake docs page. But is it worth it? The user's question is conceptual. I'm fairly confident about the policy behavior. Let me mark confidence levels appropriately and offer to verify.\n\nActually, I can use webfetch to check the cmake_minimum_required docs and CMP0091/CMP0048. Let me do that to be accurate — the AGENTS.md rules emphasize checking docs rather than trusting the model. Let me fetch:\n- https://cmake.org/cmake/help/latest/command/cmake_minimum_required.html\n- maybe CMP0091 page.\n\nLet me fetch cmake_minimum_required docs and CMP0091.\n\nI'll do parallel webfetch calls.",
