          "type": "reasoning",
          "text": "Let me answer the two questions based on the docs and my knowledge.\n\nQuestion 1: consequences of not setting cmake_minimum_required.\nFrom docs: cmake_minimum_required sets the minimum version AND policy version. Policies determine behavior. If not set:\n- CMake treats the project as if written for a very old version → policies get OLD behavior (deprecated), possibly warnings, behavior differences.\n- E.g., CMP0091 (MSVC runtime library) - if not set, OLD behavior places runtime flags in default CMAKE_<LANG>_FLAGS_<CONFIG> cache entries. Actually per the doc: \"If it is not set, CMake does not warn, and uses OLD behavior.\"\n- Also CMake 4.0+ requires min 3.5 or errors. Our cmake is 3.30, so fine.\n- Future: if you use something available only after a certain version - the docs say: if running version is lower than <min> → error. So cmake_minimum_required guards against running with older CMake than the project needs. If you don't set it, an older CMake would try and maybe fail weirdly, or newer CMake uses OLD policies.\n\nSo consequences of not setting:\n1. Policies not set → OLD behavior (deprecated) → subtle behavioral differences. E.g., CMP0091 runtime lib flags handling, CMP0074 find_package with <PackageName>_ROOT, etc.\n2. If CMake version running is old, no error - it just proceeds and may fail in weird ways.\n3. In CMake 4.0+, error if <3.5.\n4. cmake_minimum_required should be before project().\n\nFor our project specifically: CMP0091 affects MSVC runtime library selection. With OLD behavior, runtime flags /MDd /MD are placed in CMAKE_C_FLAGS_DEBUG etc. cache entries. With NEW, use CMAKE_MSVC_RUNTIME_LIBRARY abstraction. Both give same default (MultiThreaded$<$<CONFIG:Debug>:Debug>DLL → /MDd debug, /MD release). So behavior would be same but the mechanism differs. Actually the default is equivalent. But it's good practice.\n\nAlso there's the \"compatibility warnings\" - CMake 3.27+ deprecates <3.5 compatibility. If you don't set cmake_minimum_required at all, CMake may assume very old version → deprecation warning.\n\nQuestion 2: CMAKE_C_STANDARD, and the other two CMAKE_C_* params. The other two are CMAKE_C_STANDARD_REQUIRED and CMAKE_C_EXTENSIONS. \n- CMAKE_C_STANDARD: which standard (17 etc.)\n- CMAKE_C_STANDARD_REQUIRED: whether the compiler MUST support that standard; if ON and compiler doesn't support → configure error. If OFF → CMake uses the newest standard the compiler supports that's >= requested? Actually: if not required, CMake allows fallback. Actually the semantics: if required, error if not supported; if not required, CMake uses highest available? Let me recall C_STANDARD property docs: \"If the requested value is not supported by the compiler, and C_STANDARD_REQUIRED is ON, an error is reported. If OFF, CMake uses the highest standard it can.\" Hmm, actually the property doc says: \"If the value requested does not result in a compiler flag being added for the requested standard, the compiler flag is not added... If C_STANDARD_REQUIRED is ON and the compiler does not support the requested standard, an error\". And C_EXTENSIONS: whether to use compiler extensions like GNU extensions (-std=gnu17 vs -std=c17) or MSVC... For MSVC no real extension flag. C_EXTENSIONS OFF → -std=c17 on GCC, /std:c17 on MSVC. ON → -std=gnu17.\n\nActually there's nuance: CMAKE_C_STANDARD is \"default value for C_STANDARD target property\". Same for the other two.\n\nWhy C++23 not latest: In Makefile they used /std:c++latest for MSVC. In CMake, \"latest\" isn't a standard value; CMAKE_CXX_STANDARD takes numbers (98, 11, 14, 17, 20, 23, 26). Setting 23 means CMake picks the appropriate flag: MSVC → /std:c++23 (if supported) or /std:c++latest (fallback for older MSVC? Actually CMake maps cxx_std_23 to /std:c++latest on MSVC when /std:c++23 not supported). The reason for a concrete number: cross-platform — GCC uses -std=c++23, MSVC uses /std:c++23. \"latest\" is MSVC-specific, non-portable, and a moving target (not reproducible). By targeting 23 you get a fixed contract: code must work in C++23, and both compilers compile the same dialect.\n\nAlso the course (§3) says C++23. So 23 is the course target. /std:c++latest in Makefile was a means; the target is 23.\n\nNote: std::print needs C++23 and MSVC /std:c++latest or /std:c++23 (since VS 17.8 supports c++23 flag). CMake 3.30 with MSVC — need to check which flag it emits. There's uncertainty flagged: whether CMake emits /std:c++23 or /std:c++latest for MSVC. I already flagged that as a verification item.\n\nLet me also answer \"two other params\" precisely: CMAKE_C_STANDARD_REQUIRED and CMAKE_C_EXTENSIONS. Same triple for C++.\n\nLet me write concise answer in Russian. Also note plan mode - no edits.\n\nI should be concise but thorough. This is a teaching context.\n\nLet me structure:\n\nQ1: cmake_minimum_required consequences.\n- What it does: minimum version check + sets \"policy version\".\n- Policies: behaviors of CMake across versions; NEW preferred, OLD deprecated.\n- Without it: no error on old cmake (bad), policies stay unset → OLD/deprecated behavior silently, and CMake 3.27+ gives deprecation warnings (assumes ancient compatibility). On CMake 4.0+, <3.5 → error.\n- Example relevant to us: CMP0091 (MSVC runtime library). Without it, OLD behavior: runtime flags baked into CMAKE_*_FLAGS_DEBUG cache entries; NEW: CMAKE_MSVC_RUNTIME_LIBRARY abstraction. Default equivalent but mechanism differs; also future removal of OLD.\n- Your \"something only available after version\" question: partially. The min version isn't about feature availability to you (running CMake is 3.30, you get everything). It's two things: (a) guard: if someone runs older cmake than min → clear error instead of weird failure; (b) policy version: tells CMake \"assume behavior as of version X\" so newer policies are NEW not deprecated OLD. It's a contract.\n\nQ2: the three CMAKE_C_*:\n- CMAKE_C_STANDARD — язык (17)\n- CMAKE_C_STANDARD_REQUIRED — ON: обязательно, иначе ошибка конфигурации; OFF: позволить fallback (подойдёт ближайший поддерживаемый)\n- CMAKE_C_EXTENSIONS — включать ли компиляторные расширения (GNU extensions: -std=gnu17 vs -std=c17). OFF = строгий стандарт.\nSame triple for C++.\n\nWhy 23 not latest:\n- CMake's CMAKE_CXX_STANDARD is a number (standard), not \"latest\". latest is MSVC-only rolling flag.\n- Cross-platform: GCC -std=c++23, MSVC /std:c++23 (or /std:c++latest fallback). A concrete standard = reproducible, fixed contract.\n- The course targets C++23 (§3). Makefile's /std:c++latest was a means to get std::print; target is 23.\n- If code needs a feature not yet in 23 on a given compiler, that's a flag we'd revisit; for now 23 is the target.\n\nKeep it tight. Mark [ФАКТ/МНЕНИЕ] and confidence as per AGENTS protocol. Actually the user's anti-sycophancy block requests categories and confidence. Let me include tags where valuable.\n\nI should be careful about the claim on /std:c++latest fallback for MSVC. Let me note uncertainty. CMake's MSVC c++23 mapping: I believe newer CMake (3.23+?) emits /std:c++23 when the compiler supports it, else /std:c++latest. This is a detail to verify at first build. Flag it.\n\nNow write.",
