          "text": "The user asks two things:\n1. Confirm her understanding: a policy is basically \"how CMake fundamentally works, its version behavior\" - i.e., the behavior of a given CMake version.\n2. Explain what CMAKE_C_EXTENSIONS is and why you'd use -std=gnu17 instead of -std=c17.\n\nLet me answer both concisely. I'm in plan mode, read-only. Just answer.\n\nOn point 1: her understanding is roughly right but needs a nuance. A policy isn't exactly \"the version of CMake\" — it's a specific change in behavior that occurred at a specific version. cmake_minimum_required(VERSION 3.22) tells CMake \"behave as of 3.22\" — meaning all policy changes introduced up to 3.22 use their NEW behavior. But new policies introduced AFTER 3.22 will warn (or default to OLD). So the \"version\" is a reference point for policy selection. Her intuition \"это то, как фундаментально работает CMake, то есть его версия\" is close — but better: it's \"a set of behavior contracts, each tied to a version; the declared version selects which contract applies\". Confirm but refine.\n\nOn point 2: CMAKE_C_EXTENSIONS and gnu17 vs c17.\n\nWhat are GNU extensions? GCC and Clang have language extensions beyond the standard (e.g., designated initializers in C89, nested functions, `__attribute__`, etc.). `-std=gnu17` = C17 standard + GNU extensions enabled. `-std=c17` = strict C17, no GNU extensions (only standard features).\n\nWhy would you use gnu17? On Linux, some code may rely on GNU extensions. On MSVC there's no gnu17 — MSVC doesn't have that concept; CMake just ignores EXTENSIONS on MSVC. So CMAKE_C_EXTENSIONS OFF → `-std=c17` (strict, portable), ON → `-std=gnu17`.\n\nWe set it OFF for cross-platform consistency: strict standard so the code compiles the same on MSVC (which has no GNU extensions) and GCC. If we used gnu17 on Linux, code could accidentally rely on GNU-only features that MSVC doesn't have — making the code non-portable. So OFF keeps us honest.\n\nNote nuance: with C_EXTENSIONS ON on GCC, CMake uses `-std=gnu17`; with OFF uses `-std=c17`. On MSVC it doesn't map (no flag). This is why OFF = better portability.\n\nLet me give a concrete example of a GNU extension so she understands what \"extensions\" means. E.g., designated initializers `struct Point p = { .x = 1, .y = 2, .z = 3 };` — actually designated initializers are in C99 standard now, not an extension. Hmm, in C99 they became standard. Let me think of real GNU extensions:\n\n- Nested functions (a function defined inside a function) — GCC extension, not in C standard.\n- `__attribute__((...))` — GCC-specific.\n- Statement expressions `({ ... })` — GCC extension.\n- Case ranges `case 1 ... 5:` — GCC extension.\n- Variable-length arrays (VLAs) — actually C99 standard but optional in C11; GCC supports them as extension.\n- `typeof` operator.\n\nA simple example: case ranges like `case 'a' ... 'z':` — GCC allows this, standard C doesn't.\n\nActually, keep it simple. The key: standard C = what the ISO committee wrote; GNU extensions = GCC's extras on top. -std=c17 says \"only ISO C17\"; -std=gnu17 says \"C17 + GNU extras\".\n\nWhy choose OFF: to ensure code that compiles on GCC also compiles on MSVC. If you allow GNU extensions on Linux, you might write code that only works there.\n\nKeep the answer concise, in Russian, plan mode respected. She asked \"explain why do -std=gnu17 instead of -std=c17\" — actually the point is we do NOT want gnu17, we want c17 (EXTENSIONS OFF). Clarify.\n\nAlso there's a subtlety: CMAKE_C_EXTENSIONS on MSVC — CMake docs: for MSVC it's ignored / MSVC doesn't have a \"gnu\" mode. So it's a GCC/Clang thing. Our Makefile used -std=c17 on Linux (strict) — so EXTENSIONS OFF matches existing behavior. Good.\n\nLet me write the answer.",
