          "text": "The user asks: with `set(CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE Debug CACHE STRING \"Build type: Debug/Release\" FORCE)` — does the person need to input this parameter every time?\n\nLet me explain how CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE works.\n\nKey facts:\n- CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE is a cache variable. It's set at configure time and stored in the cache (CMakeCache.txt).\n- The `if(NOT CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE)` guard means: only set the default if the user didn't already provide it.\n- When you configure with `cmake -B build/debug -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug`, the value is stored in CMakeCache.txt inside the build dir.\n- On subsequent builds with `cmake --build build/debug` (or re-running `cmake -B build/debug`), the cached value is reused. So no, the person does NOT input it every time.\n- The `CACHE ... FORCE` writes to the cache. FORCE means overwrite even if exists. But it's guarded by `if(NOT CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE)`, so if user set it via -D, we skip.\n\nImportant subtlety: single-config generators (Ninja, Makefiles) bake the build type at configure time into the generated files. Multi-config generators (Visual Studio) don't use CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE — they use `--config Debug` at build time.\n\nSince we chose Ninja (single-config), the build type is chosen at configure time, stored in cache. To switch Debug → Release you configure a separate build dir (build/release). You don't re-type it each time for the same dir.\n\nThe `FORCE` keyword: actually there's a nuance. If we use `set(CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE Debug CACHE STRING ... FORCE)`, FORCE overwrites. But we guard with `if(NOT CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE)`. When a variable is passed via -D, it becomes a cache entry. Hmm, but there's a subtlety: `-D` sets the cache entry. So `if(NOT CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE)` — CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE would be defined (as a cache variable), so the block is skipped. Good.\n\nActually wait — does the guard `if(NOT CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE)` evaluate cache variables? Yes, cache variables are visible like normal variables in the current scope. So if -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release was passed, CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE is set → block skipped.\n\nActually there's a subtle detail about FORCE: With FORCE it writes regardless. Without FORCE, `set(... CACHE ...)` only sets if the cache entry doesn't exist. Since we have the guard, FORCE is somewhat redundant but harmless — actually it's common practice to include FORCE when you intend to always write the default. But combined with the guard, it's fine.\n\nAlso relevant: cmake-gui / ccache — no. Let me explain the workflow:\n\n- First configure: `cmake -B build/debug -G Ninja -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug` (or omit -D, then our default Debug kicks in and is written to cache).\n- Later: `cmake --build build/debug` — no need to re-specify; it reads from cache.\n- To add a new config dir: `cmake -B build/release -G Ninja -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release` → separate dir, cached there.\n\nThis connects to the §4.7 rule: режим — параметр сборки с раздельными каталогами артефактов. And it mirrors her Makefile CONFIG variable, but now stored in cache instead of re-passed.\n\nAnswer concisely. Also mention that if someone runs plain `cmake -B build/debug` without -D, the default Debug from our file applies and gets cached. So \"input every time\" — no.\n\nI should be careful about FORCE semantics. Let me be accurate: `set(CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE <val> CACHE STRING ... FORCE)` writes the cache entry. Our guard means we only do it if not already set. So a user could also pass `-DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release`. Good.\n\nI'm in plan mode, so just explain, no edits. Keep concise, in Russian.",
