          "type": "reasoning",
          "text": "The user asks: \"то есть если сначала прогнать без -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE будет дебаг. если во второй раз с ним и указать релиз, то релиз. то есть не только первый прогон решает, какой будет конфигурация?\"\n\nWait — this is a subtle point. Let me think carefully.\n\nThe scenario: user configures `cmake -B build/debug -G Ninja` (no -D) → our `if(NOT CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE)` sets Debug, writes to cache. Cache now has `CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE:STRING=Debug`.\n\nThen user runs `cmake -B build/debug -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release` again on the SAME build dir → `-D` sets the cache entry to Release (overwrites cache). Then build would be Release.\n\nBut wait — is that actually true? Let me think about the behavior of `-D` vs cache.\n\nYes, `-DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release` on the command line sets the cache variable, overwriting the cached value. So reconfiguring the same directory with a different -D value changes it.\n\nBut there's a subtlety with single-config generators: changing CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE in the cache of the same build dir — CMake will regenerate the build files with the new build type. Actually for single-config generators, the build type is baked into the generated files at configure time. So re-running cmake with -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release would regenerate and the ninja files would then use Release flags. So yes it would switch.\n\nBut — is this good practice? No! The course rule (§4.7) is: режим — параметр сборки с раздельными каталогами артефактов, не правка флагов на месте. Changing build type in the same dir is \"переключение на месте\" which is discouraged — it leaves stale artifacts and can cause confusion. The recommended approach is separate dirs: build/debug and build/release.\n\nSo the answer to her question: \"не только первый прогон решает?\" — Correct technically: yes, re-running with a different -D value will overwrite the cache and change the config. But it's NOT recommended, and here's why:\n\n1. It works (cache is just a file; -D overwrites it).\n2. But it's the \"правка флагов на месте\" anti-pattern from §4.7: switching config in the same dir leaves old artifacts (.obj, .exe built with old flags) mixed with new — incremental rebuild may keep stale objects, and if flags change (e.g., ASan vs not), stale objects from the previous config can silently persist. Actually ninja would rebuild because flags change cause recompilation? Ninja tracks the command line, so if flags change it rebuilds. But CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE change regenerates... Actually there can be issues with debug/release mixing.\n3. Best practice: separate build directories, never reuse a dir across configs.\n\nLet me also be careful: is it actually true that re-running with -D changes it? Let me verify. `-D` is equivalent to setting cache entry. Yes, it overwrites the existing cache value (unless the set was done with FORCE and CACHE... no, -D always sets cache). Actually there is a subtlety: our line has FORCE, which always writes on configure. If user passes -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release, then at configure time CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE is defined (from command line cache entry), so `if(NOT CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE)` is false → our line skipped → Release stays. Good, consistent.\n\nIf user does NOT pass -D, and cache already has Release from before, then CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE is set (from cache), so if(NOT...) is false → keeps Release. Good.\n\nIf cache is fresh (no value), no -D → our line sets Debug. Good.\n\nSo: the first run doesn't irreversibly decide. Re-running with -D on the same dir changes it. So her understanding is correct. But I should caution: don't do this as the normal workflow; use separate dirs. This is exactly the §4.7 teaching point.\n\nActually, let me double check one thing: for Ninja (single-config), does changing CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE in the same build dir cleanly switch? CMake will regenerate build.ninja with new flags. Ninja tracks command lines per output; changed command → rebuild. But artifacts like .obj from debug with /MDd and release /MD — names are same, ninja rebuilds because command changed. So it \"works\". But mixing/old artifact risk: e.g., CMakeCache other variables cached; libraries linked... it can work but is discouraged. Actually there are known problems: e.g., if you configured Debug then Release in same dir, sometimes incremental issues with MSVC runtime mismatch, PDB conflicts etc. Best practice remains separate dirs.\n\nLet me give a clear, honest answer: technically yes (cache can be overwritten), but it's the anti-pattern the course explicitly forbids (§4.7 \"не правка флагов на месте\"), recommended is separate dirs. The \"first run decides\" is about: without you actively changing it, the cache persists the value.\n\nI'm in plan mode - read only. Just answer the question, no edits. Keep concise.",
