          "text": "The user asks two things:\n\n1. Is this OK?:\n```\ncmake -B build/debug -G Ninja\ncmake -B build/release -G Ninja -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release\n```\nWait — first line: `cmake -B build/debug -G Ninja` without `-DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug`. The second has Release. Is this OK? Yes — for build/debug, no CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE passed → our `if(NOT CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE)` sets Debug as default. For build/release, explicit Release. So both work. It's fine. This is exactly the recommended pattern. Note: for consistency/clarity, passing `-DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug` explicitly for debug dir is also fine, but not required because of our default. Both approaches OK.\n\n2. What is `-G Ninja`? Explain generator concept.\n\nLet me answer concisely in Russian.\n\n`-G` = generator selection. CMake doesn't build itself; it generates build files for a backend tool. `-G Ninja` tells CMake to generate `build.ninja` files that the `ninja` tool will execute. Alternative generators: \"NMake Makefiles\", \"Visual Studio 17 2022\", \"Unix Makefiles\" (on Linux). Without `-G`, CMake picks default based on platform (on Windows with VS installed → Visual Studio generator by default).\n\nI'm in plan mode, read-only. Just answer. No edits.\n\nLet me verify default generator claim: CMake on Windows defaults to Visual Studio generator if found. Yes, the default is VS if present. Our earlier `cmake --help` output showed `* Visual Studio 17 2022` as default. Good, that's a fact from the actual output earlier.\n\nSo explaining: `-G Ninja` selects backend. On this machine default is Visual Studio 17 2022 (from cmake --help asterisk). So without -G, it'd generate .sln/.vcxproj — not what we want for console flow. With -G Ninja, generates build.ninja, run by ninja (parallel, fast, single-config).\n\nAlso relate to the three moments: configure (cmake reads CMakeLists, -B sets build dir, -G chooses generator) → generate (writes build.ninja) → build (ninja runs cl/link).\n\nAlso mention: -G applies per build dir (stored in cache), so not needed on re-runs. And cross-platform: on Linux same `-G Ninja` works if ninja installed, or \"Unix Makefiles\".\n\nLet me also double-check the first example: is it truly fine that debug dir has no explicit type? Our CMakeLists has the `if(NOT CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE)` default → Debug. Yes. So OK. But I should note nuance: for clarity, some prefer explicit everywhere. Both OK. The risk: if the user later removes the default block, build/debug would have empty CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE → no optimization/asan flags → silently wrong. That's a good teaching point: the default only exists because of our block.\n\nKeep it concise. Answer both questions. Since plan mode, no edits. End with confirmation to proceed with plan? The user is still in understanding phase. I'll answer and gently note plan is ready.",
