          "type": "reasoning",
          "text": "The user asks two questions:\n1. About the `set(CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE Debug CACHE STRING \"Build type: Debug / Release\" FORCE)` line — will \"Build type: Debug / Release\" be a \"пояснительная\" (explanatory) string in the cache, attached to CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE?\n2. Why is ninja faster than make, and what does \"more honest dependency graph\" mean?\n\nI'm in plan mode — read-only. Just answer questions, no edits.\n\nLet me answer accurately.\n\nFor question 1: Yes. The `set(... CACHE STRING \"documentation string\" ...)` syntax — the STRING is the variable type, and the quoted string is the documentation string. It's stored in CMakeCache.txt as a comment above the variable. When you run `cmake -LA` or use ccmake/gui, this doc string is shown. Also `cmake -LA` lists cache entries with type and help string. Let me recall the exact format of CMakeCache.txt:\n\n```\n// Build type: Debug / Release\nCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE:STRING=Debug\n```\n\nSo the doc string appears as a comment line in CMakeCache.txt. Yes. The type STRING means it's a string variable. FORCE means it overwrites the cache entry even if it exists (though combined with the if(NOT...) guard... actually there's a subtlety: FORCE on a set with CACHE will always overwrite. But the guard if(NOT CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE) prevents reaching the set when the variable is already defined. When CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE is empty/undefined, the set executes with FORCE and writes it).\n\nActually there is a subtlety about FORCE and the guard. The guard `if(NOT CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE)` — when you don't pass -D, CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE is empty (undefined), so the block executes. FORCE forces the cache write. That's fine. If you pass -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release, CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE is defined (as a cache variable), so the if-block is skipped, preserving the Release value. Good.\n\nNote: FORCE is technically redundant here since the guard ensures it only runs when undefined, but it's commonly included. Actually, careful: without FORCE, `set(... CACHE STRING \"...\")` would NOT overwrite an existing cache entry — it only sets if not already in cache. With the guard, when the block runs, the variable is undefined in normal sense but might there be a stale cache entry? Hmm. Actually if CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE is empty in the environment but has a cache value... that's an edge case. Keep it simple: the doc string appears in cache as a comment.\n\nLet me also mention `cmake -LA build/debug` to view, and that ccmake/gui shows it. This is a nice practical fact.\n\nFor question 2: Why ninja faster than make:\n- Ninja was designed from scratch to be a build tool focused on speed; make is a general-purpose tool (also used for other things, has POSIX shell in recipes, lots of legacy). \n- Make re-evaluates the entire makefile, has recursive make issues, uses shell per recipe line (spawns a shell for each rule), parses makefile each time. Ninja has no shell: commands are given directly as argv arrays, no shell interpretation, so no shell startup overhead per command.\n- Ninja does dependency analysis via .ninja_deps and .ninja_log, uses timestamp + build log (restat), parallelizes by default (-j is default = all cores? Actually ninja defaults to parallel based on CPU count). Make in GNU also supports -j but defaults to serial.\n- Ninja's input files are designed to be machine-generated (by CMake, or gn/meson), so it can be terse and fast to parse; make must re-parse a complex Makefile each invocation.\n- Ninja handles graph lazily: it only loads what it needs to build the requested targets (build.ninja is the whole graph though). Hmm, actually ninja loads the whole build.ninja but is very fast at it.\n\n\"More honest dependency graph\" — what does that mean? In make, the dependency graph is whatever you declared: you declare targets and prerequisites; make uses timestamps. But there are classic problems:\n- Make doesn't automatically know header dependencies; you add -MMD and include .d files, but make's implicit rules and ordering can be off.\n- Recursive make (sub-makes) builds each subdir separately, so the global graph isn't visible; parallelism across directories is limited/wrong ordering issues.\n- Make's default behavior: if a target's file exists and prerequisites are older, it does nothing. But \"missing dependency\" (e.g., a header you forgot to declare) → stale build.\n- Ninja: each rule lists exactly which outputs it produces and which inputs it consumes, plus a depfile (compiler-generated, e.g. /showIncludes or -MD) is loaded and merged into the graph dynamically. Ninja re-runs commands whose command line changed (not just timestamps) — it stores the command in .ninja_log and compares. So if flags change, it rebuilds even if timestamps look fine. That's the \"честный\" part: make would NOT rebuild if only the command line changed but timestamps are newer.\n\nActually let me be precise about what make does vs ninja regarding command-line changes:\n- GNU make: tracks only timestamps. If you change a flag in the Makefile but don't touch files, make sees prerequisites older than targets → does nothing → stale build. Actually if the recipe text changes, make doesn't detect it (unless .PHONY). So stale builds happen when you change flags. This is a known pain: you change CFLAGS, make says \"nothing to be done\".\n- Ninja: stores the command used for each output in .ninja_log; if the command differs from the last run, ninja rebuilds that output. So changing CMakeLists → CMake regenerates build.ninja → commands change → ninja rebuilds the affected objects. This is the honest graph: \"the rule for this file changed, so redo it\".\n\nSo \"честный граф\" refers to: ninja tracks not only file timestamps but the actual commands, and it builds exactly and only what changed, with the compiler's depfile merged in, so missing header deps don't cause stale artifacts, and flag changes cause correct rebuilds.\n\nAlso parallelism: ninja default -j = CPU count (uses all cores); make GNU defaults to serial unless -j. Also ninja batches: for each \"edge\" it may run many commands.\n\nAlso note: since we use CMake+Ninja, the dependency info (headers) comes via compiler depfiles (-MD on GCC or /showIncludes parsing on MSVC). So the graph is honest because deps come from the compiler, not from hand-written lists. That connects back to layer 15 pain: hand-maintained header dep lists in Makefile.\n\nLet me also mention that make isn't \"dishonest\" per se — it's that with hand-declared deps, the graph you give it is as good as your memory; ninja + compiler depfiles gets the true graph.\n\nI should answer concisely, in Russian, and stay in plan mode. Keep the tone per AGENTS.md. Maybe mark [ФАКТ] etc. per anti-sycophancy? The block says mark categories. I can include some tags.\n\nLet me write the answer.\n\nFor question 1, let me give the exact cache file format:\n\n```\n// Build type: Debug / Release\nCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE:STRING=Debug\n```\n\nThat's how it appears in CMakeCache.txt. And you can see it with `cmake -LA <dir>` (lists cache variables with type and help). The doc string is exactly that help.\n\nFor question 2, structure:\n- Faster: no shell per command, machine-generated terse input, lazy, parallel by default.\n- Honest graph: (a) command-line change detection vs timestamp-only; (b) compiler-generated depfiles merged in → header deps exact; (c) builds only changed.\n\nLet me write it.",
