hown: `/D_DEBUG /MDd /Ob0 /Od /RTC1`. No /Zi. And release: `/MD /O2 /Ob2 /DNDEBUG`. No /Zi. So CMake doesn't add /Zi by default. That's why in the skeleton we add it explicitly. Good.\n\nAlso note: /RTC1 in default Debug — I mentioned earlier that /RTC1 conflicts with ASan? Actually /RTC1 and /fsanitize=address — MSVC docs: ASan is incompatible with /RTC? Hmm. Actually I said earlier that /RTC1 needs to be removed. But is that true? Let me think. MSVC AddressSanitizer docs say: \"/fsanitize=address cannot be combined with /RTC1 or /RTCc\"? I'm not 100% sure. There might be a conflict. Actually I recall that when you enable ASan in VS, it turns off /RTC. Hmm.\n\nActually, to be safe, in the skeleton we could set CMAKE_MSVC_RUNTIME_LIBRARY and the default flags. But actually the way our configure_target works: we add /Zi and /fsanitize=address via target_compile_options. But the default CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_DEBUG still has /RTC1. Does /RTC1 conflict with /fsanitize=address? Let me check my memory. I believe MSVC disallows /RTC with ASan? Hmm, I don't remember exactly. Let me flag this as a verification item rather than assert. Actually, I recall that /RTC1 and /fsanitize=address ARE incompatible — cl gives error D8016? Actually not sure. Let me flag it: \"нужно проверить: дефолтный /RTC1 в Debug может конфликтовать с ASan; если конфликтует — убираем через переопределение CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_DEBUG или /RTC1-опцию\". Actually, the safest is to note this needs verification during the actual build.\n\nHmm, but I should be careful about accuracy. Let me not overstate. I'll say: CMake defaults give /Od, /O2, /MD, /MDd; /Zi we add ourselves (it's not in CMake defaults); and there's a subtlety with /RTC1 in default debug flags that we may need to handle (verify at build). Actually, wait — the current Makefile debug flags: `/Od /Zi /MDd /fsanitize=address`. No /RTC1. So if CMake adds /RTC1, we'd get extra runtime checks. RTC1 is actually not harmful per se, but if it conflicts with ASan, problem. Let me verify with a quick web fetch? I can do read-only webfetch. Let me check MSVC docs on ASan + /RTC. Actually I could fetch. But let me consider time. The user asked two specific questions. I should answer them well. I can do a quick check on the /RTC + ASan incompatibility to be accurate. Let me webfetch the MSVC /fsanitize=address doc.\n\nActually let me be efficient. I'll answer both questions and note the /RTC verification as part of first build. But given the anti-sycophancy rules and \"don't invent\", maybe I should verify. Let me do a quick webfetch on the ASan options doc.\n\n**Question 2: For debug linking under Windows, isn't ASan not needed at link, only on Linux?**\n\nThe user is asking about `target_link_options(${tgt} PRIVATE $<$<CONFIG:Debug>:/DEBUG /fsanitize=address> ...)`.\n\nKey question: On Windows/MSVC, does the link step need /fsanitize=address?\n\nIn the Makefile, the MSVC link flags for debug were just `/DEBUG` — no /fsanitize=address at link. On Linux, link flags had `-fsanitize=address` explicitly.\n\nWhy? On Linux, with GCC/Clang, the sanitizer runtime must be linked in, so you pass -fsanitize=address both at compile and link. On MSVC, /fsanitize=address at compile time emits references to the sanitizer runtime, and... does link need the flag? In the Makefile, MSVC link used just /DEBUG and it worked (the tests passed, ASan worked). So on MSVC, /fsanitize=address is only needed at compile time; the linker pulls in the runtime automatically via the compiled objects' references and /DEFAULTLIB hints (MSVC compiler embeds the library reference in the object). Actually cl with /fsanitize=address adds /DEFAULTLIB:clang_rt.asan_dynamic-x86_64 or similar? Hmm. Actually MSVC ASan: the compiler adds references, and there is automatic linking. The docs: \"The /fsanitize=address compiler option... When you build with /fsanitize=address, the linker automatically links the AddressSanitizer runtime.\" Actually I'm not 100% sure the link