        {
          "type": "reasoning",
          "text": "The user is asking a good question about the structure of `configure_target`. They're pointing at the skeleton code where `target_link_options` is inside `if(WITH_DBG)`, and asking why link options are inside WITH_DBG — shouldn't they be after endif()?\n\nLet me think about this carefully. The skeleton was:\n\n```cmake\nfunction(configure_target tgt)\n  if(MSVC)\n    target_compile_options(${tgt} PRIVATE /W4 /permissive- $<$<COMPILE_LANGUAGE:CXX>:/EHsc>)\n    if(WITH_DBG)\n      target_compile_options(${tgt} PRIVATE $<$<CONFIG:Debug>:/Zi /fsanitize=address> $<$<CONFIG:Release>:/Zi>)\n      target_link_options(${tgt} PRIVATE $<$<CONFIG:Debug>:/DEBUG /fsanitize=address> $<$<CONFIG:Release>:/DEBUG /OPT:REF /OPT:ICF>)\n    endif()\n  else()\n    ...\n```\n\nWait, the user's question: \"почему link-вещи внутри WITH_DBG? разве они не должны быть после endif()?\"\n\nSo the question is: link options are inside `if(WITH_DBG)`. Shouldn't they be outside? Actually — the point of WITH_DBG is \"this is a product target that we want to debug (adds ASan, /Zi, /DEBUG)\". The link options `/DEBUG` are part of \"debug information for this product\". The tools (run_case, check) are NOT built with WITH_DBG — they don't need /DEBUG either (we removed /Zi and asan from them in the Makefile; but /DEBUG is a linker flag for debug info).\n\nWait, actually let me reconsider. The question is about logical structure: why are link options conditional on WITH_DBG? The answer: because both compile flags (/Zi, /fsanitize) and link flags (/DEBUG) serve the same purpose — \"this is a product target that carries debug information and sanitizer\". The tools don't need any of it. So both compile and link config-specific flags belong together under WITH_DBG.\n\nActually, is there a reason link options would need to be outside? Let me think about what link options are needed even without WITH_DBG:\n- On MSVC Release, /DEBUG /OPT:REF /OPT:ICF — these are for products. Tools don't need them.\n- On Linux, the asan link flag.\n\nSo actually both compile and link config flags are conditioned on WITH_DBG because WITH_DBG = \"продукт, который надо отлаживать\" — and debug info (compile /Zi + link /DEBUG) and sanitizer (compile + link asan) belong to products.\n\nBut wait — there's a subtlety the user might be noticing: could there be link options that are needed regardless of WITH_DBG? E.g., Release flags /OPT:REF /OPT:ICF — those are optimization/link-time, not debug. Should they be applied to tools too? In the Makefile, tools were built with `$(filter-out /Zi /fsanitize=address,$(CXXFLAGS))` — so tools got the release compile flags (well, the config flags minus Zi and asan). But tools were linked via a single cl command that... let me recall. In the Makefile, tools on Windows:\n\n```\n$(BINDIR)/tools/run_case.exe: tests/run_case.cpp | $(BINDIR)/tools\n\t$(CXX) $(OBJ_OUT)$(BINDIR)/tools/run_case$(OBJ_EXT) $(EXE_OUT)$@ $(filter-out /Zi /fsanitize=address,$(CXXFLAGS)) $<\n```\n\nSo tools were compiled+linked in one cl call with CXXFLAGS minus /Zi and /fsanitize=address. Note that LINK_FLAGS (/DEBUG /OPT:REF /OPT:ICF) were NOT used for tools at all — cl invoked link implicitly without /DEBUG. So tools have no /DEBUG. So the link flags being inside WITH_DBG matches the Makefile behavior: only products get /DEBUG.\n\nSo the answer: link flags are inside WITH_DBG because WITH_DBG defines \"product targets that we build with debug information and sanitizer\". Both the compile-side (/Zi, /fsanitize) and link-side (/DEBUG, and on Linux -fsanitize) belong to that category. Tools (run_case, check) are built without WITH_DBG, so they get no /Zi, no asan, no /DEBUG — exactly matching the Makefile where tools were compiled with CXXFLAGS minus those and linked without LINK_FLAGS.\n\nBut there's a subtlety: on MSVC, the Release link flags /DEBUG /OPT:REF /OPT:ICF — if tools don't get /DEBUG, that's fine (they're not debugged). That matches Makefile.\n\nHowever, one could argue: /OPT:REF /OPT:ICF are about size optimization, not debug — maybe tools should get them in Release? But in the Makefile they didn't (LINK_FLAGS weren't used for tools). So keeping them inside WITH_DBG preserves parity.\n\nAlso note: on Linux, the tools WERE built with CXXFLAGS (which included -g and asan in debug... wait). Let me check the Makefile Linux tools rule:\n\n```\n$(BINDIR)/tools/run_case: tests/run_case.cpp | $(BINDIR)/tools\n\t$(CXX) $(EXE_OUT)$@ $(CXXFLAGS) $<\n```\n\nOn Linux, tools got FULL CXXFLAGS including asan and -g. Hmm. So on Linux, tools DO get asan (and -g). But on Windows they don't. That's an inconsistency in the Makefile noted in the journal (2026-08-28: \"убрала /Zi из флагов для run_case и check... по той же логике - тестовые программы не нуждаются в проверке - уберу и /fsanitize=address для этих программ\" — but that was for Windows branch; the Linux branch kept CXXFLAGS full). Actually looking at the journal entry, the intent was to make tools light. The Linux branch wasn't updated. So there's an existing inconsistency.\n\nFor the plan, I'll note: in CMake we can be consistent — tools without WITH_DBG on both platforms. That actually fixes the Linux/Windows inconsistency.\n\nSo the answer to the user's question: link flags are inside WITH_DBG intentionally, because WITH_DBG is the \"продукт\" flag — both debug-info compile flags and debug-info/sanitizer link flags belong to products. Tools are built without WITH_DBG and get neither. If they were moved \"after endif()\" (outside WITH_DBG), then tools would also get /DEBUG /OPT:REF /OPT:ICF — which contradicts the Makefile behavior (tools had no /DEBUG) and the goal of light tools.\n\nBut wait — I should double-check: is there a legitimate case where some link options should be outside WITH_DBG? For example, the base link flags that all executables need on Linux (like... nothing special). Actually for our project, no. The only link options we have are debug/sanitizer related (on Linux asan; on MSVC /DEBUG for products). So everything link-related stays inside WITH_DBG.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the structure more carefully. There's a design question: should WITH_DBG gate /DEBUG at all? On MSVC, /DEBUG produces a pdb for the exe. For tools we don't want that (light tools, no pdb — matches Makefile where tools had no /DEBUG). Good.\n\nNow the user's phrasing \"разве они не должны быть после endif()?\" — they think link options perhaps should be applied regardless of WITH_DBG. My answer: No — they're part of the same category as the compile debug flags. The whole `if(WITH_DBG)` block is \"product-only extras\". Link flags belong inside because they serve the same purpose: debug info (/DEBUG) and sanitizer runtime (linux -fsanitize at link). Tools don't get either.\n\nLet me give a clear, concise answer. Also I could show the corrected version (with the two agreed corrections: /RTC1 removal, and removing /fsanitize from MSVC link). Since we're in plan mode, I can show the planned corrected structure.\n\nI should also address: on Linux, MSVC — actually let me present the final intended structure of configure_target with both corrections applied, to make it concrete.\n\nLet me draft:\n\n```\nfunction(configure_target tgt)\n  if(MSVC)\n    target_compile_options(${tgt} PRIVATE /W4 /permissive- $<$<COMPILE_LANGUAGE:CXX>:/EHsc>)\n    if(WITH_DBG)\n      target_compile_options(${tgt} PRIVATE $<$<CONFIG:Debug>:/Zi /fsanitize=address> $<$<CONFIG:Release>:/Zi>)\n      target_link_options(${tgt} PRIVATE $<$<CONFIG:Debug>:/DEBUG> $<$<CONFIG:Release>:/DEBUG /OPT:REF /OPT:ICF>)\n    endif()\n  else()\n    target_compile_options(${tgt} PRIVATE -Wall -Wextra)\n    if(WITH_DBG)\n      target_compile_options(${tgt} PRIVATE $<$<CONFIG:Debug>:-fsanitize=address> $<$<CONFIG:Release>:-g>)\n      target_link_options(${tgt} PRIVATE $<$<CONFIG:Debug>:-fsanitize=address>)\n    endif()\n  endif()\nendfunction()\n```\n\nNow explain why link inside WITH_DBG. The key insight: WITH_DBG is not \"compile-only\" flag. It's \"product\" flag = \"этот таргет несёт отладочную информацию и санитайзер\". Compile-side and link-side are two halves of the same decision. Tools are built WITHOUT WITH_DBG → they get neither /Zi nor /DEBUG nor asan. This matches Makefile exactly: tools on Windows = CXXFLAGS minus /Zi minus asan, and no LINK_FLAGS at all.\n\nSo answer: link options are inside because they're the same category. After endif() (outside WITH_DBG) would mean they apply to tools too — wrong.\n\nLet me also mention: the only reason they could ever be \"outside\" is if there were link options needed for ALL targets regardless of product/tool status. In our project there are none.\n\nKeep it concise. Russian. Also note we're in plan mode — no edits. End by confirming plan is ready, ask to switch to execution.",
