          "text": "The user is looking at the Makefile and noticed that in the Makefile, `-g` is in both debug and release configs for Linux:\n\nFrom the Makefile:\n```\nifeq ($(CONFIG), debug)\n  CONFIG_FLAGS := -O0 -g -fsanitize=address\n  ...\nelse\n  CONFIG_FLAGS := -O2 -g -DNDEBUG\n  ...\nendif\n```\n\nSo yes, `-g` is present in both Debug and Release on Linux. My skeleton had:\n```\ntarget_compile_options(${tgt} PRIVATE $<$<CONFIG:Debug>:-fsanitize=address> $<$<CONFIG:Release>:-g>)\n```\n\nThis is wrong — it puts `-g` only in Release and omits it in Debug (well, CMake's default Debug flags for GCC include `-g` already, actually. Let me think).\n\nActually, CMake's default Debug flags for GCC: `-g` is in CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_DEBUG default. So in Debug, CMake would add `-g` by default. In Release, CMake default is `-O3 -DNDEBUG` (no `-g`). So my skeleton added `-g` explicitly for Release but relied on CMake default for Debug.\n\nBut the user's point is about matching the Makefile: the Makefile has `-g` in both Debug and Release explicitly. The user proposes:\n```\ntarget_compile_options(${trgt} PRIVATE $<$<CONFIG:Debug>:-g -fsanitize=address> $<$<CONFIG:Release>:-g>)\n```\n\nThis is actually correct and matches the Makefile: Debug = `-O0 -g -fsanitize=address` (well, -O0 comes from CMake default debug flags... actually CMake default Debug for GCC is `-g` only, no -O0, meaning default optimization -O0 for no optimization flag). Hmm, actually GCC defaults to -O0 if no -O flag. So Debug = `-g` (default CMake) → effectively -O0 -g. Release CMake default = `-O3 -DNDEBUG`.\n\nWait, but the user's proposal is about being explicit: put `-g` in Debug too. The user is right that the Makefile has `-g` in both. My skeleton omitted `-g` from Debug compile flags, relying on CMake default.\n\nSo the user is correct. Let me confirm: Makefile Linux debug: `-O0 -g -fsanitize=address`. Linux release: `-O2 -g -DNDEBUG`.\n\nIn my skeleton, I had Debug compile: `-fsanitize=address` only, and Release: `-g`. That means in Debug, `-g` comes from CMake default (CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_DEBUG = `-g` for GCC), so it's actually fine in practice. But the user's point about being explicit and matching Makefile is valid. However, there's a subtlety: CMake default for GCC Debug already includes `-g`. So adding `-g` explicitly for Debug is redundant but harmless — and it makes the intent explicit and matches the Makefile.\n\nBut wait — there's a bigger question here about being explicit vs relying on CMake defaults. Also the Release optimization: Makefile uses `-O2` but CMake Release default for GCC is `-O3`. Earlier I flagged this discrepancy. The user hasn't asked about that yet, but it's related.\n\nAlso -O0: CMake default Debug for GCC doesn't add `-O0` explicitly; GCC defaults to -O0 when no -O flag. So it's equivalent.\n\nSo the user is right. I should confirm: yes, `-g` should be in both Debug and Release, matching the Makefile. And acknowledge that my skeleton was wrong — I only put `-g` in Release, relying on CMake's default `-g` for Debug. The user's explicit version is better and matches the Makefile exactly.\n\nLet me verify the Makefile once more to be precise. From my earlier read:\n\n```\nifeq ($(CONFIG), debug)\n  CONFIG_FLAGS := -O0 -g -fsanitize=address\n  LINK_FLAGS := -fsanitize=address\nelse\n  CONFIG_FLAGS := -O2 -g -DNDEBUG\n  LINK_FLAGS := \nendif\n```\n\nYes: debug = `-O0 -g -fsanitize=address`, release = `-O2 -g -DNDEBUG`. `-g` in both.\n\nSo the corrected Linux compile options:\n```\n$<$<CONFIG:Debug>:-g -fsanitize=address> $<$<CONFIG:Release>:-g>\n```\n\nAnd link: Debug `-fsanitize=address` only (as I had).\n\nNow about the \"правило -g везде\" — the user's phrasing: \"мне кажется что нужно вот так, то есть -g везде\". Yes, correct.\n\nBut I should also raise the optimization-level discrepancy: Makefile `-O2` Release, CMake default `-O3`. If we're going to be explicit about matching the Makefile, should we also make Release `-O2` explicit? Since -g is now explicit, to be fully faithful we should also consider -O2. But CMake Release default is -O3, so relying on default gives -O3, not -O2. However — the course's §4.7 Release flags are: `cl /O2 /Zi /DNDEBUG /MD` for MSVC, and GCC-side... Actually the AGENTS.md §4.7 step 3 flags are given for MSVC. The Linux side is the user's adaptation. In the Makefile Linux release: `-O2 -g -DNDEBUG`.\n\nSo to be faithful to Makefile, Linux Release should be `-O2`, and CMake default gives `-O3`. If we don't override, we get -O3. This is a discrepancy I flagged earlier. The user might want to address it now since we're making compile flags explicit for Linux.\n\nAlso for Debug: Makefile `-O0`. CMake default for GCC Debug has no -O flag → GCC defaults to -O0. Equivalent. But being explicit: if we want to fully mirror Makefile, Debug should be `-O0 -g -fsanitize=address`.\n\nHmm, but there's a tradeoff: the more we override, the more we diverge from CMake idioms (relying on defaults is idiomatic). But this project's philosophy is exact parity with the Makefile. Also note: CMake sets CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_DEBUG etc. If we add our own -O2 via genex for Release, CMake's default -O3 would ALSO be there → conflict? Actually no: CMake default -O3 is in CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_RELEASE; if we add target_compile_options -O2, then both -O3 and -O2 would be passed; GCC uses the last one, so it might work, but that's fragile/ugly. The clean way to change the optimization level is to override CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_RELEASE, or use CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_RELEASE_INIT.\n\nThis is getting deep. Let me think about what the right plan-level recommendation is.\n\nThe course §4.7 step 3 Release flags for MSVC: `/O2 /Zi /DNDEBUG /MD` + linker `/DEBUG /OPT:REF /OPT:ICF`. That's what the Makefile MSVC release does. For Linux, the Makefile adapted: `-O2 -g -DNDEBUG`.\n\nFor parity and predictability, the cleanest approach in CMake for a single-config Ninja build: explicitly set the per-config flag variables in the top-level CMakeLists:\n\n```cmake\nif(MSVC)\n  # Debug: /MDd /Od /Zi /fsanitize=address  (note: /MDd via runtime lib abstraction; /Zi maybe via debug info format)\n  set(CMAKE_C_FLAGS_DEBUG \"/D_DEBUG /Ob0 /Od\")\n  set(CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_DEBUG \"/D_DEBUG /Ob0 /Od\")\n  ...\n```\n\nHmm wait, but this conflicts with the per-target approach we took with configure_target + WITH_DBG (tools don't get /Zi, /DEBUG, asan). If we set global CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_DEBUG, tools would get those too. That's why we used per-target genex approach.\n\nSo for the tools-vs-products distinction, per-target is the way. But then optimization level and -g: these could reasonably be global (apply to tools too) or per-target. In the Makefile, tools on Windows got `$(filter-out /Zi /fsanitize=address,$(CXXFLAGS))` — so tools DID get `/Od` (debug) and `/O2` (release) and `/MDd`, but not /Zi and not asan. On Linux, tools got full CXXFLAGS including -g and asan (asymmetry I noted earlier).\n\nSo the optimization flags /Od /O2 apply to tools too in the Makefile. In our configure_target, we currently only put /Zi and asan inside WITH_DBG; /Od /O2 come from CMake defaults globally (they apply to all targets including tools) — wait, do they? CMake default flags are per-config global: CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_DEBUG includes /Od /RTC1 /MDd... and CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_RELEASE includes /O2 /DNDEBUG. These apply to all targets. So tools get /Od in debug, /O2 in release — matching Makefile. Good. So the optimization levels are global (CMake defaults), matching the Makefile's behavior for tools.\n\nNow for -g on Linux: CMake default Debug includes -g. So tools get -g in debug already (matching Makefile Linux debug which gives tools full flags with -g). Release: CMake default has no -g → tools in release would NOT get -g. In Makefile, tools got full CXXFLAGS in release → -g included. Hmm, so there's a subtle difference for tools in Linux release.\n\nBut honestly this is getting into deep weeds. The user's specific point: Debug compile options on Linux should include `-g`. My skeleton had `-g` only in Release. The user's correction is right.\n\nNow, should I push back on anything? The anti-sycophancy block says: don't agree automatically; if wrong, say directly. Here the user is RIGHT about the Makefile: `-g` is in both. So I confirm they're right, and I admit my skeleton was imprecise (I relied on CMake's default -g in Debug rather than stating it explicitly, which violates the \"explicit is better, mirror the Makefile\" principle).\n\nBut I should raise the related discrepancy honestly: Release optimization -O2 vs CMake default -O3. If we're making Linux flags explicit, we should decide about -O2 as well. And about -O0 for debug.\n\nLet me also reconsider: is relying on CMake defaults \"fine\" or should we mirror Makefile exactly? For a teaching project where parity with make is the acceptance criterion, I'd argue: mirror the Makefile explicitly for the things that matter, and note where CMake defaults differ but are acceptable.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the whole approach. There might be a cleaner design: instead of setting optimization via target_compile_options genex, we could rely on CMake defaults BUT verify they match. CMake GCC defaults:\n- Debug: `-g` (no -O → GCC -O0 effectively). Makefile wants `-O0 -g -fsanitize=address`. So Debug default + our `-fsanitize=address` = `-g -fsanitize=address` ≈ matches (modulo explicit -O0 which is redundant).\n- Release: `-O3 -DNDEBUG`. Makefile wants `-O2 -g -DNDEBUG`. Difference: -O3 vs -O2, and missing -g.\n\nSo my skeleton's Linux Release had explicit `-g` (correct, because CMake release lacks it), and Debug relied on default -g (correct in practice). The user wants Debug to also explicitly state -g. That's defensible for parity/explicitness. I'll accept the user's version — it matches the Makefile literally.\n\nBut then for full parity, Release optimization: should be -O2, but CMake default is -O3. If we add target_compile_options `-O2` for release, we'd have both -O3 (from CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_RELEASE) and -O2 in the command line. GCC takes the last -O flag... actually the last one wins. Order: flags from CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS come before target_compile_options typically. So -O2 (from target options) would come after -O3 and win. But this is fragile/ugly. Cleaner: override CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_RELEASE for GCC, or set CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_RELEASE_INIT. But then that applies to tools too — which matches Makefile (tools got -O2 in release too). Hmm, tools in release with -O2 is fine.\n\nActually the cleanest to handle optimization levels: use per-config global flags (CMAKE_C_FLAGS_DEBUG/RELEASE, CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_DEBUG/RELEASE) for the optimization level + -g + /DNDEBUG (things that apply to all targets), and keep ASan + /Zi + /DEBUG + /OPT:REF /OPT:ICF per-target in WITH_DBG.\n\nBut wait, MSVC debug default includes /RTC1 which we must remove — that argues for overriding CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_DEBUG entirely on MSVC. So:\n\n```cmake\nif(MSVC)\n  set(CMAKE_C_FLAGS_DEBUG \"/D_DEBUG /MDd /Ob0 /Od\")   # /MDd comes via runtime lib; but actually with CMP0091 NEW, /MDd is NOT in flags\n  ...\n```\n\nHmm, careful. With CMP0091 NEW, CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_DEBUG default = \"/D_DEBUG /Ob0 /Od /RTC1\" (no /MDd; /MDd comes via CMAKE_MSVC_RUNTIME_LIBRARY abstraction). So to remove /RTC1, we'd set CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_DEBUG to \"/D_DEBUG /Ob0 /Od\". \n\nBut this is a global override — tools would lose /RTC1 too. Is that a problem? /RTC1 is runtime error checks; tools don't strictly need them; but losing them silently changes tool behavior. Meh, tools aren't the subject under test. Actually the Makefile tools on Windows: they got CXXFLAGS debug which = /Od /Zi /MDd /fsanitize=address minus /Zi and asan = /Od /MDd. No /RTC1 (Makefile never had /RTC1). So overriding CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_DEBUG without /RTC1 matches Makefile even for tools. Fine.\n\nOK here's the thing — we're deep in planning details. Let me step back and think about what to recommend in the plan.\n\nThe key architectural decision for flags: **global per-config flags vs per-target WITH_DBG**. I think the cleanest, matching both Makefile parity AND the tools-exclusion:\n\n1. Global (all targets, including tools): optimization level, -g (Linux both configs), /DNDEBUG, /D_DEBUG, standard, warnings. Set via CMAKE_C_FLAGS_<CONFIG> / CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_<CONFIG> overrides so we get exact Makefile parity and remove /RTC1.\n\n2. Per-target WITH_DBG (products only): /Zi, /fsanitize=address (compile), /DEBUG + /OPT:REF /OPT:ICF (MSVC link), -fsanitize=address (Linux link).\n\nWait, but should tools get ASan? Makefile: Windows no, Linux yes (asymmetry). We decided to make tools uniformly light (no ASan) — the user agreed earlier. So ASan per-target WITH_DBG. Good.\n\nShould tools get -g? Makefile: Linux yes (full flags). With global -g in both configs, tools get -g — matches Linux Makefile. Windows tools: Makefile gave /Od /MDd, no /Zi — -g is a Linux concept, N/A. So global -g is fine and even matches Linux Makefile.\n\nSo the user's correction actually pushes toward: put `-g` (and by extension -O0/-O2, /DNDEBUG) in GLOBAL flags, not in the WITH_DBG per-target block. Because -g is not a \"debug info for the product\" thing here — in the Makefile it's in CONFIG_FLAGS which applies to everything on Linux (both tools and products). Whereas /Zi is Windows-only and is product-only.\n\nHmm, but on Linux, -g IS debug info. And tools don't need it. But Makefile gave it to tools anyway. To keep it simple and match Makefile, global -g is fine.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: is it cleaner to just keep optimization/-g/NDEBUG in global per-config flags and only put ASan /Zi /DEBUG in WITH_DBG? I think yes. Let me propose that structure.\n\nSo the revised plan for flags:\n\n```cmake\n# top-level, before targets\nif(MSVC)\n  # Warnings & strict mode for C and C++\n  # (per-target or global? warnings could be global too — tools should also be warned)\n  ...\n  set(CMAKE_C_FLAGS_DEBUG   \"/D_DEBUG /MDd /Ob0 /Od\")     # /MDd via... \n```\n\nWait, careful with CMP0091 NEW: if we set CMAKE_C_FLAGS_DEBUG manually to include /MDd, then runtime lib abstraction ALSO adds /MDd → duplicate. Need to either not include /MDd (let abstraction add it) or not rely on abstraction. Simplest: set flags WITHOUT /MDd and let CMAKE_MSVC_RUNTIME_LIBRARY default add it. So:\n\n```cmake\nset(CMAKE_C_FLAGS_DEBUG   \"/D_DEBUG /Ob0 /Od\")\nset(CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_DEBUG \"/D_DEBUG /Ob0 /Od\")\nset(CMAKE_C_FLAGS_RELEASE \"/DNDEBUG /O2 /Ob2\")\nset(CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_RELEASE \"/DNDEBUG /O2 /Ob2\")\n```\n\nHmm, but /Ob2 — Makefile didn't have /Ob2. Makefile release: /O2 /Zi /DNDEBUG /MD. So just \"/O2 /DNDEBUG\". Fine.\n\nBut wait — MSVC with CMP0091 NEW: does setting CMAKE_C_FLAGS_DEBUG override the default including removing /RTC1? Yes, if we set the variable it replaces the default. Good.\n\nFor Linux:\n```cmake\nset(CMAKE_C_FLAGS_DEBUG   \"-g\")\nset(CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_DEBUG \"-g\")\nset(CMAKE_C_FLAGS_RELEASE \"-O2 -g -DNDEBUG\")\nset(CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_RELEASE \"-O2 -g -DNDEBUG\")\n```\n\nGCC default debug is -g already, but explicit -g in both is what Makefile had and what user proposed. Explicit is fine.\n\nHmm, but wait: is overriding CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_DEBUG globally the idiomatic way, or is it \"hacky\"? Setting per-config flag variables at top level is a very common and accepted CMake pattern (especially for MSVC). It's documented. OK.\n\nBut there's tension: the course AGENTS says \"абстракция toolchain-флагов (ifeq-блоки исчезают)\". Overriding config flag variables is a clean way. Alternatively per-target genex. Both valid. \n\nGiven the user is asking specifically about Linux debug `-g`, I should:\n1. Confirm user is RIGHT: Makefile has `-g` in both Linux configs; my skeleton only had it in Release (I relied on CMake default -g for Debug).\n2. Recommend where it belongs: since -g (like -O2/-O0, /DNDEBUG) in Makefile lives in CONFIG_FLAGS which applies to ALL targets (tools too), the cleanest is to put these in the GLOBAL per-config flags, not in the WITH_DBG block. The WITH_DBG block should only contain product-only things: /Zi, ASan, /DEBUG, /OPT:REF/ICF.\n3. Also raise the -O2 vs -O3 discrepancy (Makefile -O2, CMake GCC release default -O3) — if we go explicit, decide -O2.\n4. Also note MSVC: overriding global Debug flags also solves the /RTC1 problem cleanly (global, affects tools too — but Makefile tools never had /RTC1, so no regression).\n\nHmm wait — but if global config flags include ASan... no, we keep ASan in WITH_DBG. Good.\n\nLet me reconsider whether moving -g/-O2 to global is what the user wants, or whether they just want the genex line fixed. The user's proposal:\n```\ntarget_compile_options(${trgt} PRIVATE $<$<CONFIG:Debug>:-g -fsanitize=address> $<$<CONFIG:Release>:-g>)\n```\nThis is inside the WITH_DBG block (in the current skeleton). If we keep it there, then tools (WITHOUT WITH_DBG) get NO -g on Linux — but Makefile gave tools -g. Divergence. If -g is in global config flags, tools get -g matching Makefile. \n\nWhich is more important? Tools' -g matters only if we want to debug tools (we don't). But parity is the acceptance criterion. And conceptually: -g in Makefile is part of CONFIG_FLAGS (build-mode flags applied to everything), not a product-only thing. So global is more faithful to the Makefile's structure.\n\nAlso: /DNDEBUG and -O2/-O0 — global. /Zi is Windows-only product thing (in Makefile it was in CXXFLAGS which tools got filtered out — so /Zi is product-only, WITH_DBG). ASan product-only (Windows Makefile filtered it for tools; Linux Makefile didn't but we chose uniform light tools). /DEBUG product-only.\n\nSo the revised model:\n- Global per-config flags: optimization (-O0/-O2, /Od//O2), -g (Linux), /DNDEBUG, /D_DEBUG. These mirror Makefile's CONFIG_FLAGS.\n- WITH_DBG block: /Zi, ASan (compile), /DEBUG, /OPT:REF /OPT:ICF, Linux link -fsanitize.\n\nWait, but if we set CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_DEBUG globally to include -g, and ALSO the tools... tools get -g. Fine (matches Linux Makefile). And ASan stays product-only (matches Windows Makefile; Linux Makefile gave asan to tools but we're fixing that intentionally — agreed earlier).\n\nHmm, actually wait: on Windows, Makefile tools got /MDd (debug CRT). With CMP0091 abstraction, all targets get /MDd in debug globally. Good, matches.\n\nOK. So my recommendation: move -g (and -O2/-O0, /DNDEBUG) into the global per-config flags; keep ASan /Zi /DEBUG /OPT in WITH_DBG. This means the Linux compile genex in WITH_DBG becomes just `$<$<CONFIG:Debug>:-fsanitize=address>` for compile, and link `$<$<CONFIG:Debug>:-fsanitize=address>`.\n\nBut actually, hmm, there's a subtlety about the tools being built in Debug config: if tools get global -g and -O0 and /MDd, that's fine.\n\nWait, but actually there's a concern: should tools get -g? It's harmless. OK.\n\nActually, let me reconsider once more whether to use global config flags vs keep per-target. The per-target genex approach has an advantage: everything is visible in one function, WITH_DBG clearly separates products from tools. The global-flags approach separates by CMake convention (config flags). \n\nFor teaching: I think the global per-config flags is actually the more standard CMake idiom and directly mirrors Makefile's `CONFIG_FLAGS` concept. And it resolves /RTC1 cleanly for MSVC. I'll recommend it but present both, and let the user decide? The user seems to be actively reviewing the skeleton line by line — they're engaged in the design. I should give a clear recommendation with reasoning and ask them to confirm.\n\nActually, I realize I should be careful not to over-complicate. Let me re-read the user's message: they say \"я посмотрела мейк файлик и мне кажется что нужно вот так, то есть -g везде\". They just want -g in both Debug and Release. The simplest fix honoring their request within the current structure: put -g in both genex branches inside the WITH_DBG block. But I should point out the alternative (global) because it's more faithful to the Makefile structure (CONFIG_FLAGS applies to tools too) and note the -O2 vs -O3 issue.\n\nGiven the anti-sycophancy rules: the user is right on the specific point (-g everywhere). I confirm. But I add nuance: there are actually two ways to place it, and one is more faithful to the Makefile. Also raise the -O3/-O2 thing so it's a conscious decision.\n\nLet me also double check the MSVC side: does my skeleton need similar? Makefile MSVC debug: /Od /Zi /MDd /fsanitize=address. CMake MSVC debug default: /D_DEBUG /MDd /Ob0 /Od /RTC1. We remove /RTC1, add /Zi + asan in WITH_DBG. /Od is default. Good.\n\nAlso note: CMake's MSVC debug info: CMAKE_MSVC_DEBUG_INFORMATION_FORMAT default = ProgramDatabase for Debug → /Zi automatically in Debug. So we may not even need /Zi in Debug explicitly (but for Release we do, since Release default has no /Zi... actually CMAKE_MSVC_DEBUG_INFORMATION_FORMAT ProgramDatabase applies to Debug and RelWithDebInfo, not Release). So keeping /Zi explicit for both is safe and matches Makefile. Actually if CMake adds /Zi in Debug automatically AND we add /Zi → duplicate flag, harmless. Fine.\n\nNow for the plan update. I'm in plan mode, read-only. I should present the correction and the revised flags design, and ask for confirmation.\n\nLet me structure the response:\n1. Confirm: you're right, Makefile has -g in both Linux configs. My skeleton had it only in Release (relied on CMake default -g for Debug). That was imprecise.\n2. But there's a design decision: WHERE does -g belong? Two options:\n   a. In the WITH_DBG genex (your proposal) — tools won't get -g.\n   b. In global per-config flags (CMAKE_C_FLAGS_DEBUG/RELEASE) — mirrors Makefile CONFIG_FLAGS which applied to ALL targets including tools.\n   The Makefile structure says (b) is more faithful: -g/-O2/-O0//DNDEBUG are build-mode flags for everything. Also (b) fixes MSVC /RTC1 cleanly.\n3. Also flag: Makefile Linux release is -O2, but CMake GCC default release is -O3. If we go global explicit, decide -O2 for parity.\n4. Propose the revised flags layout table.\n\nLet me make the response concise but complete. Present a small table of where each flag lives now.\n\nLet me draft the revised flag layout:\n\nGlobal (per-config, all targets — mirrors Makefile CONFIG_FLAGS):\n| | MSVC Debug | MSVC Release | GCC Debug | GCC Release |\n|CMAKE_<LANG>_FLAGS_DEBUG/RELEASE| /D_DEBUG /Ob0 /Od | /O2 /DNDEBUG | -g (GCC default, explicit ok) | -O2 -g -DNDEBUG |\n\nHmm, actually for MSVC: /D_DEBUG /Ob0 /Od in debug; /O2 /DNDEBUG in release. /MDd//MD via runtime lib abstraction. /RTC1 removed (not in Makefile anyway).\n\nWITH_DBG block (products only — mirrors Makefile's per-product extras):\n| MSVC compile Debug | /Zi /fsanitize=address |\n| MSVC compile Release | /Zi |\n| MSVC link Debug | /DEBUG |\n| MSVC link Release | /DEBUG /OPT:REF /OPT:ICF |\n| GCC compile Debug | -fsanitize=address |\n| GCC link Debug | -fsanitize=address |\n\nNote: -g now NOT in WITH_DBG — it's global. So the user's proposed genex line would change: WITH_DBG GCC compile becomes just `$<$<CONFIG:Debug>:-fsanitize=address>` (no -g), because -g comes from global config flags.\n\nWait, but should I push for the global approach, or accept their simpler fix? Let me think about which is pedagogically better and technically cleaner.\n\nArgument for global config flags:\n- Mirrors Makefile structure exactly (CONFIG_FLAGS vs product extras).\n- Resolves /RTC1 (MSVC) cleanly in one place.\n- Tools get -g/-O2//DNDEBUG exactly like Makefile did.\n- Avoids duplicate -O3/-O2 conflict for Release (if we set CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_RELEASE to -O2, no -O3 at all).\n\nArgument for keeping everything in configure_target per-target:\n- Everything visible in one place.\n- But tools would diverge from Makefile (no -g in debug for tools on Linux... actually with their fix, tools get no -g at all; Makefile gave tools -g on Linux).\n\nI'll recommend the global-config-flags approach, with the explicit note that it mirrors Makefile's CONFIG_FLAGS. And confirm user's observation about -g.\n\nBut I must be careful: is overriding CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_DEBUG considered bad practice? It's actually widely done for MSVC (/RTC1 removal is a classic reason). It's fine.\n\nOne more consideration: the tools. Actually — do we even want tools to have -g? It adds nothing. But parity... The acceptance is \"parity with make\". On Linux make, tools had -g. So for strict parity, global -g gives tools -g. OK.\n\nHmm, but actually wait: there's ANOTHER subtlety. If tools get -g and -O2 but NOT asan, on Linux that matches make exactly. On Windows, tools get /Od /MDd (global) + no /Zi (WITH_DBG only) — matches make Windows tools. \n\nNow, one thing to double-check about global flags and the tools compile: the tools are C++ (run_case.cpp, check.cpp). They'll get CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_DEBUG = /D_DEBUG /Ob0 /Od. Fine.\n\nLet me also reconsider: with global config flags set, do we still need the `$<$<CONFIG:Debug>...>` genex at all? For WITH_DBG we still need config-dependent /Zi//DEBUG//OPT + asan. For the global ones, no genex needed (config flags are inherently per-config). Good — cleaner.\n\nAlso standard and warnings: keep as before (per-target or global via add_compile_options). Warnings could be global too. Let me not churn that now; keep as is.\n\nAlright. Also, should I mention that in Debug, CMake GCC default already gives -g, so even without explicit -g it'd be there? Yes — that's why my skeleton \"worked\" conceptually but was imprecise. Worth stating honestly: \"I relied on CMake default -g in Debug; you're right that explicit is better for parity\".\n\nLet me write the response. Keep it focused. Since we're in plan mode and near finalizing the plan, I'll present the corrected flags design and ask to confirm before execution.\n\nI should keep response reasonably concise. Use the table. Confirm correctness. Ask which placement they prefer (or recommend global).\n\n