          "text": "The user is asking a really good question. They're suggesting: why not put `-g` (and `-Wall -Wextra`) as unconditional flags in the Linux part of `configure_target`, i.e. `target_compile_options(${tgt} PRIVATE -Wall -Wextra -g)` and only put `-fsanitize=address` in the Debug-conditional.\n\nLet me think about whether this is correct or not.\n\nThe question: `-g` is present in both Debug and Release in the Makefile (Linux). So putting it unconditionally in `configure_target` would work for products. But wait — `configure_target` is called for ALL targets, including tools (run_case, check). Tools on Linux in the Makefile were compiled with full CXXFLAGS including `-g` (the filter-out was only on Windows). Actually let me check the Makefile again.\n\nLooking at the Makefile Linux tools rules:\n```\n$(BINDIR)/tools/run_case: tests/run_case.cpp | $(BINDIR)/tools\n\t$(CXX) $(EXE_OUT)$@ $(CXXFLAGS) $<\n```\nSo on Linux, tools get full CXXFLAGS including `-g` and `-fsanitize=address`. On Windows, tools get `$(filter-out /Zi /fsanitize=address,$(CXXFLAGS))` — so they get everything except /Zi and asan.\n\nSo in the Makefile, on Linux, tools DO get `-g` and ASan. Interesting. That was the asymmetry I noted earlier.\n\nNow the user proposes: put `-Wall -Wextra -g` unconditional in configure_target, and only asan behind a Debug conditional (and presumably behind a DBG flag for products).\n\nIs this valid? Yes, functionally. `-g` is harmless for tools. Actually for parity with the Makefile Linux behavior, tools got `-g` anyway.\n\nBut wait — there's a subtlety. The user's suggestion conflates two things:\n1. The `-g` flag: present in both Debug and Release (per Makefile CONFIG_FLAGS). So putting it unconditional is fine — it matches \"mode flags apply to everything\".\n2. But they put `-g` inside `configure_target` (per-target), not global. \n\nThe question is whether `-g` should be per-target (in configure_target) or global (in CMAKE_C_FLAGS_DEBUG/RELEASE). \