ntarget_compile_options(${tgt} PRIVATE /W4 /permissive- $<$<COMPILE_LANGUAGE:CXX>:/EHsc>)\n```\nAnd then -g equivalent on MSVC is /Zi which is Debug AND Release (per Makefile: /Od /Zi debug, /O2 /Zi release). So /Zi unconditional? But tools shouldn't get /Zi (Windows Makefile filtered it out). Hmm! So on MSVC, /Zi is NOT unconditional — it's product-only (WITH_DBG). This is where the Windows/Linux asymmetry bites:\n\n- Linux: `-g` in Makefile is in CONFIG_FLAGS → tools got it too.\n- Windows: `/Zi` in Makefile is in CXXFLAGS but tools used filter-out to remove /Zi → tools did NOT get it.\n\nSo `-g` (Linux) is a \"mode flag for everyone\", while `/Zi` (Windows) is a \"product flag\". They're semantically the same thing (debug info) but treated differently in the Makefile. \n\nSo the user's proposal for Linux (`-g` unconditional in configure_target) matches the Makefile. But if we mirror it on Windows, we'd put /Zi unconditional — which would NOT match the Makefile (tools shouldn't get /Zi).\n\nSo the cleanest consistent approach: keep per-platform differences as they are. For Linux, `-g` unconditional is fine (and matches Makefile). For Windows, /Zi inside WITH_DBG.\n\nBut then the structure differs between platforms, which is a bit confusing pedagogically. Alternatively, use Variant B global flags for both, which handles both: put -g in global flags (Linux Debug AND Release — wait, can I put -g in both via global? CMAKE_C_FLAGS_DEBUG=\"-g\", CMAKE_C_FLAGS_RELEASE=\"-O2 -g -DNDEBUG\"). Yes. And on Windows, global Debug flags \"/D_DEBUG /Ob0 /Od\" (no /Zi — /Zi stays in WITH_DBG as product-only). So Variant B is consistent: mode flags global, product flags per-target.\n\nThe user's approach: ALL flags per-target in configure_target, with -g unconditional.\n\nThe real difference: \n- Variant B: optimization/NDEBUG/-g in global. Product extras (/Zi, asan, /DEBUG) per-target.\n- User's: -Wall -Wextra -g per-target unconditional, asan per-target in debug.\n\nBoth work for