eanest for LEARNING:\n\nKeep it per-target for the visible/learning stuff (warnings, -g on Linux, asan, /Zi, /DEBUG) — matches user's instinct — BUT for MSVC, the /RTC1 removal and default overrides are done via global CMAKE_C_FLAGS_DEBUG because that's the only clean way.\n\nHmm, but then -g (Linux) in configure_target vs /Zi (Windows) in configure_target-with-WITH_DBG — inconsistent placement between platforms for \"the same thing\".\n\nLet me reconsider: maybe the cleanest is to NOT fight CMake defaults at all on the flags CMake already provides, and only add what Makefile had EXTRA.\n\nActually, let me step back and think about what the ACTUAL parity target is. The Makefile flags:\n\nLinux Debug: `-std=c17/c++23 -Wall -Wextra -MMD -MP -O0 -g -fsanitize=address` + link `-fsanitize=address`\nLinux Release: `-std=c17/c++23 -Wall -Wextra -MMD -MP -O2 -g -DNDEBUG` + link `(empty)`\n\nCMake defaults (GCC): Debug: `-g` (CMAKE_C_FLAGS_DEBUG = \"-g\"). Release: `-O3 -DNDEBUG` (CMAKE_C_FLAGS_RELEASE = \"-O3 -DNDEBUG\"). Plus -std from C_STANDARD.\n\nSo if we do the user's approach (add `-Wall -Wextra -g` per-target, asan per-target debug):\n- Debug: CMake gives -g, we add -Wall -Wextra -g (dup -g, harmless), asan. -O0: CMake Debug doesn't add -O → GCC default is -O0. ✓ matches -O0!\n- Release: CMake gives -O3 -DNDEBUG, we add -Wall -Wextra -g. Result: -O3 instead of Makefile's -O2. ✗ mismatch -O2 vs -O3.\n- link: asan in debug ✓; release no link flags ✓ (empty).\n\nSo the ONLY mismatch in user's approach is -O3 vs -O2 in GCC Release. And MSVC /RTC1 (but that's Windows, different concern).\n\nSo actually, for Linux, the user's approach is essentially perfect EXCEPT the -O2/-O3 question.\n\nFor MSVC with per-target approach:\n- CMake Debug default: /D_DEBUG /MDd /Ob0 /Od /RTC1. /RTC1 conflicts with asan. Problem.\n- CMake Release default: /MD /O2 /Ob2 /DNDEBUG. Matches Makefile's /O2 /MD /DNDEBUG, extra /Ob2 (harmless).\n\nSo on MSVC, the /RTC1 issue forces a global overrid