AGS_DEBUG   \"/D_DEBUG /Ob0 /Od\")\nset(CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_DEBUG \"/D_DEBUG /Ob0 /Od\")\n```\nAnd Release: default is \"/O2 /Ob2 /DNDEBUG\" (matches Makefile's /O2 /DNDEBUG; /Ob2 extra harmless). So no need to override Release on MSVC.\n\nFor Linux, user's approach: no global override needed at all if we accept -O3, OR override Release to -O2:\n```cmake\n# option: to keep parity with Makefile's -O2:\nset(CMAKE_C_FLAGS_RELEASE \"-O2 -DNDEBUG\")   # but then -g is per-target already\nset(CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_RELEASE \"-O2 -DNDEBUG\")\n```\nHmm, if we keep -g per-target and override Release to -O2, then:\nDebug (Linux): CMake default -g, +per-target -Wall -Wextra -g + asan. = -Wall -Wextra -g -fsanitize ✓ (dup -g harmless)\nRelease (Linux): CMake override -O2 -DNDEBUG, +per-target -Wall -Wextra -g. = -Wall -Wextra -g -O2 -DNDEBUG ✓ matches Makefile!\n\nSo the clean final: \n- per-target (configure_target): warnings + `-g` unconditional (Linux), asan in WITH_DBG.\n- global: MSVC Debug override (drop /RTC1), Linux Release override (drop -O3→-O2, keep -DNDEBUG).\n\nActually for Linux Release override: CMAKE_C_FLAGS_RELEASE default is \"-O3 -DNDEBUG\". To get -O2 -g -DNDEBUG we'd override to \"-O2 -DNDEBUG\" and rely on per-target -g. That works but splits -g between per-target and global — slightly awkward. Alternative: put -g in global Release override too and remove from per-target? But then tools get -g via global in Release, and via CMake default -g in Debug (CMake default Debug includes -g). Actually CMake default CMAKE_C_FLAGS_DEBUG for GCC = \"-g\". So -g already comes globally in Debug! So we don't even need per-target -g in Debug. And for Release we add -g in the global override \"-O2 -g -DNDEBUG\".\n\nOh interesting. So actually on Linux:\n- Debug: CMake default \"-g\" ✓ (that's why my original skeleton \"worked\").\n- Release: need to add -g because default Release has no -g.\n- Optimization: Debug default no -O (effective -O0) ✓; Release default -O3 (need -O2 for parity).\n\nSo if we want strict parity on Linux, global override Release = \"-O2 -g -DNDEBUG\". Debug default = \"-g\" ✓. Then per-target only needs warnings + asan!\n\nThat's actually the cleanest: \n- Linux per-target: `-Wall -Wextra` unconditional, asan in WITH_DBG+Debug.\n- Linux global: CMAKE_*_FLAGS_RELEASE = \"-O2 -g -DNDEBUG\" (parity + -g in release). Debug untouched (default -g ✓).\n\nHmm, but then the user's proposal of putting `-g` per-target unconditional — it's harmless duplication with the global Debug default. If they prefer explicit per-target -g (visible), that's fine too and achieves parity. Both fine.\n\nSo the honest answer to the user: yes your version works and is clean; the only things it leaves to CMake defaults are (a) -O2 vs -O3 in Release, (b) it makes tools get -g (which matches Makefile Linux). If you're OK noting -O3 for later, fine; or we fix -O2 now. And Windows can't use exactly this pattern because /Zi must exclude tools and /RTC1 needs removing.\n\nLet me present this concisely and ask for the decision on -O2 and the final structure. I must NOT edit files (plan mode). Just discuss.\n\nLet me keep response reasonably short but complete. I'll present:\n1. Yes, your version is valid and cleaner (DRY) — explain why (-g config-independent).\n2. Two consequences: -O2 vs -O3 (GCC Release default), and tools get -g (matches Makefile ✓).\n3. Windows can't mirror it exactly: /Zi is product-only in Makefile, /RTC1 needs global removal → so Windows keeps global+WITH_DBG. This asymmetry mirrors the Makefile.\n4. Proposed final structure, ask for confirmation on -O2 handling.\n\nKeep it tight.",
