          "text": "The user asks three questions. Let me answer them carefully.\n\n1. What is /RTC1 that I keep talking about? She never had that flag.\n2. Will CMake automatically set -O0 -DNDEBUG -g or does it need to be explicit?\n3. She shows a version of configure_target that she says mirrors her Makefile structure, and asks: is it correct that it mirrors the Makefile, and is the only problem the asymmetry of debug info handling?\n\nLet me think carefully about each.\n\n**Question 1: /RTC1**\n\n/RTC1 is a Microsoft compiler flag (MSVC). It stands for Runtime Checks (run-time error checks). /RTC1 is a shorthand for /RTCsu = stack frame checks (s) + uninitialized variables usage (u). It's part of the DEFAULT debug flags that CMake sets for MSVC when you configure a Debug build. It's not in her Makefile — because she wrote flags by hand and never added it. But CMake's default CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_DEBUG for MSVC includes /RTC1. That's why I mentioned it: since CMake adds it by default, and ASan is incompatible with /RTC (per MSVC docs: \"it isn't compatible with edit-and-continue, incremental linking, and /RTC\"), we need to remove /RTC1 when using ASan. So it's not a flag she had — it's a flag CMake would add by default, which we need to strip.\n\nActually, let me verify: CMake default CMAKE_C_FLAGS_DEBUG for MSVC: `/D_DEBUG /MDd /Ob0 /Od /RTC1`. And CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_DEBUG similar. Yes, /RTC1 is in there by default in CMake for MSVC Debug. So the answer: it's not her flag, it's CMake's default Debug flag that we must remove because ASan conflicts with it.\n\n**Question 2: Will CMake set -O0 -DNDEBUG -g automatically?**\n\nFor GCC (Linux):\n- Debug: CMake default CMAKE_C_FLAGS_DEBUG = \"-g\". No -O0 explicitly (GCC default optimization is -O0 when no -O flag given, so effectively -O0). No -DNDEBUG in debug. So Debug gets -g automatically, effectively -O0 automatically (by omission), NDEBUG NOT set (which is correct for debug — you don't want NDEBUG in debug).\n- Release: CMake default CMAKE_C_FLAGS_RELEASE = \"-O3 -DNDEBUG\". So Release gets -O3 (not -O2!) and -DNDEBUG automatically. But NOT -g. So -g in Release must be added explicitly if we want it.\n\nSo the answer: \n- -O0 in debug: automatic (by omission; GCC default is -O0).\n- -DNDEBUG: automatic in Release, NOT set in Debug. Good — that matches Makefile (Makefile has /DNDEBUG only in release).\n- -g: automatic in Debug (CMake default includes -g), NOT in Release (must add explicitly).\n\nIn her proposed version she wrote `$<$<CONFIG:Release>: -O2>` in the Linux compile options. Wait, that's odd — she put -O2 only in Release genex for compile. But actually the -O level is set globally by CMake per config (release -O3). Adding -O2 per target would override. Hmm, but actually mixing global -O3 (from CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_RELEASE) and target -O2 (from target_compile_options) — the last one wins in GCC? Actually order matters. Both would be passed: -O3 -O2, and GCC uses the last one. CMake puts global flags first, then target options. So -O2 would win. But this is fragile. Better to override CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_RELEASE globally with -O2.\n\nLet me reconsider her version carefully:\n\n```cmake\nif(MSVC)\n  target_compile_options(${trgt} PRIVATE $<$<COMPILE_LANGUAGE:CXX>:/EHsc> /W4 /permissive-)\n  if(DBG)\n    target_compile_options(${trgt} PRIVATE $<$<CONFIG:Debug>:/Zi /fsanitize=address> $<$<CONFIG:Release>:/Zi>)\n    target_link_options(${trgt} PRIVATE $<$<CONFIG:Debug>:/DEBUG> $<$<CONFIG:Release>:/DEBUG /OPT:REF /OPT:ICF>)\n  endif()\nelse()\n  target_compile_options(${trgt} PRIVATE -Wall -Wextra -g)\n  if (DBG)\n    target_compile_options(${trgt} PRIVATE $<$<CONFIG:Debug>: -fsanitize=address> $<$<CONFIG:Release>: -O2>)\n    target_link_options(${trgt} PRIVATE $<$<CONFIG:Debug>: -fsanitize=address>)\n  endif()\nendif()\n```\n\nIssues in her version:\n1. She put `-O2` in Release inside `if(DBG)`. But tools (run_case, check) are built WITHOUT DBG → they would NOT get -O2 in Release, and would get CMake default -O3. That's an asymmetry: products -O2, tools -O3. Also, -O2 in Release only for DBG targets is weird. Actually wait — does she want tools optimized? In Makefile, tools were built with $(filter-out /Zi /fsanitize=address,$(CXXFLAGS)) on Windows, and full CXXFLAGS on Linux. So on Linux tools got -O2 (release) too. So putting -O2 in the Linux branch unconditional would match Makefile better.\n\n2. In MSVC branch she doesn't handle /RTC1. She removed the global CMAKE_C_FLAGS_DEBUG override that I proposed. So /RTC1 would still be in CMake default Debug flags → conflict with ASan. Wait, does she not have the global set()? Her version only shows configure_target function, not the global flags section. She removed the global flags entirely (the `set(CMAKE_C_FLAGS_DEBUG ...)` part). So she dropped the /RTC1 handling. Need to point that out.\n\n3. `-g` unconditional in Linux branch (her version) — matches Makefile (both configs have -g). Good. But CMake Debug default also adds -g → duplicate -g in debug, harmless. And Release needs -g, which she now adds unconditionally. Good.\n\n4. She says \"как я понимаю полностью повторяет структуру моего makefile, верно? и в этом плохого только асимметрия работы с отладочной информацией?\"\n\nLet me assess: Does her version fully mirror the Makefile? Not quite. Let me compare.\n\nMakefile MSVC debug: /Od /Zi /MDd /fsanitize=address, link /DEBUG. CMake gives /Od and /MDd automatically. She adds /Zi /fsanitize=address. OK.\n\nMakefile MSVC release: /O2 /Zi /DNDEBUG /MD, link /DEBUG /OPT:REF /OPT:ICF. CMake gives /O2 /DNDEBUG /MD automatically. She adds /Zi and link flags. OK.\n\nMakefile Linux debug: -O0 -g -fsanitize=address, link -fsanitize=address. CMake gives -g, and effectively -O0. She adds -g (uncond) and -fsanitize (debug). OK. Note: -O0 effectively automatic.\n\nMakefile Linux release: -O2 -g -DNDEBUG. CMake gives -O3 -DNDEBUG automatically. She adds -O2 (only for DBG targets) and -g (uncond). So -O2 for products but -O3 for tools. In Makefile, tools got -O2 too (Linux). So asymmetry: tools would be -O3.\n\nSo her claim \"полностью повторяет структуру makefile\" is not fully correct. Differences:\na) Tools on Linux: Makefile gave them -O2 (release) and -g and even -fsanitize. Her version: tools get -g (uncond) but -O3 (release, CMake default) and no -fsanitize. Actually wait — in Makefile Linux, tools got FULL CXXFLAGS including -fsanitize=address. That was the asymmetry I mentioned earlier — Linux tools DID have ASan. Hmm, earlier I said \"асимметрия: на Linux инструменты собирались с полными CXXFLAGS (с ASan и -g), на Windows — облегчёнными\". So on Linux Makefile, tools DID have ASan. Her version gives tools no ASan on Linux (since -fsanitize is in if(DBG)). That's a deliberate deviation we agreed on (uniformly light tools). That's fine — it's the \"осознанное улучшение\".\n\nb) /RTC1: not handled in her version → CMake default Debug flags include /RTC1 → conflict with ASan. This is a real problem she's missing.\n\nc) -O2 placement: she put it in if(DBG) on Linux, so tools get -O3. Minor, and arguably fine to just accept CMake default -O3 everywhere and drop -O2. OR put -O2 globally.\n\nNow the user's question: \"как я понимаю полностью повторяет структуру моего makefile, верно? и в этом плохого только асимметрия работы с отладочной информацией?\"\n\nAnswer: \n- Structurally yes, the if(MSVC)/else() mirrors the Makefile's two branches.\n- But not \"fully\" — two gaps: (1) /RTC1 not handled → conflict with ASan in Debug; (2) -O2 only for DBG targets → tools on Linux get -O3 not -O2 (minor).\n- And the asymmetry of debug info: on Windows tools don't get /Zi, on Linux tools get -g. This asymmetry is INHERITED from the Makefile, so it's \"parity\", but it's a wart. Actually is that the ONLY problem? No — /RTC1 is a problem too. And the -O2/tools thing.\n\nWait, also let me reconsider: the user's comment in the code: \"# сюда будет заходить только для программ из задач, не для программ для тестирования (run_case, check) - им эти флаги не нужны\". This is about the if(DBG) — tools don't get /Zi, /DEBUG, asan. Correct.\n\nBut actually there's a subtle thing: she has `target_compile_options(${trgt} PRIVATE $<$<COMPILE_LANGUAGE:CXX>:/EHsc> /W4 /permissive-)` in MSVC branch. /W4 /permissive- unconditional — applies to tools too. Good, matches Makefile (tools got /W4 /permissive-).\n\nLet me also double check: does the user's version handle the \"tools\" correctly for link? Tools get no link options (no /DEBUG). Good — matches Makefile (tools weren't linked with LINK_FLAGS).\n\nNow, one more consideration on question 2: `-O0`. Does CMake set -O0? CMake's default CMAKE_C_FLAGS_DEBUG for GCC is \"-g\" (no -O). GCC default without -O flag is -O0. So effectively -O0, but not explicitly written. If you want it explicit, you could add it, but it's redundant. I'll explain this.\n\nActually, let me verify: CMake default for GCC debug: CMAKE_C_FLAGS_DEBUG = \"-g\". Yes. Release: CMAKE_C_FLAGS_RELEASE = \"-O3 -DNDEBUG\". Yes.\n\nFor MSVC: CMAKE_C_FLAGS_DEBUG = \"/D_DEBUG /MDd /Ob0 /Od /RTC1\" (note: with CMP0091 NEW, /MDd might be removed from the string and added via abstraction). Release: \"/MD /O2 /Ob2 /DNDEBUG\" (with /MD removed via abstraction under CMP0091 NEW). Let me be careful about CMP0091: NEW behavior = not placing runtime lib flags in the default cache entries. So /MDd and /MD are NOT in the flags strings; they come via CMAKE_MSVC_RUNTIME_LIBRARY abstraction (default MultiThreaded$<$<CONFIG:Debug>:Debug>DLL). So under our 3.22 min, /MDd/MD come via abstraction. Good — I should mention that.\n\nSo question 2 answer:\n- Debug (-O0): automatic effectively (GCC default). Not explicitly written. Fine to leave.\n- -DNDEBUG: automatic in Release only (both MSVC and GCC). Correct behavior — debug shouldn't have NDEBUG.\n- -g: Debug automatic (CMake default), Release NOT automatic — must add explicitly. In her version she adds -g unconditionally → covers Release. Good.\n\nNow let me also verify whether -O0 for MSVC debug: CMake default includes /Od. Yes.\n\nSo my answer should:\n1. Explain /RTC1: it's not her flag — it's CMake's default Debug flag for MSVC (Runtime Checks). It's incompatible with ASan, so we strip it. She never wrote it because she hand-wrote flags; but CMake adds it by default. So we MUST override CMAKE_C_FLAGS_DEBUG to drop it. Without that override, Debug + ASan would conflict.\n\nLet me double-check the incompatibility claim. MSVC docs (asan.md): \"However, it isn't compatible with edit-and-continue, incremental linking, and /RTC.\" Yes confirmed from the fetched doc. Good.\n\n2. Explain -O0, -DNDEBUG, -g automatic vs explicit.\n\n3. Answer \"полностью повторяет структуру makefile?\" — mostly, with two gaps: /RTC1 not handled, and -O2 scoped to DBG (tools get -O3 on Linux). And the debug-info asymmetry is inherited from Makefile (Windows tools no /Zi, Linux tools -g) — it's a wart but it IS parity. Not the only problem though.\n\nLet me also think: is her version actually going to build? If /RTC1 stays and ASan on → would it error or just be incompatible? MSVC: /RTC1 with /fsanitize=address — I believe cl errors or warns. Actually the docs say \"isn't compatible\" and VS turns off /RTC1 when enabling ASan. From the command line, you might get error or the runtime checks just don't work well. This could be a real failure at build time. Either way, we should strip it. I'll frame it as: this could break the Debug+ASan build, so we handle it in the global flags.\n\nActually, I recall that clang-cl and MSVC: using /RTC1 and /fsanitize=address — I'm not 100% sure it's a hard error vs just incompatible behavior. I should present it as \"документация заявляет несовместимость; чтобы не рисковать — убираем\". Confidence: the doc statement is FACT; whether it hard-errors I'm not sure. I'll say [ФАКТ — несовместимость по доке; СРЕДНИЙ — что именно произойдёт при сборке, проверим].\n\nNow, one thing I want to reconsider: her version removed the global flags entirely and relies on CMake defaults + per-target. Is there anything else that needs global override? On MSVC, /RTC1. That's it, I think. So we still need the global Debug override on MSVC. On Linux, if we accept -O3 for release (or set -O2 globally), no global override needed if we put -g unconditional per-target (her way). Actually for Linux Release -g: her unconditional -g covers it. For -O2: if she wants -O2 for all (products AND tools), put it unconditional (outside if(DBG)) or globally. Since tools in Makefile got -O2, unconditional matches. But honestly tools at -O3 vs -O2 — irrelevant practically. The cleanest: set -O2 globally via CMAKE_*_FLAGS_RELEASE (overrides CMake default -O3), or add -O2 unconditional per-target.\n\nHmm, but wait: if we set CMAKE_C_FLAGS_RELEASE = \"-O2 -DNDEBUG\" globally, and per-target we add -g unconditional, that works and tools get -O2 too. But if we put -g unconditional per-target, debug targets get -g twice (CMake debug default -g + ours). Harmless.\n\nAlternatively keep it simple: accept CMake defaults, only fix what's necessary:\n- MSVC: override Debug flags to drop /RTC1.\n- Linux: add -g for Release (since CMake doesn't). Decide -O2 vs -O3.\n\nLet me present a clean recommended version and note the deltas from her version. Actually, the user asked specific questions. Let me answer them directly and concisely, then present the corrected version.\n\nLet me structure the answer:\n\n**1. /RTC1** — it's not from your Makefile. It's a default Debug flag CMake adds for MSVC. Explain what it is (Runtime Checks = stack frame + uninitialized var checks). Why I mention it: ASan incompatible per docs. You never wrote it because hand flags; CMake auto-adds it. So we override CMAKE_C_FLAGS_DEBUG to remove it. If we don't, Debug+ASan breaks/conflicts.\n\n**2. -O0, -DNDEBUG, -g automatic?**\nTable:\n- /Od or -O0 Debug: automatic (MSVC /Od in default; GCC no -O → effective -O0).\n- /O2 Release: MSVC automatic /O2; GCC default is -O3 NOT -O2 (Makefile had -O2). So GCC Release: NOT matching automatically.\n- /DNDEBUG Release: automatic (both). Debug: correctly absent.\n- -g: Debug automatic; Release NOT automatic — add explicitly.\nSo your unconditional `-g` in Linux branch is right. -O2: if you want Makefile parity, set explicitly; else accept -O3.\n\n**3. Does your version fully mirror Makefile?** Mostly. Two gaps + note on debug-info asymmetry:\n- Gap A: /RTC1 not handled (see Q1).\n- Gap B: -O2 placed inside if(DBG) → tools on Linux get -O3, products -O2. In Makefile tools got -O2 too. Minor, decide.\n- Debug-info asymmetry: inherited from Makefile — Windows tools no /Zi, Linux tools -g. It's parity (matches Makefile), but a wart; the only \"problem\" it causes is slight inconsistency. Not the ONLY issue though (RTC1 is the bigger one).\n\nThen present the corrected final version.\n\nLet me write the corrected configure_target + global flags:\n\n```cmake\n# --- Глобальные: правим только то, где дефолт CMake ≠ Makefile ---\nif(MSVC)\n  set(CMAKE_C_FLAGS_DEBUG   \"/D_DEBUG /Ob0 /Od\")   # выкинули /RTC1; /MDd придёт из CMP0091\n  set(CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_DEBUG \"/D_DEBUG /Ob0 /Od\")\nelse()\n  set(CMAKE_C_FLAGS_RELEASE   \"-O2 -DNDEBUG\")      # -O3 → -O2 (паритет); NDEBUG и так был бы\n  set(CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_RELEASE \"-O2 -DNDEBUG\")\nendif()\n\nfunction(configure_target tgt)\n  if(MSVC)\n    target_compile_options(${tgt} PRIVATE /W4 /permissive- $<$<COMPILE_LANGUAGE:CXX>:/EHsc>)\n    if(WITH_DBG)\n      target_compile_options(${tgt} PRIVATE $<$<CONFIG:Debug>:/Zi /fsanitize=address> $<$<CONFIG:Release>:/Zi>)\n      target_link_options(${tgt} PRIVATE $<$<CONFIG:Debug>:/DEBUG> $<$<CONFIG:Release>:/DEBUG /OPT:REF /OPT:ICF>)\n    endif()\n  else()\n    target_compile_options(${tgt} PRIVATE -Wall -Wextra -g)\n    if(WITH_DBG)\n      target_compile_options(${tgt} PRIVATE $<$<CONFIG:Debug>:-fsanitize=address>)\n      target_link_options(${tgt} PRIVATE $<$<CONFIG:Debug>:-fsanitize=address>)\n    endif()\n  endif()\nendfunction()\n```\n\nWait — I should double check whether overriding CMAKE_C_FLAGS_DEBUG entirely is safe. If I set it to \"/D_DEBUG /Ob0 /Od\", I lose nothing important? Default was \"/D_DEBUG /MDd /Ob0 /Od /RTC1\". Under CMP0091 NEW, /MDd comes from abstraction not the string, so the string is \"/D_DEBUG /Ob0 /Od /RTC1\". Removing /RTC1 → \"/D_DEBUG /Ob0 /Od\". Good. And /MDd still comes via CMAKE_MSVC_RUNTIME_LIBRARY. Good.\n\nFor Release on Linux override \"-O2 -DNDEBUG\": default was \"-O3 -DNDEBUG\". We change -O3→-O2. Good. But note: CMAKE_C_FLAGS_RELEASE and CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_RELEASE both need setting.\n\nAlso note: is -g in Release handled? Yes — unconditional -g in Linux branch of configure_target covers both configs, applied to all targets (products AND tools). In Makefile, Linux tools got -g too. Parity. Good.\n\nNow, do tools get -g on Release on Linux? Yes (unconditional). In Makefile tools got full CXXFLAGS so -g yes. Parity. OK.\n\nNow let me double check the claim \"CMake default Release for GCC is -O3 -DNDEBUG\". Yes, that's standard. Confidence high.\n\nAlso \"CMake default Debug for GCC is -g\". Yes. High confidence.\n\nAlso verify /RTC1 in default MSVC Debug: CMAKE_C_FLAGS_DEBUG for MSVC includes /RTC1. Yes, historically. High confidence. With CMP0091 NEW, /MDd removed but /RTC1 remains. Good.\n\nNow also, the user wrote in their version: `# PRIVATE даёт нетранзитивность: А (этот объект) и Б к нему сможет линковаться. а когда К линк. к Б, то К не будет линк. к А`. That's their understanding comment about PRIVATE — correct: PRIVATE means the link relationship isn't propagated to targets that link to this one. Actually more precisely: for target_link_libraries PRIVATE, the linked lib is used only for linking this target, not propagated. Their analogy is right. I don't need to correct it. Actually there's nuance: PRIVATE for compile options means not propagated to consumers. Fine.\n\nAlso they noted \"сюда будет заходить только для программ из задач, не для программ для тестирования (run_case, check)\". This refers to if(DBG) — tools called without DBG. Correct.\n\nBut wait — there's a subtlety. In their structure, `-g` on Linux is OUTSIDE if(DBG) (unconditional), so tools DO get -g. And on MSVC, /Zi is INSIDE if(DBG), so tools DON'T get /Zi. This is the debug-info asymmetry. They asked \"в этом плохого только асимметрия работы с отладочной информацией?\" — the asymmetry being: Linux tools get -g, Windows tools don't get /Zi.\n\nActually is that even \"плохого\"? It matches the Makefile exactly. So it's parity. The \"problem\" is it's a bit ugly/inconsistent. On Linux, tools have debug info (can be debugged), on Windows they don't. It's inherited behavior. We could make it uniform (both no debug info) by putting -g inside if(DBG) too, which would be cleaner — tools don't need debug info. But then Linux tools lose -g, deviating from Makefile. That was the \"осознанное улучшение\" option.\n\nHmm, actually which is better pedagogically? The course's rule: \"Временный инструмент, удаляется перед коммитом\" — tools are infrastructure, we don't debug them. So making tools uniformly light (no -g on Linux either) is the cleaner design. But it deviates from Makefile parity on Linux (Makefile gave tools -g). Since -g is harmless, keeping it is also fine. I'll present both and let her choose. Actually earlier we agreed tools should be light. So maybe put -g inside if(DBG) on Linux too for uniformity? But wait — if tools on Linux get -g, that's parity. The asymmetry point: Windows tools no debug info, Linux tools yes. \n\nLet me just present it clearly: the only asymmetry is -g (Linux, unconditional) vs /Zi (Windows, inside DBG). To make tools uniform (both without debug info), move -g inside DBG. To keep exact Makefile parity, leave as is. Since -g is harmless, either is fine; recommend uniform (tools don't need debug info).\n\nActually wait, if we move -g inside if(DBG), then Release -g: products get -g in release (inside DBG unconditional by config). Good — products always have -g both configs, tools never. That's uniform and clean. And it matches the course rule that tools don't need debugging. Deviation from Makefile: Linux tools lose -g — acceptable improvement, note in journal.\n\nHmm, but there's a subtlety: if -g is inside if(DBG) and DBG targets are products, fine. But what about the common libraries (geometry, string_utils)? They're products too (linked into products, may be debugged). They get WITH_DBG. Good.\n\nOK let me also reconsider: in her MSVC branch, `if(DBG)` includes /Zi for both Debug and Release. Tools don't get /Zi. Good.\n\nNow let me answer concisely but completely. I must not make edits (plan mode). Just answer and present final recommended version, ask for confirmation.\n\nLet me also answer question about whether CMake sets -O0 -DNDEBUG -g automatically — I'll make a clear table:\n\n| Флаг | Debug | Release |\n|---|---|---|\n| -O0 / /Od | автомат (MSVC /Od; GCC без -O = -O0) | — |\n| -O2 | — | MSVC: автомат /O2; GCC: НЕТ, дефолт -O3 |\n| -DNDEBUG | не ставится (и правильно) | автомат (обе платформы) |\n| -g | автомат (обе платформы) | НЕТ — добавляем вручную |\n\nGood. Now write the response.\n\nI should be careful about length. Three questions; answer each clearly. Keep it structured. Russian language.\n\nLet me also double-check the claim that GCC default Release = -O3. Yes, CMake sets -O3 for GCC release by default. High confidence.\n\nAnd MSVC debug default includes /RTC1 — I'm fairly confident. Let me state it as fact with medium-high confidence, and note we'll verify at first build (we planned a verification anyway).\n\nOne more: the user asked \"и в этом плохого только ассиметрия работы с отладочной информацией?\" — I'll answer: no, there are actually two issues besides that: (1) /RTC1, (2) -O2 placement for tools. And the debug-info asymmetry is inherited from Makefile (parity), not a regression.\n\nLet me write it now.",
