ора в корне — это не про наши флаги, а про «исправить несовместимый дефолт тулчейна». Это можно сделать чище: `string(REPLACE \"/RTC1\" \"\" CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_DEBUG ${CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_DEBUG})` — то есть не переписывать флаги заново, а только вырезать конфликтующую часть. Так глобальная правка сводится к минимуму и видно, что мы только удаляем одно.\n\n4. **Вариант целиком в CMake-стиле** — можно вообще убрать кастомную функцию и сделать INTERFACE-библиотеки, несущие флаги. Но это наверное избыточно для масштаба проекта, и `configure_target` как функция — тоже принятый CMake-паттерн (helpers). Стоит спросить пользователя.\n\nActually, let me think about what's genuinely more CMake-idiomatic for the whole project. There are two schools:\n- Function-based (as we have) — pragmatic, common in real projects.\n- Interface-library-based — more \"pure CMake data model\".\n\nFor a learning project, and given the course's emphasis on understanding, I'd argue the function is fine but we should note that INTERFACE libraries are the alternative that becomes important at scale.\n\nLet me also reconsider the -O2 on Linux Release. The user's version had `-O2` inside `if(DBG)`. I suggested global. In CMake style, per-target `$<$<CONFIG:Release>:-O2>` would be the genex way. But tools (run_case, check) don't need -O2... actually do they? In Makefile, tools got full CXXFLAGS including -O2 in release. Hmm, but honestly tools don't need optimization, and in the CMake world the default -O3 for tools is fine. The user's instinct to put -O2 only for products is actually more \"CMake-ish\" (targets get what they need). \n\nWait, but actually — is -O2 even needed to be explicit for products? The default for GCC Release is -O3. If we don't set -O2 anywhere, products build with -O3. The Makefile had -O2. So for parity we need to decide. Putting `$<$<CONFIG:Release>:-O2>` per-target for products (WITH_DBG) is the CMake-style way to do it. That's actually the user's original instinct! I over-corrected by moving it global.\n\nHmm, but then tools get -O3 (CMake default) while products get -O2. In Makefile both got -O2. Is this a parity violation? Yes, technically. But it's harmless and arguably more correct (tools don't matter). The user seems to care more about CMake-idiom than exact parity here.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider whether we should even fight -O3 vs -O2. For this Debug-only stage (layer 16, single config Debug), Release flags don't matter at all yet. The Release path won't even be exercised until layer 26. So maybe the cleanest CMake-idiomatic answer is: **don't override Release optimization at all** — accept CMake defaults (-O3 GCC, /O2 MSVC), and note in journal \"Release настроим на слое 26 с замерами\". This is actually the most CMake-y: let CMake defaults do their thing, tune later when benchmarks demand.\n\nThat resolves the -O2 question cleanly: drop the explicit -O2 entirely for now. MSVC default Release is /O2 (matches Makefile), GCC default is -O3 (differs, but we're not at Release stage; document it).\n\nHmm, but the course rule §4.7: Release flags given: `cl /O2 /Zi /DNDEBUG /MD`. And the Makefile GCC release had -O2. But the course is MSVC-focused for flags. GCC -O3 vs -O2... At layer 26, benchmarks will be measured, and the table of measurements is the artifact. So leaving GCC default -O3 is defensible and CMake-idiomatic. I'll present this as an option and let the user decide.\n\nNow the /RTC1 problem in pure CMake style: \n\nOption A: keep a minimal global edit: `string(REPLACE \"/RTC1\" \"\" ...)`. This is the standard idiom and doesn't look like make — it's a targeted correction of a toolchain default.\n\nOption B: Actually — is there a way to NOT have /RTC1? There's `CMAKE_MSVC_DEBUG_INFORMATION_FORMAT`... no. There's `/RTC1-`? I need to check if MSVC supports negative forms. Actually cl DOES support turning off some default options with a trailing `-`... no wait, that's for things like `/Zc` or `/permissive-`. Hmm. I genuinely am not sure MSVC supports `/RTC1-`. Let me not claim it.\n\nActually, I recall now: MSVC does NOT have a way to un-set /RTC1 per translation unit except by not having it in the flags. And CMake puts it in the default flags. So the string-REPLACE approach (or setting the flags) is THE way.\n\nActually there's another angle: we could enable ASan via `target_compile_options` AND the conflict with /RTC1 — maybe the real issue is only /RTC1 when ASan is on. So the global edit needs to be applied conditionally... but ASan is only in Debug, and /RTC1 is only in Debug defaults, so a Debug-only global correction is consistent.\n\nOK let me structure the response:\n\n1. **Ты права.** Признать: глобальные `set(CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_...)` — это make-стиль (аналог CONFIG_FLAGS), перетащенный в CMake. Правильно — свойства таргетов + genex, что в твоём варианте уже есть.\n\n2. **Что в CMake-стиле, а что нет** — разбор:\n   - `target_compile_options`/`target_link_options` с `$<$<CONFIG:...>>` — идиоматично.\n   - `if(MSVC)/else()` в корне — нормально (платформенные отличия всё равно придётся выражать).\n   - глобальные флаговые переменные — вот это make-запах.\n   - кастомная функция `configure_target` — допустимый helper-паттерн; альтернатива — INTERFACE-библиотеки (чище модель данных, но тяжелее для текущего масштаба).\n\n3. **Единственное честное исключение — /RTC1.** Это дефолт тулчейна (не наш флаг), конфликтующий с ASan, и его нельзя убрать per-target (нет отрицательной формы). Минимальная глобальная правка — не «переписать флаги», а «вырезать одно несовместимое слово»:\n   ```cmake\n   if(MSVC)\n     string(REPLACE \"/RTC1\" \"\" CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_DEBUG \"${CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_DEBUG}\")\n     string(REPLACE \"/RTC1\" \"\" CMAKE_C_FLAGS_DEBUG \"${CMAKE_C_FLAGS_DEBUG}\")\n   endif()\n   ```\n   Это точечная коррекция дефолта тулчейна, а не наш CONFIG_FLAGS.\n\n4. **Release-оптимизация** — предлагаю вообще не трогать сейчас: это Debug-этап, Release придёт на слое 26 с замерами. Дефолт MSVC `/O2` совпадает с Makefile; GCC `-O3` — зафиксируем в журнале «расхождение, решим на слое 26». Тогда и `-O2` нигде явно не пишем — полностью CMake-стиль.\n\n5. Итоговый скелет функции configure_target (твой стиль, только genex per-target):\n\n```cmake\nfunction(configure_target tgt)\n  if(MSVC)\n    target_compile_options(${tgt} PRIVATE /W4 /permissive- $<$<COMPILE_LANGUAGE:CXX>:/EHsc>)\n    if(WITH_DBG)\n      target_compile_options(${tgt} PRIVATE $<$<CONFIG:Debug>:/Zi /fsanitize=address> $<$<CONFIG:Release>:/Zi>)\n      target_link_options(${tgt} PRIVATE $<$<CONFIG:Debug>:/DEBUG> $<$<CONFIG:Release>:/DEBUG /OPT:REF /OPT:ICF>)\n    endif()\n  else()\n    target_compile_options(${tgt} PRIVATE -Wall -Wextra -g)\n    if(WITH_DBG)\n      target_compile_options(${tgt} PRIVATE $<$<CONFIG:Debug>:-fsanitize=address>)\n      target_link_options(${tgt} PRIVATE $<$<CONFIG:Debug>:-fsanitize=address>)\n    endif()\n  endif()\nendfunction()\n```\n\nWait — but the user objected to `-g` inside or outside? Their version had `-Wall -Wextra -g` unconditional in else branch. That means tools get -g. That's fine (parity with Linux Makefile). And /Zi only for WITH_DBG. So the asymmetry (Linux tools get -g, Windows tools don't get /Zi) — I called this out before. The user asked \"в этом плохого только асимметрия?\" — and I said no, there are 2 more issues. Now user objects to make-style. So I should resolve: the asymmetry is inherited from Makefile, it's fine, just document.\n\nActually, wait — if we want to be MORE CMake-idiomatic, we might want to reconsider `-g` unconditional. On Linux, tools get -g — harmless. Keep as parity. Fine.\n\nLet me also reconsider: is `configure_target` function even needed vs just putting flags inline? For 8 executables + 4 libraries + 2 tools, a helper function is justified (DRY, §4.1.9 \"повторила дважды — выноси\"). Good.\n\nOne more consideration: INTERFACE library approach — should I offer it? The user said \"если это cmake то и всё надо писать в соотв. стиле\". Maybe they'd like the interface library idea. Let me present it briefly as the \"more data-driven\" alternative, and ask if they want function or interface library. Actually, offering too many options might overwhelm. But the user is engaged and asking for CMake style — this is exactly the moment to explain the interface library idiom. Let me present both and let them choose, with a recommendation.\n\nLet me think about what the interface-library version looks like:\n\n```cmake\n# flags as data\nadd_library(base_flags INTERFACE)\nif(MSVC)\n  target_compile_options(base_flags INTERFACE /W4 /permissive- $<$<COMPILE_LANGUAGE:CXX>:/EHsc>)\nelse()\n  target_compile_options(base_flags INTERFACE -Wall -Wextra -g)\nendif()\n\nadd_library(product_flags INTERFACE)\nif(MSVC)\n  target_compile_options(product_flags INTERFACE $<$<CONFIG:Debug>:/Zi /fsanitize=address> $<$<CONFIG:Release>:/Zi>)\n  target_link_options(product_flags INTERFACE $<$<CONFIG:Debug>:/DEBUG> $<$<CONFIG:Release>:/DEBUG /OPT:REF /OPT:ICF>)\nelse()\n  target_compile_options(product_flags INTERFACE $<$<CONFIG:Debug>:-fsanitize=address>)\n  target_link_options(product_flags INTERFACE $<$<CONFIG:Debug>:-fsanitize=address>)\nendif()\n\n# usage:\ntarget_link_libraries(t1_cpp PRIVATE common_cpp_static base_flags product_flags)\ntarget_link_libraries(run_case PRIVATE base_flags)\n```\n\nThis is the \"model as data\" style. It removes the configure_target function and puts the platform branching in interface-library definitions. It's arguably more CMake-idiomatic (targets and their properties), and it makes the \"products get product_flags, tools get only base_flags\" relationship explicit via target_link_libraries — which is the CMake-native way to express it.\n\nTrade-offs:\n- Function: fewer target objects, one place, but it's imperative (a function mutating targets).\n- Interface libs: more declarative, uses the native linking mechanism to express \"what a target needs\", scales better (later: real libs added to link line naturally). Slightly more \"moving parts\" (two extra INTERFACE targets).\n\nFor the course, the interface-library approach is a better lesson about CMake's model. And it directly answers \"make-style vs cmake-style\". I'll recommend it, but keep the function as a valid simpler alternative and let her choose.\n\nHmm, but there's a subtlety with INTERFACE libraries and genex: `$<COMPILE_LANGUAGE:CXX>` in an INTERFACE target's options — works. `$<CONFIG:Debug>` works. Good.\n\nAlso, one nuance: for the INTERFACE library carrying -g unconditional on Linux — that goes to tools too (base_flags). Fine, parity.\n\nAlso the `product_flags` on Linux carries only ASan (debug). In Release, products on Linux get no special compile options — default -O3, no -g from product_flags... wait, -g is in base_flags which products also link. Good, so products have -g via base_flags, tools have -g via base_flags. Parity with Makefile (tools had -g too). Good.\n\nWait, but actually — should -g be in base_flags (given to tools too) or is that the \"asymmetry\" thing? The user earlier asked about the asymmetry. Let me keep -g in base_flags (parity, tools get it, harmless) and note it.\n\nNow, the /RTC1 correction still needs the global string(REPLACE). I'll present that as the single legitimate toolchain-default fix, separate from our flags.\n\nLet me also double check: do we still need `set(CMAKE_C_STANDARD ...)` etc. — those are project-level standards, that's fine and CMake-idiomatic (CMAKE_*_STANDARD variables ARE the idiomatic way — the docs literally say CMAKE_C_STANDARD is \"default value for C_STANDARD target property\"). Good, that's not make-style.\n\nOK, let me also address: the user said \"мне не нравится что это больше похоже на make-стиль. если это cmake то и всё надо писать в соотв. стиле\". So they want the whole thing CMake-style. My answer: agree, here's the pure-CMake-style version (interface libraries + per-target genex), with one unavoidable global toolchain-default fix (/RTC1). Ask if they want interface-lib style or keep function style (both are acceptable CMake; function is a common helper pattern too).\n\nActually — I should be careful not to be wishy-washy. The user asked for CMake style. The most defensible CMake style is: per-target properties, genex, interface libraries for shared flag bundles. Let me recommend that clearly, present the full skeleton, note the /RTC1 exception, and the Release decision (drop -O2, document, revisit at layer 26). Then ask for confirmation.\n\nLet me be concise but complete. Format with headers/bullets. In Russian.\n\nI should present the full proposed root CMakeLists skeleton + common/CMakeLists for t1 so we're ready to implement. Since this is the final plan piece, let me assemble the complete picture.\n\nLet me write the final proposed files:\n\n**Root CMakeLists.txt:**\n\n```cmake\ncmake_minimum_required(VERSION 3.22)\nproject(inobitec_stud LANGUAGES C CXX)\n\n# --- Стандарты (это и есть идиоматичный CMake: объявляем свойства проекта) ---\nset(CMAKE_C_STANDARD 17)\nset(CMAKE_C_STANDARD_REQUIRED ON)\nset(CMAKE_C_EXTENSIONS OFF)\nset(CMAKE_CXX_STANDARD 23)\nset(CMAKE_CXX_STANDARD_REQUIRED ON)\nset(CMAKE_CXX_EXTENSIONS OFF)\n\n# Debug по умолчанию\nif(NOT CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE)\n  set(CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE Debug CACHE STRING \"Build type: Debug/Release\" FORCE)\nendif()\n\n# --- Единственная коррекция дефолта тулчейна: /RTC1 несовместим с ASan ---\nif(MSVC)\n  string(REPLACE \"/RTC1\" \"\" CMAKE_C_FLAGS_DEBUG \"${CMAKE_C_FLAGS_DEBUG}\")\n  string(REPLACE \"/RTC1\" \"\" CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_DEBUG \"${CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_DEBUG}\")\nendif()\n\n# --- Флаги как данные: INTERFACE-библиотеки ---\n# base_flags — всем таргетам (warnings, строгость, -g)\nadd_library(base_flags INTERFACE)\nif(MSVC)\n  target_compile_options(base_flags INTERFACE /W4 /permissive- $<$<COMPILE_LANGUAGE:CXX>:/EHsc>)\nelse()\n  target_compile_options(base_flags INTERFACE -Wall -Wextra -g)\nendif()\n\n# product_flags — только продуктам (отладочная информация, ASan)\nadd_library(product_flags INTERFACE)\nif(MSVC)\n  target_compile_options(product_flags INTERFACE\n    $<$<CONFIG:Debug>:/Zi /fsanitize=address>\n    $<$<CONFIG:Release>:/Zi>)\n  target_link_options(product_flags INTERFACE\n    $<$<CONFIG:Debug>:/DEBUG>\n    $<$<CONFIG:Release>:/DEBUG /OPT:REF /OPT:ICF>)\nelse()\n  target_compile_options(product_flags INTERFACE\n    $<$<CONFIG:Debug>:-fsanitize=address>)\n  target_link_options(product_flags INTERFACE\n    $<$<CONFIG:Debug>:-fsanitize=address>)\nendif()\n\n# --- Задачи ---\nfunction(add_task dir target src lib)\n  add_executable(${target} ${src})\n  set_target_properties(${target} PROPERTIES\n    OUTPUT_NAME main\n    RUNTIME_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY ${CMAKE_BINARY_DIR}/${dir})\n  target_link_libraries(${target} PRIVATE ${lib} base_flags product_flags)\nendfunction()\n\nadd_subdirectory(common)\n\nadd_task(t1_dist_matrix_cpp t1_cpp t1_dist_matrix_cpp/main.cpp common_cpp_static)\nadd_task(t1_dist_matrix_c   t1_c   t1_dist_matrix_c/main.c     common_c_static)\n```\n\nWait, tools need base_flags. tools are in tests/CMakeLists. So they link base_flags there. And add_task applies base_flags+product_flags to all tasks. Good.\n\n**common/CMakeLists.txt:**\n```cmake\nadd_library(common_cpp_static STATIC geometry.cpp)\nadd_library(common_c_static   STATIC geometry.c)\n\ntarget_compile_definitions(common_cpp_static PUBLIC COMMON_STATIC)\ntarget_compile_definitions(common_c_static   PUBLIC COMMON_STATIC)\n\ntarget_link_libraries(common_cpp_static PUBLIC base_flags product_flags)\ntarget_link_libraries(common_c_static   PUBLIC base_flags product_flags)\n```\n\nHmm, wait — libraries should get base_flags (warnings, -g). product_flags? For static libs, /Zi at compile time produces .obj.pdb — in Makefile the static lib objects DID get /Zi (CONFIG_FLAGS included /Zi). And ASan on the library objects — yes, in Makefile the common objs got asan too. So libraries should link base_flags + product_flags. And PUBLIC so consumers inherit? No — consumers (t1_cpp) already link base_flags+product_flags themselves via add_task. If common also propagates PUBLIC, duplicate flags (harmless but noisy). \n\nActually, better: libraries link `PRIVATE` — flags apply to compiling the library itself, not propagated. Then tasks get their own base_flags+product_flags via add_task. That's cleaner and avoids duplication. But wait: the consumers need COMMON_STATIC define — that's PUBLIC compile definition (already handled). The flags themselves don't need to propagate.\n\nSo: `target_link_libraries(common_cpp_static PRIVATE base_flags product_flags)`.\n\nHmm, but for SHARED libraries later (t3-t4), the dll build needs /fsanitize etc. too — same pattern. Good.\n\nAlso note: linking an INTERFACE library via target_link_libraries just applies its options — it's the idiomatic way. \n\nNow the static lib with /Zi: produces per-object pdb? CMake handles /Zi and pdb paths per target. Fine, and that addresses the old vc140.pdb conflict.\n\nOK. Let me also mention: this design means the \"asymmetry\" (Linux tools get -g, Windows tools don't get /Zi) is still there because base_flags carries -g (all targets) and product_flags carries /Zi (products only). On Windows base_flags has no /Zi → tools no debug info; on Linux base_flags has -g → tools get debug info. Inherited parity. Document it.\n\nAlright, I think I have a complete answer. Let me also double check: is `string(REPLACE ...)` on CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_DEBUG before project()? Need project() called first? CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_DEBUG is set by enable_language/project. So the string(REPLACE) must come AFTER project(). Place it after project() and after the language is enabled. Good — I'll put it after project().\n\nAlso standards set via CMAKE_C_STANDARD before/after project — both fine, but CMake recommends setting before targets; typically after project() is fine. I'll place after project().\n\nNow, does genex $<COMPILE_LANGUAGE:CXX> work in INTERFACE target options consumed by a C-only target? Yes — it evaluates per compile. Good.\n\nAlso note: `add_task` uses `OUTPUT_NAME main` — both c and cpp tasks named main but in different dirs. Good.\n\nLet me write the final response. Keep it focused: agree, explain make-style vs cmake-style concretely, present interface-library design as the cmake-idiomatic one, note /RTC1 exception and Release decision, ask to confirm.\n\nLength: medium. Use code blocks. In Russian.",
