еется ввиду когда говорится что DBG - глобал. он вроде нигде и не определялся... или он бы передавался флагом?\"\n\nAnswer:\n- In the first skeleton, WITH_DBG was referenced inside the function but never declared as a parameter and never set anywhere. In CMake, a variable read inside a function that isn't a parameter/function-local is looked up in the calling scope (and up the scope chain). Since nobody ever set it, it's empty → `if(WITH_DBG)` is FALSE always. So the first skeleton would silently never apply debug flags. This was a latent defect — good you caught the pattern.\n- \"Глобал\" here means: a variable living in the directory scope (not function scope), which the function reads implicitly. If we instead set `set(DBG ON)` at the top of the root CMakeLists (or passed as -D), then every later call to configure_target — including the ones in tests/ (subdirectory inherits parent variables) — would see ON. The value \"sticks\": there's no automatic reset between calls, so the last assignment wins for everything after it.\n- With a parameter, each call is explicit: configure_target(run_case OFF) vs configure_target(t1_cpp ON). No hidden state.\n\nAlso should mention: in add_task we call configure_target internally — so add_task would pass ON. Tools in tests/CMakeLists would call configure_target directly with OFF (and they don't go through add_task). \n\nLet me verify the function-visibility-in-subdirectory question because our design depends on it (tests/CMakeLists calls configure_target defined in root). Let me fetch the CMake function docs to confirm.\n\nAlso, tools are in tests/CMakeLists.txt — need add_subdirectory(tests) after function definition. Since function definitions propagate to subdirectories added after... I'll verify.\n\nLet me do the web fetch for function() docs.\n\nAlso, I should probably double-check the \"last -O wins\" claim is fine to state — it's a well-known GCC behavior. I'll state with medium-high confidence.\n\nLet me fetch the CMake function docs.\n\nI'm in plan mode, read-only. Web fetch is read-only, allowed.\n\nLet me do the fetch and then compose the answer.\n\nActually let me also reconsider: the user's 3rd proposal — is there any issue with having -O2 in both if(DBG) Release and else() Release? No conflict — different targets. Products get -g -O2, tools get -O2.\n\nOne more subtlety about the tools: tools are C++ (run_case.cpp, check.cpp). They use std::print, std::expected. On Linux they need C++23. Configure_target applies -Wall -Wextra but standards come from CMAKE_CXX_STANDARD globally. Fine.\n\nAlright, let me also think: should I mention that with her else() approach, tools on Debug Linux get -g from CMake default (creating slight inconsistency with Windows tools)? Minor — I'll note it briefly as \"отметить в журнале\" maybe. Actually this level of detail might be too much. Let me keep the answer focused.\n\nLet me verify the function scope question via docs.",
