nct is correct!\n\nSo her else() branch: for Linux tools in Release, adds -O2. On Windows tools, no need — CMake default Release for MSVC is already /O2. So the else() branch is only in the Linux side, fine. Actually her structure has else() inside the Linux branch (the code shows `else()` after `if(DBG)` within the `else()` of `if(MSVC)`). So it only affects Linux. On Windows, tools get CMake default /O2. Good, symmetric.\n\nSo her solution is valid. But I should note: it works because GCC's last -O wins. And there's a subtlety — but it's fine.\n\nAlso, is -O2 on tools even needed? In Makefile yes it was there. So for parity, yes. But it's harmless either way. I should validate it works and note the \"last flag wins\" dependency, and note that this is fine for GCC.\n\nActually one more consideration: her else() branch means in Debug, tools get nothing special — they get CMake default Debug flags: -g (GCC), /D_DEBUG /Od (MSVC after RTC1 removal). Good — tools get -g on Linux Debug (matching Makefile where tools got -g). Actually wait, in Makefile Linux tools got -g too (full CXXFLAGS). So tools having -g on Linux is parity. And on Windows tools get no /Zi (filtered out in Makefile). CMake default Debug for MSVC has no /Zi unless we add it — we only add /Zi inside if(DBG). So Windows tools: no /Zi. Parity. Good.\n\nSo her final structure is actually consistent with Makefile parity. \n\nNow let me also double check the -O2 \"last wins\" claim for GCC — yes, GCC manual: \"If you use multiple -O options, with or without level numbers, the last such option is the one that is effective.\" Confirmed from knowledge.\n\nAlso for MSVC /O2: CMake default Release for MSVC is /O2 already, so no need.\n\nNow, one more thing to address: the user asked \"что имеется ввиду когда говорится что DBG - глобал. он вроде нигде и не определялся... или он бы передавался флагом?\" \n\nI should clarify the two possible designs:\n- Design A (my first skeleton, flawed): WITH_DBG referenced but 