          "text": "The user asks a conceptual question: does `add_executable(${trgt} ${src})` mean \"add prerequisite src to target trgt\"?\n\nI need to clarify the make-vs-cmake mental model difference. In make, `target: prerequisites` means \"target depends on prerequisites, run recipe to build target\". In CMake, `add_executable(name source...)` is not \"adding prerequisites\" — it CREATES the target and declares its sources. The executable doesn't exist until you \"build\" it via cmake --build. The target is a logical entity; sources are inputs. There's no \"recipe\" you write.\n\nLet me explain the difference carefully and simply, using her make background.\n\nThe analogy: in make, the line `$(BINDIR)/t1/main.exe: $(BINDIR)/t1/main.obj ...` + recipe. In CMake, `add_executable(t1_cpp main.cpp)` creates a target named t1_cpp whose source is main.cpp. CMake then derives the build rule (compile main.cpp → obj → link → exe) itself. You never write the recipe.\n\nKey points:\n1. `add_executable` CREATES the target (the name), not \"adds\" to an existing one. In make, a target only exists if there's a file/rule; in CMake, the target is a first-class object created by the command.\n2. The sources are inputs, but CMake figures out the full dependency chain (obj, headers via depfiles, link) automatically. You don't declare the obj or the link step.\n3. It's not \"adding a prerequisite\" — it's \"declaring what the target is\". The target is a unit that carries properties (sources, flags, links), and later commands like target_compile_options/target_link_libraries attach properties TO it.\n\nContrast with make: in make, `target: prereq` — prereqs are things that must be up-to-date before recipe runs. In CMake, source list is more like \"the ingredients of the target\", and the compile/link rules are derived.\n\nShe also may be wondering: does the executable output file become \"trgt\"? Yes — the output binary (main.exe) is produced, and the target name (t1_cpp) is the handle you reference everywhere else.\n\nLet me give a clean, concise answer with the make contrast table, and a small example showing that after add_executable you attach things to the target by name.\n\nAlso I should point out: in our add_task function, `add_executable(${trgt} ${src})` creates the target; then set_target_properties and target_link_libraries attach properties. So it's \"create a named logical object and give it sources\", not \"add prerequisite\".\n\nKeep it in Russian. This is conceptual, no tools needed. Plan mode - just answer.\n\nLength: keep it focused, not too long. Use a small contrast.",
