rgets are edges producing files. There's `build build/debug/t1_dist_matrix_cpp/main.exe: CXX_EXECUTABLE_LINKER__t1_cpp ...`. The \"t1_cpp\" appears in the rule name (CXX_EXECUTABLE_LINKER__t1_cpp) but the edge output is the file. Also there are phony edges in Ninja: `build t1_cpp: phony build/debug/t1_dist_matrix_cpp/main.exe`. Yes! Ninja generates a phony alias so you can `ninja t1_cpp`. So in the Ninja build file, `t1_cpp` IS a phony target aliasing the real file target.\n\nSo the answer: conceptually — CMake targets are logical; the real output is a file. In Ninja, an alias phony edge `t1_cpp -> main.exe` is generated so you can `ninja t1_cpp`. But that's the generator's internal detail; you don't manage PHONY yourself in CMakeLists.\n\nThe key distinction: in make, you must manually declare `.PHONY` for targets that don't produce files. In CMake, every `add_*` target is a logical object; CMake decides internally how to map it to the build tool — for executables/libraries it maps to a real output file plus (in ninja) a phony alias; for utility targets (add_custom_target without output) it creates a phony.\n\nSo the answer to \"будет ли t1_cpp PHONY?\" — No, not in the sense she might think. The target produces a real file. It is NOT phony. If we're talking about the Ninja internal alias, that's a generator implementation detail, not something she declares.\n\nContrast with make: in make, `all`, `test` etc. are phony because no file named \"all\" is produced. In CMake, `add_executable(t1_cpp ...)` → file main.exe exists. So not phony.\n\nAlso worth mentioning: her Makefile had `.PHONY: all t1 t2 ...`. Those will disappear — CMake handles it. `add_custom_target` (which we'll use for format targets) creates phony-like targets in CMake — that's the analog.\n\nI should be concise and clear. Use the anti-sycophancy protocol? The user hasn't re-sent the block but it's in context. I should mark [ФАКТ] etc. where relevant. Keep it reasonably short.\n\nLet me answer in Rus