        {
          "type": "reasoning",
          "text": "The user is asking a conceptual question about PRIVATE in target_link_libraries. Let me think about her question carefully.\n\nShe asks: why write PRIVATE in target_link_libraries(${trgt} PRIVATE ${lib})? So that main.obj links only with main.exe? And then why write PRIVATE in link — the product of linking, the executable file, won't link with anything else anymore. All its resources are only its own and can't be carried further?\n\nThere's a subtle misconception here. PRIVATE/PUBLIC/INTERFACE in CMake is about **transitive propagation of usage requirements** (compile definitions, include dirs, compile options, link libraries) to *consumers* of the target. It's NOT about linking main.obj with main.exe.\n\nThe key point: `t1_cpp` is an executable. Executables are typically \"leaf\" targets — nothing links *against* them. So for an executable, PRIVATE vs PUBLIC makes little practical difference because nothing consumes it. The PRIVATE there is mostly for clarity/consistency.\n\nBut the important thing to explain is the *mechanics*: PRIVATE/PUBLIC/INTERFACE describe how the target's own properties (specifically the link libraries and anything transitively required) propagate to *other targets that link this target*.\n\nLet me structure:\n- PRIVATE here is not about \"main.obj links with main.exe\". Linking main.obj into main.exe is what `add_executable` + link step does regardless.\n- PRIVATE/PUBLIC/INTERFACE describe propagation *through* the target to its consumers.\n- For executable: since nothing links against an exe, PRIVATE vs PUBLIC is equivalent in effect. It's written for consistency and intent.\n- The interesting case is the *library*: `target_link_libraries(common_cpp_static PRIVATE base_flags product_flags)` — here PRIVATE matters because if it were PUBLIC, then consumers of common_cpp_static (i.e., t1_cpp) would inherit base_flags/product_flags transitively.\n\nLet me give the three-flag semantics table:\n- PRIVATE: usage requirements are used by the target itself, not propagated to consumers. \"Линкуется с lib, и lib не светится наружу\".\n- INTERFACE: not used by the target itself, only propagated to consumers (for header-only libs).\n- PUBLIC: both.\n\nActually, there's an important nuance: `target_link_libraries(exe PRIVATE lib)` — the PRIVATE affects whether `lib` appears in the *link interface* of the exe. Since an executable's link interface is not consumed by anything, it's moot.\n\nAlso I should correct her statement \"чтобы main.obj линковалось только с main.exe?\" — no, that's not what PRIVATE does. main.obj is compiled, then linked into main.exe as part of the target build. PRIVATE is about whether the linkage to common_cpp_static is exposed as a usage requirement to anything that would link against t1_cpp later.\n\nAlso: \"все его ресурсы - только его и не могут потащиться дальше?\" — For an executable, yes, resources are leaf, but the reason isn't PRIVATE — it's that executables are not consumed. If someone did `target_link_libraries(other_exe t1_cpp)` (linking an exe to an exe — generally not meaningful), then PUBLIC would propagate. But normally you don't do that.\n\nLet me also give the concrete example in our project to make it meaningful:\n- `target_link_libraries(t1_cpp PRIVATE common_cpp_static base_flags product_flags)`\n- `target_link_libraries(common_cpp_static PRIVATE base_flags product_flags)`\n\nWhy is PRIVATE right for the library? Because if it were PUBLIC, then t1_cpp would inherit base_flags/product_flags transitively through common_cpp_static — but we already apply them directly to t1_cpp. So PRIVATE avoids double-propagation and keeps the dependency surface explicit. Also, the compile definition COMMON_STATIC is PUBLIC on the library — that one MUST propagate (consumer main.cpp needs to see COMMON_STATIC). That's the meaningful contrast: definitions propagate PUBLICly because they affect compilation of the consumer; link libraries don't need to propagate here.\n\nThis is a great teaching moment: PRIVATE vs PUBLIC matters for the *library*, not for the executable.\n\nLet me write a concise but clear explanation in Russian. Since plan mode is active, I just answer the conceptual question. Keep it focused.\n\nLet me draw a small diagram.\n\nTarget t1_cpp (exe):\n- add_executable\n- target_link_libraries(t1_cpp PRIVATE common_cpp_static) → main.obj+common obj → link → main.exe\n\nThe PRIVATE keyword here: if later someone did target_link_libraries(something t1_cpp), the PRIVATE libs wouldn't propagate. But since nothing links to an exe, it's effectively inert. Written for intent/consistency.\n\nThe meaningful case: the library.\n\nLet me answer.",
