fig says `utf-8` (no BOM) and the editor follows strictly, it might strip the BOM on save. That's harmless for CMake. But to be accurate: the existing files have BOM. Let me verify by reading the .editorconfig file and checking the actual state.\n\nAlso the syntax: multiple patterns in one section — `[*.md, CMakeLists.txt]` — editorconfig allows comma-separated patterns in a section header. This is valid per spec. Actually, let me double check: the editorconfig spec says sections are separated by square brackets, and each section header can contain multiple patterns separated by commas. Yes, `[*.md, CMakeLists.txt]` is valid.\n\nBut note: the existing file already has `[*.md]` section. If we add `[*.md, CMakeLists.txt]`, we'd have two sections matching .md files — that could cause confusion or the later one overriding. Better approach: modify the existing `[*.md]` section to `[*.md, CMakeLists.txt]`, or add a new section `[CMakeLists.txt]`. Actually simpler: just add `[CMakeLists.txt] charset = utf-8` as its own section, keeping the existing `[*.md]`.\n\nAlso, is charset actually going to do anything for CMakeLists? Editors will save as UTF-8. CMake doesn't care. It's a nice-to-have for consistency.\n\nThe user asks \"clang-format - вот так сработает корректно\". Wait — they may be confusing .editorconfig with clang-format. clang-format does NOT read .editorconfig; it reads .clang-format. The .editorconfig affects editors. Let me clarify this point since it's important.\n\nLet me read the current .editorconfig and verify the CMakeLists encoding.",
