          "type": "reasoning",
          "text": "The user asks: is `-fPIC` a kind of glue `-f + PIC`, so that's why it's written that way and not `--fPIC`?\n\nLet me explain. `-fPIC` is a GCC/Clang compiler flag, not a CMake flag. The `-f` prefix in GCC denotes \"flag\" family options. So `-fPIC` = `-f` + `PIC` where `-f` is the family prefix meaning \"language/feature flag\". This is GCC convention, not POSIX getopt convention. It's not \"glue of short options\" in the getopt sense — it's a GCC naming scheme: `-f` prefix for feature flags (e.g., `-fPIC`, `-fno-rtti`, `-fexceptions`), `-W` prefix for warnings, `-O` for optimization, `-std=` for standard, `-m` for machine.\n\nSo the answer: `-fPIC` is a GCC flag, different convention from CMake's `-B`/`--build`. The `-f` is a \"family prefix\" that GCC uses to group feature flags. It's not really \"glue\" in the getopt sense. There is no `--fPIC` because GCC doesn't use long-option double-dash for this family — GCC mostly uses single-dash prefixes even for long-ish names.\n\nActually, GCC does accept some `--` options (like `--version`, `--help`, `-fno-...` has `-f` prefix). GCC option parsing is not POSIX getopt strict — it has its own convention. `-fPIC` is one token that the compiler recognizes as a whole; it's not parsed as `-f` + `PIC` by getopt, but conceptually the `-f` prefix denotes the family.\n\nLet me be careful: in GCC, `-fPIC` is a single option. The compiler parses the whole string `-fPIC`. It happens that many options share the `-f` prefix, and GCC internally treats `-f` as a \"family\". You can even write `-fpic` (lowercase). So it's not a glue of separate short options.\n\nContrast with MSVC where the equivalent is just `/Oi` or `/fPIC`? Actually MSVC doesn't have PIC — the whole point is Windows DLLs don't need PIC. On MSVC there's no `-fPIC` at all.\n\nSo the answer to \"почему так а не --fPIC\": because it's not a CMake option at all — it's a GCC compiler flag following GCC's own convention, where `-f` is a prefix meaning \"feature flag\", and GCC's option syntax differs from both POSIX getopt and CMake. CMake passes it through verbatim to the compiler. When CMake writes the ninja file, it passes `-fPIC` to GCC. CMake itself doesn't add `-fPIC` as a \"glue\" — actually CMake adds `-fPIC` automatically for SHARED targets (POSITION_INDEPENDENT_CODE), but it writes the GCC-style flag `-fPIC`.\n\nAlso worth noting: CMake has its own `-D`, `-B`, `--build` etc. The compiler flags like `-fPIC`, `-Wall`, `-W4`? No, `-Wall` is GCC. These follow compiler conventions, not CMake's.\n\nLet me also mention: `-f` family: `-fPIC`, `-fno-exceptions`, `-fno-rtti`, `-fopenmp`, `-fsanitize=address`. All start with `-f`. And `-W` family: `-Wall`, `-Wextra`. And `-O`: `-O0`, `-O2`, `-O3`. And `-std=c17` uses `-std=` prefix. So GCC groups flags by functional family prefixes with single dash, even when the flag name is a word. That's why no `--`.\n\nLet me answer concisely in Russian, being precise. Also clarify that CMake doesn't \"glue\" anything — it just writes the compiler's own flag into the build.ninja.\n\nAlso note the interesting detail: in our CMakeLists we don't even write -fPIC ourselves; CMake adds it automatically for SHARED (layer of abstraction). But the flag that ends up on the command line is GCC's -fPIC.\n\nKeep it short per style guidelines but this is a teaching context, so moderate detail is fine.",
