        {
          "type": "reasoning",
          "text": "I have the key info from the docs. Let me answer the user's question about options after `cmake --build`.\n\nKey facts from the doc:\n- `cmake --build <dir> [<options>] [-- <build-tool-options>]`\n- `-j [<jobs>], --parallel [<jobs>]` — max concurrent processes (added 3.12)\n- `-t <tgt>..., --target <tgt>...` — build specific target\n- `--config <cfg>` — for multi-config\n- `--clean-first`\n- `-v, --verbose`\n- `--` — pass remaining options to native tool\n\nSo yes, you can pass `-j8` (or `-j 8`, `--parallel 8`) after `cmake --build build/cmake-debug`. And `--` passes options directly to ninja.\n\nNote: ninja is parallel by default (uses all cores), whereas make is serial by default. So -j is less critical for ninja but still supported.\n\nThe user's analogy: `cmake --build build/cmake_debug` ≈ `make all` (very roughly). Yes — builds default target (all). Good analogy with caveats.\n\nAlso note `--build <dir>` requires the dir first. And there's the note: ninja builds in parallel by default.\n\nI should answer concisely. Plan mode - no edits. Just answer the question.\n\nLet me note: in our project, `cmake --build` without `--target` builds the default target which is `all` (our CMakeLists has no explicit default, so all targets). Actually with no `--target`, builds the default target = ALL. Our project's default target builds everything (both t1 tasks + common libs). So yes, roughly `make all`.\n\nI'll confirm -j8 works, list the useful options, and note ninja parallel-by-default nuance.",
