othing\n- `if 1==1 echo A & echo B` → prints both\n\nSo the `&` chain IS inside the if. So `if errorlevel 1 echo msg & exit /b 1` means: if errorlevel>=1 then echo AND exit. If not, neither. That's actually the INTENDED behavior!\n\nWait, but I should double check — this is a well-known pitfall where people get it wrong. Let me reconsider the actual semantics.\n\nHmm, actually I recall now: the classic problem is with `&&`:\n- `if cond cmd1 & cmd2` — `&` is part of if body? Per this test, yes.\n- Actually no. Let me recall the real rules.\n\nThe real cmd rule: `if` command — everything after the condition is the command. `&` separates commands but the way `if` parses... Actually the empirical test just showed it. Let me not overthink. The empirical test is ground truth: `&` after `if condition` is part of the if body.\n\nHmm wait, but actually that contradicts something I know. Let me reconsider. There's a famous gotcha:\n\n`if not exist file.txt echo not found & exit /b 1`\n\nPeople say this exits always... Actually no. Let me think about what's REALLY documented.\n\nThe real story: In cmd.exe, the `if` statement consumes the rest of the line as its command when using the simple form. When the line is parsed, `&` IS a command separator but... \n\nOK actually the empirical result is unambiguous and I ran it for real. Let me just trust the empirical result: the `&` chain is inside the `if`. So the script's `if errorlevel 1 echo ... & exit /b 1` behaves as intended: only when errorlevel>=1 does it echo AND exit.\n\nBut wait — there's a subtlety! The `%errorlevel%` expansion. Inside the script at line 21-24:\n```\n%t1_cpp_cmake% < tests/input_data/t1_abc > nul 2> nul\nset last_errlevel=%errorlevel%\n%run_case% \"...\" %last_errlevel%\nif errorlevel 1 echo ... & exit /b 1\n```\n\nThis is fine — `%errorlevel%` on line 22 is expanded at line-parse time (each line separate in batch). Actually wait, `%errorlevel%` inside a batch file expands when the LINE is parsed, and since each line is parsed separately (no parentheses block), `%errorlevel%` on line 22 reflects the exit code from line 21. That's correct.\n\nThen `%run_case% ...` line 23, and `if errorlevel 1` on line 24 checks the exit code of run_case. run_case returns 0 on match, 1 on mismatch. So `if errorlevel 1` → errorlevel >= 1 → mismatch → echo fail & exit 1. Correct.\n\nBut hold on — there's a critical issue. `if errorlevel 1 echo t1_cpp abc ������ ������ ������ & exit /b 1` — if the test PASSES (errorlevel = 0), then `if errorlevel 1` is false, and per my empirical test, the `& exit /b 1` is inside the if body, so it won't run. Good — intended.\n\nWait, but let me re-verify with a proper errorlevel test, because my test used `1==2` / `1==1`, not errorlevel. Let me test errorlevel semantics specifically since there's a subtle difference. Actually the structure is the same. But let me be careful: the empirical test showed `&` is inside the if. Let me run one more test with actual errorlevel to be thorough.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the whole thing. I ran:\n`cmd /c \"if 1==2 echo SHOULD_NOT_PRINT & echo AFTER_AMP\"` → output nothing.\n`cmd /c \"if 1==1 echo CONDITION_TRUE & echo AFTER_AMP\"` → output both.\n\nSo the `&` is part of the if body. Good.\n\nBut wait — this contradicts the well-known cmd behavior that I thought I knew. Let me double-check with `errorlevel`:\n\n`cmd /c \"cmd /c exit /b 1 & if errorlevel 1 echo GOT_1 & echo OUTSIDE\"` — hmm this is getting complicated. Let me just directly test errorlevel.\n\nActually, I realize I should verify the errorlevel semantics too because there's a known gotcha: `if errorlevel 1` means \"if errorlevel >= 1\", and combined with `&`... Let me run a clean test.\n\nLet me also reconsider: is there actually a bug in this script? Let me think about the logic more carefully.\n\nThe script's logic for each exit-code test:\n1. Run the CMAKE binary on input, capture its exit code in `last_errlevel`.\n2. Run `run_case` on the MAKE binary with expected code = `last_errlevel`.\n3. If run_case fails (mismatch), echo fail and exit 1.\n\nThis checks parity of exit codes between make and cmake builds. That's the point of the parity check. Good.\n\nPotential issues:\n1. `%errorlevel%` vs `errorlevel` — line 22 `set last_errlevel=%errorlevel%` expands `%errorlevel%` at parse time of line 22. In batch, this works because line 21 executed before line 22 was parsed... actually no! In cmd batch files, lines are parsed one at a time (unless inside a parenthesized block). So `%errorlevel%` on line 22 reflects the errorlevel from line 21. Correct.\n\n2. Path with forward slashes: `build/temp/t1_cpp_cmake_test1` — on Windows cmd, `>` redirect with forward slashes works fine in most cases. Actually `cmd` redirect handles forward slashes in paths? Hmm. `> build/temp/file` — cmd generally accepts forward slashes in redirection paths. Yes, it works.\n\n3. The `--equal` comparison: `%check% --equal build/temp/t1_cpp_cmake_test1 build/temp/t1_cpp_make_test1`. The check tool compares two files. But wait — there's a subtle issue: the cmake output file was written by the CMAKE binary directly (line 48), while the make output was written by run_case running the make binary (line 50). Both compare the program outputs. Good.\n\nBut hold on — line 48 redirects stderr to nul for the cmake run and writes stdout to file. Line 50: run_case runs make binary, stdout → file, but stderr... the run_case command string `\"%t1_cpp_make% < tests/input_data/t1_test1 > build/temp/t1_cpp_make_test1\"` — no `2> nul`, so stderr of make binary goes to the console (through run_case's cmd). That's a minor cosmetic thing, not a correctness bug. Actually, hmm, in the Makefile the corresponding test was:\n```\n$(RUN_CASE_CMD) \"$(call WCMD,$(T1_CPP_EXE) < tests/input_data/t1_test1 > $(BINDIR)/t1_dist_matrix_cpp/t1_test1 2> $(NULLDEV))\" 0\n```\nSo make version redirected stderr to nul. The script doesn't for the make side — stderr will leak to console but exit code unaffected. Minor.\n\n4. Bigger issue: the parity check as written only checks t1_cpp! The script defines t1_c_make and t1_c_cmake variables but never uses them! Let me re-read... Lines 6-9 define all four variables. Then lines 21-53 only test t1_cpp (abc, float, low, high, nul, test1). t1_c is NEVER tested. So the script is incomplete — it checks only C++ parity, not C parity. That's a real gap worth flagging.\n\n5. Another subtle issue: `if errorlevel 1 echo ... & exit /b 1` — inside the script this is on a line by itself (not in a block), so it works per my test. BUT there's a catch: `& exit /b 1` on the same line after echo — my test confirmed `&` is inside if body. But let me double check with errorlevel to be 100% sure, because if I'm wrong, the script has a serious bug (always exits).\n\nActually, I realize the empirical result is solid: `if falsecond echo X & echo Y` printed nothing. So `& echo Y` is inside the if. Confirmed.\n\nHmm wait, but actually now I doubt my memory. Let me recall the actual cmd behavior again. I believe the actual rule is: `if condition command1 & command2` — the `& command2` IS part of the if's command when... \n\nOK you know what, the empirical test is what matters. It printed nothing for false, both for true. That means `& echo AFTER_AMP` is inside the if body. So the script's `if errorlevel 1 echo fail & exit /b 1` → on success (errorlevel 0), neither echo nor exit runs; on failure, both run. Correct behavior.\n\nBut wait, I need to double-check: is `%errorlevel%` the issue? The script uses `if errorlevel 1` (the special errorlevel check, not %errorlevel% expansion). Line 24: `if errorlevel 1 echo t1_cpp abc ������ ������ ������ & exit /b 1`. This checks the errorlevel from line 23 (run_case). Good.\n\n6. Encoding: the echo messages contain Cyrillic. The batch file is saved in... let me check. The read showed garbled Cyrillic (cp1251 bytes read as something else?). Actually the file uses `chcp 1251` at start, and messages are likely in cp1251. But wait — the user was told CMake files in UTF-8. But this is a .bat file, which is a different thing — .bat files traditionally use the OEM/ANSI codepage. The read tool showed garbled text which means the file is cp1251 (not UTF-8). That's fine for a .bat on Russian Windows. Actually, hmm — but the echo messages will only display correctly if the console is chcp 1251, which it sets at line 4. OK.\n\nActually wait, there's a subtle encoding issue: line 24 `if errorlevel 1 echo t1_cpp abc ������ ������ ������ & exit /b 1` — the message \"код возврата различается\" contains Cyrillic. If the .bat is saved as cp1251 and console is chcp 1251, it displays fine. The `& exit /b 1` after Cyrillic — no problem.\n\n7. The comparison at line 48-53: t1_test1 normal data. Line 48 writes cmake output. But it checks exit code of cmake via run_case against make's exit code. Wait, line 49-50: `set last_errlevel=%errorlevel%` after the cmake run, then run_case checks make binary returns last_errlevel. So parity of exit codes. Then line 52-53: check --equal compares output files. But note: check --equal normalizes CRLF. Both outputs from Windows builds so both have CRLF or... the cmake output is written by the cmake-built binary directly to file, the make output by the make-built binary. Both same binary behavior. Equal. OK.\n\n8. One more: line 46-47 `if not exist build mkdir build` and `if not exist build\\temp mkdir build\\temp`. Fine.\n\n9. The script never verifies the make build actually exists or is up-to-date. But that's a usage concern.\n\n10. `%run_case%` and `%check%` point to `build\\debug\\tools\\run_case.exe` — the make-built tools. Good, they're the tools from make build.\n\nSo the MAIN findings for the script:\n- **Bug/gap 1 (real): t1_c (C version) is never tested** — variables defined but unused. The parity check covers only C++.\n- **Minor: make-side stderr not redirected to nul in the test1 run** — cosmetic, stderr leaks to console but doesn't affect exit code.\n- The `if errorlevel 1 ... & exit /b 1` pattern works (verified empirically).\n\nNow let me also check the log.txt for the build. Key observations from log:\n- Line 4: C compile for common_c_static: flags `/nologo -DCOMMON_STATIC /DWIN32 /D_WINDOWS /Zi /Ob0 /Od -std:c17 -MDd /W4 /permissive- /Zi /fsanitize=address`. Interesting:\n  - `-std:c17` — CMake emitted `/std:c17`. Good.\n  - `/Zi /Ob0 /Od` — CMake's default debug flags (and our /Zi). Wait, `/Zi` appears TWICE (once from CMake default for MSVC debug, once from our genex). Actually line 4: `/Zi /Ob0 /Od -std:c17 -MDd /W4 /permissive- /Zi /fsanitize=address`. Yes /Zi twice. Harmless duplicate.\n  - `/MDd` present (from CMP0091). \n  - `/fsanitize=address` present.\n  - **`/RTC1` is NOT present** — our string(REPLACE) worked!\n  - `/DCOMMON_STATIC` present — the PUBLIC define propagated to the library itself.\n  \n- Line 26: link for t1_c: `link.exe /nologo ... /out:t1_dist_matrix_c\\main.exe /implib:main.lib /pdb:... /debug /INCREMENTAL /subsystem:console /DEBUG common\\common_c_static.lib ...`. \n  - Interesting: `/INCREMENTAL` is present! CMake default adds `/INCREMENTAL` in Debug. ASan docs say incompatible with incremental linking. But build succeeded... Actually /INCREMENTAL with ASan — MSVC ASan docs list incremental linking as incompatible. Hmm, but the build worked and there's `common_c_static.lib` linked. Wait, there's also `/implib:main.lib` — that creates an import library for the exe even though it's static? That's normal CMake behavior (it always passes /IMPLIB). Actually `/implib:main.lib` — creates main.lib import library. Hmm, but main.exe isn't a DLL. CMake passes /IMPLIB anyway? Odd but harmless.\n  - `/DEBUG` appears once from... line 26: `/debug /INCREMENTAL /subsystem:console /DEBUG` — /debug twice (once from CMake default LINK_FLAGS_DEBUG which includes /DEBUG, once from our genex). Wait, CMake default link flags for MSVC Debug include /DEBUG. And we added /DEBUG in genex. Duplicate. Harmless.\n  - `/INCREMENTAL` — this is CMake default for Debug. ASan incompatible with /INCREMENTAL per docs. But it built. Hmm. Actually the incompatibility means ASan might not work correctly with incremental linking. This is worth noting! Actually, MSVC's vs_link_exe wrapper is used. Let me note it as a concern to verify (does ASan actually catch errors in this build?).\n\nWait — actually let me reconsider. The ASan doc said \"isn't compatible with edit-and-continue, incremental linking, and /RTC\". /INCREMENTAL is present. So potentially ASan may not function. This is worth flagging as something to verify. But actually, the make build used link flags `/DEBUG` only (no /INCREMENTAL). So make build didn't have /INCREMENTAL. CMake adds it. This is a divergence AND a potential ASan problem.\n\nHmm, but did the build actually run? The log shows all 6 steps completed (1-6). And it seems successful (no errors). But does ASan actually instrument? /fsanitize=address is present in compile. The /INCREMENTAL might cause ASan to not work or work incorrectly. Actually in practice, MSVC ASan + /INCREMENTAL — I believe MSVC handles it or it's a known limitation. Let me note it as a verification point rather than assert.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider the /INCREMENTAL. Modern MSVC ASan documentation: The incompatible options listed are /RTC1, /ZI (edit-and-continue), /INCREMENTAL. If /INCREMENTAL is on with ASan... I recall that in recent MSVC, ASan forces /INCREMENTAL off or it just works. Not sure. I'll flag it as \"verify ASan actually catches errors in cmake build — /INCREMENTAL is present which docs list as incompatible\".\n\nActually, let me check whether CMake added /INCREMENTAL or our genex. Line 26 link cmd: `/debug /INCREMENTAL /subsystem:console /DEBUG`. Our genex adds `/DEBUG /OPT:REF /OPT:ICF` for release, `/DEBUG` for debug. So `/DEBUG` appears from both CMake default and our genex. `/INCREMENTAL` is CMake's default for Debug link. So to remove it, we'd set CMAKE_EXE_LINKER_FLAGS_DEBUG or per-target. Hmm. Actually, is /INCREMENTAL really bad with ASan? Let me think... The ASan MSVC implementation links against a runtime that's not compatible with incremental linking because incremental linking changes COMDAT handling. In practice, I believe ASan + /INCREMENTAL is silently accepted but the linker may warn or the ASan may not work. This needs verification: run a test that triggers ASan (e.g., the t1_nul? no. Actually the existing test suite has ASan-triggering cases? t2 had heap-use-after-free historically... but we're testing t1 now). \n\nHmm, for the parity check on t1 specifically, ASan won't trigger on t1's normal/error cases (no memory bugs). So parity check passes regardless. But the /INCREMENTAL-ASan interaction matters for the broader Debug+ASan requirement. Worth noting for the next steps.\n\nActually, let me reconsider whether to even raise this. The key question the user asked: \"проверь скрипт на корректность\". So the focus is the script. I should:\n1. Report the main finding: t1_c never tested (variables defined but unused) — script only checks C++.\n2. Confirm the exit-code comparison logic is sound.\n3. Note the minor stderr redirect omission.\n4. Report build log findings: /RTC1 gone (good), /std:c++latest emitted (the uncertainty resolved — MSVC 14.43 → /std:c++latest, not /std:c++23; matches Makefile), /Zi duplicated, /INCREMENTAL present (ASan concern to verify), -MDd present.\n\nWait, line 27: C++ compile for common_cpp_static: `/TP -DCOMMON_STATIC /DWIN32 /D_WINDOWS /EHsc /Zi /Ob0 /Od -std:c++latest -MDd /EHsc /W4 /permissive- /Zi /fsanitize=address`. \n- `-std:c++latest` — CMake chose /std:c++latest (not /std:c++23) for this MSVC. This resolves our uncertainty: MSVC 14.43, CMake 3.30 emits c++latest. Actually interesting — CMake usually maps CXX_STANDARD 23 to /std:c++latest unless the compiler is newer. Actually MSVC 14.43 supports /std:c++23 but CMake might still pick latest. Either way, it's what Makefile used (c++latest), so parity. Good.\n- `/EHsc` twice (CMake default + our genex). Harmless.\n- Note: NO `/D_COMMON_STATIC`? Wait it shows `-DCOMMON_STATIC`. Yes present.\n\nLine 144: link t1_cpp exe. Same pattern.\n\nSo build is essentially correct. The parity script has the t1_c gap.\n\nAlso: one more script correctness point. The `%last_errlevel%` captured from cmake run — if cmake binary somehow exits 0 when it should exit nonzero, and make also exits 0 (both \"agree\"), the parity passes but both are wrong vs the spec. This is inherent to parity testing — it compares against make, not against the spec. The plan's parity check compared codes between the two builds AND output vs expected for normal data. Here test1 compares outputs between make and cmake builds (both to temp files), not against tests/expect/t1_test1! The Makefile's t1_cpp_norm test compared against tests/expect/t1_test1. This script compares cmake output vs make output instead. For parity that's the point, but it means if BOTH are wrong identically, it passes. Better: compare cmake output against tests/expect/t1_test1 directly. Worth suggesting.\n\nActually the user said \"скрипт проверяет равенство кодов от прогона Make и от CMake\". So the intent is parity (make vs cmake). For exit codes, parity is what's asked. For normal data, comparing outputs make-vs-cmake is parity; comparing against expected would be correctness. Both are valid for the stage; but I should note that comparing against tests/expect would also verify the programs are still correct, not just identical.\n\nHmm, but per the course rules §4.9 п.3: \"Эталон считает независимая референс-реализация — не прогон тестируемой программы, иначе тест сравнивает её саму с собой.\" Parity here compares cmake-built program vs make-built program — two builds of the SAME program. That's closer to \"comparing it with itself\" which the course warns against! But the purpose of the parity check IS to verify the two build systems produce equivalent results, which is legitimate for a migration. However, the reference for the cmake build should ideally still be the independent expected values. I should note this nuance: for the migration, make-built output is a reasonable \"reference\" since make was already validated, but the cleanest is to also check cmake output against tests/expect.\n\nLet me now also double check the script's use of `%run_case%` — run_case takes command + expected code. On line 23: `%run_case% \"%t1_cpp_make% < tests/input_data/t1_abc > nul 2> nul\" %last_errlevel%`. run_case runs the command via std::system (cmd), gets exit code, compares to expected. The command string uses forward slashes for path: `build\\debug\\t1_dist_matrix_cpp\\main.exe` — wait, `%t1_cpp_make%` = `build\\debug\\t1_dist_matrix_cpp\\main.exe` (backslashes). Inside cmd, `< tests/input_data/t1_abc` with forward slashes — cmd handles forward slashes in redirect fine? Generally yes on modern Windows. The Makefile used WCMD to convert to backslashes. Here paths in the command string: `build\\debug\\...\\main.exe < tests/input_data/t1_abc > nul 2> nul`. cmd: backslash path for exe, forward slash for input file. `< tests/input_data/t1_abc` — cmd accepts forward slashes in most redirection contexts. Probably works (user says it returns all equal). OK.\n\nNow, `%last_errlevel%` could be empty if errorlevel was not set — no, it's set from the cmake run which always sets it.\n\nOne more potential issue: line 22 `set last_errlevel=%errorlevel%` — `%errorlevel%` expands at parse time of that line. Each line in batch is parsed when reached (outside blocks), so it gets the value from line 21. Correct.\n\nBut CRITICAL subtlety: In cmd, `%errorlevel%` inside a batch file is expanded when the LINE is parsed. Since line 21 and line 22 are separate lines, this works. BUT the script is not inside parentheses blocks, so it's fine.\n\nNow, let me verify the `if errorlevel 1` with errorlevel semantics and the `&` behavior with a direct errorlevel test, to be thorough.\n\nLet me run: `cmd /c \"cmd /c exit /b 5 & if errorlevel 1 echo HAS_ERR & echo ALWAYS_IF_INSIDE\"` — hmm this gets confusing. Let me instead create a clean test:\n`cmd /c \"exit /b 0 & if errorlevel 1 echo BAD1 & echo BAD2\"` and `cmd /c \"exit /b 2 & if errorlevel 1 echo GOOD1 & echo GOOD2\"`.\n\nWait, `exit /b 0 & if errorlevel 1 ...` — the `&` chains: exit runs, then if checks errorlevel. exit /b 0 sets errorlevel 0. Then `if errorlevel 1 echo BAD1 & echo BAD2` — if errorlevel 1 false → nothing? or echo BAD2 (outside)? Per earlier test, `& echo` inside if body. So expect nothing. Let me run to confirm.\n\nActually my earlier test already confirmed the `&` is inside the if. I'll run one errorlevel-specific test for completeness.\n\nLet me do that.",
