          "text": "Let me analyze the cmd behavior carefully.\n\nTest 1: `cmd /c \"exit /b 3 & if errorlevel 1 echo FIRED_TRUE & echo TAIL_TRUE\"` → output \"FIRED_TRUE\" and \"TAIL_TRUE\". So `echo TAIL_TRUE` ran even after `if errorlevel 1 echo FIRED_TRUE` was true. So `& echo TAIL` runs.\n\nWait, but the first test earlier: `cmd /c \"if 1==2 echo SHOULD_NOT_PRINT & echo AFTER_AMP\"` → output nothing (neither SHOULD_NOT_PRINT nor AFTER_AMP). Hmm, that contradicts.\n\nLet me re-examine. First test earlier:\n```\ncmd /c \"if 1==2 echo SHOULD_NOT_PRINT & echo AFTER_AMP\"\n```\nOutput: nothing. So `echo AFTER_AMP` did NOT run when condition false. That means `& echo AFTER_AMP` is part of the if body? No wait...\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The second batch file test output was empty too:\n```\n=== batch file test ===\n```\nAnd the batch file was:\n```\n@echo off\ncmd /c exit /b 0\nif errorlevel 1 echo NO_FIRE & echo TAIL_WHEN_FALSE\n```\nOutput: nothing. So with errorlevel 0, `echo TAIL_WHEN_FALSE` did NOT run. So `& echo TAIL_WHEN_FALSE` is INSIDE the if body.\n\nBut then in the latest test:\n```\ncmd /c \"cmd /c exit /b 3 & if errorlevel 1 echo FIRED_TRUE & echo TAIL_TRUE\"\n```\nOutput: FIRED_TRUE and TAIL_TRUE. With errorlevel 3 (true), both echo run. That's consistent with `&` being inside the if body.\n\nAnd:\n```\ncmd /c \"cmd /c exit /b 0 & if errorlevel 1 echo FIRED_FALSE & echo TAIL_FALSE\"\n```\nOutput: nothing. With errorlevel 0 (false), neither runs. Consistent.\n\nSo the behavior: in cmd, `if errorlevel 1 echo X & echo Y` — the `& echo Y` is part of the if body? That seems to contradict common knowledge that `&` separates commands regardless of if. But empirically here, when the condition is false, `& echo AFTER_AMP` does NOT run.\n\nWait, let me reconsider. Actually the earlier test:\n```\ncmd /c \"if 1==2 echo SHOULD_NOT_PRINT & echo AFTER_AMP\"\n```\nOutput was empty (just `---exit: 0`). Hmm. And:\n```\ncmd /c \"if 1==1 echo CONDITION_TRUE & echo AFTER_AMP\"\n```\nOutput: CONDITION_TRUE and AFTER_AMP.\n\nSo indeed, when condition true, both run; when false, neither runs. So `& echo AFTER_AMP` is part of the `if` body in cmd parsing.\n\nHmm, that's actually a subtle cmd quirk: `if` takes the command up to... Actually no. The known cmd behavior is that `if condition command1 & command2` — the `& command2` is part of the if body! Let me recall... Actually, I recall now that this IS the correct behavior: `if` in cmd extends its command to the end of the line (when not parenthesized), so `&` after it becomes part of the if body. This is a classic gotcha the OTHER way: people expect `& command2` to run always, but it doesn't.\n\nWait, no. Let me think again. Actually the classic gotcha is: `if exist file echo found & exit /b 1` — here `& exit /b 1`... hmm. Actually there's a well-known difference between cmd's `if` and other constructs.\n\nActually the empirical result is clear: `if 1==2 echo A & echo B` printed NOTHING. So `echo B` did not run when the condition was false. This means `& echo B` is part of the `if` command body in cmd.\n\nOK so for the script: `if errorlevel 1 echo t1_cpp abc ������ ������ ������ & exit /b 1`\n- If run_case returns errorlevel >= 1 (i.e., mismatch), echo AND exit /b 1 run.\n- If errorlevel is 0 (match), neither runs — script continues.\n\nThat's actually the DESIRED behavior! So the script's `& exit /b 1` is fine — it only exits when errorlevel >= 1. Good. So no bug there. Actually wait — this contradicts my initial concern. Let me re-verify with the exact pattern.\n\nActually, hold on. There's a subtlety: `echo` returns 0. In the line `if errorlevel 1 echo A & exit /b 1`:\n- condition true (errorlevel>=1): echo A runs (sets errorlevel 0), then `exit /b 1` runs. Exit code 1. Good.\n- condition false (errorlevel=0): nothing runs. Script continues. Good.\n\nSo the pattern works as intended. \n\nBut wait — the earlier test `cmd /c \"if 1==2 echo SHOULD_NOT_PRINT & echo AFTER_AMP\"` output nothing. This confirms `& echo AFTER_AMP` is inside the if body. So in this script, `& exit /b 1` is inside the if body, and only executes on failure. \n\nSo the `&` behavior is actually fine for the script's intent.\n\nNow, there are real issues in the script to flag. Let me go through carefully.\n\nScript `check_eq_make_cmake.bat`:\n\n```bat\n:: проверка кода для make и cmake версии на одинаковых входных данных\n@echo off\nchcp 1251 >nul\n\nset t1_cpp_make=build\\debug\\t1_dist_matrix_cpp\\main.exe\nset t1_c_make=build\\debug\\t1_dist_matrix_c\\main.exe\nset t1_cpp_cmake=build\\cmake_debug\\t1_dist_matrix_cpp\\main.exe\nset t1_c_cmake=build\\cmake_debug\\t1_dist_matrix_c\\main.exe\n\n:: test-утилиты\nset run_case=build\\debug\\tools\\run_case.exe\nset check=build\\debug\\tools\\check.exe\n\n:: отладка\n::echo abc | %t1_cpp_make% > nul 2> nul\n::echo t1_cpp_make errorlevel: %errorlevel%\n::echo abc | %t1_cpp_cmake% > nul 2> nul\n::echo t1_cpp_cmake errorlevel: %errorlevel%\n\n%t1_cpp_cmake% < tests/input_data/t1_abc > nul 2> nul\nset last_errlevel=%errorlevel%\n%run_case% \"%t1_cpp_make% < tests/input_data/t1_abc > nul 2> nul\" %last_errlevel%\nif errorlevel 1 echo t1_cpp abc ������ ������ ������ & exit /b 1\n```\n\nIssues:\n\n1. **The core logic**: It runs the CMAKE binary on the input, captures its exit code as `last_errlevel`. Then runs `run_case` on the MAKE binary with the expected code = last_errlevel (cmake's exit code). If they differ, run_case returns 1 → script errors.\n\n   This is a parity check: cmake-built exit code vs make-built exit code. It's the reverse of what I'd have done (run make first, check cmake matches), but logically equivalent: it checks the two builds produce the same exit code. \n\n   Wait, but there's a subtle issue: `%errorlevel%` capture. Line: `%t1_cpp_cmake% < ... > nul 2> nul` then next line `set last_errlevel=%errorlevel%`. In a batch file, `%errorlevel%` is expanded at parse time of the line. Since each line is parsed separately (not in a block), line 22 `set last_errlevel=%errorlevel%` is parsed AFTER line 21 executed. So `%errorlevel%` reflects the exit code of the cmake run. Good — this works because they're separate lines, not in a parenthesized block.\n\n2. **Potential issue: the script only tests t1_cpp, not t1_c!** Looking at the whole script: it defines `t1_c_make` and `t1_c_cmake` variables but NEVER uses them! Only t1_cpp is tested. So the parity check for the C version is missing. That's a real gap — the C build (`t1_dist_matrix_c`) is not parity-checked at all.\n\n3. **The norm test**: \n   ```\n   if not exist build mkdir build\n   if not exist build\\temp mkdir build\\temp\n   %t1_cpp_cmake% < tests/input_data/t1_test1 > build/temp/t1_cpp_cmake_test1 2> nul\n   set last_errlevel=%errorlevel%\n   %run_case% \"%t1_cpp_make% < tests/input_data/t1_test1 > build/temp/t1_cpp_make_test1\" %last_errlevel%\n   if errorlevel 1 echo ... & exit /b 1\n   %check% --equal build/temp/t1_cpp_cmake_test1 build/temp/t1_cpp_make_test1\n   if errorlevel 1 echo ... & exit /b 1 \n   ```\n   - It writes cmake output to build/temp/t1_cpp_cmake_test1, then run_case runs make binary writing to build/temp/t1_cpp_make_test1 (note: no `2> nul` for make here — its stderr goes to console, minor). Then compares with check --equal. That's fine, checks stdout equality.\n\n   Wait — there's a subtle issue with paths: `> build/temp/t1_cpp_cmake_test1` uses forward slashes. In cmd, redirect with forward slash path — cmd generally handles forward slashes in redirect paths? Actually cmd can have issues with forward slashes in some contexts, but for redirection it usually works. The Makefile used WCMD to convert to backslashes for a reason. But since the script reportedly worked (user says it outputs all equal), forward slashes in redirect apparently work here. Still worth noting as fragile.\n\n   - `set last_errlevel=%errorlevel%` after cmake norm run: should be 0 for normal data. Then run_case checks make binary returns 0. OK.\n\n4. **The `mkdir build`/`mkdir build\\temp` placement**: These are AFTER the exit-code tests (which don't need temp), right before the norm test. OK, they're in the right place.\n\n5. **Encoding of messages**: The echo messages contain Russian text. The .bat is in CP1251 (matches repo). `chcp 1251` sets console to 1251. Fine.\n\n6. **`exit /b 1` with `&`**: As determined, `& exit /b 1` is part of the `if` body, so only runs on errorlevel>=1. Good. BUT — wait, actually I need to double check this once more, because my earlier test was flawed. Let me reconsider.\n\nEarlier test: `cmd /c \"if 1==2 echo SHOULD_NOT_PRINT & echo AFTER_AMP\"` → output empty. So `echo AFTER_AMP` did not run. This means `& echo AFTER_AMP` is inside the if body.\n\nHmm, but actually there's a known cmd gotcha that the OPPOSITE is true: `if condition command1 & command2` — command2 runs regardless. Let me test more carefully because this is crucial.\n\nActually wait, the test I ran was conclusive: `if 1==2 echo SHOULD_NOT_PRINT & echo AFTER_AMP` printed NOTHING. And `if 1==1 echo CONDITION_TRUE & echo AFTER_AMP` printed BOTH. So `& echo AFTER_AMP` is conditional. Hmm, that contradicts the classic gotcha.\n\nActually, I recall now: the classic gotcha is about `&&` vs `&`? No. Let me think... The real cmd rule: `if` takes the entire rest of the line as its command when... no.\n\nActually, I believe the truth is: cmd's `if condition command` — the `command` extends to the end of the line, and `&` inside it is part of the command? That contradicts what I said earlier about `&` separating. Hmm.\n\nLet me just trust the empirical test. The empirical results:\n- `if 1==2 echo A & echo B` → nothing\n- `if 1==1 echo A & echo B` → A, B\n\nSo the `& echo B` is part of the if body. This is actually the real cmd behavior: `if` conditionally executes the whole remainder of the line (as multiple commands joined by `&`). \n\nHmm wait, but that's weird because I'm pretty sure `if exist file echo found & exit /b 0` — if file doesn't exist, `exit /b 0` wouldn't run. And indeed many people get bitten by the OPPOSITE: they expect `&` to be conditional but it isn't... \n\nUgh, let me just re-test explicitly once more to be 100% sure, because the correctness of the script depends on it. Actually I already ran it twice with consistent results. Let me trust: `&` after `if condition command` IS part of the conditional body in this cmd. \n\nHmm, wait — actually no! I think I need to recheck. Let me look at the FIRST test output again:\n\n```\ncmd /c \"if 1==2 echo SHOULD_NOT_PRINT & echo AFTER_AMP\"; Write-Output \"---exit: $LASTEXITCODE\"\n```\nOutput: `---exit: 0`\n\nSo nothing printed. OK.\n\n```\ncmd /c \"if 1==1 echo CONDITION_TRUE & echo AFTER_AMP\"; Write-Output \"---exit: $LASTEXITCODE\"\n```\nOutput: `CONDITION_TRUE`, `AFTER_AMP`, `---exit: 0`\n\nSo both printed.\n\nThis is conclusive: `& echo AFTER_AMP` runs only when the if condition is true. So `if errorlevel 1 echo msg & exit /b 1` runs `exit /b 1` only when errorlevel >= 1. \n\nWait, but that means the classic gotcha I remembered is wrong? Actually no — I think the classic gotcha is different. The real gotcha: `if %var%==1 echo a & echo b` — `%var%` is expanded at PARSE time, and `&` separates... no. \n\nActually the correct understanding per Microsoft's own docs and per cmd behavior: `IF [NOT] ERRORLEVEL number command` — the command is the rest of the line. When you write `if cond cmd1 & cmd2`, cmd parses the ENTIRE line and the `&` makes cmd1 & cmd2 both part of... \n\nHmm, I've seen conflicting info. Let me just run one more decisive test with errorlevel to settle the script's behavior definitively. Actually, I already did:\n- `cmd /c \"cmd /c exit /b 3 & if errorlevel 1 echo FIRED_TRUE & echo TAIL_TRUE\"` → FIRED_TRUE, TAIL_TRUE\n- `cmd /c \"cmd /c exit /b 0 & if errorlevel 1 echo FIRED_FALSE & echo TAIL_FALSE\"` → nothing\n\nSo errorlevel=0 (false): nothing runs. errorlevel=3 (true): both run. Confirmed: `& echo TAIL` is conditional.\n\nGood — so the script's `if errorlevel 1 echo ... & exit /b 1` works correctly: on failure echo + exit, on success continue.\n\nWait, but this is actually surprising and I want to make sure the user understands this correctly. Actually, hold on. Let me reconsider whether cmd behaves this way. There IS a well-known gotcha documented as: `if 1==1 echo one & echo two` prints both, but `if 1==2 echo one & echo two` prints NOTHING — meaning `& echo two` is part of the if. And the gotcha is people WHO WANT it to always run. Yes, this is a real known behavior: cmd's `if` command consumes the whole rest of the line including `&`-joined commands.\n\nHmm, actually no. I recall now more clearly: The cmd behavior IS that `&` joins the commands and the `if` body extends through `&`. This is why the standard advice is to use parentheses `( )` for multi-command if bodies. OK. So the script is CORRECT.\n\nOK so now the real findings for the script:\n\n**Finding 1 (real gap): t1_c is never parity-tested.** Variables `t1_c_make` and `t1_c_cmake` are defined but unused. Only t1_cpp is checked. The C build parity is not verified. This is the biggest issue.\n\n**Finding 2 (minor): forward slashes in redirect paths.** `> build/temp/...`, `< tests/input_data/...` use forward slashes. cmd's redirection with forward slashes generally works but is fragile (Makefile converted to backslashes via WCMD deliberately). Since it works in practice, it's a note, not a blocker.\n\nActually wait — in the run_case command string, the path is `%t1_cpp_make%` which is `build\\debug\\t1_dist_matrix_cpp\\main.exe` (backslashes). And `< tests/input_data/t1_abc` — forward slashes for input. Inside run_case's std::system(cmd), the `<` redirect with forward slash path — cmd handles `< file` where path uses forward slashes? Usually yes for file redirection. OK.\n\n**Finding 3 (minor): the norm test doesn't redirect stderr for the make binary.** `%run_case% \"%t1_cpp_make% < tests/input_data/t1_test1 > build/temp/t1_cpp_make_test1\"` — no `2> nul`, so any stderr from the make binary (should be none for normal data) leaks to console. Cosmetic.\n\n**Finding 4 (design consideration): the parity test direction.** It runs cmake first, captures code, then asserts make matches. This validates \"cmake behavior == make behavior\" but with cmake as the reference. Since make was already validated in layer 15 (tests/expect, exit codes), the trusted reference should arguably be make (or the spec constants 64/65/66/0), and cmake should be checked against it. The current approach uses cmake's exit code as the expected. If cmake has a bug, this test would happily pass (both wrong identically) — but that's the nature of parity testing; the absolute correctness comes from the make test suite. It would be stronger to assert cmake against the known expected codes directly. This is worth noting: parity ≠ correctness. But for \"does cmake build behave same as make build\", it's valid.\n\n**Finding 5: no check of stderr content in parity.** For the norm case, only stdout is compared. For exit-code cases, stderr is discarded (`2> nul`). If cmake build printed different diagnostics to stderr (e.g., different message text), the parity script wouldn't catch it. The Makefile's test suite checks stderr text via `--contains` for error cases. So parity of stderr is unverified. This is a gap worth noting, though for t1 the Makefile's error-case tests mostly check exit codes + some --contains. Actually looking at Makefile t1 tests: t1_abc, t1_float are exit-code only (via run_case with DATA code); t1_low/high exit-code; t1_nul exit-code; t1_norm is equal + exit 0. So t1 parity in the script matches the t1 test structure (exit codes + norm equality). Fine. But the script drops stderr checking — acceptable for parity since the exit code parity + stdout equality is the main signal.\n\n**Finding 6: `if errorlevel 1 echo ... & exit /b 1`** — works correctly as verified. But note: `errorlevel` here refers to run_case's exit code, which is 1 on mismatch. Good.\n\n**Finding 7: encoding of the .bat file.** The file is CP1251 (garbled in my UTF-8 read). That's consistent with repo convention. The `chcp 1251` handles console display. Fine.\n\n**Finding 8: The `echo ������ �������` at the end** — success message. And the script returns 0 on success (last command echo returns 0). Good, honest exit code. On failure, `exit /b 1`. Good.\n\nWait — is there an issue that the success path doesn't explicitly return 0? The last line `echo ������ �������` returns 0. Fine.\n\n**Finding 9 (minor): the commented-out debug block at top** — leftover debug, per §4.1 should be removed before commit. Not a bug but a self-review item.\n\n**Finding 10: missing norm test for t1_c** — same as Finding 1, the C version has no norm test either.\n\nNow the build log analysis:\n\nFrom log.txt, the commands:\n1. C compile of geometry.c: `cl /nologo -DCOMMON_STATIC /DWIN32 /D_WINDOWS /Zi /Ob0 /Od -std:c17 -MDd /W4 /permissive- /Zi /fsanitize=address /showIncludes ...`\n   - Wait: `/Zi /Ob0 /Od` appears — where did /Zi come from at the start? CMake's default Debug flags for MSVC include `/Zi`? Actually CMake adds `/Zi` via CMAKE_MSVC_DEBUG_INFORMATION_FORMAT (default ProgramDatabase for Debug). So `/Zi` appears twice (once from CMake default, once from our genex). Harmless duplicate.\n   - `/Ob0` — CMake default debug includes `/Ob0` (no inlining). Fine.\n   - `/RTC1` is GONE — our string(REPLACE) worked! Great.\n   - `-std:c17` — CMake mapped C_STANDARD 17 → `/std:c17`. Good.\n   - `/MDd` — from CMP0091 runtime lib abstraction. Good.\n   - `/W4 /permissive-` — our flags. Good.\n   - `/fsanitize=address` — our genex. Good.\n   - `/DWIN32 /D_WINDOWS` — CMake adds platform defines automatically. These are new vs Makefile (Makefile didn't have them explicitly but cl adds WIN32 defines? Actually /DWIN32 is CMake default; harmless).\n   - `-DCOMMON_STATIC` — our PUBLIC define. Good.\n\n2. lib.exe creates common_c_static.lib. Good.\n\n3. Link t1_c: `link.exe ... main.c.obj /out:t1_dist_matrix_c\\main.exe /implib:main.lib /pdb:t1_dist_matrix_c\\main.pdb ... /debug /INCREMENTAL /subsystem:console /DEBUG common\\common_c_static.lib kernel32.lib ...`\n   - Interesting: `/implib:main.lib` — it's creating an import library named main.lib for the exe? That's odd. For an executable, CMake passes /IMPLIB? Actually CMake passes /IMPLIB even for exes in some cases? Hmm, that creates a `main.lib` in the build dir. That's... unusual but harmless. Actually wait — `/implib:main.lib` — this generates main.lib (import lib) alongside. For a plain exe this is unnecessary. Where does it come from? Possibly CMake always passes /IMPLIB on Windows for targets. Actually it might be from `vs_link_exe` wrapper. It's harmless (gitignored *.lib).\n   - `/debug /INCREMENTAL /subsystem:console /DEBUG` — `/DEBUG` twice (once CMake default debug link flags, once our genex). Duplicate, harmless. `/INCREMENTAL` — CMake adds /INCREMENTAL by default for Debug. ASan docs say ASan is incompatible with /INCREMENTAL! Hmm. The doc said: \"isn't compatible with edit-and-continue, incremental linking, and /RTC\". So /INCREMENTAL + ASan — this is a potential issue! But the build succeeded and tests pass. Actually, does /INCREMENTAL break ASan? The doc lists incremental linking as incompatible. This might be a real concern for Debug+ASan correctness. Worth flagging: CMake adds `/INCREMENTAL` in Debug; we may need to remove it (like /RTC1) for ASan compatibility. Hmm, but the user's tests passed... ASan might still work but be flagged. This is worth noting as a follow-up, not blocking.\n\n   Actually, wait — let me check: is /INCREMENTAL actually incompatible, or just not recommended? MSVC docs for ASan: \"it isn't compatible with edit-and-continue, incremental linking, and /RTC\". So /INCREMENTAL should be turned off for ASan. CMake's default Debug linker flags include /INCREMENTAL. So we have a real deviation from the Makefile (Makefile link flags: just `/DEBUG`). This is worth flagging as a potential issue to verify (does ASan still catch bugs?). But not a blocker for parity.\n\n   - `kernel32.lib user32.lib ...` — CMake adds default system libs. Fine.\n   - `/machine:x64 /version:0.0 /subsystem:console` — CMake defaults.\n\n4. C++ compile geometry.cpp: `cl /nologo /TP -DCOMMON_STATIC /DWIN32 /D_WINDOWS /EHsc /Zi /Ob0 /Od -std:c++latest -MDd /EHsc /W4 /permissive- /Zi /fsanitize=address`\n   - `/EHsc` appears twice (CMake default for C++ + our genex). Harmless.\n   - **`-std:c++latest`** — NOT `/std:c++23`! CMake mapped CXX_STANDARD 23 → `/std:c++latest` on this MSVC. Interesting — this resolves our earlier uncertainty: CMake emits `/std:c++latest` (not /std:c++23). This matches the Makefile which used /std:c++latest. So parity holds. Good — and it answers the \"which std flag\" question: on MSVC 14.43, CMake 3.30 emits c++latest.\n\n   Wait, actually — why does CMake emit /std:c++latest for CXX_STANDARD 23? Because MSVC's /std:c++23 might not be recognized by the CMake version, or CMake maps 23 to latest when the compiler's \"23\" support detection... Actually CMake checks compiler support: MSVC 14.43 supports /std:c++23 (since VS 2022 17.8). But CMake might map C++23 → /std:c++latest because it treats 23 as \"not fully standardized\" → uses latest. Either way, result = c++latest = matches Makefile. Fine.\n\n5. lib.exe common_cpp_static.lib. Fine.\n\n6. Link t1_cpp: same as t1_c but with common_cpp_static.lib.\n\nSo the build log shows flags are largely correct: /RTC1 removed ✓, ASan ✓, /W4 /permissive- ✓, /std:c++latest ✓ (matches make), /MDd ✓, /DCOMMON_STATIC ✓, static libs built ✓, both exes built ✓.\n\nNotable deviations from Makefile (worth noting, mostly harmless):\n- `/INCREMENTAL` in Debug link (CMake default) — ASan docs list incremental linking as incompatible. **Potential issue to verify.**\n- `/Zi` duplicated, `/EHsc` duplicated, `/DEBUG` duplicated — harmless duplicates from CMake defaults overlapping our genex.\n- `/implib:main.lib` for exe — harmless.\n- `/DWIN32 /D_WINDOWS`, `/Ob0`, `/showIncludes`, `/FS`, `/Fd`, default system libs — CMake machinery, fine.\n- `/nologo` — fine.\n\nAlso: the exe output. Log shows `/out:t1_dist_matrix_c\\main.exe` relative to build/cmake_debug → build/cmake_debug/t1_dist_matrix_c\\main.exe. Matches what the parity script expects (`build\\cmake_debug\\t1_dist_matrix_c\\main.exe`). Good. And the build dir listing confirms both exes exist.\n\nWait, one important thing about the exe extension: on the exe the log shows `/out:t1_dist_matrix_c\\main.exe` — so OUTPUT_NAME main + exe extension added. Good.\n\nAnother thing: the build directory listing shows `main.lib`? No, I don't see main.lib in the listing. Hmm, the link line had `/implib:main.lib` — but no main.lib in the build listing. Maybe it was removed or it's elsewhere. Actually the listing shows no main.lib. Odd but not important.\n\nNow — the parity script: it compares cmake-built exe's exit code against make-built exe's exit code. But there's a subtle issue: **the make-built exe path** — `build\\debug\\t1_dist_matrix_cpp\\main.exe`. Does that exist? The user has been building with make, so build/debug exists. The parity script relies on it. OK.\n\nBut wait — there's a real concern about **the norm test comparing cmake stdout vs make stdout** — but not against `tests/expect/t1_test1`. The Makefile's t1_cpp_norm test compared against tests/expect/t1_test1. The parity script compares make-vs-cmake output (parity), which is the point. But it doesn't validate cmake against the reference. For a true parity+congruence check, could also compare against tests/expect. Optional improvement.\n\nAlso: **the script writes cmake output to `build/temp/t1_cpp_cmake_test1` with forward slashes in redirect** — cmd redirect to a forward-slash path. Works? The user says the script \"вроде выдает что все выходы