е записи, /FS вызывает конфликткы, верно?\"\n\n/FS = Force synchronous PDB writes. It's a compiler option that makes cl.exe write PDB data synchronously (wait for mspdbsrv to finish writing) instead of async. It's used to avoid PDB corruption when multiple cl processes compile in parallel and write to the same PDB. \n\nWait — the user's history: on 2026-08-26 they had the vc140.pdb conflict issue with make -j8, and chose /Fd (per-object PDB) to avoid conflicts, rejecting /FS. Now CMake adds /FS by default. \n\nCMake adds /FS by default for Ninja generator with MSVC? Actually yes: CMake adds `/FS` (Force Synchronous PDB Writes) when the generator is Ninja and compilation is parallel — actually CMake adds /FS when MSVC and the build tool can run in parallel? Let me recall. CMake's MSVC flags: CMake adds `/FS` to CMAKE_<LANG>_FLAGS when using a generator that can compile in parallel and MSVC version supports it? Actually I recall CMake adds `/FS` by default in `CMAKE_C_FLAGS` for MSVC since 3.x? Hmm.\n\nActually, /FS makes PDB writes SYNCHRONOUS — it serializes them through mspdbsrv, so parallel compilers don't corrupt a shared PDB. So /FS is exactly the fix for the vc140.pdb parallel conflict, NOT a cause. Wait, but user's journal said \"с /FS при первом прогоне: warning LNK4099 PDB... компоновка объекта выполняется как при отсутствии отладочных данных\" — that was a different problem (PDB timing window between obj ready and pdb ready).\n\nThe user's question: \"мне не нужны принудительные параллельные записи, /FS вызывает конфликты, верно?\" — This is a misconception to correct. /FS = \"Force Synchronous PDB Writes\" — it does NOT force parallel writes; it forces SYNCHRONOUS (serialized) writes. It's precisely to prevent conflicts. Without /FS, async writes can conflict. So /FS is not the cause of conflicts — it's the cure.\n\nBut wait — in CMake, does /FS come from CMake's default or from our flags? Looking at the log line 4: `/Zi /Ob0 /Od -std:c17 -MDd /W4 /permissive- /Zi /fsanitize=address /showIncludes /Focommon\\... /Fdcommon\\... /FS -c`. \n\n/FS appears. Where does it come from? CMake adds `/FS` by default for MSVC compilers in newer versions (it's in CMAKE_C_FLAGS default? Actually CMake adds /FS to the default flags when MSVC). Hmm, I believe CMake added /FS to default MSVC flags in some version to make parallel builds safe. Actually I recall CMake does NOT add /FS by default; but Ninja generator... hmm. Let me verify. Actually there's `MSVC_DEBUG_INFORMATION_FORMAT` and CMake adds /FS when using Ninja.\n\nLet me check the actual CMake docs or generated flags. The log shows `/FS` present. CMake default for MSVC Debug: `CMAKE_C_FLAGS_DEBUG` default is `/D_DEBUG /MDd /Ob0 /Od /RTC1` (we replaced RTC1 out). CMake also has default `CMAKE_C_FLAGS` = `/DWIN32 /D_WINDOWS ... /FS`? Hmm. I think /FS is added by CMake for MSVC when the generator can do parallel builds (Ninja) — actually it's added when `CMAKE_MSVC_DEBUG_INFORMATION_FORMAT` is ProgramDatabase and... \n\nActually, I recall now: CMake adds `/FS` for MSVC compilers by default since CMake 3.15-ish? No. Let me just check: the flags in the log are `/DWIN32 /D_WINDOWS` (CMake platform default) then `/Zi /Ob0 /Od` (debug default) `-std:c17 -MDd` then ours `/W4 /permissive-` then `/Zi /fsanitize=address` (ours), then `/showIncludes /Fo... /Fd... /FS`. \n\nThe /FS at the end — could be from CMake default for Ninja generator. Actually I'm fairly confident: CMake adds `/FS` to the compiler flags when using the Ninja generator with MSVC, to prevent PDB corruption during parallel builds. Let me verify via docs rather than guess.\n\nActually, I should check: does /FS cause conflicts? The user's concern stems from their journal entry about LNK4099 warning with /FS. But /FS is generally safe and is the recommended setting for parallel MSVC builds. The conflicts they saw in make were because cl was writing to a SHARED vc140.pdb with /Zi and parallel -j8 — the classic fix is /Fd (separate PDBs per obj). /FS + /Fd together is fine.\n\nSo answer for /FS: /FS = Force Synchronous PDB writes. It does NOT create parallel writes — it serializes them. It prevents the very conflict you hit with make -j8. In CMake/Ninja it's added by default (I believe) to make parallel builds safe. It's not a problem. But verify via docs.\n\nActually, I want to be careful and accurate. Let me fetch the MSVC /FS documentation and the asan-known-issues to answer both questions accurately. That's the responsible approach.\n\nLet me fetch:\n1. https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/cpp/build/reference/fs-force-synchronous-pdb-writes\n2. https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/cpp/sanitizers/asan-known-issues (or asan-building)\n\nLet me do that.\n\nAlso, about the ASan not catching stack OOB: There's a documented MSVC ASan limitation. Let me recall the \"asan-known-issues\" page... I believe it mentions: \"stack-buffer-overflow detection: Instrumentation is only applied to stack variables whose address is taken\"? Or there's the known issue about `_chkstk`? \n\nActually, I now strongly recall: MSVC ASan instruments stack buffers, BUT there's a documented limitation that it doesn't always insert redzones between adjacent locals in optimized builds, and in `/Od` builds... hmm. Let me just fetch the docs.\n\nLet me fetch the known-issues page and the /FS page in parallel.",
