Form hypothesis. I have several.\n4. Test the hypothesis.\n\nBut I'm in plan mode — read-only. I can't run the exe to test. So I should propose a plan to investigate, and give my best assessment with confidence labels.\n\nLet me think about what the most likely cause is, and what the verification steps would be.\n\nActually — I DO now recall a concrete documented MSVC ASan limitation that fits perfectly: **MSVC ASan's stack instrumentation only poisons redzones around stack variables whose address is taken, OR it places redzones but with a granularity where overflows of a few bytes adjacent to the redzone boundary may be missed if the access falls within an 8-byte shadow granule that also covers addressable memory.**\n\nHmm, let me think about the shadow memory granularity. Shadow byte covers 8 address bytes. `lineN` is 32 bytes = 4 granules. Redzone follows. If redzone starts at byte 32, then byte 33 is in the granule [32..39]. If that granule is marked \"partially addressable\" (e.g., 0x00 = fully addressable, or a code meaning \"partially accessible\"), ASan checks the offset within the granule. Write at byte 33 = offset 1 in granule [32..39]. If the granule is marked addressable up to offset 0 (i.e., 32 is the last... no). Hmm, this is getting deep.\n\nActually — I recall the REAL documented MSVC behavior now, and I'm fairly confident: **MSVC ASan instruments stack accesses and DOES catch stack-buffer-overflow in many cases, but there's a documented issue that stack instrumentation in MSVC ASan is based on redzones placed ONLY at function entry/exit around locals that \"escape\" or are of certain sizes.** \n\nNo wait. Let me just be honest with confidence levels. The truth is I don't have a verified, documented reason in front of me. The right move is:\n\n1. State the FACTS (code is OOB, file was recompiled, ASan silent).\n2. Give my leading hypothesis with [ПРЕДПОЛОЖЕНИЕ, СРЕДНИЙ/НИЗКИЙ] confidence.\n3. Propose a verification plan (since plan mode) — how to test the hypothesis when we switch to execution.\n\nMy leading hypothesis: **MSVC ASan does not always poison the stack redzone right after a small local array; the redzone placement/granularity means a 1-2 byte overflow into the immediately-following padding isn't detected.** The standard way to verify: overflow by a LARGER amount (e.g., `lineN[33]` → try `lineN[200]` or write into `points` array via negative/positive big index), or heap-buffer-overflow test which ASan definitely catches. If a large stack overflow IS caught but small isn't → granularity hypothesis confirmed. If large stack overflow is ALSO silent → MSVC stack instrumentation is simply not effective here.\n\nActually — hmm, wait. Let me reconsider. There's another VERY important possibility I should check: **Is ASan actually linked into the binary?** From the log, the link step for t1_c was: `link.exe ... /debug /INCREMENTAL /subsystem:console /DEBUG common\\common_c_static.lib kernel32.lib ...`. I don't see ASan runtime libs explicitly, but MSVC ASan links automatically (per the docs: \"The AddressSanitizer libraries (.lib files) are linked for you automatically\"). Also `/INCREMENTAL` is present — and the known-issues page says incremental linking is UNSUPPORTED with ASan and should be disabled! \n\nTHIS is important! The log shows `/INCREMENTAL` in the link. Per the known-issues page I just fetched: \"**Incremental linking is unsupported, and should be disabled**\" when using /fsanitize=address. And `/INCREMENTAL` is there in the link line (log.txt line 26, log1.txt... well the link command isn't shown in log1.txt with flags, but log.txt showed `/INCREMENTAL`).\n\nSo there's a real potential problem: CMake's default Debug link flags include `/INCREMENTAL`, which is incompatible with ASan. This could break ASan entirely or partially. That's a strong candidate explanation — and it connects to something I already flagged earlier (\"CMake добавил /INCREMENTAL... надо проверить\").\n\nBut wait — does /INCREMENTAL actually disable ASan detection? The docs say \"unsupported, should be disabled.\" \"Unsupported\" doesn't necessarily mean \"silently disables all detection\" — it means not guaranteed to work. But it's a strong suspect.\n\nAlso, `/RTC` was removed (good, we did that). But there's ANOTHER thing: `Edit-and-Continue` — the docs mention /ZI (Edit and Continue) is incompatible. Wait, the ASan overview said incompatible with edit-and-continue (/ZI) and incremental linking and /RTC. We used `/Zi` (Program Database, NOT /ZI Edit-and-Continue) — good, /Zi is fine. But /INCREMENTAL is there.\n\nHmm, but actually — does MSVC ASan + /INCREMENTAL fail to catch stack overflows? I genuinely don't know the exact interaction. \n\nLet me also reconsider: maybe the simplest explanation is even more basic. Let me re-examine: is `lineN` maybe placed such that the write at index 33 lands on ANOTHER local variable (not in a redzone)? At /Od, locals are laid out in declaration order-ish. `lineN[32]`, then `size_t sz`, `char* endptr`, `long int N`, `struct Point points[20]`. Writing `lineN[33]` could clobber `sz` or padding — but MSVC ASan, IF it poisons stack, would poison redzones between locals. If ASan's stack redzone instrumentation isn't working (due to /INCREMENTAL or a limitation), the write just lands in adjacent memory and no check fires.\n\nOK. So my analysis:\n- The code IS OOB (FACT).\n- It WAS compiled into the binary (FACT).\n- ASan is silent (user observation).\n- Most likely causes, in order:\n  1. `/INCREMENTAL` in Debug link (CMake default) is incompatible with ASan per MSVC docs — could disable/break ASan detection. This is a documented incompatibility, and I already flagged it as a suspicion from log.txt. [ПРЕДПОЛОЖЕНИЕ, СРЕДНИЙ]\n  2. MSVC ASan stack instrumentation limitation — small local array overflow into adjacent padding may not be detected; MSVC stack redzone behavior differs from Clang. [ПРЕДПОЛОЖЕНИЕ, СРЕДНИЙ]\n\nActually, I want to verify hypothesis 1 more concretely. The known-issues page explicitly lists incremental linking as \"unsupported and should be disabled\". The safest verification: remove /INCREMENTAL (add `/INCREMENTAL:NO` to link options in Debug, or set per-target) and re-test. If ASan then fires → confirmed.\n\nAlso worth noting: I should double check whether ASan is even active. A quick positive-control test: heap-buffer-overflow (e.g., `malloc(4); arr[1]=5;` or use-after-free) — ASan definitely catches those on MSVC. If heap overflow IS caught but stack ISN'T → it's a stack-instrumentation-specific gap. This is a great control experiment.\n\nNow, about /FS: The user asks \"вижу /FS среди флагов. а мне не нужны принудительные параллельные записи, /FS вызывает конфликты, верно?\"\n\n/FS docs (fetched): \"Forces writes to the program database (PDB) file... to be serialized through MSPDBSRV.EXE.\" So /FS = Force SYNCHRONOUS PDB writes. It does NOT force parallel writes — quite the opposite: it SERIALIZES writes through mspdbsrv to avoid conflicts when multiple cl.exe processes write to the same PDB. \n\nThe user's memory from 2026-08-26: \"с /FS при первом прогоне: warning LNK4099 PDB... deepseek рассказал почему pdb может теряться\". That was about make with shared vc140.pdb. But the user concluded \"/FS вызывает конфликты\". Actually the docs say /FS PREVENTS conflicts (serializes access). The LNK4099 issue they saw was a different problem (PDB timing window between obj and pdb ready). \n\nSo the user's belief is INVERTED: /FS is what you WANT to avoid PDB conflicts in parallel builds. It serializes, doesn't parallelize. Without /FS, cl locks the PDB, and multiple cl accessing the same PDB can fail. With Ninja (parallel), /FS helps.\n\nWait, but here CMake added /FS. Why? Because CMake knows MSVC + Ninja parallel builds, so it adds /FS to make PDB writes safe. Actually, is /FS added by CMake or by the compiler default? The docs say \"/MP option enables /FS by default\". CMake with Ninja doesn't pass /MP... Actually, CMake does add /FS for MSVC in some generators. Hmm. Actually the /FS appears in the cl command: `/Zi /Ob0 /Od -std:c17 -MDd /W4 /permissive- /Zi /fsanitize=address /showIncludes /Fo... /Fd... /FS`. \n\nWhere does /FS come from? It's in CMake's default flags for MSVC? Actually I believe CMake adds /FS when using Ninja generator to enable safe parallel PDB writes. Or it might be from... hmm. Let me check — actually CMake's default `CMAKE_C_FLAGS` for MSVC includes `/DWIN32 /D_WINDOWS` and I believe it does NOT include /FS by default. But Ninja generator... Actually I recall CMake adds `/FS` when the generator is Ninja and MSVC, to support parallel builds safely.\n\nEither way, the answer to the user: /FS does NOT force parallel writes and does NOT cause conflicts — it's the OPPOSITE. It forces SYNCHRONOUS (serialized) PDB writes through MSPDBSRV to PREVENT the exact conflicts that multiple cl.exe processes can cause when sharing a PDB. It's added precisely because Ninja builds in parallel. So it's good and should stay, not be removed. The user's memory from the make era (LNK4099) was a different root cause (PDB timing, fixed later by /Fd).\n\nHmm wait, but actually there's a nuance: /FS \"may make builds significantly longer\" (docs). And \"it doesn't prevent all errors\". So it's a tradeoff but generally desirable with parallel MSVC builds. Since each target here has its own PDB (per-target /Fd), conflicts are less likely, but /FS is belt-and-suspenders. It's harmless and beneficial; not a source of conflicts.\n\nNow, actually — I realize I should double check whether the ASan issue relates to something in the compile flags for main.c. In log1.txt, the compile command for main.c.obj isn't shown with flags (the -v output shows \"Building C object\" + showIncludes lines, but the actual cl command line isn't in the log... wait, log.txt (first build) showed full cl command lines. log1.txt shows \"Building C object CMakeFiles\\t1_c.dir\\t1_dist_matrix_c\\main.c.obj\" then showIncludes. Hmm, log1.txt seems to be a different format — maybe `cmake --build --verbose` vs ninja -v. Actually log1.txt line 1: \"[1/8] Building C object ...\" — this is ninja's verbose-ish output but the cl command itself isn't echoed. So I can't see main.c's flags from log1.txt. But from log.txt (the first -v build), the C compile for geometry.c had: `/Zi /Ob0 /Od -std:c17 -MDd /W4 /permissive- /Zi /fsanitize=address /showIncludes /Fo... /Fd... /FS`. main.c would get the same flags.\n\nKey facts from log.txt compile: `/fsanitize=address` IS present on compile. Link (log.txt line 26): `link.exe ... /debug /INCREMENTAL /subsystem:console /DEBUG common\\common_c_static.lib ...` — /INCREMENTAL present, no explicit asan lib (auto-linked).\n\nSo ASan is compiled in. The question is whether it's functional at runtime. Given /INCREMENTAL incompatibility is documented, that's my leading suspect.\n\nActually — hold on. Let me reconsider whether /INCREMENTAL really breaks stack detection. The MSVC docs list it as \"unsupported... should be disabled\". I think the practical effect: with /INCREMENTAL, the linker may not set up properly for ASan's shadow, or the ASan runtime init conflicts. In practice, I've seen reports that ASan + /INCREMENTAL produces the error \"LNK4099\" or ASan just doesn't work. It's plausible.\n\nBut I can't be 100% sure without testing. So my answer should:\n1. Confirm the facts: yes, `lineN[33]` on `char lineN[32]` IS a stack out-of-bounds write. And main.c WAS recompiled (log1.txt [2/8]) and relinked ([4/8]) — so the bad write is in the exe.\n2. Explain ASan silence — not a definite single cause, give the leading hypothesis (/INCREMENTAL incompatibility, documented) + the stack-instrumentation granularity possibility, with confidence labels.\n3. Propose verification plan (execution phase): \n   a. Remove /INCREMENTAL from Debug link (add `/INCREMENTAL:NO`) → retest. If ASan fires → cause found.\n   b. Positive control: heap-buffer-overflow test (malloc OOB) → confirms ASan works at all.\n   c. Larger stack overflow test → distinguishes granularity.\n4. /FS: correct the misconception — /FS serializes, doesn't parallelize; prevents conflicts; keep it. Cite the doc.\n5. Also note: the `lineN[33]` test line needs to be REMOVED before commit (it's test code).\n\nAlso I should note: since I'm in plan mode, I can't run the tests. I present the plan.\n\nLet me also double check the ASan docs statement about stack-buffer-overflow being detected — the main asan.md lists \"Stack buffer overflow and underflow\" as error types. So MSVC ASan DOES support it in principle. This strengthens that something is wrong with the build config (like /INCREMENTAL) rather than \"MSVC can't do stack ASan at all\".\n\nLet me verify the /INCREMENTAL hypothesis source: the known-issues page explicitly says: \"**Incremental linking is unsupported, and should be disabled**\" — under \"Incompatible options and functionality\" for /fsanitize=address. And log.txt line 26 shows `/INCREMENTAL` in the link command. So we have a documented incompatibility AND the flag present in our build. This is a solid, documented lead. Confidence: [ПРЕДПОЛОЖЕНИЕ, СРЕДНИЙ] — it's documented as incompatible, but whether it fully kills stack detection vs heap detection, we verify empirically.\n\nWait — I should double-check: does CMake add /INCREMENTAL in Debug by default? Yes — CMake's default MSVC Debug linker flags historically include /INCREMENTAL (from MSBuild-style defaults; CMake adds `/INCREMENTAL` in Debug since... actually CMake's CMAKE_EXE_LINKER_FLAGS_DEBUG for MSVC includes `/debug /INCREMENTAL`? Let me recall. Actually I think CMake adds /INCREMENTAL for Debug builds on MSVC by default (it's in the default linker flags). The log confirms /INCREMENTAL is present. So we should disable it: add `/INCREMENTAL:NO` to target_link_options for Debug, or set CMAKE_EXE_LINKER_FLAGS_DEBUG.\n\nHmm — actually, does disabling incremental