Zi /Ob0 /Od  -std:c++latest -MDd /EHsc /W4 /permissive- /Zi /fsanitize=address`\n\nBreakdown:\n- `/DWIN32 /D_WINDOWS` — CMake platform defines\n- `/EHsc` — from CMake default CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS (MSVC: /EHsc)\n- `/Zi /Ob0 /Od` — from CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_DEBUG (MSVC debug: /Zi /Ob0 /Od /RTC1, but we removed /RTC1 via string(REPLACE))\n- `-std:c++latest` — from CXX_STANDARD 23 → MSVC latest\n- `/MDd` — from runtime library abstraction (CMP0091)\n- `/EHsc` — our genex `$<$<COMPILE_LANGUAGE:CXX>:/EHsc>`\n- `/W4 /permissive-` — our genex\n- `/Zi /fsanitize=address` — our genex `$<$<CONFIG:Debug>:/Zi /fsanitize=address>`\n\nSo yes: /EHsc appears twice (CMake default + ours), /Zi twice (CMake default debug + ours in Debug).\n\nFor the linker: `LINK_FLAGS = /machine:x64 /debug /INCREMENTAL /subsystem:console /DEBUG` — /DEBUG appears twice (CMake default debug link flags include /DEBUG, plus our genex /DEBUG).\n\nNow: should we remove our duplicates? The question is architectural. CMake already provides:\n- /EHsc (for C++ on MSVC) by default\n- /Zi in Debug by default (via CMAKE_MSVC_DEBUG_INFORMATION_FORMAT / CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS_DEBUG)\n- /DEBUG in Debug link by default\n\nBut our intent with the genex was explicitness and control. If we remove /Zi from Debug genex, we rely on CMake default to provide /Zi. But we still need /Zi in Release (CMake does NOT add /Zi in Release by default). And /Zi in Release we need to keep explicit.\n\nSimilarly /DEBUG: CMake adds /DEBUG in Debug link by default, but in Release it does NOT add /DEBUG by default (Release has no /DEBUG), so our Release genex /DEBUG /OPT:REF /OPT:ICF stays needed.\n\n/EHsc: CMake adds for MSVC C++ always (both configs). So our explicit /EHsc is redundant. But it documents intent. Removing it means relying on CMake default.\n\nSo the honest answer: yes duplicates are harmless, and we CAN remove the redundant ones, but there's a tradeoff between \"explicit intent in one place\" vs \"lean flags\". Since this is a learning project and the whole point was transparency, I'd argue: it's fine to keep them, OR to remove the clearly-redundant ones with a comment. But the important thing: don't remove them blindly, because each has a different status:\n- /EHsc: redundant in both configs (CMake always adds for MSVC C++)\n- /Zi Debug: redundant (CMake debug adds it) \n- /Zi Release: NOT redundant (CMake doesn't add /Zi in Release)\n- /DEBUG Debug link: redundant\n- /DEBUG Release link: NOT redundant\n\nHmm, but actually there's a subtlety about /Zi in Debug. If we rely on CMake default /Zi in Debug, is that robust? CMAKE_MSVC_DEBUG_INFORMATION_FORMAT defaults to ProgramDatabase for Debug, which is /Zi. That's reasonably stable. But relying on it is less explicit.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The cleanest answer for a learning project: the duplicates come from CMake defaults, and it's fine. Removing them is optional cleanup. But if she removes them, she must be careful: for /Zi and /DEBUG, keep the Release ones. For /EHsc, it's fully redundant.\n\nActually wait — I should double-check: does CMake add /Zi in Release? No. CMAKE_MSVC_DEBUG_INFORMATION_FORMAT defaults to ProgramDatabase for Debug and RelWithDebInfo configs, not Release. So Release doesn't get /Zi by default → our Release genex /Zi is needed.\n\nLet me also reconsider: is /EHsc added by CMake for C++ always? Yes, CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS for MSVC includes /EHsc by default. Confirmed in the log: `/EHsc` appears before our flags.\n\nSo answer to Q1: The duplicates are harmless (MSVC tolerates repeated flags). Removing them is optional. If we remove:\n- /EHsc — safe to remove (CMake always adds it for MSVC C++)\n- /Zi in Debug — safe to remove (CMake adds in Debug), but then our configure_target's Debug genex becomes just /fsanitize=address\n- /DEBUG in Debug link — safe to remove (CMake adds in Debug link), Debug genex becomes empty\n- Keep /Zi in Release and /DEBUG /OPT:REF /OPT:ICF in Release — NOT provided by CMake.\n\nBUT: there's a design consideration. If we remove them, configure_target becomes inconsistent between Debug (relies on CMake defaults) and Release (explicit). That's a bit ugly. And the whole point of the course's explicit approach is to see exactly what happens. I'd lean toward: keep the explicit flags, since they document the contract, and the duplicates are harmless. OR, if she wants clean logs, remove the redundant ones but note it.\n\nActually, there's an even better perspective: the real question is whether relying on CMake defaults is \"magic\". The course says don't trust defaults blindly. Keeping explicit flags means the -v log shows what we intend. I'll recommend: keep them, they're harmless, but understand where each comes from. If she prefers lean logs, remove /EHsc and Debug /Zi /DEBUG. I'll present the tradeoff and let her decide.\n\n**Question 2: /INCREMENTAL:NO placement**\n\nShe put it at line 55, inside `if(MSVC)` but OUTSIDE `if(DBG)`. So it applies to ALL MSVC targets that call configure_target — including run_case and check (when we add them with DBG=OFF). \n\nThe question: should /INCREMENTAL:NO be in DBG branch or for all?\n\nAnalysis: /INCREMENTAL is added by CMake to Debug LINK_FLAGS (CMAKE_EXE_LINKER_FLAGS_DEBUG includes /INCREMENTAL for MSVC). It appears in Debug only. And it's a linker flag. It's incompatible with ASan.\n\nKey insight: /INCREMENTAL:NO needs to be wherever /INCREMENTAL is present — i.e., Debug builds, on MSVC. It's not really a \"product vs tool\" distinction: tools (run_case, check) don't have ASan, so /INCREMENTAL doesn't break them, but it's still fine to disable it uniformly. Actually, tools don't need /INCREMENTAL either. Disabling it everywhere in Debug is correct and harmless.\n\nBut wait — should it be in DBG branch? The DBG branch is about product-specific flags (ASan, /Zi). /INCREMENTAL is not product-specific — it's a general MSVC Debug concern. CMake adds it for ALL targets in Debug. So disabling it for all targets in Debug is the right call, and putting it OUTSIDE if(DBG) is actually MORE correct than inside, because:\n- Inside DBG: only products get /INCREMENTAL:NO, but tools would still get /INCREMENTAL from CMake default → inconsistent, and tools would have /INCREMENTAL while products don't.\n- Outside DBG (but inside MSVC): all targets get /INCREMENTAL:NO → uniform.\n\nSo her placement (inside MSVC, outside DBG) is actually the right one! Good. But there's a subtlety: it's gated with `$<$<CONFIG:Debug>:/INCREMENTAL:NO>` — so in Release it produces nothing. That's fine, since /INCREMENTAL is only in Debug.\n\nWait, but one more consideration: is /INCREMENTAL only in Debug? Yes, CMake adds /INCREMENTAL to Debug linker flags for MSVC. Release has /OPT:REF etc. but not /INCREMENTAL. So gating by CONFIG:Debug is correct.\n\nHowever — there's a question whether we want to disable incremental linking entirely or just for products. Actually, since tools are built without ASan, /INCREMENTAL is harmless for them, but there's no reason to keep it either. Uniform is better. Her placement is correct.\n\nActually, hold on. Let me reconsider whether the genex should be `$<$<CONFIG:Debug>:/INCREMENTAL:NO>` or just `/INCREMENTAL:NO` unconditionally. If unconditional, in Release it would pass /INCREMENTAL:NO which is a no-op (linker already incremental-off in release). Harmless but noise. The genex version is cleaner. Her version is fine.\n\nOne more thing: the `if(DBG)` in the MSVC branch also adds link options. So there are now TWO target_link_options calls in MSVC branch. That's fine (they accumulate).\n\nSo answer to Q2: her placement is correct (outside DBG, inside MSVC, gated by Debug). The reason: /INCREMENTAL is a CMake default for ALL MSVC targets in Debug (not product-specific), so disabling it for all targets uniformly is right. Actually — should I double check that CMake adds /INCREMENTAL for all targets? Yes, CMAKE_EXE_LINKER_FLAGS_DEBUG applies to all exe targets. So run_case and check also get /INCREMENTAL currently. Disabling everywhere is consistent.\n\nBut wait — actually there's a nuance. Is it truly correct that /INCREMENTAL:NO should apply to tools? The tools don't have ASan, so they don't strictly need /INCREMENTAL:NO. But it's harmless and uniform. Alternatively one could argue tools should keep /INCREMENTAL for faster rebuilds. But incremental linking in Debug is generally discouraged and the gain is small. Uniform /INCREMENTAL:NO is fine and simpler.\n\nI'll confirm: placement correct, keep as is. But add a comment explaining why it's outside DBG (it's not product-specific).\n\n**Question 3: /FS — why was there a conflict before?**\n\nShe's asking: if /FS prevents conflicts (serializes PDB writes), why did she have the vc140.pdb conflict on layer 15?\n\nLet me recall the journal 2026-08-26: \"fatal error C1041 при make -j8 ... предлагаемое решение /FS ... но был LNK4099\". So she had C1041 conflict with parallel cl writing to vc140.pdb. The proposed fix was /FS (or /Fd). She chose /Fd (per-obj PDB).\n\nSo the answer: the conflict happened BECAUSE /FS was NOT present. In the Makefile build, there was no /FS — cl.exe by default LOCKS the PDB file to write type info. With multiple cl processes (-j8) writing to the same shared vc140.pdb, they conflicted (C1041). /FS is exactly the fix for that: it serializes PDB writes through MSPDBSRV, preventing the lock conflict. She solved it differently (with /Fd, per-object PDBs), which also avoids the shared-file conflict by giving each object its own PDB.\n\nSo: /FS is the cure, not the cause. The conflict was precisely because /FS was absent (and she used the shared vc140.pdb). Now CMake adds /FS by default, so no such conflict.\n\nWait — but in the journal she actually tried /FS and got LNK4099 warnings. Let me recall: \"с /FS при первом прогоне: warning LNK4099 PDB... компоновка объекта выполняется как при отсутствии отладочных данных\". That was a different issue — PDB timing (obj ready but PDB not yet