UG, the explicit ones are redundant. But removing them makes the code shorter and the -v log cleaner (easier to verify at control points). There's a real benefit to clean logs: when mentor reviews, a cluttered log hides signal. \n\nI think the balanced recommendation: remove the clearly-redundant ones that CMake guarantees:\n- /EHsc (CMake adds for MSVC C++ always)\n- /Zi in Debug (CMake debug info format ProgramDatabase adds it) — but careful: this relies on CMAKE_MSVC_DEBUG_INFORMATION_FORMAT default. It's a documented default. OK.\n- /DEBUG in Debug link (CMake adds /DEBUG in debug link flags)\n\nKeep:\n- /fsanitize=address (CMake doesn't add)\n- /Zi in Release (CMake doesn't add /Zi in Release)\n- /DEBUG /OPT:REF /OPT:ICF in Release (CMake release link has no /DEBUG by default)\n\nBut wait — if we remove Debug /Zi, then configure_target's Debug compile genex = `/fsanitize=address` only. And then we depend on CMake's /Zi in Debug. Is that robust? CMAKE_MSVC_DEBUG_INFORMATION_FORMAT default = ProgramDatabase (which produces /Zi). This is stable. But it IS a default — the course philosophy says don't trust defaults blindly. Hmm.\n\nActually — I think the honest answer is: there are two valid styles, and this is a preference/design decision. Let me present it and let her decide, with my recommendation. Given the course's emphasis on explicit control and the mentor's review of the flags, I lean toward KEEPING the explicit flags (they make the -v log self-documenting: you see exactly what the product needs) and adding a comment explaining the duplicates. But I should mention the option to trim.\n\nActually, you know what — let me reconsider. There's a subtle argument for keeping: if later someone changes CMake defaults or the debug info format variable, the explicit flags still guarantee behavior. Explicit > implicit in this course. Keep. But note the redundancy honestly.\n\nHmm, but she asked \"м.б убрать дубли?\" — she's inclined to remove. I should give a clear recommendation. Let me recommend: keep them (they're harmless and self-documenting, and removing creates asymmetry), but if she wants cleaner -v output, remove only the truly redundant ones with a comment. I'll ask her preference via the question tool? Actually I can just present the tradeoff and let her decide in her reply. Since we're in plan mode and she asked a question, I answer and recommend.\n\n**Q2: /INCREMENTAL:NO placement** — she already did it correctly (outside DBG, inside MSVC, Debug-gated). Confirm it's right and explain why. Actually wait — I need to double check: is /INCREMENTAL added by CMake to ALL exe link flags in Debug, including tools? Yes, CMAKE_EXE_LINKER_FLAGS_DEBUG is global default for all exe targets. So tools get /INCREMENTAL too. So disabling for all is uniform. Good. Her placement is correct. \n\nOne more check: should it maybe be unconditional (not Debug-gated)? /INCREMENTAL only appears in Debug. In Release, /INCREMENTAL:NO is a no-op but harmless. The Debug-gate keeps Release flags clean. Her version with gate is good.\n\n**Q3: /FS conflict** — explain it was absence of /FS that caused C1041; /FS is the cure; she chose /Fd instead; CMake adds /FS automatically now. No action needed.\n\nLet me also update the plan: the remaining actions after these clarifications:\n1. /INCREMENTAL:NO — already added (line 55), placement correct. Verify by rebuild + ASan test.\n2. Duplicates — decide (keep or trim).\n3. /FS — no action.\n4. Then the ASan test verification (heap control test + lineN[33] retest after /INCREMENTAL:NO).\n5. Remove test code lineN[33] before commit.\n\nLet me present this concisely.",
