          "text": "Let me analyze what I've found.\n\nKey observations:\n1. `cmake_clean.cmake` files were not found in the listing (the command returned nothing). Interesting — so the clean in CMake/Ninja might not use cmake_clean.cmake files? Actually with Ninja generator, `cmake --build --target clean` runs `ninja clean`, which uses the `.ninja` clean rules. The PDB files remain because... Let me think.\n\nActually, looking at the file listing: after clean, we still see:\n- `t1_dist_matrix_c\\main.pdb` — but NO `main.exe`, NO `.obj` for t1_c\n- `CMakeFiles\\t1_c.dir\\vc140.pdb`\n- common pdb files\n\nSo clean removed .obj, .exe, .lib but LEFT the .pdb files. Why?\n\nBecause in Ninja, `clean` removes all \"outputs\" of build edges. The `.pdb` files ARE outputs of the compile edge (`/Fd...pdb`) and link edge (`/pdb:...pdb`). Hmm, but they remained.\n\nWait — actually the ninja_log shows `clean` ran (first and last entries are \"clean\"). But pdb files remain. So CMake's clean target does NOT include pdb files in the clean list.\n\nActually, CMake's Ninja generator: the `clean` target uses a special rule that reads `CMakeFiles/<target>.dir/cmake_clean.cmake` files (generated scripts listing files to delete). My search found NO cmake_clean.cmake files at all! That's odd. Let me reconsider.\n\nActually for Ninja generator, CMake generates `CMakeFiles/cmake_clean.cmake` or per-target clean rules? Hmm. Actually CMake generates a `ninja_clean` ... no. Let me think.\n\nFor Ninja generator, `cmake --build --target clean` runs ninja's built-in `clean` target. Ninja's clean removes all outputs of build statements in the graph. But CMake marks PDB files as... Actually, CMake may declare PDB as output in the build.ninja edge, OR it may not.\n\nActually the important thing: the user asked \"почему он оставляет pdb\" — why does clean leave pdb? The answer: the PDB files are not registered as cleanable outputs in the Ninja graph, OR they're registered as order-only / not as prima