          "text": "Let me analyze this carefully.\n\nFacts found:\n1. `log2.txt` exists.\n2. `build.ninja` LINK_FLAGS for t1_c: `/machine:x64 /debug  /subsystem:console  /DEBUG` — `/INCREMENTAL` is GONE! The string(REPLACE) worked. And `/INCREMENTAL:NO` also gone (she replaced it too? Actually the string(REPLACE \"/INCREMENTAL\" \"\" ...) would also remove `/INCREMENTAL:NO` if it was in the flags... but the current CMakeLists — let me check what she has now. Actually the replace removes `/INCREMENTAL` from CMAKE_EXE_LINKER_FLAGS_DEBUG, which was the CMake default. Her previous /INCREMENTAL:NO addition via target_link_options would still be there unless... wait, the LINK_FLAGS shows neither. Let me look: `LINK_FLAGS = /machine:x64 /debug  /subsystem:console  /DEBUG`. There's no /INCREMENTAL:NO either. So she must have removed her /INCREMENTAL:NO line from configure_target, OR the string(REPLACE) happened at a level that... no, target_link_options adds after. Hmm.\n\nActually wait — the build.ninja FLAGS line 91: `/Zi /Ob0 /Od -std:c17 -MDd /W4 /permissive- /Zi /fsanitize=address`. This is the C compile for t1_c. Good, ASan present.\n\nBut the LINK_FLAGS line 70 (t1_cpp) and 107 (t1_c): `/machine:x64 /debug /subsystem:console /DEBUG`. No /INCREMENTAL. Good — so the replace worked and her manual /INCREMENTAL:NO target_link_options must have been removed (otherwise it would show as `/INCREMENTAL:NO`). Let me check the current CMakeLists to be sure. Actually I don't need to — the key fact: incremental linking is now disabled. \n\n3. `main.c` has the heap test at lines 19-21.\n4. `main.exe` was rebuilt at 18:00:55, .ninja_log shows main.c.obj rebuilt at 8099780554... and main.exe at 8099780555... So the heap test IS in the freshly built binary.\n\nSo: the experiment was set up correctly. But the user says \"ошибки от эксперимента всё равно нет\". \n\nKey question: WHERE did she look for the error? She ran `cmake --build build/cmake_debug 2> log2.txt` — the build itself. But ASan error occurs at RUNTIME, when you RUN main.exe, not at build time! The build doesn't execute the program. So log2.txt contains build output, not ASan output.\n\nWait — but she said \"протестировала\" earlier. Let me re-read her message:\n\n\"1. с incremental NO - протестировала. поток ошибки пустой. подскажи у меня верный порядок действий? вписала int* p = ... в первую Си задачу. потом cmake --build build/cmake_debug --target clean потом cmake --build build/cmake_debug 2> log2.txt ?\"\n\nSo her sequence: edit main.c (heap test) → clean → build with 2> log2.txt. Then \"ошибки от эксперимента всё равно нет\" (point 2).\n\nThe critical bug in her procedure: **ASan reports at runtime when the program executes, not during build.** She redirected build output to log2.txt and presumably looked there for the ASan error. But the build just compiles and links — it doesn't run main.exe. To see the ASan error, she needs to RUN main.exe (with input) and capture ITS stderr.\n\nWait, but in point 1 she said \"с incremental NO - протестировала. поток ошибки пустой.\" — meaning she tested and the error stream was empty. Maybe she DID run it? Or maybe she's conflating. Let me be careful.\n\nActually the sequence she describes in point 1 is: with INCREMENTAL:NO added, she tested, error stream empty. Then point 2: she replaced with the string(REPLACE) approach and ALSO the heap experiment still no error.\n\nHmm. So the question is really: after all this, is ASan actually active? Two possibilities:\nA) ASan isn't being linked/instrumented at all in this CMake build.\nB) She's looking in the wrong place (build log instead of running the exe).\n\nKey insight to verify: Was the ASan runtime actually linked? In the log.txt earlier, the link command was `link.exe /nologo ... main.c.obj /out:... /implib:main.lib /pdb:... /debug /INCREMENTAL /subsystem:console /DEBUG common\\common_c_static.lib kernel32.lib ...` — I don't see any explicit ASan library in the link line. But MSVC ASan works via `/INFERASANLIBS` (on by default) which auto-links `clang_rt.asan*`. That's automatic, doesn't appear in command line.\n\nBut wait — there's a subtlety. ASan needs BOTH:\n1. Compile with `/fsanitize=address` (present: FLAGS show it)\n2. Link: the object files reference ASan symbols, and `/INFERASANLIBS` links the runtime.\n\nActually let me think about what could make ASan silently not catch things:\n- If the CRT is `/MDd` but... no, doc says /MDd works.\n- The known-issues doc says incremental linking should be disabled — now it is.\n\nActually, wait. There's a REALLY important subtlety I should check: does the binary actually have ASan runtime linked? Let me think about whether I can check. I can run `dumpbin /dependents main.exe` to see if clang_rt.asan_dynamic DLL is a dependency. That's read-only. Actually dumpbin is available in the VS tools. Let me check if it's on PATH.\n\nAlso I could check the actual ASan error behavior. But I'm in plan mode — read-only. I can run read-only inspection commands like dumpbin, but NOT run main.exe with input? Actually running main.exe is executing a program — it's a \"system change\"? It reads stdin. Hmm. Running a program is arguably fine for inspection but the plan-mode reminder says commands may ONLY read/inspect. Running main.exe with a pipe would execute code. I should be cautious. dumpbin /dependents is inspection.\n\nLet me use dumpbin to check if ASan runtime DLL is a dependency of main.exe. That would tell us definitively whether ASan was linked.\n\nAlso, let me reconsider: is the order of operations she described correct? Let's evaluate:\n\nSequence: edit main.c → `cmake --build --target clean` → `cmake --build 2> log2.txt`.\n\n`--target clean` runs clean (removes .obj/.exe/.lib) — but note clean does NOT reconfigure. However, she changed CMakeLists.txt (added string(REPLACE)) — when you run `cmake --build`, CMake checks if CMakeLists.txt changed and re-runs configure+generate (regeneration) automatically. Actually with ninja, the build.ninja itself has a rule to regenerate build.ninja when CMakeLists.txt changes (the RERUN_CMAKE rule we discussed). So `cmake --build` would trigger regeneration. And indeed build.ninja now shows no /INCREMENTAL, confirming regeneration happened.\n\nBut wait — `cmake --build --target clean` — does that trigger regeneration? The clean target... ninja would check build.ninja freshness before/at start. The .ninja_log shows: `20 131 ... build.ninja c020739a...` and `21 130 ... build.ninja` entries near the clean entries — those look like build.ninja was regenerated (the log tracks build.ninja as a target). Actually the entries `20 131 8099780517051644 build.ninja` appear BEFORE the clean entries `30 43 8099780517676489 clean`. So build.ninja regenerated, then clean ran. Then the rebuild happened. OK so the sequence is actually fine for the FLAGS, and main.c.obj/main.exe got rebuilt fresh with the heap test.\n\nSo the build itself is correct and current. The heap test IS in the binary. \n\nNow the REAL issue: \"ошибки нет\" — where did she look? The ASan error appears at runtime. Running `cmake --build ... 2> log2.txt` only builds; the program is not executed. So log2.txt (build log) will NEVER contain an ASan report. She must RUN `main.exe` and feed it input, capturing ITS stderr.\n\nSo my hypothesis: the procedure is missing the \"run the exe\" step. She's looking at the build log instead of running the program.\n\nBut point 1 says \"протестировала. поток ошибки пустой\" — she claims she tested and error stream was empty. Maybe she did run it? Or maybe \"протестировала\" means ran the parity script? Hmm, ambiguous.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. Maybe she DID run main.exe and got no ASan error even for the heap overflow. That would mean ASan is genuinely not working. The most likely cause: **the ASan runtime DLL isn't being linked/found**, OR **the CRT runtime library selection**.\n\nWait — actually there's a crucial subtlety about MSVC ASan and the runtime DLL. The doc says: `clang_rt.asan*.dll` runtime files are \"installed next to the compilers\" and \"never placed in System32\". For the program to run with ASan, the DLL must be findable. But if the DLL weren't found, the program would fail to START with a \"DLL not found\" error, not silently run. So if main.exe runs at all, the ASan DLL is found.\n\nHmm. Let me think about whether the heap OOB would be caught. `int* p = (int*)malloc(sizeof(int)); p[2] = 5; free(p);` — malloc(4), then write at p+8. That's a heap-buffer-overflow, 8 bytes past a 4-byte allocation. ASan definitely catches this — it's the classic case. If ASan is active, this MUST fire.\n\nSo either:\n1. She never actually ran main.exe (just looked at build log) → most likely.\n2. ASan runtime isn't linked → but then DLL missing error or unresolved symbols.\n\nLet me verify with dumpbin whether clang_rt.asan is in the imports. That's read-only inspection. Let me find dumpbin (in VS toolchain). It's typically at the same place as cl. Let me check via `where dumpbin` or use the full path.\n\nActually, I can also check via PowerShell reading the PE imports? That's complex. dumpbin is the clean way.\n\nLet me check dumpbin availability. Also, I should verify whether ASan actually works by checking the log2.txt content — maybe it contains a hint (e.g., linker warnings). Let me read log2.txt.\n\nLet me read log2.txt and check dumpbin.\n\nAlso I realize: the answer to her question \"подскажи у меня верный порядок действий?\" — the correct order is:\n1. Edit main.c with the experiment\n2. Rebuild (clean is optional, but fine)\n3. **RUN the freshly built main.exe with input and capture ITS stderr** (the ASan report appears at runtime, not at build time)\n\nThe critical missing step is running the program. Let me confirm log2.txt contents to see if it's build output.\n\nLet me also double check current CMakeLists to see what she did with /INCREMENTAL:NO (removed the target_link_options line? since build.ninja no longer shows /INCREMENTAL:NO). Actually the LINK_FLAGS = `/machine:x64 /debug  /subsystem:console  /DEBUG` — no /INCREMENTAL:NO. So either she removed her configure_target line or... the string(REPLACE) on CMAKE_EXE_LINKER_FLAGS_DEBUG wouldn't affect a target_link_options. So she must have removed the target_link_options(/INCREMENTAL:NO) line. Let me check the current CMakeLists.txt.\n\nLet me read log2.txt, current CMakeLists.txt, and try dumpbin.",
