4, 72) 'endptr' <== Memory access at offset 577 overflows this variable\". \n\nThe `<==` marker in ASan output points at the specific object that was overflowed. It appears after 'endptr' in the paste. But offset 577 is not within endptr [64,72). This is confusing.\n\nHmm wait. Maybe the offsets listed are not byte offsets but... no. Actually, maybe the paste is missing some content (e.g., there could be more objects, and the arrow points at a different object not fully shown). Or the layout: in the actual ASan output, the objects are listed and the one overflowed has the `<==` before it.\n\nActually, I realize the numbers might be relative to the stack frame base in a way I can't fully reconstruct from the pasted text. What matters: SUMMARY: stack-buffer-overflow at main.c:18. That line IS `lineN[33] = 'k';`. So it caught our intended bug.\n\nBut wait — could it be catching the fgets write instead? fgets writes up to sizeof(lineN)=32 bytes starting at lineN[0], so max index 31 — not an overflow. The write at lineN[33] is index 33 — overflow. And the summary line says line 18 which is the `lineN[33] = 'k';`. So it's our bug. Good.\n\nHmm, but \"Memory access at offset 577 overflows this variable\" — if lineN base is at frame offset 544 (so that 544+33=577), then the object 'lineN' would be at [544, 576). But the paste shows lineN at [48, 80). These don't match. So either the paste is garbled, or the frame listing I'm interpreting is wrong.\n\nI think I should not overanalyze the offsets — the definitive signal is the SUMMARY line: stack-buffer-overflow at main.c:18. I'll confirm that confidently and note that the offset details in MSVC's report can look confusing but the summary line is what identifies the bug location.\n\nActually — let me reconsider whether the numbers could be right. Perhaps in MSVC ASan, the \"offset\" is relative to a different base (like the shadow offset or the frame), and the object ranges shown are... Actually the object ranges [48,80) 'lineN' and [32,512) 'points' OVERLAP, which is impossible for real stack layout. So the paste must be garbled / the ranges are not what they appear. I won't try to reconstruct; I'll rely on the summary.\n\nOK so now let me formulate the response and plan.\n\nKey conclusions:\n1. **ASan works in the CMake build** — confirmed empirically for both heap-buffer-overflow and stack-buffer-overflow, WITH /INCREMENTAL present.\n2. **My /INCREMENTAL hypothesis was wrong** — I should admit this clearly.\n3. **Root cause of the earlier \"silence\"** was methodological: ASan writes its report to stderr, and the test runs hid stderr via `2>nul` (in the bat) — and earlier, looking at a build log instead of a program run.\n4. The user's line of questioning was correct and led to the right diagnosis — good active recall.\n\nDecision needed:\n- What to do with /INCREMENTAL: \n  - Option A: leave CMake default /INCREMENTAL (empirically ASan works). Simpler, fewer hacks.\n  - Option B: keep doc-recommended removal (/INCREMENTAL:NO or REPLACE). MS docs explicitly say unsupported with ASan; following vendor guidance is the \"documented\" path; cost is one line.\n  \nMy recommendation: I lean toward keeping the doc-aligned removal (Option B) because:\n- MS explicitly documents /INCREMENTAL as unsupported with ASan; \"unsupported\" means not guaranteed, not \"definitely broken\". Our 2-case empirical check doesn't overturn a vendor compatibility statement.\n- It costs nothing and avoids potential subtle issues (e.g., PDB/symbol oddities, future MSVC changes).\n- BUT the comment should now be honest: \"по доке MS несовместим; эмпирически работает, но оставляем убратым по доке\".\n\nHowever, I should present both and let the user decide — they might reasonably prefer simplicity. Actually, given the course's \"don't do cargo cult\" and \"if it works, keep it simple\" — hmm. But also \"follow documentation\". \n\nLet me think about what's actually best engineering: In real production with ASan, disabling incremental linking is standard practice (documented). Keeping the removal is the safe, conventional choice. I'll recommend keeping it but be honest that it's doc-driven, not empirically-driven now.\n\nAlso cleanup plan:\n1. Remove test code from main.c: line 18 `lineN[33] = 'k';` (and heap test already removed).\n2. Decide /INCREMENTAL.\n3. Rebuild, run parity bat (which now has C tests too — the earlier gap about t1_c being untested was fixed, I saw C tests in the bat).\n4. The bat's 2>nul — that's fine for the bat's purpose (exit codes), but ASan reports go to nul. Actually wait — this matters! If the bat hides stderr, then ASan reports won't be visible when running tests via the bat. For the course, the bat is a parity checker (exit codes + stdout equality). ASan reports are stderr — hiding them in the bat is intentional there (we only care about exit code parity). But it means: if the test intentionally triggers an error that should be caught by ASan, the bat won't show it. That's fine — the bat isn't the ASan verification tool.\n\nHmm, but there's a subtle point: if a test case DOES trigger an ASan error (a real bug), the exit code would be non-zero and the bat would catch a mismatch — so it's still caught, just without the nice message. OK.\n\n5. Journal entry (AGENTS.md §7) — document the findings: ASan works in CMake build; /INCREMENTAL empirically fine but kept removed per docs; the 2>nul lesson; parity bat now covers C.\n\nLet me also verify: is the heap test still in main.c? Earlier read showed lines 19-21 had the heap test; now the read shows line 18 is `lineN[33] = 'k';` and line 19 is `if (fgets...`. So heap test is removed. Good — user said \"попробовала убрать с поинтером ошибку и раскомментировать ту\". Confirmed.\n\nSo current main.c test state: only `lineN[33] = 'k';` at line 18.\n\nNow, the plan for the response:\n1. Confirm: yes, ASan caught it. SUMMARY: stack-buffer-overflow main.c:18, line 18 = lineN[33] write. \n2. Confirm the big conclusion: /INCREMENTAL hypothesis dead — ASan works with /INCREMENTAL for both heap and stack. Admit my hypothesis was wrong.\n3. Explain why it \"didn't catch\" earlier: 2>nul in the bat hid stderr (user's own realization — confirm it's correct), plus earlier we looked at a build log not a run.\n4. Note: this is actually the same reason the parity bat \"showed no error\" for t1_c — the C tests existed in the bat (I now saw them, 52-86) but 2>nul hid the ASan report; also exit codes might have... wait, if ASan fires, exit code is non-zero. In the bat, `%t1_c_cmake% < input > nul 2> nul` then `set last_errlevel=%errorlevel%` then run_case expects make to return same code. If ASan fired, last_errlevel would be non-zero (ASan abort code), and make version (also has the same bug if make build also has lineN[33]... wait, does the make build have lineN[33]? The make build compiles the same main.c! So both cmake and make binaries have lineN[33]. Both would trigger ASan with same-ish exit code... hmm. Actually if both trigger ASan, both exit non-zero with same code → parity holds → bat passes! That's why bat \"no error\" — both crash identically, so exit codes match! Interesting.)\n\nOh that's a key insight. The bat checks parity between make and cmake builds. If BOTH have the same bug, both ASan-crash with the same exit code → parity OK → bat passes. The bat is a parity check, not a correctness check. So the bat \"passing\" doesn't mean no bug — it means make and cmake behave identically (both buggy). That's exactly the \"parity vs correctness\" distinction I mentioned earlier. Good to reinforce.\n\n5. Decision on /INCREMENTAL + cleanup plan.\n\nLet me be concise but complete. This is a plan-mode response, so no edits. I'll present findings + plan + ask the /INCREMENTAL decision.\n\nActually, I realize I should double check one thing: whether the offsets in the report matter. The user asked \"line 18 это должно быть оно\" — I confirm based on SUMMARY line. Good enough.\n\nLet me also reconsider: the report says \"Memory access at offset 577 overflows this variable\" with arrow pointing to endptr area. If the arrow truly points at endptr, that'd be odd. But I genuinely think the paste is partially garbled (overlapping ranges impossible). I'll say: the definitive line is SUMMARY; offset details in MSVC output can be confusing; don't worry about them.\n\nHmm, actually, wait. Let me reconsider the possibility that the write is being reported as overflowing a different object. `lineN[33] = 'k'` writes 1 byte at lineN+33. That's 2 bytes past lineN's end (lineN is 32 bytes, so +33 is 1 byte past end... indices 0-31, so index 33 is 2 past). ASan should report it as overflowing lineN. The arrow should point at lineN. In the paste the arrow is after endptr. But there might be more objects in the frame not shown (the paste might be truncated). I'll just confirm based on the summary line which is unambiguous: main.c:18.\n\nOK. Now also: the user might wonder — since /INCREMENTAL works, should we also reconsider /RTC1? I'll proactively address: no, /RTC1 is different (actively instruments stack, documented incompatible), keep it removed. But I could offer to test it too if they want full empirical confirmation. Actually, that's a reasonable thing to offer — \"попытайся оспорить меня\" spirit. But keep scope tight.\n\nLet me write the response.",
