ll in its dependencies (including libjsoncpp, librhash) automatically. You don't install them manually — you just run `apt install cmake` and apt handles the rest.\n\nI should verify: is libjsoncpp actually a dependency of cmake? Let me think. On Ubuntu, `apt-cache depends cmake` shows something like:\n- libarchive13\n- libcurl4\n- libjsoncpp25\n- librhash0\n- ...\n\nYes, cmake depends on libjsoncpp and librhash. So these get installed automatically.\n\nThe user's \"libharsh\" is almost certainly \"librhash\" (rHash library) — a common mishearing/mistyping. There's no \"libharsh\" package.\n\nSo the plan-mode answer: \n- No need to manually install them. apt install cmake resolves and installs all dependencies automatically (as long as you have `sudo` and network).\n- libharsh is likely librhash — a dependency of cmake.\n- One caveat: the Ubuntu `apt cmake` version is often old (e.g., 3.28 on newer Ubuntu, but on older LTS like 20.04 it's 3.16). Since we require cmake_minimum_required 3.22, the Ubuntu 20.04 cmake (3.16) would fail. So for the Linux VM, better to check the version or install a newer one (pip/kitware repo). This is a relevant point since the project requires 3.22.\n\nI should mention this since it's relevant to the plan (cross-platform verification on Ubuntu VM). But keep it focused.\n\nI should also note: since I'm in plan mode, I can only observe/plan. Let me answer the question directly and concisely.\n\nLet me not over-verify with webfetch. This is a well-known fact about apt. But per the anti-sycophancy protocol, I should mark confidence. The claim \"apt auto-installs dependencies\" is [ФАКТ, ВЫСОКИЙ]. The claim \"librhash not libharsh\" — [ПРЕДПОЛОЖЕНИЕ, СРЕДНИЙ] since I don't have the exact package list in front of me. Actually I'm quite confident there's no \"libharsh\" package and cmake depends on librhash. But I can note the user should check `apt-cache depends cmake` to see actual deps.\n\nSince plan mode, I could suggest read-only verification: `apt-cache depends cmake` — but that's a command on the Ubuntu machine, not here. On this Windows machine I can't run apt. I'll just tell her what to run on Ubuntu.\n\nKeep answer concise.",
