matic CMake: use `target_link_libraries(... m)` under Linux, or CMake has `CMAKE_DL_LIBS`, but for libm there's... Actually CMake has no built-in for libm detection. There's a common pattern: `find_library(M_LIB m)` or `target_link_libraries(tgt m)` guarded by `if(UNIX)`. On Linux, `-lm` via `m` in link libs. On macOS, libm is part of the system (no -lm needed). On Windows, no.\n\nActually there's `CMAKE_HAVE_LIBM`? Hmm, I don't recall a standard variable. There's `find_package` no. There is a check: `check_library_exists(m sqrt \"\" HAVE_LIBM)` from `CheckLibraryExists` module. That's the \"proper detection\" route the user hints at (\"как-то смотреть надо ли libm или нет\").\n\nBut wait — the user asks me to advise but not give a ready solution. They ask whether to:\n(a) go through CMake flags and somehow detect whether libm is needed, or\n(b) just pass \"\" or \"-lm\" to configure_target.\n\nLet me give advice on the tradeoffs without writing the code.\n\nKey insight: The cleanest conceptual approach — platform condition, not detection. The need for `-lm` is a property of the *platform/toolchain*, not of whether the code uses sqrt. You know deterministically: Linux+GCC → need `-lm`; Windows+MSVC → no; macOS → no (libm is in libSystem). So you don't need runtime detection; you need a platform-conditional flag. Detection (check_library_exists) is overkill here and is for the case when you don't know the platform ahead of time — but the toolchain is determined by the platform anyway.\n\nHowever, there's a subtlety: \"detection\" via `check_library_exists` would be more robust if someone builds with a different libc (musl on Alpine — musl has libm too, needs -lm). Actually musl needs -lm too. So Linux → -lm universally. The detection adds robustness but also complexity.\n\nAlso architectural point: linking against libm is a *linking concern*, not a *compile-options concern*. In CMake terms, it's `target_link_libraries`, not `target_compile_options`. The user's configure_target currently does both compile and link options. Adding `-lm` is a link library, so the \"right\" CMake way is `target_link_libraries(${trgt} PRIVATE m)` on Linux — CMake translates `m` to `-lm` for GCC. But careful: linking `m` — CMake treats `m` as a library name and passes `-lm`. That's idiomatic.\n\nBut the user wants to route through configure_target with \"\" or \"-lm\". That's a function parameter approach: pass a link-flag argument. That also works but it's less CMake-idiomatic than target_link_libraries(m). However, given their project structure (configure_target is the single place for flags), a parameter is acceptable.\n\nWait — important consideration: WHERE does sqrt get used? In t1 (C and C++) main, in geometry.c? t1_dist_matrix_c/main.c uses sqrt, cos, sin, pow. t1_cpp main.cpp uses std::hypot (std version — needs -lm too on Linux!). geometry.c uses strtod, no math. t2 uses isspace. t3/t4 use hypot. So actually every task that links needs -lm on Linux (they all use math indirectly?). t1 needs it definitely. So it should apply to products (DBG) at least, probably all exes.\n\nAlso the run_case/check tools — they don't use math, don't need -lm, but adding -lm is harmless.\n\nNow, on the tradeoff question the user poses: \"лучше идти через флаги cmake и как-то смотреть надо ли libm или нет, или просто передавать флаг \"\" или \"-lm\" в ф-цию configure_target\"\n\nMy advice: \n- The cleaner model: it's a *link library*, so the natural CMake idiom is platform-conditional `target_link_libraries` with `m` on Linux. That's \"флаг cmake\"-ish and idiomatic. \n- \"Detection\" (check_library_exists) is not needed here because the answer is deterministic from the platform: Linux (glibc/musl) → -lm; Windows/MSVC → not needed (CRT has math); macOS → not needed (libSystem). You know at configure time which platform you're on via `if(UNIX)` / `if(NOT WIN32)`. Detection adds value only if the same source might link against unknown libc variants — not your case.\n- BUT there's a nuance: since glibc 2.35, `libm` became a stub? Actually no. Let me not overclaim. Actually: glibc 2.35+ still ships libm. However, I recall that in glibc 2.35, math functions were NOT moved. There was discussion. Safe: -lm needed on Linux. Also note: with GCC's `-ffast-math` or `-lm` interplay — not relevant.\n\nHmm, actually let me reconsider the glibc 2.35 claim because being accurate matters. In glibc 2.35 (Feb 2022), there was \"the libm functions are now in libc\"? No — I recall glibc 2.35 added a stub libm.a for linking? Let me not make a false claim. The empirical fact: the user's build on Ubuntu failed with sqrt undefined unless -lm. That's the ground truth for their system. So they need -lm. I'll go with: on Linux, -lm is required (their own build proves it). Don't need to speculate about glibc versions.\n\nThe architectural axis here (per AGENTS.md §4.2: name architectural axes before proposing a fix): the axis is \"where do platform-specific link requirements live?\" — options:\n1. In configure_target as a parameter (their suggestion): keeps all flags in one place, but conflates compile-options with link-libraries, and every call site must pass it (or use a default).\n2. Platform-conditional in configure_target directly (`if(NOT WIN32) target_link_libraries(... m)`): single place, no call-site burden, but makes configure_target branch again.\n3. Idiomatic: `target_link_libraries(${trgt} PRIVATE m)` at call sites (in add_task), guarded by platform: but then every add_task needs it.\n4. Proper CMake: use `find_library(MATH_LIBRARY m)` + conditional, or CheckLibraryExists.\n\nActually there's a subtle CMake-idiomatic point: `target_link_libraries(tgt m)` — CMake has a special variable `CMAKE_DL_LIBS` for dlopen (dl), not for m. For m, no built-in. There's also `m` being found automatically? No.\n\nHmm, one more consideration: the tools run_case/check — should they get -lm? They don't use math. If we put -lm globally for all targets, harmless. If we gate by DBG (products), fine too. But note: on Linux the library `m` is always available, so linking it is free.\n\nAnother important point: their current Linux link flags in configure_target are empty (`LINK_FLAGS` empty for GCC, they removed the /DEBUG equivalents). So there's no place currently passing link flags on Linux except the DBG branch with `-fsanitize=address`. So the -lm is a NEW link requirement for Linux products.\n\nMy recommendation (without giving ready code): \n- Do it as a **link-library concern**, not a raw flag string, because CMake distinguishes \"libraries\" from \"flags\" and `m` is a library.\n- Don't do runtime detection — the need is deterministic from platform; use a platform condition. Detection (`check_library_exists`) is legitimate and robust, but for a learning project where platform is known (Windows+MSVC / Linux+GCC), a simple platform conditional is the honest minimum. If they want to be thorough and learn, `check_library_exists(m sqrt ...)` is the \"proper detection\" path — but it costs a configure-time try-link and adds a module.\n- Placement: the cleanest is in the link stage. Given their architecture (configure_target as the single flag factory), putting it there behind a platform condition keeps it in one place and matches how they already handle /RTC1, /INCREMENTAL (platform-conditional). But note the asymmetry: configure_target currently branches on MSVC; adding `else() target_link_libraries(m)` is consistent with that existing pattern.\n\nWait, but there's a wrinkle: configure_target uses target_link_options for linker flags. `-lm` is not a linker option per se — it's a library specification. In CMake you can pass `-lm` via target_link_options, but the idiomatic way is target_link_libraries(PRIVATE m). Both produce the same link command (`-lm`). For learning, worth noting the difference: `m` as a library name gets proper handling (e.g., on systems where m is in a non-standard location, or ordering). Passing raw `-lm` as an option is more fragile/less idiomatic.\n\nAlso: linking `m` as a library — CMake will pass `-lm`. Good.\n\nAnother consideration for the user's specific question \"как-то смотреть надо ли libm или нет\": they could be thinking of checking whether math functions are used. That's not detectable at CMake level (CMake doesn't parse sources). The honest statement: CMake cannot know whether your code calls sqrt; the need for -lm is decided by (platform) × (math usage in code). Since the code is fixed, the only variable is platform → deterministic condition, no detection needed. If they want a *check* rather than a *condition*, check_library_exists is the tool, but it answers \"does this libc provide sqrt\" — which on Linux is always yes, so it's a tautology. So detection adds nothing here.\n\nSo my advice: platform condition, not detection. And prefer target_link_libraries with `m` over raw flag. And they should verify on the Ubuntu VM after.\n\nLet me also mention: only needed for Linux (glibc/musl). macOS: `sqrt` is in libSystem, `-lm` is a no-op stub but harmless; MSVC: CRT, no lib. So `if(UNIX AND NOT APPLE)` or just `if(NOT WIN32)` — both fine; the precise condition depends on their cross-platform matrix (they target Windows + Linux).\n\nAlso note: `std::hypot` / `std::sqrt` (C++ <cmath>) — on GCC these also resolve to libm functions, so the C++ side needs -lm too. And their C++ t1 uses std::hypot, t3/t4 use std::hypot. So ALL task exes need -lm on Linux. Tools don't use math → don't need it, but harmless.\n\nLet me also raise an architectural-axis framing per AGENTS.md: the axes here are (1) where the knowledge \"Linux needs libm\" lives (in configure_target vs add_task vs a dedicated library target), and (2) condition vs detection. I'll name these before advising, per §4.2.\n\nI should also ask whether they want the minimal conditional approach or want to practice the detection route (check_library_exists) — that's a genuine tradeoff worth asking. Also ask whether -lm should apply to all targets or just products.\n\nAlso I should point out one subtle thing: since it's the DBG (products) that use math, and tools don't, the \"correct\" scoping is: products link `m`; tools don't need it. But simpler: all targets. This is a decision for them.\n\nSince I'm in plan mode, I must not edit. I give advice, name axes, ask clarifying questions. Keep it concise but educational. In Russian.\n\nLet me structure:\n1. Почему возникает (факт: на Linux sqrt/cos/sin/hypot живут в libm; на MSVC — в CRT; macOS — в libSystem). Проверено их сборкой.\n2. Ключевое различие: это link-library, а не флаг компиляции. CMake отличает target_link_libraries(m) от флага -lm.\n3. Оси архитектуры (назвать до правки):\n   - Где живёт знание «Linux нужен -lm»: configure_target / add_task / библиотека.\n   - Условие vs детекция: CMake не парсит исходники, не узнает «используется ли sqrt»; потребность детерминирована платформой → условие достаточно; check_library_exists — тавтология на Linux (там sqrt всегда есть). Детекция нужна, когда платформа неизвестна заранее (cross-build на неизвестный libc) — не ваш случай.\n4. Рекомендация: платформенное условие в configure_target (в духе того, как там уже /RTC1, /INCREMENTAL), через target_link_libraries(m), а не сырой флаг. Или параметром — но параметр заставляет каждый вызов помнить об этом.\n5. Вопросы к ней: (а) минимальный путь — условие, или хочешь потренироваться в check_library_exists? (б) применять только к продуктам (DBG) или ко всем таргетам? (в) macOS учитываем или только Windows/Linux?\n\nKeep it tight.",
