        {
          "type": "reasoning",
          "text": "The user has a conceptual misunderstanding about static libraries and libm. Let me explain carefully.\n\nTheir question 1: \"Разве не будет так что библиотека слинкуется с libm.so, у неё в бинаре будет всё что надо, и потом при соединении оно будет доступно и для main? Т.е. я понимаю как транзитивность тут работает но не понимаю зачем если libm.so уже есть в статик библиотеке.\"\n\nThe key misunderstanding: a STATIC library (.a / .lib) does NOT link with anything. Static library is just an archive of object files. It doesn't link against libm.so at all. The linking happens only at the final exe link step. So the static library \"common_c_static.a\" contains just geometry.c.o — no math references resolved, no libm.so inside.\n\nSo when main links, the linker must resolve sqrt from main.o and geometry.o. libm.so is a shared library (dynamic), not a static one. Static lib common.a has no code from libm.\n\nActually wait — there's another subtlety. Even if libm were linked into the static lib (it can't be), the point is: static lib = archive of objects, no linking. And the reference to sqrt in main.o itself needs -lm too.\n\nLet me explain:\n- Static library (.a) = archive (zip) of .o files. No linking happens at `lib`/`ar` step.\n- The `-lm` (libm.so) is a shared dynamic library. \n- The final exe link resolves all undefined symbols from all .o files (from main.o AND from common.a's members) against the listed libraries (libm.so).\n- If -lm is not in the final link line, sqrt is undefined → error.\n\nSo \"libm.so already in static library\" is a misconception: static library can't contain libm.so, and doesn't link.\n\nQuestion 2: \"target_link_libraries не потащит же -lm в виндоус ветку, верно?\"\n\ntarget_link_libraries(common_c_static PUBLIC m) — `m` is a raw library name. On Linux CMake maps it to -lm. On Windows, CMake would try to link \"m.lib\" which doesn't exist → link error. So it WOULD pull `m` into Windows too if written unconditionally! That's why it must be guarded by a platform condition (if(UNIX) / if(NOT WIN32)).\n\nSo the answer to #2: No, it WILL try on Windows too — `m` as a bare name has no platform condition. That's exactly why you need to guard it. Good catch that they're asking.\n\nWait — but actually, is `m` recognized specially? No. `target_link_libraries(tgt m)` passes `-lm` on Linux, and on MSVC tries to find `m.lib` → error \"cannot open file m.lib\". So yes, it needs guarding.\n\nNow also address the deeper question about the architecture: they said \"видимо надо явно указать каждому для линка\". Actually with PUBLIC on the static lib, it's NOT each — it's one place. That's the whole point of PUBLIC: declared once on the library, propagates to all consumers. That's the advantage over \"явно указать каждому\". So I should clarify: the PUBLIC mechanism IS the way to avoid saying -lm for each task.\n\nBut the reason PUBLIC is needed: static lib doesn't link, so the dependency must be recorded as a requirement that each consumer's link step uses. That's precisely INTERFACE_LINK_LIBRARIES — \"whoever links me must also link m\".\n\nLet me also address: could the sqrt be in geometry.c? Let me check — geometry.c uses strtod, not sqrt. Actually in t1, sqrt is in main.c itself. t3/t4 use hypot. So actually sqrt/hypot are in the task mains, not in common lib. Hmm, so `m` is needed by main anyway. But the PUBLIC on common still demonstrates the mechanism. Actually for t1_c, main.c uses sqrt directly, so even without linking common_c_static, main.c.obj would need -lm. So the PUBLIC m through common works for t1_c's main.c reference too (since m ends up on the link line).\n\nThis is important: the reference to sqrt lives in main.c.obj, not necessarily in the static lib. So the \"static lib has everything main needs\" idea is doubly wrong — main's own sqrt reference also needs libm on the final link.\n\nLet me structure the answer:\n\n1. The misconception: static lib doesn't link. Explain archive = zip of .o. The link step that resolves sqrt happens only at final exe link, and needs -lm on THAT command line. Explain libm.so is shared/dynamic, cannot be \"inside\" the static lib.\n\n2. Then: so why PUBLIC? Because the requirement \"link with m\" must be attached to every consumer's final link. PUBLIC = record it in the library's interface, and every consumer automatically gets -lm. This is the alternative to writing -lm for each task manually. One place instead of many.\n\n3. Q2: No — `m` as bare name has no platform guard. It WILL also appear on Windows (as m.lib → link error). So it must be wrapped in a platform condition. Confirm her suspicion, and note this is the cross-platform axis we discussed.\n\nAlso note: the -lm is only needed on Linux; on Windows MSVC math is in CRT. And macOS has libm in libSystem (no -lm needed, but harmless). So condition: if(UNIX AND NOT APPLE) or just NOT WIN32... Actually on macOS -lm is a stub/symlink and works but unnecessary. For their matrix (Windows+Linux), `if(UNIX)` or `if(NOT WIN32)` suffices.\n\nI'm in plan mode, read only. No edits. Just explain and confirm plan. Let me keep it focused and educational.\n\nLet me use an analogy: static library = коробка с деталями (каталог деталей), а не собранный механизм. Сборка всего механизма происходит в конце, и там нужен весь набор смазок/болтов (libm).\n\nFormat: concise but clear. Two parts matching their two questions. Mark [ФАКТ].",
